              <description>Port A-H Pin[n] Rising Edge or High Level Interrupt Trigger Type Enable Bit\nThe RHIEN (Px_INTEN[n+16]) bit is used to enable the interrupt for each of the corresponding input Px.n pin. Set bit to 1 also enable the pin wake-up function. \nWhen setting the RHIEN (Px_INTEN[n+16]) bit to 1 :\nIf the interrupt is level trigger (TYPE (Px_INTTYPE[n]) bit is set to 1), the input Px.n pin will generate the interrupt while this pin state is at high level.\nIf the interrupt is edge trigger (TYPE (Px_INTTYPE[n]) bit is set to 0), the input Px.n pin will generate the interrupt while this pin state changed from low to high.\nNote: For more information about Px.n, please refer to the 'PIN CONFIGURATION' chapter.</description>
              <bitOffset>30</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Px.n level high or low to high interrupt Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Px.n level high or low to high interrupt Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RHIEN15</name>
              <description>Port A-H Pin[n] Rising Edge or High Level Interrupt Trigger Type Enable Bit\nThe RHIEN (Px_INTEN[n+16]) bit is used to enable the interrupt for each of the corresponding input Px.n pin. Set bit to 1 also enable the pin wake-up function. \nWhen setting the RHIEN (Px_INTEN[n+16]) bit to 1 :\nIf the interrupt is level trigger (TYPE (Px_INTTYPE[n]) bit is set to 1), the input Px.n pin will generate the interrupt while this pin state is at high level.\nIf the interrupt is edge trigger (TYPE (Px_INTTYPE[n]) bit is set to 0), the input Px.n pin will generate the interrupt while this pin state changed from low to high.\nNote: For more information about Px.n, please refer to the 'PIN CONFIGURATION' chapter.</description>
              <bitOffset>31</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Px.n level high or low to high interrupt Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Px.n level high or low to high interrupt Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
