            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>UARTx_TOUT</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>UARTx_TOUT</displayName>
          <description>UART Time-out Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x20</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>TOIC</name>
              <description>Time-out Interrupt Comparator</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>8</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>DLY</name>
              <description>TX Delay Time Value \nThis field is used to program the transfer delay time between the last STOP bit and next START bit. The unit is bit time.</description>
              <bitOffset>8</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>8</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BITOMEN</name>
              <description>Bus Idle Time-out Mode Enable Bit\nIf BITOMEN (UART_TOUT[31]) is enabled, the reset conditions of the time-out counter and RXTOIF (UART_INTSTS[4]) will be changed to detect the bus idle.\nWhen BITOMEN (UART_TOUT[31]) is disabled, the time-out counter and RXTOIF (UART_INTSTS[4]) maintain reset value whenever the RX FIFO is empty. In addition, Any RX FIFO activities, such as a new incoming data or user reading data, will clear RXTOIF (UART_INTSTS[4]) and reset RX time-out counter.. \nOn the other hand, when BITOMEN (UART_TOUT[31]) is enabled, the RX FIFO empty state will not reset the time-out counter and RXTOIF (UART_INTSTS[4]), and RX FIFO activities, such as a new incoming data or user reading data, will not clear RXTOIF (UART_INTSTS[4]). However, the time-out counter needs to receive a first incoming data before it can start counting. After receiving the first incoming data, the time-out counter will count even if the RX FIFO is empty.</description>
              <bitOffset>31</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Bus idle time-out mode Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Bus idle time-out mode Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>UARTx_BAUD</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>UARTx_BAUD</displayName>
          <description>UART Baud Rate Divider Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x24</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x0F0000FF</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>BRD</name>
              <description>Baud Rate Divider\nThe field indicates the baud rate divider. This filed is used in baud rate calculation. The detail description is shown in Table 6.214.</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>16</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BRFD</name>
              <description>Baud Rate Fractional Divider\nThis field is the fractional part of the baud rate divisor.  \nWhen BRFDEN (UART_BAUD[30]) is set, the Baud Rate Equation goes to UART_CLK / ((BRD+2) + (BRFD/256)). </description>
              <bitOffset>16</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>8</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>EDIVM1</name>
              <description>Extra Divider for BAUD Rate Mode 1\nThis field is used for baud rate calculation in mode 1 and has no effect for baud rate calculation in mode 0 and mode 2. The detail description is shown in Table 6.214.</description>
              <bitOffset>24</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>4</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BAUDM0</name>
              <description>BAUD Rate Mode Selection Bit 0\nThis bit is baud rate mode selection bit 0. UART provides three baud rate calculation modes. This bit combines with BAUDM1 (UART_BAUD[29]) to select baud rate calculation mode. The detail description is shown in Table 6.214.</description>
              <bitOffset>28</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BAUDM1</name>
              <description>BAUD Rate Mode Selection Bit 1\nThis bit is baud rate mode selection bit 1. UART provides three baud rate calculation modes. This bit combines with BAUDM0 (UART_BAUD[28]) to select baud rate calculation mode. The detail description is shown in Table 6.214.\nNote: In IrDA mode must be operated in mode 0.</description>
              <bitOffset>29</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BRFDEN</name>
              <description>Baud Rate Fractional Divider Enable Bit\nNote: This bit has effect only at baud rate mode 2 (BAUDM1 (UART_BAUD[29]) and BAUDM0 (UART_BAUD[28]) must be 1).</description>
              <bitOffset>30</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Baud Rate Fractional Divider Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Baud Rate Fractional Divider Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>UARTx_IRDA</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>UARTx_IRDA</displayName>
          <description>UART IrDA Control Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x28</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000040</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>TXEN</name>
              <description>IrDA Receiver/Transmitter Selection Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>1</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>IrDA Transmitter Disabled and Receiver Enabled. (Default)</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>IrDA Transmitter Enabled and Receiver Disabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TXINV</name>
              <description>IrDA Inverse Transmitting Output Signal \nNote 1: Before setting this bit, TXRXDIS (UART_FUNCSEL[3]) should be set then waited for TXRXACT (UART_FIFOSTS[31]) is cleared. When the configuration is done, cleared TXRXDIS (UART_FUNCSEL[3]) to activate UART controller.\nNote 2: This bit is valid when FUNCSEL (UART_FUNCSEL[2:0]) is select IrDA function.</description>
              <bitOffset>5</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>None inverse transmitting signal. (Default)</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Inverse transmitting output signal</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RXINV</name>
              <description>IrDA Inverse Receive Input Signal \nNote 1: Before setting this bit, TXRXDIS (UART_FUNCSEL[3]) should be set then waited for TXRXACT (UART_FIFOSTS[31]) is cleared. When the configuration is done, cleared TXRXDIS (UART_FUNCSEL[3]) to activate UART controller.\nNote 2: This bit is valid when FUNCSEL (UART_FUNCSEL[2:0]) is select IrDA function.</description>
              <bitOffset>6</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>None inverse receiving input signal</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Inverse receiving input signal. (Default)</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>UARTx_ALTCTL</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>UARTx_ALTCTL</displayName>
          <description>UART Alternate Control/Status Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x2C</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x0000000C</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>BRKFL</name>
              <description>UART LIN Break Field Length\nThis field indicates a 4-bit LIN TX break field count.\nNote 1: This break field length is BRKFL + 1.</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>4</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>LINRXEN</name>
              <description>LIN RX Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>6</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>LIN RX mode Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>LIN RX mode Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>LINTXEN</name>
              <description>LIN TX Break Mode Enable Bit\nNote: When TX break field transfer operation finished, this bit will be cleared automatically.</description>
              <bitOffset>7</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>LIN TX Break mode Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>LIN TX Break mode Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RS485NMM</name>
              <description>RS-485 Normal Multi-drop Operation Mode\nNote: It cannot be active with RS-485_AAD operation mode.</description>
              <bitOffset>8</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>RS-485 Normal Multi-drop Operation mode (NMM) Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>RS-485 Normal Multi-drop Operation mode (NMM) Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RS485AAD</name>
              <description>RS-485 Auto Address Detection Operation Mode\nNote: It cannot be active with RS-485_NMM operation mode.</description>
              <bitOffset>9</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>RS-485 Auto Address Detection Operation mode (AAD) Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>RS-485 Auto Address Detection Operation mode (AAD) Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RS485AUD</name>
              <description>RS-485 Auto Direction Function\nNote: It can be active with RS-485_AAD or RS-485_NMM operation mode.</description>
              <bitOffset>10</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>RS-485 Auto Direction Operation function (AUD) Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>RS-485 Auto Direction Operation function (AUD) Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ADDRDEN</name>
              <description>RS-485 Address Detection Enable Bit\nThis bit is used to enable RS-485 Address Detection mode. \nNote: This bit is used for RS-485 any operation mode.</description>
              <bitOffset>15</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Address detection mode Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Address detection mode Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ABRIF</name>
              <description>Auto-baud Rate Interrupt Flag (Read Only) \nThis bit is set when auto-baud rate detection function finished or the auto-baud rate counter was overflow and if ABRIEN(UART_INTEN[18]) is set then the auto-baud rate interrupt will be generated. \nNote: This bit is read only, but it can be cleared by writing '1' to ABRDTOIF (UART_FIFOSTS[2]) and ABRDIF(UART_FIFOSTS[1]).</description>
              <bitOffset>17</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No auto-baud rate interrupt flag is generated</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Auto-baud rate interrupt flag is generated</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ABRDEN</name>
              <description>Auto-baud Rate Detect Enable Bit\nNote: This bit is cleared automatically after auto-baud detection is finished.</description>
              <bitOffset>18</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Auto-baud rate detect function Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Auto-baud rate detect function Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ABRDBITS</name>
              <description>Auto-baud Rate Detect Bit Length \nNote: The calculation of bit number includes the START bit.</description>
              <bitOffset>19</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>2</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>1-bit time from START bit to the 1st rising edge. The input pattern shall be 0x01</description>
                    <value>#00</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>2-bit time from START bit to the 1st rising edge. The input pattern shall be 0x02</description>
                    <value>#01</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>2</name>
                    <description>4-bit time from START bit to the 1st rising edge. The input pattern shall be 0x08</description>
                    <value>#10</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>3</name>
                    <description>8-bit time from START bit to the 1st rising edge. The input pattern shall be 0x80</description>
                    <value>#11</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ADDRMV</name>
              <description>Address Match Value \nThis field contains the RS-485 address match values.\nNote: This field is used for RS-485 auto address detection mode.</description>
              <bitOffset>24</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>8</bitWidth>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>UARTx_FUNCSEL</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>UARTx_FUNCSEL</displayName>
          <description>UART Function Select Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x30</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>FUNCSEL</name>
              <description>Function Select</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>3</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>UART function</description>
                    <value>#000</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>LIN function</description>
                    <value>#001</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>2</name>
                    <description>IrDA function</description>
                    <value>#010</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>3</name>
                    <description>RS-485 function</description>
                    <value>#011</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>4</name>
                    <description>UART Single-wire function</description>
                    <value>#100</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TXRXDIS</name>
              <description>TX and RX Disable Bit\nSetting this bit can disable TX and RX.\nNote: The TX and RX will not be disabled immediately when this bit is set. The TX and RX complete current task before TX and RX are disabled. When TX and RX are disabled, the TXRXACT (UART_FIFOSTS[31]) is cleared.</description>
              <bitOffset>3</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>TX and RX Enabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>TX and RX Disabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>DGE</name>
              <description>Deglitch Enable Bit\nNote 1: When this bit is set to logic 1, any pulse width less than about 150 ns will be considered a glitch and will be removed in the serial data input (RX). This bit acts only on RX line and has no effect on the transmitter logic.\nNote 2: It is recommended to set this bit only when operating at baud rate under 2.5 Mbps.</description>
              <bitOffset>6</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Deglitch Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Deglitch Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TXRXSWP</name>
              <description>TX and RX Swap Enable Bit\nSetting this bit Swaps TX pin and RX pin.</description>
              <bitOffset>7</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>TX and RX Swap Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>TX and RX Swap Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>UARTx_LINCTL</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>UARTx_LINCTL</displayName>
          <description>UART LIN Control Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x34</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x000C0000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>SLVEN</name>
              <description>LIN Slave Mode Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>LIN slave mode Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>LIN slave mode Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SLVHDEN</name>
              <description>LIN Slave Header Detection Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>1</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>LIN slave header detection Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>LIN slave header detection Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SLVAREN</name>
              <description>LIN Slave Automatic Resynchronization Mode Enable Bit\nNote 2: When operation in Automatic Resynchronization mode, the baud rate setting must be mode 2 (BAUDM1 (UART_BAUD[29]) and BAUDM0 (UART_BAUD[28]) must be 1).\nNote 3: The control and interactions of this field are explained in 6.21.5.10 (Slave mode with automatic resynchronization).\nNote 4: The minimum detection range of the target BRD (UART_BAUD[15:0]) is 0x30.</description>
              <bitOffset>2</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>LIN automatic resynchronization Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>LIN automatic resynchronization Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SLVDUEN</name>
              <description>LIN Slave Divider Update Method Enable Bit\nNote 2: This bit used for LIN Slave Automatic Resynchronization mode. (for Non-Automatic Resynchronization mode, this bit should be kept cleared) \nNote 3: The control and interactions of this field are explained in 6.21.5.10 (Slave mode with automatic resynchronization).</description>
              <bitOffset>3</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>UART_BAUD updated is written by software (if no automatic resynchronization update occurs at the same time)</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>UART_BAUD is updated at the next received character. User must set the bit before checksum reception</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>MUTE</name>
              <description>LIN Mute Mode Enable Bit\nNote: The exit from mute mode condition and each control and interactions of this field are explained in 6.21.5.10 (LIN slave mode).</description>
              <bitOffset>4</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>LIN mute mode Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>LIN mute mode Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RTOUTEN</name>
              <description>LIN Response Time-out Detection Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>5</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>LIN response time-out detection Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>LIN response time-out detection Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SENDH</name>
              <description>LIN TX Send Header Enable Bit\nThe LIN TX header can be 'break field' or 'break and sync field' or 'break, sync and frame ID field', it is depend on setting HSEL (UART_LINCTL[23:22]).\nNote 1: This bit is shadow bit of LINTXEN (UART_ALTCTL[7]); user can read/write it by setting LINTXEN (UART_ALTCTL[7]) or SENDH (UART_LINCTL[8]).\nNote 2: When transmitter header field (it may be 'break' or 'break + sync' or 'break + sync + frame ID' selected by HSEL (UART_LINCTL[23:22]) field) transfer operation finished, this bit will be cleared automatically.</description>
              <bitOffset>8</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Send LIN TX header Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Send LIN TX header Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>IDPEN</name>
              <description>LIN ID Parity Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>9</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>LIN frame ID parity Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>LIN frame ID parity Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BRKDETEN</name>
              <description>LIN Break Detection Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>10</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>LIN break detection Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>LIN break detection Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>LINRXOFF</name>
              <description>LIN Receiver Disable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>11</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>LIN receiver Enabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>LIN receiver Disabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BITERREN</name>
              <description>Bit Error Detect Enable Bit </description>
              <bitOffset>12</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Bit error detection function Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Bit error detection function Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BRKFL</name>
              <description>LIN Break Field Length \nThis field indicates a 4-bit LIN TX break field count.\nNote 1: These registers are shadow registers of BRKFL (UART_ALTCTL[3:0]). User can read/write it by setting BRKFL (UART_ALTCTL[3:0]) or BRKFL (UART_LINCTL[19:16]).\nNote 2: This break field length is BRKFL + 1.</description>
              <bitOffset>16</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>4</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BSL</name>
              <description>LIN Break/Sync Delimiter Length \nNote: This bit used for LIN master to sending header field.</description>
              <bitOffset>20</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>2</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>The LIN break/sync delimiter length is 1-bit time</description>
                    <value>#00</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>The LIN break/sync delimiter length is 2-bit time</description>
                    <value>#01</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>2</name>
                    <description>The LIN break/sync delimiter length is 3-bit time</description>
                    <value>#10</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>3</name>
                    <description>The LIN break/sync delimiter length is 4-bit time</description>
                    <value>#11</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>HSEL</name>
              <description>LIN Header Select </description>
              <bitOffset>22</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>2</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>The LIN header includes 'break field'</description>
                    <value>#00</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>The LIN header includes 'break field' and 'sync field'</description>
                    <value>#01</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>2</name>
                    <description>The LIN header includes 'break field', 'sync field' and 'frame ID field'</description>
                    <value>#10</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>3</name>
                    <description>Reserved.</description>
                    <value>#11</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>PID</name>
              <description>LIN PID Bits\nIf the parity generated by hardware, user fill ID0~ID5 (PID[29:24]), hardware will calculate P0 (PID[30]) and P1 (PID[31]), otherwise user must filled frame ID and parity in this field.\nNote 1: User can fill any 8-bit value to this field and the bit 24 indicates ID0 (LSB first).\nNote 2: This field can be used for LIN master mode or slave mode.</description>
              <bitOffset>24</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>8</bitWidth>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>UARTx_LINSTS</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>UARTx_LINSTS</displayName>
          <description>UART LIN Status Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x38</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>SLVHDETF</name>
              <description>LIN Slave Header Detection Flag\nThis bit is set by hardware when a LIN header is detected in LIN slave mode and be cleared by writing 1 to it.\nNote 3: When enable ID parity check IDPEN (UART_LINCTL[9]), if hardware detect complete header ('break + sync + frame ID'), the SLVHDETF will be set whether the frame ID correct or not.</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>LIN header not detected</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>LIN header detected (break + sync + frame ID)</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SLVHEF</name>
              <description>LIN Slave Header Error Flag\nThis bit is set by hardware when a LIN header error is detected in LIN slave mode and be cleared by writing 1 to it. The header errors include 'break delimiter is too short (less than 0.5 bit time)', 'frame error in sync field or Identifier field', 'sync field data is not 0x55 in Non-Automatic Resynchronization mode', 'sync field deviation error with Automatic Resynchronization mode', 'sync field measure time-out with Automatic Resynchronization mode' and 'LIN header reception time-out'.</description>
              <bitOffset>1</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>LIN header error not detected</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>LIN header error detected</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SLVIDPEF</name>
              <description>LIN Slave ID Parity Error Flag \nThis bit is set by hardware when receipted frame ID parity is not correct.</description>
              <bitOffset>2</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No active</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Receipted frame ID parity is not correct</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SLVSYNCF</name>
              <description>LIN Slave Sync Field\nThis bit indicates that the LIN sync field is being analyzed in Automatic Resynchronization mode. When the receiver header have some error been detect, user must reset the internal circuit to re-search new frame header by writing 1 to this bit.\nNote 2: This bit can be cleared by writing 1 to it.\nNote 3: When writing 1 to it, hardware will reload the initial baud rate and re-search a new frame header.</description>
              <bitOffset>3</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>The current character is not at LIN sync state</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>The current character is at LIN sync state</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SLVHTOF</name>
              <description>LIN Slave Header Time-out Flag\nThis bit is set by hardware when a LIN header reception time-out is detected in LIN slave mode and be cleared by writing 1 to it. When this bit is set, SLVHEF (UART_LINSTS[1]) will also be set.</description>
              <bitOffset>4</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>LIN header time-out not detected</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>LIN header time-out detected</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RTOUTF</name>
              <description>LIN Response Time-out Flag\nThis bit is set when no LIN response received and the time-out counter equal to or bigger than LINRTOIC (UART_LINRTOUT[23:0]). If LINIEN (UART_INTEN[8]) is enabled, the LIN Bus Interrupt will be generated.\nNote 1: This bit can be cleared by writing 1 to it.</description>
              <bitOffset>5</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>LIN response time-out not detected</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>LIN response time-out detected</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BRKDETF</name>
              <description>LIN Break Detection Flag\nThis bit is set by hardware when a break is detected and be cleared by writing 1 to it through software.</description>
              <bitOffset>8</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>LIN break not detected</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>LIN break detected</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BITEF</name>
              <description>Bit Error Detect Status Flag \nAt TX transfer state, hardware will monitor the bus state, if the input pin (UART_RXD) state not equals to the output pin (UART_TXD) state, BITEF (UART_LINSTS[9]) will be set.</description>
              <bitOffset>9</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Bit error not detected</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Bit error detected</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>UARTx_BRCOMP</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>UARTx_BRCOMP</displayName>
          <description>UART Baud Rate Compensation Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x3C</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>BRCOMP</name>
              <description>Baud Rate Compensation Patten\nThese 9-bits are used to define the relative bit is compensated or not. \nBRCOMP[7:0] is used to define the compensation of DAT (UART_DAT[7:0]) and BRCOM[8] is used to define PARITY (UART_DAT[8]).</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>9</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BRCOMPDEC</name>
              <description>Baud Rate Compensation Decrease</description>
              <bitOffset>31</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Positive (increase one module clock) compensation for each compensated bit</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Negative (decrease one module clock) compensation for each compensated bit</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>UARTx_WKCTL</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>UARTx_WKCTL</displayName>
          <description>UART Wake-up Control Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x40</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>WKCTSEN</name>
              <description>nCTS Wake-up Enable Bit\nNote: When the system is in Power-down mode, an external nCTS change will wake up system from Power-down mode.</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>nCTS Wake-up system function Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>nCTS Wake-up system function Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>WKDATEN</name>
              <description>Incoming Data Wake-up Enable Bit\nNote: When the system is in Power-down mode, incoming data will wake-up system from Power-down mode.</description>
              <bitOffset>1</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Incoming data wake-up system function Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Incoming data wake-up system function Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>WKRFRTEN</name>
              <description>Received Data FIFO Reached Threshold Wake-up Enable Bit\nNote: When the system is in Power-down mode, Received Data FIFO reached threshold will wake-up system from Power-down mode.</description>
              <bitOffset>2</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Received Data FIFO reached threshold wake-up system function Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Received Data FIFO reached threshold wake-up system function Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>WKRS485EN</name>
              <description>RS-485 Address Match Wake-up Enable Bit\nNote 1: When the system is in Power-down mode, RS-485 Address Match will wake-up system from Power-down mode.\nNote 2: This bit is used for RS-485 Auto Address Detection (AAD) mode in RS-485 function mode and ADDRDEN (UART_ALTCTL[15]) is set to 1.</description>
              <bitOffset>3</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>RS-485 Address Match (AAD mode) wake-up system function Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>RS-485 Address Match (AAD mode) wake-up system function Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>WKTOUTEN</name>
              <description>Received Data FIFO Reached Threshold Time-out Wake-up Enable Bit\nNote 1: When the system is in Power-down mode, Received Data FIFO reached threshold time-out will wake up system from Power-down mode.\nNote 2: It is suggested the function is enabled when the WKRFRTEN (UART_WKCTL[2]) is set to 1.</description>
              <bitOffset>4</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Received Data FIFO reached threshold time-out wake-up system function Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Received Data FIFO reached threshold time-out wake-up system function Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>UARTx_WKSTS</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>UARTx_WKSTS</displayName>
          <description>UART Wake-up Status Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x44</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>CTSWKF</name>
              <description>nCTS Wake-up Flag\nThis bit is set if chip wake-up from power-down state by nCTS wake-up.\nNote 1: If WKCTSEN (UART_WKCTL[0]) is enabled, the nCTS wake-up cause this bit is set to '1'.\nNote 2: This bit can be cleared by writing '1' to it.</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Chip stays in power-down state</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Chip wake-up from power-down state by nCTS wake-up</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>DATWKF</name>
              <description>Incoming Data Wake-up Flag\nThis bit is set if chip wake-up from power-down state by data wake-up.\nNote 1: If WKDATEN (UART_WKCTL[1]) is enabled, the Incoming Data wake-up cause this bit is set to '1'.\nNote 2: This bit can be cleared by writing '1' to it.</description>
              <bitOffset>1</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Chip stays in power-down state</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Chip wake-up from power-down state by Incoming Data wake-up</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RFRTWKF</name>
              <description>Received Data FIFO Reached Threshold Wake-up Flag\nThis bit is set if chip wake-up from power-down state by Received Data FIFO reached threshold wake-up.\nNote 1: If WKRFRTEN (UART_WKCTL[2]) is enabled, the Received Data FIFO Reached Threshold wake-up cause this bit is set to '1'.\nNote 2: This bit can be cleared by writing '1' to it.</description>
              <bitOffset>2</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Chip stays in power-down state</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Chip wake-up from power-down state by Received Data FIFO Reached Threshold wake-up</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RS485WKF</name>
              <description>RS-485 Address Match Wake-up Flag\nThis bit is set if chip wake-up from power-down state by RS-485 Address Match (AAD mode).\nNote 1: If WKRS485EN (UART_WKCTL[3]) is enabled, the RS-485 Address Match (AAD mode) wake-up cause this bit is set to '1'.\nNote 2: This bit can be cleared by writing '1' to it.</description>
              <bitOffset>3</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Chip stays in power-down state</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Chip wake-up from power-down state by RS-485 Address Match (AAD mode) wake-up</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TOUTWKF</name>
              <description>Received Data FIFO Threshold Time-out Wake-up Flag\nThis bit is set if chip wake-up from power-down state by Received Data FIFO Threshold Time-out\nwake-up.\nNote 1: If WKTOUTEN (UART_WKCTL[4]) is enabled, the Received Data FIFO reached threshold time-out wake-up cause this bit is set to '1'.\nNote 2: This bit can be cleared by writing '1' to it.</description>
              <bitOffset>4</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Chip stays in power-down state</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Chip wake-up from power-down state by Received Data FIFO reached threshold time-out wake-up</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>UARTx_DWKCOMP</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>UARTx_DWKCOMP</displayName>
          <description>UART Incoming Data Wake-up Compensation Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x48</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>STCOMP</name>
              <description>START Bit Compensation Value\nThese bits field indicate how many clock cycle selected by UART_CLK do the UART controller can get the 1st bit (START bit) when the device is wake-up from Power-down mode.\nNote: It is valid only when WKDATEN (UART_WKCTL[1]) is set.</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>16</bitWidth>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>UARTx_RS485DD</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>UARTx_RS485DD</displayName>
          <description>UART RS485 Transceiver Deactivate Delay Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x4C</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>RTSDDLY</name>
              <description>RS485 Transceiver Deactivate Delay Value\nThese bits field indicate how many clock cycles selected by UART_CLK do the UART controller delay the RS485 transceiver state trancing when the state trancing of RS485 transceiver is from TX to RX state. These bits field have no effect when the state trancing of RS485 transceiver is from RX to TX state.\nNote: It is valid only when RS485AUD (UART_ALTCTL[10]) is set.</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>16</bitWidth>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>UARTx_LINRTOUT</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>UARTx_LINRTOUT</displayName>
          <description>UART LIN Response Time-out Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x50</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00FFFFFF</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>LINRTOIC</name>
              <description>LIN Response Time-out Comparator</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>24</bitWidth>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>UARTx_LINWKCTL</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>UARTx_LINWKCTL</displayName>
          <description>UART LIN Wake-up Control Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x54</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x000000C0</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>LINWKC</name>
              <description>LIN Send Wake-up Signal Length Counter</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>24</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SENDLINW</name>
              <description>LIN Send Wake-up Enable Bit\nNote 1: When this bit is set, the UART will send LIN wake-up automatically. When LIN wake-up transfer operation finished, this bit will be cleared automatically.</description>
              <bitOffset>24</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Send LIN Wake-up Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Send LIN Wake-up Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>LINWKEN</name>
              <description>LIN Wake-up Enable Bit\nNote 1: When the system is in Power-down mode, LIN wake-up event will wake up system from Power-down mode.</description>
              <bitOffset>28</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>LIN wake-up system function Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>LIN wake-up system function Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>LINWKF</name>
              <description>LIN Wake-up Flag\nThis bit is set if chip wake-up from power-down state by LIN wake-up.\nNote 1: If LINWKEN (UART_LINWKCTL[28]) is enabled, the LIN wake-up event will cause this bit set to '1'.\nNote 2: This bit can be cleared by writing '1' to it.</description>
              <bitOffset>29</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Chip stays in power-down state</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Chip wake-up from power-down state by LIN wake-up</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
      </registers>
    </peripheral>
    <peripheral>
      <name>UART5</name>
      <description>UART Register Map</description>
      <groupName>UART</groupName>
      <baseAddress>0x40075000</baseAddress>
      <!-- d -> no access permission -->
      <addressBlock>
        <offset>0x0</offset>
        <size>0x58</size>
        <usage>registers</usage>
      </addressBlock>
      <registers>
       <register>
          <name>UARTx_DAT</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>UARTx_DAT</displayName>
          <description>UART Receive/Transmit Buffer Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x0</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0x00000000</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>DAT</name>
              <description>Data Receive/Transmit Buffer\nWrite Operation:\nBy writing one byte to this register, the data byte will be stored in transmitter FIFO. The UART controller will send out the data stored in transmitter FIFO top location through the UART_TXD.\nRead Operation:\nBy reading this register, the UART controller will return an 8-bit data received from receiver FIFO.</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>8</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>PARITY</name>
              <description>PARITY Bit Receive/Transmit Buffer\nWrite Operation:\nBy writing to this bit, the PARITY bit will be stored in transmitter FIFO. If PBE (UART_LINE[3]) and PSS (UART_LINE[7]) are set, the UART controller will send out this bit follow the DAT (UART_DAT[7:0]) through the UART_TXD.\nRead Operation:\nIf PBE (UART_LINE[3]) and PSS (UART_LINE[7]) are enabled, the PARITY bit can be read by this bit.\nNote: This bit has effect only when PBE (UART_LINE[3]) and PSS (UART_LINE[7]) are set.</description>
              <bitOffset>8</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>UARTx_INTEN</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>UARTx_INTEN</displayName>
          <description>UART Interrupt Enable Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x4</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>RDAIEN</name>
              <description>Receive Data Available Interrupt Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Receive data available interrupt Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Receive data available interrupt Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>THREIEN</name>
              <description>Transmit Holding Register Empty Interrupt Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>1</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Transmit holding register empty interrupt Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Transmit holding register empty interrupt Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RLSIEN</name>
              <description>Receive Line Status Interrupt Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>2</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Receive Line Status interrupt Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Receive Line Status interrupt Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>MODEMIEN</name>
              <description>Modem Status Interrupt Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>3</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Modem status interrupt Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Modem status interrupt Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RXTOIEN</name>
              <description>RX Time-out Interrupt Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>4</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>RX time-out interrupt Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>RX time-out interrupt Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BUFERRIEN</name>
              <description>Buffer Error Interrupt Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>5</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Buffer error interrupt Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Buffer error interrupt Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>WKIEN</name>
              <description>Wake-up Interrupt Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>6</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Wake-up Interrupt Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Wake-up Interrupt Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>LINIEN</name>
              <description>LIN Bus Interrupt Enable Bit\nNote: This bit is used for LIN function mode.</description>
              <bitOffset>8</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>LIN bus interrupt Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>LIN bus interrupt Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TOCNTEN</name>
              <description>Receive Buffer Time-out Counter Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>11</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Receive Buffer Time-out counter Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Receive Buffer Time-out counter Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ATORTSEN</name>
              <description>nRTS Auto-flow Control Enable Bit\nNote: When nRTS auto-flow is enabled, if the number of bytes in the RX FIFO equals the RTSTRGLV (UART_FIFO[19:16]), the UART will de-assert nRTS signal.</description>
              <bitOffset>12</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>nRTS auto-flow control Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>nRTS auto-flow control Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ATOCTSEN</name>
              <description>nCTS Auto-flow Control Enable Bit\nNote: When nCTS auto-flow is enabled, the UART will send data to external device if nCTS input assert (UART will not send data to device until nCTS is asserted).</description>
              <bitOffset>13</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>nCTS auto-flow control Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>nCTS auto-flow control Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TXPDMAEN</name>
              <description>TX PDMA Enable Bit\nNote: If RLSIEN (UART_INTEN[2]) is enabled and HWRLSINT (UART_INTSTS[26]) is set to 1, the RLS (Receive Line Status) Interrupt is caused. If RLS interrupt is caused by Break Error Flag BIF(UART_FIFOSTS[6]), Frame Error Flag FEF(UART_FIFO[5]) or Parity Error Flag PEF(UART_FIFOSTS[4]), UART PDMA transmit request operation is stopped. Clear Break Error Flag BIF or Frame Error Flag FEF or Parity Error Flag PEF by writing '1' to corresponding BIF, FEF and PEF to make UART PDMA transmit request operation continue.</description>
              <bitOffset>14</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>TX PDMA Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>TX PDMA Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RXPDMAEN</name>
              <description>RX PDMA Enable Bit\nThis bit can enable or disable RX PDMA service.\nNote: If RLSIEN (UART_INTEN[2]) is enabled and HWRLSINT (UART_INTSTS[26]) is set to 1, the RLS (Receive Line Status) Interrupt is caused. If RLS interrupt is caused by Break Error Flag BIF(UART_FIFOSTS[6]), Frame Error Flag FEF(UART_FIFO[5]) or Parity Error Flag PEF(UART_FIFOSTS[4]), UART PDMA receive request operation is stopped. Clear Break Error Flag BIF or Frame Error Flag FEF or Parity Error Flag PEF by writing '1' to corresponding BIF, FEF and PEF to make UART PDMA receive request operation continue.</description>
              <bitOffset>15</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>RX PDMA Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>RX PDMA Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SWBEIEN</name>
              <description>Single-wire Bit Error Detection Interrupt Enable Bit\nSet this bit, the Single-wire Half Duplex Bit Error Detection Interrupt SWBEINT(UART_INTSTS[24]) is generated when Single-wire Bit Error Detection SWBEIF(UART_INTSTS[16]) is set.\nNote: This bit is valid when FUNCSEL (UART_FUNCSEL[2:0]) is select UART Single-wire mode.</description>
              <bitOffset>16</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Single-wire Bit Error Detect Interrupt Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Single-wire Bit Error Detect Interrupt Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ABRIEN</name>
              <description>Auto-baud Rate Interrupt Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>18</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Auto-baud rate interrupt Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Auto-baud rate interrupt Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TXENDIEN</name>
              <description>Transmitter Empty Interrupt Enable Bit\nIf TXENDIEN (UART_INTEN[22]) is enabled, the Transmitter Empty interrupt TXENDINT (UART_INTSTS[30]) will be generated when TXENDIF (UART_INTSTS[22]) is set (TX FIFO (UART_DAT) is empty and the STOP bit of the last byte has been transmitted).</description>
              <bitOffset>22</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Transmitter empty interrupt Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Transmitter empty interrupt Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>UARTx_FIFO</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>UARTx_FIFO</displayName>
          <description>UART FIFO Control Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x8</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000101</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>RXRST</name>
              <description>RX Field Software Reset\nWhen RXRST (UART_FIFO[1]) is set, all the byte in the receiver FIFO and RX internal state machine are cleared.\nNote 1: This bit will automatically clear at least 3 UART peripheral clock cycles.\nNote 2: Before setting this bit, it should wait for the RXIDLE (UART_FIFOSTS[29]) be set.</description>
              <bitOffset>1</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No effect</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Reset the RX internal state machine and pointers</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TXRST</name>
              <description>TX Field Software Reset\nWhen TXRST (UART_FIFO[2]) is set, all the byte in the transmit FIFO and TX internal state machine are cleared.\nNote 1: This bit will automatically clear at least 3 UART peripheral clock cycles.\nNote 2: Before setting this bit, it should wait for the TXEMPTYF (UART_FIFOSTS[28]) be set.</description>
              <bitOffset>2</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No effect</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Reset the TX internal state machine and pointers</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RFITL</name>
              <description>RX FIFO Interrupt Trigger Level\nWhen the number of bytes in the receive FIFO equals the RFITL, the RDAIF (UART_INTSTS[0]) will be set (if RDAIEN (UART_INTEN[0]) enabled, and an interrupt will be generated).</description>
              <bitOffset>4</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>4</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>RX FIFO Interrupt Trigger Level is 1 byte</description>
                    <value>#0000</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>RX FIFO Interrupt Trigger Level is 4 bytes</description>
                    <value>#0001</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>2</name>
                    <description>RX FIFO Interrupt Trigger Level is 8 bytes</description>
                    <value>#0010</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>3</name>
                    <description>RX FIFO Interrupt Trigger Level is 14 bytes</description>
                    <value>#0011</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RXOFF</name>
              <description>Receiver Disable Bit\nThe receiver is disabled or not (set 1 to disable receiver).\nNote: This bit is used for RS-485 Normal Multi-drop mode. It should be programmed before RS485NMM (UART_ALTCTL[8]) is programmed.</description>
              <bitOffset>8</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Receiver Enabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Receiver Disabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RTSTRGLV</name>
              <description>nRTS Trigger Level for Auto-flow Control\nNote: This field is used for auto nRTS flow control.</description>
              <bitOffset>16</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>4</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>nRTS Trigger Level is 1 byte</description>
                    <value>#0000</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>nRTS Trigger Level is 4 bytes</description>
                    <value>#0001</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>2</name>
                    <description>nRTS Trigger Level is 8 bytes</description>
                    <value>#0010</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>3</name>
                    <description>nRTS Trigger Level is 14 bytes</description>
                    <value>#0011</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>UARTx_LINE</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>UARTx_LINE</displayName>
          <description>UART Line Control Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0xC</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>WLS</name>
              <description>Word Length Selection\nThis field sets UART word length.</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>2</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>5 bits</description>
                    <value>#00</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>6 bits</description>
                    <value>#01</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>2</name>
                    <description>7 bits</description>
                    <value>#10</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>3</name>
                    <description>8 bits</description>
                    <value>#11</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>NSB</name>
              <description>Number of 'STOP Bit'</description>
              <bitOffset>2</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>One 'STOP bit' is generated in the transmitted data</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>When select 5-bit word length, 1.5 'STOP bit' is generated in the transmitted data. When select 6-, 7- and 8-bit word length, 2 'STOP bit' is generated in the transmitted data</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>PBE</name>
              <description>PARITY Bit Enable Bit\nNote: PARITY bit is generated on each outgoing character and is checked on each incoming data.</description>
              <bitOffset>3</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>PARITY bit generated Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>PARITY bit generated Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>EPE</name>
              <description>Even Parity Enable Bit\nNote: This bit has effect only when PBE (UART_LINE[3]) is set.</description>
              <bitOffset>4</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Odd number of logic 1's is transmitted and checked in each word</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Even number of logic 1's is transmitted and checked in each word</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SPE</name>
              <description>Stick Parity Enable Bit\nNote: If PBE (UART_LINE[3]) and EPE (UART_LINE[4]) are logic 1, the PARITY bit is transmitted and checked as logic 0. If PBE (UART_LINE[3]) is 1 and EPE (UART_LINE[4]) is 0 then the PARITY bit is transmitted and checked as 1.</description>
              <bitOffset>5</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Stick parity Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Stick parity Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BCB</name>
              <description>Break Control Bit\nNote: When this bit is set to logic 1, the transmitted serial data output (TX) is forced to the Spacing State (logic 0). This bit acts only on TX line and has no effect on the transmitter logic.</description>
              <bitOffset>6</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Break Control Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Break Control Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>PSS</name>
              <description>PARITY Bit Source Selection\nThe PARITY bit can be selected to be generated and checked automatically or by software.\nNote 1: This bit has effect only when PBE (UART_LINE[3]) is set.\nNote 2: If PSS is 0, the PARITY bit is transmitted and checked automatically. If PSS is 1, the transmitted PARITY bit value can be determined by writing PARITY (UART_DAT[8]) and the PARITY bit can be read by reading PARITY (UART_DAT[8]).</description>
              <bitOffset>7</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>PARITY bit is generated by EPE (UART_LINE[4]) and SPE (UART_LINE[5]) setting and checked automatically</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>PARITY bit generated and checked by software</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TXDINV</name>
              <description>TX Data Inverted\nNote 1: Before setting this bit, TXRXDIS (UART_FUNCSEL[3]) should be set then waited for TXRXACT (UART_FIFOSTS[31]) is cleared. When the configuration is done, cleared TXRXDIS (UART_FUNCSEL[3]) to activate UART controller.\nNote 2: This bit is valid when FUNCSEL (UART_FUNCSEL[2:0]) is select UART, LIN or RS485 function.</description>
              <bitOffset>8</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Transmitted data signal inverted Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Transmitted data signal inverted Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RXDINV</name>
              <description>RX Data Inverted\nNote 1: Before setting this bit, TXRXDIS (UART_FUNCSEL[3]) should be set then waited for TXRXACT (UART_FIFOSTS[31]) is cleared. When the configuration is done, cleared TXRXDIS (UART_FUNCSEL[3]) to activate UART controller.\nNote 2: This bit is valid when FUNCSEL (UART_FUNCSEL[2:0]) is select UART, LIN or RS485 function.</description>
              <bitOffset>9</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Received data signal inverted Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Received data signal inverted Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>UARTx_MODEM</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>UARTx_MODEM</displayName>
          <description>UART Modem Control Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x10</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000200</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>RTS</name>
              <description>nRTS Signal Control\nThis bit is direct control internal nRTS (Request-to-send) signal active or not, and then drive the nRTS pin output with RTSACTLV bit configuration.\nNote 1: The nRTS signal control bit is not effective when nRTS auto-flow control is enabled in UART function mode.\nNote 2: The nRTS signal control bit is not effective when RS-485 auto direction mode (AUD) is enabled in RS-485 function mode.\nNote 3: Single-wire mode is support this feature.</description>
              <bitOffset>1</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>nRTS signal is active</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>nRTS signal is inactive</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RTSACTLV</name>
              <description>nRTS Pin Active Level\nThis bit defines the active level state of nRTS pin output.\nNote 1: Refer to Figure 6.2113 and Figure 6.2114 for UART function mode.\nNote 2: Refer to Figure 6.2124 and Figure 6.2125 for RS-485 function mode.\nNote 3: Before setting this bit, TXRXDIS (UART_FUNCSEL[3]) should be set then waited for TXRXACT (UART_FIFOSTS[31]) is cleared. When the configuration is done, cleared TXRXDIS (UART_FUNCSEL[3]) to activate UART controller.</description>
              <bitOffset>9</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>nRTS pin output is high level active</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>nRTS pin output is low level active. (Default)</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RTSSTS</name>
              <description>nRTS Pin Status (Read Only)\nThis bit mirror from nRTS pin output of voltage logic status.</description>
              <bitOffset>13</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>nRTS pin output is low level voltage logic state</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>nRTS pin output is high level voltage logic state</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>UARTx_MODEMSTS</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>UARTx_MODEMSTS</displayName>
          <description>UART Modem Status Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x14</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000110</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>CTSDETF</name>
              <description>Detect nCTS State Change Flag\nThis bit is set whenever nCTS input has change state, and it will generate Modem interrupt to CPU when MODEMIEN (UART_INTEN[3]) is set to 1.\nNote: This bit can be cleared by writing '1' to it.</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>nCTS input has not change state</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>nCTS input has change state</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>CTSSTS</name>
              <description>nCTS Pin Status (Read Only)\nThis bit mirror from nCTS pin input of voltage logic status.\nNote: This bit echoes when UART controller peripheral clock is enabled, and nCTS multi-function port is selected.</description>
              <bitOffset>4</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>nCTS pin input is low level voltage logic state</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>nCTS pin input is high level voltage logic state</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>CTSACTLV</name>
              <description>nCTS Pin Active Level\nThis bit defines the active level state of nCTS pin input.\nNote: Before setting this bit, TXRXDIS (UART_FUNCSEL[3]) should be set then waited for TXRXACT (UART_FIFOSTS[31]) is cleared. When the configuration is done, cleared TXRXDIS (UART_FUNCSEL[3]) to activate UART controller.</description>
              <bitOffset>8</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>nCTS pin input is high level active</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>nCTS pin input is low level active. (Default)</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>UARTx_FIFOSTS</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>UARTx_FIFOSTS</displayName>
          <description>UART FIFO Status Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x18</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0xB0404000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>RXOVIF</name>
              <description>RX Overflow Error Interrupt Flag\nThis bit is set when RX FIFO overflow.\nIf the number of bytes of received data is greater than RX_FIFO (UART_DAT) size 16 bytes, this bit will be set.\nNote: This bit can be cleared by writing '1' to it.</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>RX FIFO is not overflow</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>RX FIFO is overflow</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ABRDIF</name>
              <description>Auto-baud Rate Detect Interrupt Flag\nThis bit is set to logic '1' when auto-baud rate detect function is finished.\nNote: This bit can be cleared by writing '1' to it.</description>
              <bitOffset>1</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Auto-baud rate detect function is not finished</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Auto-baud rate detect function is finished</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ABRDTOIF</name>
              <description>Auto-baud Rate Detect Time-out Interrupt Flag\nThis bit is set to logic '1' in Auto-baud Rate Detect mode when the baud rate counter is overflow.\nNote: This bit can be cleared by writing '1' to it.</description>
              <bitOffset>2</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Auto-baud rate counter is underflow</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Auto-baud rate counter is overflow</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ADDRDETF</name>
              <description>RS-485 Address Byte Detect Flag\nNote 1: This field is used for RS-485 function mode and ADDRDEN (UART_ALTCTL[15]) is set to 1 to enable Address detection mode.\nNote 2: This bit can be cleared by writing '1' to it.</description>
              <bitOffset>3</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Receiver detects a data that is not an address bit (bit 9 ='0')</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Receiver detects a data that is an address bit (bit 9 ='1')</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>PEF</name>
              <description>Parity Error Flag\nThis bit is set to logic 1 whenever the received character does not have a valid 'PARITY bit'.\nNote: This bit can be cleared by writing '1' to it.</description>
              <bitOffset>4</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No parity error is generated</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Parity error is generated</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>FEF</name>
              <description>Framing Error Flag\nThis bit is set to logic 1 whenever the received character does not have a valid 'STOP bit' (that is, the STOP bit following the last data bit or PARITY bit is detected as logic 0).\nNote: This bit can be cleared by writing '1' to it.</description>
              <bitOffset>5</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No framing error is generated</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Framing error is generated</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BIF</name>
              <description>Break Interrupt Flag\nThis bit is set to logic 1 whenever the received data input (RX) is held in the 'spacing state' (logic 0) for longer than a full word transmission time (that is, the total time of 'START bit' + data bits + parity + STOP bits).\nNote: This bit can be cleared by writing '1' to it.</description>
              <bitOffset>6</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No Break interrupt is generated</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Break interrupt is generated</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RXPTR</name>
              <description>RX FIFO Pointer (Read Only)\nThis field indicates the RX FIFO Buffer Pointer. When UART receives one byte from external device, RXPTR increases one. When one byte of RX FIFO is read by CPU, RXPTR decreases one.\nThe Maximum value shown in RXPTR is 15. When the using level of RX FIFO Buffer equal to 16, the RXFULL bit is set to 1 and RXPTR will show 0. As one byte of RX FIFO is read by CPU, the RXFULL bit is cleared to 0 and RXPTR will show 15.</description>
              <bitOffset>8</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>6</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RXEMPTY</name>
              <description>Receiver FIFO Empty (Read Only)\nThis bit initiate RX FIFO empty or not.\nNote: When the last byte of RX FIFO has been read by CPU, hardware sets this bit high. It will be cleared when UART receives any new data.</description>
              <bitOffset>14</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>RX FIFO is not empty</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>RX FIFO is empty</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RXFULL</name>
              <description>Receiver FIFO Full (Read Only)\nThis bit initiates RX FIFO full or not.\nNote: This bit is set when the number of usage in RX FIFO Buffer is equal to 16, otherwise it is cleared by hardware.</description>
              <bitOffset>15</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>RX FIFO is not full</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>RX FIFO is full</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TXPTR</name>
              <description>TX FIFO Pointer (Read Only)\nThis field indicates the TX FIFO Buffer Pointer. When CPU writes one byte into UART_DAT, TXPTR increases one. When one byte of TX FIFO is transferred to Transmitter Shift Register, TXPTR decreases one.\nThe Maximum value shown in TXPTR is 15. When the using level of TX FIFO Buffer equal to 16, the TXFULL bit is set to 1 and TXPTR will show 0. As one byte of TX FIFO is transferred to Transmitter Shift Register, the TXFULL bit is cleared to 0 and TXPTR will show 15.</description>
              <bitOffset>16</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>6</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TXEMPTY</name>
              <description>Transmitter FIFO Empty (Read Only)\nThis bit indicates TX FIFO empty or not.\nNote: When the last byte of TX FIFO has been transferred to Transmitter Shift Register, hardware sets this bit high. It will be cleared when writing data into UART_DAT (TX FIFO not empty).</description>
              <bitOffset>22</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>TX FIFO is not empty</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>TX FIFO is empty</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TXFULL</name>
              <description>Transmitter FIFO Full (Read Only)\nThis bit indicates TX FIFO full or not.\nNote: This bit is set when the number of usage in TX FIFO Buffer is equal to 16, otherwise it is cleared by hardware.</description>
              <bitOffset>23</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>TX FIFO is not full</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>TX FIFO is full</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TXOVIF</name>
              <description>TX Overflow Error Interrupt Flag\nIf TX FIFO (UART_DAT) is full, an additional write to UART_DAT will cause this bit to logic 1.\nNote: This bit can be cleared by writing '1' to it.</description>
              <bitOffset>24</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>TX FIFO is not overflow</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>TX FIFO is overflow</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TXEMPTYF</name>
              <description>Transmitter Empty Flag (Read Only)\nThis bit is set by hardware when TX FIFO (UART_DAT) is empty and the STOP bit of the last byte has been transmitted.\nNote: This bit is cleared automatically when TX FIFO is not empty or the last byte transmission has not completed.</description>
              <bitOffset>28</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>TX FIFO is not empty or the STOP bit of the last byte has been not transmitted</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>TX FIFO is empty and the STOP bit of the last byte has been transmitted</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RXIDLE</name>
              <description>RX Idle Status (Read Only)\nThis bit is set by hardware when RX is idle.</description>
              <bitOffset>29</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>RX is busy</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>RX is idle. (Default)</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TXRXACT</name>
              <description>TX and RX Active Status (Read Only)\nThis bit indicates TX and RX are active or inactive.\nNote: When TXRXDIS (UART_FUNCSEL[3]) is set and both TX and RX are in idle state, this bit is cleared. The UART controller cannot transmit or receive data at this moment. Otherwise this bit is set.</description>
              <bitOffset>31</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>TX and RX are inactive</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>TX and RX are active. (Default)</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>UARTx_INTSTS</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>UARTx_INTSTS</displayName>
          <description>UART Interrupt Status Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x1C</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00400002</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>RDAIF</name>
              <description>Receive Data Available Interrupt Flag (Read Only)\nWhen the number of bytes in the RX FIFO equals the RFITL then the RDAIF(UART_INTSTS[0]) will be set. If RDAIEN (UART_INTEN[0]) is enabled, the RDA interrupt will be generated.\nNote: This bit is read only and will be cleared when the number of unread bytes of RX FIFO drops below the threshold level (RFITL(UART_FIFO[7:4]).</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No RDA interrupt flag is generated</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>RDA interrupt flag is generated</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>THREIF</name>
              <description>Transmit Holding Register Empty Interrupt Flag (Read Only)\nThis bit is set when the last data of TX FIFO is transferred to Transmitter Shift Register. If THREIEN (UART_INTEN[1]) is enabled, the THRE interrupt will be generated.\nNote: This bit is read only and will be cleared when writing data into UART_DAT (TX FIFO is not empty).</description>
              <bitOffset>1</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No THRE interrupt flag is generated</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>THRE interrupt flag is generated</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RLSIF</name>
              <description>Receive Line Interrupt Flag (Read Only)\nThis bit is set when the RX receive data have parity error, frame error or break error (at least one of 3 bits, BIF(UART_FIFOSTS[6]), FEF(UART_FIFOSTS[5]) and PEF(UART_FIFOSTS[4]), is set). If RLSIEN (UART_INTEN[2]) is enabled, the RLS interrupt will be generated.\nNote 2: This bit is read only and reset to 0 when all bits of BIF (UART_FIFOSTS[6]), FEF(UART_FIFOSTS[5]) and PEF(UART_FIFOSTS[4]) are cleared.\nNote 3: In RS-485 function mode, this bit is read only and reset to 0 when all bits of BIF (UART_FIFOSTS[6]), FEF(UART_FIFOSTS[5]), PEF(UART_FIFOSTS[4]) and ADDRDETF (UART_FIFOSTS[3]) are cleared.\nNote 4: This bit is valid only when RXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[15]) and TXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[14]) are both 0.</description>
              <bitOffset>2</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No RLS interrupt flag is generated</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>RLS interrupt flag is generated</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>MODEMIF</name>
              <description>MODEM Interrupt Flag (Read Only)\nNote 1: This bit is read only and reset to 0 when bit CTSDETF is cleared by a write 1 on CTSDETF(UART_MODEMSTS[0]).\nNote 2: This bit is valid only when RXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[15]) and TXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[14]) are both 0.</description>
              <bitOffset>3</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No Modem interrupt flag is generated</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Modem interrupt flag is generated</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RXTOIF</name>
              <description>RX Time-out Interrupt Flag (Read Only)\nThis bit is set when the RX FIFO is not empty and no activities occurred in the RX FIFO and the time-out counter equal to TOIC (UART_TOUT[7:0]). If RXTOIEN (UART_INTEN[4]) is enabled, the RX time-out interrupt will be generated.\nNote 1: This bit can be cleared by writing '1' to it.\nNote 2: If BITOMEN (UART_TOUT[31]) is reset to 0, user can read UART_DAT (RX is in active) to clear this bit.\nNote 3: This bit is valid only when RXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[15]) and TXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[14]) are both 0.</description>
              <bitOffset>4</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No RX time-out interrupt flag is generated</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>RX time-out interrupt flag is generated</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BUFERRIF</name>
              <description>Buffer Error Interrupt Flag (Read Only)\nThis bit is set when the TX FIFO or RX FIFO overflows (TXOVIF (UART_FIFOSTS[24]) or RXOVIF (UART_FIFOSTS[0]) is set). When BUFERRIF (UART_INTSTS[5]) is set, the transfer is not correct. If BUFERRIEN (UART_INTEN[5]) is enabled, the buffer error interrupt will be generated.\nNote 1: This bit is cleared if both of RXOVIF(UART_FIFOSTS[0]) and TXOVIF(UART_FIFOSTS[24]) are cleared to 0 by writing 1 to RXOVIF(UART_FIFOSTS[0]) and TXOVIF(UART_FIFOSTS[24]).\nNote 2: This bit is valid only when RXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[15]) and TXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[14]) are both 0.</description>
              <bitOffset>5</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No buffer error interrupt flag is generated</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Buffer error interrupt flag is generated</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>WKIF</name>
              <description>UART Wake-up Interrupt Flag (Read Only)\nThis bit is set when LINWKF (UART_LINWKCTL[29]), TOUTWKF (UART_WKSTS[4]), RS485WKF (UART_WKSTS[3]), RFRTWKF (UART_WKSTS[2]), DATWKF (UART_WKSTS[1]) or CTSWKF(UART_WKSTS[0]) is set to 1.\nNote: This bit is cleared if all of LINWKF, TOUTWKF, RS485WKF, RFRTWKF, DATWKF and CTSWKF are cleared to 0 by writing 1 to the corresponding interrupt flag.</description>
              <bitOffset>6</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No UART wake-up interrupt flag is generated</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>UART wake-up interrupt flag is generated</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>LINIF</name>
              <description>LIN Bus Interrupt Flag\nNote: This bit is cleared when SLVHDETF (UART_LINSTS[0]), SLVHEF (UART_LINSTS[1]), SLVIDPEF (UART_LINSTS[2]), SLVHTOF (UART_LINSTS[4]), RTOUTF (UART_LINSTS[5]), BRKDETF (UART_LINSTS[8]), and BITEF (UART_LINSTS[9]) all are cleared and software writing '1' to LINIF (UART_INTSTS[7]).</description>
              <bitOffset>7</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>None of SLVHDETF, SLVHEF, SLVIDPEF, SLVHTOF, RTOUTF, BITEF, and BRKDETF is generated</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>At least one of SLVHDETF, SLVHEF, SLVIDPEF, SLVHTOF, RTOUTF, BITEF, and BRKDETF is generated</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RDAINT</name>
              <description>Receive Data Available Interrupt Indicator (Read Only)\nThis bit is set if RDAIEN (UART_INTEN[0]) and RDAIF (UART_INTSTS[0]) are both set to 1.</description>
              <bitOffset>8</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No RDA interrupt is generated</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>RDA interrupt is generated</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>THREINT</name>
              <description>Transmit Holding Register Empty Interrupt Indicator (Read Only)\nThis bit is set if THREIEN (UART_INTEN[1]) and THREIF(UART_INTSTS[1]) are both set to 1.</description>
              <bitOffset>9</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No THRE interrupt is generated</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>THRE interrupt is generated</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RLSINT</name>
              <description>Receive Line Status Interrupt Indicator (Read Only) \nThis bit is set if RLSIEN (UART_INTEN[2]) and RLSIF(UART_INTSTS[2]) are both set to 1.\nNote: This bit is valid only when RXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[15]) and TXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[14]) are both 0.</description>
              <bitOffset>10</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No RLS interrupt is generated</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>RLS interrupt is generated</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>MODEMINT</name>
              <description>MODEM Status Interrupt Indicator (Read Only)\nThis bit is set if MODEMIEN(UART_INTEN[3]) and MODEMIF(UART_INTSTS[3]) are both set to 1\nNote: This bit is valid only when RXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[15]) and TXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[14]) are both 0.</description>
              <bitOffset>11</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No Modem interrupt is generated</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Modem interrupt is generated</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RXTOINT</name>
              <description>RX Time-out Interrupt Indicator (Read Only)\nThis bit is set if RXTOIEN (UART_INTEN[4]) and RXTOIF(UART_INTSTS[4]) are both set to 1.\nNote: This bit is valid only when RXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[15]) and TXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[14]) are both 0.</description>
              <bitOffset>12</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No RX time-out interrupt is generated</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>RX time-out interrupt is generated</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BUFERRINT</name>
              <description>Buffer Error Interrupt Indicator (Read Only)\nThis bit is set if BUFERRIEN(UART_INTEN[5]) and BUFERRIF(UART_ INTSTS[5]) are both set to 1.\nNote: This bit is valid only when RXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[15]) and TXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[14]) are both 0.</description>
              <bitOffset>13</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No buffer error interrupt is generated</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Buffer error interrupt is generated</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>WKINT</name>
              <description>UART Wake-up Interrupt Indicator (Read Only)\nThis bit is set if WKIEN (UART_INTEN[6]) and WKIF (UART_INTSTS[6]) are both set to 1.</description>
              <bitOffset>14</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No UART wake-up interrupt is generated</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>UART wake-up interrupt is generated</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>LININT</name>
              <description>LIN Bus Interrupt Indicator (Read Only)\nThis bit is set if LINIEN (UART_INTEN[8]) and LINIF(UART_INTSTS[7]) are both set to 1.</description>
              <bitOffset>15</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No LIN Bus interrupt is generated</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>The LIN Bus interrupt is generated</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SWBEIF</name>
              <description>Single-wire Bit Error Detection Interrupt Flag\nThis bit is set when the single wire bus state not equals to UART controller TX state in Single-wire mode.\nNote 1: This bit is active when FUNCSEL (UART_FUNCSEL[2:0]) is select UART Single-wire mode.\nNote 2: This bit can be cleared by writing '1' to it.</description>
              <bitOffset>16</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No single-wire bit error detection interrupt flag is generated</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Single-wire bit error detection interrupt flag is generated</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>HWRLSIF</name>
              <description>PDMA Mode Receive Line Status Flag (Read Only)\nThis bit is set when the RX receive data have parity error, frame error or break error (at least one of 3 bits, BIF (UART_FIFOSTS[6]), FEF (UART_FIFOSTS[5]) and PEF (UART_FIFOSTS[4]) is set). If RLSIEN (UART_INTEN[2]) is enabled, the RLS interrupt will be generated.\nNote 2: In UART function mode, this bit is read only and reset to 0 when all bits of BIF(UART_FIFOSTS[6]), FEF(UART_FIFOSTS[5]) and PEF(UART_FIFOSTS[4]) are cleared. \nNote 3: In RS-485 function mode, this bit is read only and reset to 0 when all bits of BIF(UART_FIFOSTS[6]), FEF(UART_FIFOSTS[5]), PEF(UART_FIFOSTS[4]) and ADDRDETF (UART_FIFOSTS[3]) are cleared.</description>
              <bitOffset>18</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No RLS interrupt flag is generated when RXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[15]) or TXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[14]) is 1</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>RLS interrupt flag is generated when RXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[15]) or TXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[14]) is 1</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>HWMODIF</name>
              <description>PDMA Mode MODEM Interrupt Flag (Read Only)\nNote: This bit is read only and reset to 0 when the bit CTSDETF (UART_MODEMSTS[0]) is cleared by writing 1 on CTSDETF (UART_MODEMSTS[0]).</description>
              <bitOffset>19</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No Modem interrupt flag is generated when RXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[15]) or TXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[14]) is 1</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Modem interrupt flag is generated when RXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[15]) or TXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[14]) is 1</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>HWTOIF</name>
              <description>PDMA Mode RX Time-out Interrupt Flag (Read Only)\nThis bit is set when the RX FIFO is not empty and no activities occurred in the RX FIFO and the time-out counter equal to TOIC (UART_TOUT[7:0]). If RXTOIEN (UART_INTEN[4]) is enabled, the RX time-out interrupt will be generated. \nNote: This bit is read only and user can read UART_DAT (RX is in active) to clear it.</description>
              <bitOffset>20</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No RX time-out interrupt flag is generated when RXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[15]) or TXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[14]) is 1</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>RX time-out interrupt flag is generated when RXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[15]) or TXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[14]) is 1</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>HWBUFEIF</name>
              <description>PDMA Mode Buffer Error Interrupt Flag (Read Only)\nThis bit is set when the TX or RX FIFO overflows (TXOVIF (UART_FIFOSTS[24]) or RXOVIF (UART_FIFOSTS[0]) is set). When BUFERRIF (UART_INTSTS[5]) is set, the transfer maybe is not correct. If BUFERRIEN (UART_INTEN[5]) is enabled, the buffer error interrupt will be generated.\nNote: This bit is cleared when both TXOVIF (UART_FIFOSTS[24]]) and RXOVIF  (UART_FIFOSTS[0]) are cleared.</description>
              <bitOffset>21</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No buffer error interrupt flag is generated when RXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[15]) or TXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[14]) is 1</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Buffer error interrupt flag is generated when RXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[15]) or TXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[14]) is 1</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TXENDIF</name>
              <description>Transmitter Empty Interrupt Flag (Read Only)\nThis bit is set when TX FIFO (UART_DAT) is empty and the STOP bit of the last byte has been transmitted (TXEMPTYF (UART_FIFOSTS[28]) is set). If TXENDIEN (UART_INTEN[22]) is enabled, the Transmitter Empty interrupt will be generated.\nNote: This bit is cleared automatically when TX FIFO is not empty or the last byte transmission has not completed.</description>
              <bitOffset>22</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No transmitter empty interrupt flag is generated</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Transmitter empty interrupt flag is generated</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SWBEINT</name>
              <description>Single-wire Bit Error Detect Interrupt Indicator (Read Only)\nThis bit is set if SWBEIEN (UART_INTEN[16]) and SWBEIF (UART_INTSTS[16]) are both set to 1.</description>
              <bitOffset>24</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No Single-wire Bit Error Detection Interrupt generated</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Single-wire Bit Error Detection Interrupt generated</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>HWRLSINT</name>
              <description>PDMA Mode Receive Line Status Interrupt Indicator (Read Only)\nThis bit is set if RLSIEN (UART_INTEN[2]) and HWRLSIF(UART_INTSTS[18]) are both set to 1.</description>
              <bitOffset>26</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No RLS interrupt is generated when RXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[15]) or TXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[14]) is 1</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>RLS interrupt is generated when RXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[15]) or TXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[14]) is 1</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>HWMODINT</name>
              <description>PDMA Mode MODEM Status Interrupt Indicator (Read Only)\nThis bit is set if MODEMIEN (UART_INTEN[3]) and HWMODIF(UART_INTSTS[19]) are both set to 1.</description>
              <bitOffset>27</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No Modem interrupt is generated when RXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[15]) or TXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[14]) is 1</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Modem interrupt is generated when RXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[15]) or TXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[14]) is 1</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>HWTOINT</name>
              <description>PDMA Mode RX Time-out Interrupt Indicator (Read Only)\nThis bit is set if RXTOIEN (UART_INTEN[4]) and HWTOIF(UART_INTSTS[20]) are both set to 1.</description>
              <bitOffset>28</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No RX time-out interrupt is generated when RXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[15]) or TXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[14]) is 1</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>RX time-out interrupt is generated when RXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[15]) or TXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[14]) is 1</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>HWBUFEINT</name>
              <description>PDMA Mode Buffer Error Interrupt Indicator (Read Only)\nThis bit is set if BUFERRIEN (UART_INTEN[5]) and HWBUFEIF (UART_INTSTS[21]) are both set to 1.</description>
              <bitOffset>29</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No buffer error interrupt is generated when RXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[15]) or TXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[14]) is 1</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Buffer error interrupt is generated when RXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[15]) or TXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[14]) is 1</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TXENDINT</name>
              <description>Transmitter Empty Interrupt Indicator (Read Only) \nThis bit is set if TXENDIEN (UART_INTEN[22]) and TXENDIF(UART_INTSTS[22]) are both set to 1.</description>
              <bitOffset>30</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No Transmitter Empty interrupt is generated</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Transmitter Empty interrupt is generated</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ABRINT</name>
              <description>Auto-baud Rate Interrupt Indicator (Read Only)\nThis bit is set if ABRIEN (UART_INTEN[18]) and ABRIF (UART_ALTCTL[17]) are both set to 1.</description>
              <bitOffset>31</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No Auto-baud Rate interrupt is generated</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>The Auto-baud Rate interrupt is generated</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>UARTx_TOUT</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>UARTx_TOUT</displayName>
          <description>UART Time-out Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x20</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>TOIC</name>
              <description>Time-out Interrupt Comparator</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>8</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>DLY</name>
              <description>TX Delay Time Value \nThis field is used to program the transfer delay time between the last STOP bit and next START bit. The unit is bit time.</description>
              <bitOffset>8</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>8</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BITOMEN</name>
              <description>Bus Idle Time-out Mode Enable Bit\nIf BITOMEN (UART_TOUT[31]) is enabled, the reset conditions of the time-out counter and RXTOIF (UART_INTSTS[4]) will be changed to detect the bus idle.\nWhen BITOMEN (UART_TOUT[31]) is disabled, the time-out counter and RXTOIF (UART_INTSTS[4]) maintain reset value whenever the RX FIFO is empty. In addition, Any RX FIFO activities, such as a new incoming data or user reading data, will clear RXTOIF (UART_INTSTS[4]) and reset RX time-out counter.. \nOn the other hand, when BITOMEN (UART_TOUT[31]) is enabled, the RX FIFO empty state will not reset the time-out counter and RXTOIF (UART_INTSTS[4]), and RX FIFO activities, such as a new incoming data or user reading data, will not clear RXTOIF (UART_INTSTS[4]). However, the time-out counter needs to receive a first incoming data before it can start counting. After receiving the first incoming data, the time-out counter will count even if the RX FIFO is empty.</description>
              <bitOffset>31</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Bus idle time-out mode Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Bus idle time-out mode Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>UARTx_BAUD</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>UARTx_BAUD</displayName>
          <description>UART Baud Rate Divider Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x24</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x0F0000FF</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>BRD</name>
              <description>Baud Rate Divider\nThe field indicates the baud rate divider. This filed is used in baud rate calculation. The detail description is shown in Table 6.214.</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>16</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BRFD</name>
              <description>Baud Rate Fractional Divider\nThis field is the fractional part of the baud rate divisor.  \nWhen BRFDEN (UART_BAUD[30]) is set, the Baud Rate Equation goes to UART_CLK / ((BRD+2) + (BRFD/256)). </description>
              <bitOffset>16</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>8</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>EDIVM1</name>
              <description>Extra Divider for BAUD Rate Mode 1\nThis field is used for baud rate calculation in mode 1 and has no effect for baud rate calculation in mode 0 and mode 2. The detail description is shown in Table 6.214.</description>
              <bitOffset>24</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>4</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BAUDM0</name>
              <description>BAUD Rate Mode Selection Bit 0\nThis bit is baud rate mode selection bit 0. UART provides three baud rate calculation modes. This bit combines with BAUDM1 (UART_BAUD[29]) to select baud rate calculation mode. The detail description is shown in Table 6.214.</description>
              <bitOffset>28</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BAUDM1</name>
              <description>BAUD Rate Mode Selection Bit 1\nThis bit is baud rate mode selection bit 1. UART provides three baud rate calculation modes. This bit combines with BAUDM0 (UART_BAUD[28]) to select baud rate calculation mode. The detail description is shown in Table 6.214.\nNote: In IrDA mode must be operated in mode 0.</description>
              <bitOffset>29</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BRFDEN</name>
              <description>Baud Rate Fractional Divider Enable Bit\nNote: This bit has effect only at baud rate mode 2 (BAUDM1 (UART_BAUD[29]) and BAUDM0 (UART_BAUD[28]) must be 1).</description>
              <bitOffset>30</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Baud Rate Fractional Divider Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Baud Rate Fractional Divider Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>UARTx_IRDA</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>UARTx_IRDA</displayName>
          <description>UART IrDA Control Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x28</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000040</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>TXEN</name>
              <description>IrDA Receiver/Transmitter Selection Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>1</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>IrDA Transmitter Disabled and Receiver Enabled. (Default)</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>IrDA Transmitter Enabled and Receiver Disabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TXINV</name>
              <description>IrDA Inverse Transmitting Output Signal \nNote 1: Before setting this bit, TXRXDIS (UART_FUNCSEL[3]) should be set then waited for TXRXACT (UART_FIFOSTS[31]) is cleared. When the configuration is done, cleared TXRXDIS (UART_FUNCSEL[3]) to activate UART controller.\nNote 2: This bit is valid when FUNCSEL (UART_FUNCSEL[2:0]) is select IrDA function.</description>
              <bitOffset>5</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>None inverse transmitting signal. (Default)</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Inverse transmitting output signal</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RXINV</name>
              <description>IrDA Inverse Receive Input Signal \nNote 1: Before setting this bit, TXRXDIS (UART_FUNCSEL[3]) should be set then waited for TXRXACT (UART_FIFOSTS[31]) is cleared. When the configuration is done, cleared TXRXDIS (UART_FUNCSEL[3]) to activate UART controller.\nNote 2: This bit is valid when FUNCSEL (UART_FUNCSEL[2:0]) is select IrDA function.</description>
              <bitOffset>6</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>None inverse receiving input signal</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Inverse receiving input signal. (Default)</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>UARTx_ALTCTL</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>UARTx_ALTCTL</displayName>
          <description>UART Alternate Control/Status Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x2C</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x0000000C</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>BRKFL</name>
              <description>UART LIN Break Field Length\nThis field indicates a 4-bit LIN TX break field count.\nNote 1: This break field length is BRKFL + 1.</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>4</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>LINRXEN</name>
              <description>LIN RX Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>6</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>LIN RX mode Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>LIN RX mode Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>LINTXEN</name>
              <description>LIN TX Break Mode Enable Bit\nNote: When TX break field transfer operation finished, this bit will be cleared automatically.</description>
              <bitOffset>7</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>LIN TX Break mode Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>LIN TX Break mode Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RS485NMM</name>
              <description>RS-485 Normal Multi-drop Operation Mode\nNote: It cannot be active with RS-485_AAD operation mode.</description>
              <bitOffset>8</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>RS-485 Normal Multi-drop Operation mode (NMM) Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>RS-485 Normal Multi-drop Operation mode (NMM) Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RS485AAD</name>
              <description>RS-485 Auto Address Detection Operation Mode\nNote: It cannot be active with RS-485_NMM operation mode.</description>
              <bitOffset>9</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>RS-485 Auto Address Detection Operation mode (AAD) Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>RS-485 Auto Address Detection Operation mode (AAD) Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RS485AUD</name>
              <description>RS-485 Auto Direction Function\nNote: It can be active with RS-485_AAD or RS-485_NMM operation mode.</description>
              <bitOffset>10</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>RS-485 Auto Direction Operation function (AUD) Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>RS-485 Auto Direction Operation function (AUD) Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ADDRDEN</name>
              <description>RS-485 Address Detection Enable Bit\nThis bit is used to enable RS-485 Address Detection mode. \nNote: This bit is used for RS-485 any operation mode.</description>
              <bitOffset>15</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Address detection mode Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Address detection mode Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ABRIF</name>
              <description>Auto-baud Rate Interrupt Flag (Read Only) \nThis bit is set when auto-baud rate detection function finished or the auto-baud rate counter was overflow and if ABRIEN(UART_INTEN[18]) is set then the auto-baud rate interrupt will be generated. \nNote: This bit is read only, but it can be cleared by writing '1' to ABRDTOIF (UART_FIFOSTS[2]) and ABRDIF(UART_FIFOSTS[1]).</description>
              <bitOffset>17</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No auto-baud rate interrupt flag is generated</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Auto-baud rate interrupt flag is generated</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ABRDEN</name>
              <description>Auto-baud Rate Detect Enable Bit\nNote: This bit is cleared automatically after auto-baud detection is finished.</description>
              <bitOffset>18</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Auto-baud rate detect function Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Auto-baud rate detect function Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ABRDBITS</name>
              <description>Auto-baud Rate Detect Bit Length \nNote: The calculation of bit number includes the START bit.</description>
              <bitOffset>19</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>2</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>1-bit time from START bit to the 1st rising edge. The input pattern shall be 0x01</description>
                    <value>#00</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>2-bit time from START bit to the 1st rising edge. The input pattern shall be 0x02</description>
                    <value>#01</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>2</name>
                    <description>4-bit time from START bit to the 1st rising edge. The input pattern shall be 0x08</description>
                    <value>#10</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>3</name>
                    <description>8-bit time from START bit to the 1st rising edge. The input pattern shall be 0x80</description>
                    <value>#11</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ADDRMV</name>
              <description>Address Match Value \nThis field contains the RS-485 address match values.\nNote: This field is used for RS-485 auto address detection mode.</description>
              <bitOffset>24</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>8</bitWidth>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>UARTx_FUNCSEL</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>UARTx_FUNCSEL</displayName>
          <description>UART Function Select Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x30</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>FUNCSEL</name>
              <description>Function Select</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>3</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>UART function</description>
                    <value>#000</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>LIN function</description>
                    <value>#001</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>2</name>
                    <description>IrDA function</description>
                    <value>#010</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>3</name>
                    <description>RS-485 function</description>
                    <value>#011</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>4</name>
                    <description>UART Single-wire function</description>
                    <value>#100</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TXRXDIS</name>
              <description>TX and RX Disable Bit\nSetting this bit can disable TX and RX.\nNote: The TX and RX will not be disabled immediately when this bit is set. The TX and RX complete current task before TX and RX are disabled. When TX and RX are disabled, the TXRXACT (UART_FIFOSTS[31]) is cleared.</description>
              <bitOffset>3</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>TX and RX Enabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>TX and RX Disabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>DGE</name>
              <description>Deglitch Enable Bit\nNote 1: When this bit is set to logic 1, any pulse width less than about 150 ns will be considered a glitch and will be removed in the serial data input (RX). This bit acts only on RX line and has no effect on the transmitter logic.\nNote 2: It is recommended to set this bit only when operating at baud rate under 2.5 Mbps.</description>
              <bitOffset>6</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Deglitch Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Deglitch Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TXRXSWP</name>
              <description>TX and RX Swap Enable Bit\nSetting this bit Swaps TX pin and RX pin.</description>
              <bitOffset>7</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>TX and RX Swap Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>TX and RX Swap Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>UARTx_LINCTL</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>UARTx_LINCTL</displayName>
          <description>UART LIN Control Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x34</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x000C0000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>SLVEN</name>
              <description>LIN Slave Mode Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>LIN slave mode Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>LIN slave mode Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SLVHDEN</name>
              <description>LIN Slave Header Detection Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>1</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>LIN slave header detection Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>LIN slave header detection Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SLVAREN</name>
              <description>LIN Slave Automatic Resynchronization Mode Enable Bit\nNote 2: When operation in Automatic Resynchronization mode, the baud rate setting must be mode 2 (BAUDM1 (UART_BAUD[29]) and BAUDM0 (UART_BAUD[28]) must be 1).\nNote 3: The control and interactions of this field are explained in 6.21.5.10 (Slave mode with automatic resynchronization).\nNote 4: The minimum detection range of the target BRD (UART_BAUD[15:0]) is 0x30.</description>
              <bitOffset>2</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>LIN automatic resynchronization Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>LIN automatic resynchronization Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SLVDUEN</name>
              <description>LIN Slave Divider Update Method Enable Bit\nNote 2: This bit used for LIN Slave Automatic Resynchronization mode. (for Non-Automatic Resynchronization mode, this bit should be kept cleared) \nNote 3: The control and interactions of this field are explained in 6.21.5.10 (Slave mode with automatic resynchronization).</description>
              <bitOffset>3</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>UART_BAUD updated is written by software (if no automatic resynchronization update occurs at the same time)</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>UART_BAUD is updated at the next received character. User must set the bit before checksum reception</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>MUTE</name>
              <description>LIN Mute Mode Enable Bit\nNote: The exit from mute mode condition and each control and interactions of this field are explained in 6.21.5.10 (LIN slave mode).</description>
              <bitOffset>4</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>LIN mute mode Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>LIN mute mode Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RTOUTEN</name>
              <description>LIN Response Time-out Detection Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>5</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>LIN response time-out detection Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>LIN response time-out detection Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SENDH</name>
              <description>LIN TX Send Header Enable Bit\nThe LIN TX header can be 'break field' or 'break and sync field' or 'break, sync and frame ID field', it is depend on setting HSEL (UART_LINCTL[23:22]).\nNote 1: This bit is shadow bit of LINTXEN (UART_ALTCTL[7]); user can read/write it by setting LINTXEN (UART_ALTCTL[7]) or SENDH (UART_LINCTL[8]).\nNote 2: When transmitter header field (it may be 'break' or 'break + sync' or 'break + sync + frame ID' selected by HSEL (UART_LINCTL[23:22]) field) transfer operation finished, this bit will be cleared automatically.</description>
              <bitOffset>8</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Send LIN TX header Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Send LIN TX header Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>IDPEN</name>
              <description>LIN ID Parity Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>9</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>LIN frame ID parity Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>LIN frame ID parity Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BRKDETEN</name>
              <description>LIN Break Detection Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>10</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>LIN break detection Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>LIN break detection Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>LINRXOFF</name>
              <description>LIN Receiver Disable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>11</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>LIN receiver Enabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>LIN receiver Disabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BITERREN</name>
              <description>Bit Error Detect Enable Bit </description>
              <bitOffset>12</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Bit error detection function Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Bit error detection function Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BRKFL</name>
              <description>LIN Break Field Length \nThis field indicates a 4-bit LIN TX break field count.\nNote 1: These registers are shadow registers of BRKFL (UART_ALTCTL[3:0]). User can read/write it by setting BRKFL (UART_ALTCTL[3:0]) or BRKFL (UART_LINCTL[19:16]).\nNote 2: This break field length is BRKFL + 1.</description>
              <bitOffset>16</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>4</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BSL</name>
              <description>LIN Break/Sync Delimiter Length \nNote: This bit used for LIN master to sending header field.</description>
              <bitOffset>20</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>2</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>The LIN break/sync delimiter length is 1-bit time</description>
                    <value>#00</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>The LIN break/sync delimiter length is 2-bit time</description>
                    <value>#01</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>2</name>
                    <description>The LIN break/sync delimiter length is 3-bit time</description>
                    <value>#10</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>3</name>
                    <description>The LIN break/sync delimiter length is 4-bit time</description>
                    <value>#11</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>HSEL</name>
              <description>LIN Header Select </description>
              <bitOffset>22</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>2</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>The LIN header includes 'break field'</description>
                    <value>#00</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>The LIN header includes 'break field' and 'sync field'</description>
                    <value>#01</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>2</name>
                    <description>The LIN header includes 'break field', 'sync field' and 'frame ID field'</description>
                    <value>#10</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>3</name>
                    <description>Reserved.</description>
                    <value>#11</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>PID</name>
              <description>LIN PID Bits\nIf the parity generated by hardware, user fill ID0~ID5 (PID[29:24]), hardware will calculate P0 (PID[30]) and P1 (PID[31]), otherwise user must filled frame ID and parity in this field.\nNote 1: User can fill any 8-bit value to this field and the bit 24 indicates ID0 (LSB first).\nNote 2: This field can be used for LIN master mode or slave mode.</description>
              <bitOffset>24</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>8</bitWidth>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>UARTx_LINSTS</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>UARTx_LINSTS</displayName>
          <description>UART LIN Status Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x38</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>SLVHDETF</name>
              <description>LIN Slave Header Detection Flag\nThis bit is set by hardware when a LIN header is detected in LIN slave mode and be cleared by writing 1 to it.\nNote 3: When enable ID parity check IDPEN (UART_LINCTL[9]), if hardware detect complete header ('break + sync + frame ID'), the SLVHDETF will be set whether the frame ID correct or not.</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>LIN header not detected</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>LIN header detected (break + sync + frame ID)</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SLVHEF</name>
              <description>LIN Slave Header Error Flag\nThis bit is set by hardware when a LIN header error is detected in LIN slave mode and be cleared by writing 1 to it. The header errors include 'break delimiter is too short (less than 0.5 bit time)', 'frame error in sync field or Identifier field', 'sync field data is not 0x55 in Non-Automatic Resynchronization mode', 'sync field deviation error with Automatic Resynchronization mode', 'sync field measure time-out with Automatic Resynchronization mode' and 'LIN header reception time-out'.</description>
              <bitOffset>1</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>LIN header error not detected</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>LIN header error detected</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SLVIDPEF</name>
              <description>LIN Slave ID Parity Error Flag \nThis bit is set by hardware when receipted frame ID parity is not correct.</description>
              <bitOffset>2</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No active</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Receipted frame ID parity is not correct</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SLVSYNCF</name>
              <description>LIN Slave Sync Field\nThis bit indicates that the LIN sync field is being analyzed in Automatic Resynchronization mode. When the receiver header have some error been detect, user must reset the internal circuit to re-search new frame header by writing 1 to this bit.\nNote 2: This bit can be cleared by writing 1 to it.\nNote 3: When writing 1 to it, hardware will reload the initial baud rate and re-search a new frame header.</description>
              <bitOffset>3</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>The current character is not at LIN sync state</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>The current character is at LIN sync state</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SLVHTOF</name>
              <description>LIN Slave Header Time-out Flag\nThis bit is set by hardware when a LIN header reception time-out is detected in LIN slave mode and be cleared by writing 1 to it. When this bit is set, SLVHEF (UART_LINSTS[1]) will also be set.</description>
              <bitOffset>4</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>LIN header time-out not detected</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>LIN header time-out detected</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RTOUTF</name>
              <description>LIN Response Time-out Flag\nThis bit is set when no LIN response received and the time-out counter equal to or bigger than LINRTOIC (UART_LINRTOUT[23:0]). If LINIEN (UART_INTEN[8]) is enabled, the LIN Bus Interrupt will be generated.\nNote 1: This bit can be cleared by writing 1 to it.</description>
              <bitOffset>5</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>LIN response time-out not detected</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>LIN response time-out detected</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BRKDETF</name>
              <description>LIN Break Detection Flag\nThis bit is set by hardware when a break is detected and be cleared by writing 1 to it through software.</description>
              <bitOffset>8</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>LIN break not detected</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>LIN break detected</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BITEF</name>
              <description>Bit Error Detect Status Flag \nAt TX transfer state, hardware will monitor the bus state, if the input pin (UART_RXD) state not equals to the output pin (UART_TXD) state, BITEF (UART_LINSTS[9]) will be set.</description>
              <bitOffset>9</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Bit error not detected</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Bit error detected</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>UARTx_BRCOMP</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>UARTx_BRCOMP</displayName>
          <description>UART Baud Rate Compensation Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x3C</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>BRCOMP</name>
              <description>Baud Rate Compensation Patten\nThese 9-bits are used to define the relative bit is compensated or not. \nBRCOMP[7:0] is used to define the compensation of DAT (UART_DAT[7:0]) and BRCOM[8] is used to define PARITY (UART_DAT[8]).</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>9</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BRCOMPDEC</name>
              <description>Baud Rate Compensation Decrease</description>
              <bitOffset>31</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Positive (increase one module clock) compensation for each compensated bit</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Negative (decrease one module clock) compensation for each compensated bit</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>UARTx_WKCTL</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>UARTx_WKCTL</displayName>
          <description>UART Wake-up Control Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x40</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>WKCTSEN</name>
              <description>nCTS Wake-up Enable Bit\nNote: When the system is in Power-down mode, an external nCTS change will wake up system from Power-down mode.</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>nCTS Wake-up system function Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>nCTS Wake-up system function Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>WKDATEN</name>
              <description>Incoming Data Wake-up Enable Bit\nNote: When the system is in Power-down mode, incoming data will wake-up system from Power-down mode.</description>
              <bitOffset>1</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Incoming data wake-up system function Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Incoming data wake-up system function Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>WKRFRTEN</name>
              <description>Received Data FIFO Reached Threshold Wake-up Enable Bit\nNote: When the system is in Power-down mode, Received Data FIFO reached threshold will wake-up system from Power-down mode.</description>
              <bitOffset>2</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Received Data FIFO reached threshold wake-up system function Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Received Data FIFO reached threshold wake-up system function Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>WKRS485EN</name>
              <description>RS-485 Address Match Wake-up Enable Bit\nNote 1: When the system is in Power-down mode, RS-485 Address Match will wake-up system from Power-down mode.\nNote 2: This bit is used for RS-485 Auto Address Detection (AAD) mode in RS-485 function mode and ADDRDEN (UART_ALTCTL[15]) is set to 1.</description>
              <bitOffset>3</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>RS-485 Address Match (AAD mode) wake-up system function Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>RS-485 Address Match (AAD mode) wake-up system function Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>WKTOUTEN</name>
              <description>Received Data FIFO Reached Threshold Time-out Wake-up Enable Bit\nNote 1: When the system is in Power-down mode, Received Data FIFO reached threshold time-out will wake up system from Power-down mode.\nNote 2: It is suggested the function is enabled when the WKRFRTEN (UART_WKCTL[2]) is set to 1.</description>
              <bitOffset>4</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Received Data FIFO reached threshold time-out wake-up system function Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Received Data FIFO reached threshold time-out wake-up system function Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>UARTx_WKSTS</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>UARTx_WKSTS</displayName>
          <description>UART Wake-up Status Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x44</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>CTSWKF</name>
              <description>nCTS Wake-up Flag\nThis bit is set if chip wake-up from power-down state by nCTS wake-up.\nNote 1: If WKCTSEN (UART_WKCTL[0]) is enabled, the nCTS wake-up cause this bit is set to '1'.\nNote 2: This bit can be cleared by writing '1' to it.</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Chip stays in power-down state</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Chip wake-up from power-down state by nCTS wake-up</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>DATWKF</name>
              <description>Incoming Data Wake-up Flag\nThis bit is set if chip wake-up from power-down state by data wake-up.\nNote 1: If WKDATEN (UART_WKCTL[1]) is enabled, the Incoming Data wake-up cause this bit is set to '1'.\nNote 2: This bit can be cleared by writing '1' to it.</description>
              <bitOffset>1</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Chip stays in power-down state</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Chip wake-up from power-down state by Incoming Data wake-up</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RFRTWKF</name>
              <description>Received Data FIFO Reached Threshold Wake-up Flag\nThis bit is set if chip wake-up from power-down state by Received Data FIFO reached threshold wake-up.\nNote 1: If WKRFRTEN (UART_WKCTL[2]) is enabled, the Received Data FIFO Reached Threshold wake-up cause this bit is set to '1'.\nNote 2: This bit can be cleared by writing '1' to it.</description>
              <bitOffset>2</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Chip stays in power-down state</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Chip wake-up from power-down state by Received Data FIFO Reached Threshold wake-up</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RS485WKF</name>
              <description>RS-485 Address Match Wake-up Flag\nThis bit is set if chip wake-up from power-down state by RS-485 Address Match (AAD mode).\nNote 1: If WKRS485EN (UART_WKCTL[3]) is enabled, the RS-485 Address Match (AAD mode) wake-up cause this bit is set to '1'.\nNote 2: This bit can be cleared by writing '1' to it.</description>
              <bitOffset>3</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Chip stays in power-down state</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Chip wake-up from power-down state by RS-485 Address Match (AAD mode) wake-up</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TOUTWKF</name>
              <description>Received Data FIFO Threshold Time-out Wake-up Flag\nThis bit is set if chip wake-up from power-down state by Received Data FIFO Threshold Time-out\nwake-up.\nNote 1: If WKTOUTEN (UART_WKCTL[4]) is enabled, the Received Data FIFO reached threshold time-out wake-up cause this bit is set to '1'.\nNote 2: This bit can be cleared by writing '1' to it.</description>
              <bitOffset>4</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Chip stays in power-down state</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Chip wake-up from power-down state by Received Data FIFO reached threshold time-out wake-up</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>UARTx_DWKCOMP</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>UARTx_DWKCOMP</displayName>
          <description>UART Incoming Data Wake-up Compensation Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x48</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>STCOMP</name>
              <description>START Bit Compensation Value\nThese bits field indicate how many clock cycle selected by UART_CLK do the UART controller can get the 1st bit (START bit) when the device is wake-up from Power-down mode.\nNote: It is valid only when WKDATEN (UART_WKCTL[1]) is set.</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>16</bitWidth>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>UARTx_RS485DD</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>UARTx_RS485DD</displayName>
          <description>UART RS485 Transceiver Deactivate Delay Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x4C</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>RTSDDLY</name>
              <description>RS485 Transceiver Deactivate Delay Value\nThese bits field indicate how many clock cycles selected by UART_CLK do the UART controller delay the RS485 transceiver state trancing when the state trancing of RS485 transceiver is from TX to RX state. These bits field have no effect when the state trancing of RS485 transceiver is from RX to TX state.\nNote: It is valid only when RS485AUD (UART_ALTCTL[10]) is set.</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>16</bitWidth>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>UARTx_LINRTOUT</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>UARTx_LINRTOUT</displayName>
          <description>UART LIN Response Time-out Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x50</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00FFFFFF</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>LINRTOIC</name>
              <description>LIN Response Time-out Comparator</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>24</bitWidth>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>UARTx_LINWKCTL</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>UARTx_LINWKCTL</displayName>
          <description>UART LIN Wake-up Control Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x54</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x000000C0</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>LINWKC</name>
              <description>LIN Send Wake-up Signal Length Counter</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>24</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SENDLINW</name>
              <description>LIN Send Wake-up Enable Bit\nNote 1: When this bit is set, the UART will send LIN wake-up automatically. When LIN wake-up transfer operation finished, this bit will be cleared automatically.</description>
              <bitOffset>24</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Send LIN Wake-up Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Send LIN Wake-up Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>LINWKEN</name>
              <description>LIN Wake-up Enable Bit\nNote 1: When the system is in Power-down mode, LIN wake-up event will wake up system from Power-down mode.</description>
              <bitOffset>28</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>LIN wake-up system function Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>LIN wake-up system function Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>LINWKF</name>
              <description>LIN Wake-up Flag\nThis bit is set if chip wake-up from power-down state by LIN wake-up.\nNote 1: If LINWKEN (UART_LINWKCTL[28]) is enabled, the LIN wake-up event will cause this bit set to '1'.\nNote 2: This bit can be cleared by writing '1' to it.</description>
              <bitOffset>29</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Chip stays in power-down state</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Chip wake-up from power-down state by LIN wake-up</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
      </registers>
    </peripheral>
    <peripheral>
      <name>UART6</name>
      <description>UART Register Map</description>
      <groupName>UART</groupName>
      <baseAddress>0x40076000</baseAddress>
      <!-- d -> no access permission -->
      <addressBlock>
        <offset>0x0</offset>
        <size>0x58</size>
        <usage>registers</usage>
      </addressBlock>
      <registers>
       <register>
          <name>UARTx_DAT</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>UARTx_DAT</displayName>
          <description>UART Receive/Transmit Buffer Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x0</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0x00000000</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>DAT</name>
              <description>Data Receive/Transmit Buffer\nWrite Operation:\nBy writing one byte to this register, the data byte will be stored in transmitter FIFO. The UART controller will send out the data stored in transmitter FIFO top location through the UART_TXD.\nRead Operation:\nBy reading this register, the UART controller will return an 8-bit data received from receiver FIFO.</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>8</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>PARITY</name>
              <description>PARITY Bit Receive/Transmit Buffer\nWrite Operation:\nBy writing to this bit, the PARITY bit will be stored in transmitter FIFO. If PBE (UART_LINE[3]) and PSS (UART_LINE[7]) are set, the UART controller will send out this bit follow the DAT (UART_DAT[7:0]) through the UART_TXD.\nRead Operation:\nIf PBE (UART_LINE[3]) and PSS (UART_LINE[7]) are enabled, the PARITY bit can be read by this bit.\nNote: This bit has effect only when PBE (UART_LINE[3]) and PSS (UART_LINE[7]) are set.</description>
              <bitOffset>8</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>UARTx_INTEN</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>UARTx_INTEN</displayName>
          <description>UART Interrupt Enable Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x4</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>RDAIEN</name>
              <description>Receive Data Available Interrupt Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Receive data available interrupt Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Receive data available interrupt Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>THREIEN</name>
              <description>Transmit Holding Register Empty Interrupt Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>1</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Transmit holding register empty interrupt Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Transmit holding register empty interrupt Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RLSIEN</name>
              <description>Receive Line Status Interrupt Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>2</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Receive Line Status interrupt Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Receive Line Status interrupt Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>MODEMIEN</name>
              <description>Modem Status Interrupt Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>3</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Modem status interrupt Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Modem status interrupt Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RXTOIEN</name>
              <description>RX Time-out Interrupt Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>4</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>RX time-out interrupt Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>RX time-out interrupt Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BUFERRIEN</name>
              <description>Buffer Error Interrupt Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>5</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Buffer error interrupt Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Buffer error interrupt Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>WKIEN</name>
              <description>Wake-up Interrupt Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>6</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Wake-up Interrupt Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Wake-up Interrupt Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>LINIEN</name>
              <description>LIN Bus Interrupt Enable Bit\nNote: This bit is used for LIN function mode.</description>
              <bitOffset>8</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>LIN bus interrupt Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>LIN bus interrupt Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TOCNTEN</name>
              <description>Receive Buffer Time-out Counter Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>11</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Receive Buffer Time-out counter Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Receive Buffer Time-out counter Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ATORTSEN</name>
              <description>nRTS Auto-flow Control Enable Bit\nNote: When nRTS auto-flow is enabled, if the number of bytes in the RX FIFO equals the RTSTRGLV (UART_FIFO[19:16]), the UART will de-assert nRTS signal.</description>
              <bitOffset>12</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>nRTS auto-flow control Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>nRTS auto-flow control Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ATOCTSEN</name>
              <description>nCTS Auto-flow Control Enable Bit\nNote: When nCTS auto-flow is enabled, the UART will send data to external device if nCTS input assert (UART will not send data to device until nCTS is asserted).</description>
              <bitOffset>13</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>nCTS auto-flow control Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>nCTS auto-flow control Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TXPDMAEN</name>
              <description>TX PDMA Enable Bit\nNote: If RLSIEN (UART_INTEN[2]) is enabled and HWRLSINT (UART_INTSTS[26]) is set to 1, the RLS (Receive Line Status) Interrupt is caused. If RLS interrupt is caused by Break Error Flag BIF(UART_FIFOSTS[6]), Frame Error Flag FEF(UART_FIFO[5]) or Parity Error Flag PEF(UART_FIFOSTS[4]), UART PDMA transmit request operation is stopped. Clear Break Error Flag BIF or Frame Error Flag FEF or Parity Error Flag PEF by writing '1' to corresponding BIF, FEF and PEF to make UART PDMA transmit request operation continue.</description>
              <bitOffset>14</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>TX PDMA Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>TX PDMA Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RXPDMAEN</name>
              <description>RX PDMA Enable Bit\nThis bit can enable or disable RX PDMA service.\nNote: If RLSIEN (UART_INTEN[2]) is enabled and HWRLSINT (UART_INTSTS[26]) is set to 1, the RLS (Receive Line Status) Interrupt is caused. If RLS interrupt is caused by Break Error Flag BIF(UART_FIFOSTS[6]), Frame Error Flag FEF(UART_FIFO[5]) or Parity Error Flag PEF(UART_FIFOSTS[4]), UART PDMA receive request operation is stopped. Clear Break Error Flag BIF or Frame Error Flag FEF or Parity Error Flag PEF by writing '1' to corresponding BIF, FEF and PEF to make UART PDMA receive request operation continue.</description>
              <bitOffset>15</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>RX PDMA Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>RX PDMA Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SWBEIEN</name>
              <description>Single-wire Bit Error Detection Interrupt Enable Bit\nSet this bit, the Single-wire Half Duplex Bit Error Detection Interrupt SWBEINT(UART_INTSTS[24]) is generated when Single-wire Bit Error Detection SWBEIF(UART_INTSTS[16]) is set.\nNote: This bit is valid when FUNCSEL (UART_FUNCSEL[2:0]) is select UART Single-wire mode.</description>
              <bitOffset>16</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Single-wire Bit Error Detect Interrupt Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Single-wire Bit Error Detect Interrupt Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ABRIEN</name>
              <description>Auto-baud Rate Interrupt Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>18</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Auto-baud rate interrupt Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Auto-baud rate interrupt Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TXENDIEN</name>
              <description>Transmitter Empty Interrupt Enable Bit\nIf TXENDIEN (UART_INTEN[22]) is enabled, the Transmitter Empty interrupt TXENDINT (UART_INTSTS[30]) will be generated when TXENDIF (UART_INTSTS[22]) is set (TX FIFO (UART_DAT) is empty and the STOP bit of the last byte has been transmitted).</description>
              <bitOffset>22</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Transmitter empty interrupt Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Transmitter empty interrupt Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>UARTx_FIFO</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>UARTx_FIFO</displayName>
          <description>UART FIFO Control Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x8</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000101</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>RXRST</name>
              <description>RX Field Software Reset\nWhen RXRST (UART_FIFO[1]) is set, all the byte in the receiver FIFO and RX internal state machine are cleared.\nNote 1: This bit will automatically clear at least 3 UART peripheral clock cycles.\nNote 2: Before setting this bit, it should wait for the RXIDLE (UART_FIFOSTS[29]) be set.</description>
              <bitOffset>1</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No effect</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Reset the RX internal state machine and pointers</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TXRST</name>
              <description>TX Field Software Reset\nWhen TXRST (UART_FIFO[2]) is set, all the byte in the transmit FIFO and TX internal state machine are cleared.\nNote 1: This bit will automatically clear at least 3 UART peripheral clock cycles.\nNote 2: Before setting this bit, it should wait for the TXEMPTYF (UART_FIFOSTS[28]) be set.</description>
              <bitOffset>2</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No effect</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Reset the TX internal state machine and pointers</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RFITL</name>
              <description>RX FIFO Interrupt Trigger Level\nWhen the number of bytes in the receive FIFO equals the RFITL, the RDAIF (UART_INTSTS[0]) will be set (if RDAIEN (UART_INTEN[0]) enabled, and an interrupt will be generated).</description>
              <bitOffset>4</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>4</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>RX FIFO Interrupt Trigger Level is 1 byte</description>
                    <value>#0000</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>RX FIFO Interrupt Trigger Level is 4 bytes</description>
                    <value>#0001</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>2</name>
                    <description>RX FIFO Interrupt Trigger Level is 8 bytes</description>
                    <value>#0010</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>3</name>
                    <description>RX FIFO Interrupt Trigger Level is 14 bytes</description>
                    <value>#0011</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RXOFF</name>
              <description>Receiver Disable Bit\nThe receiver is disabled or not (set 1 to disable receiver).\nNote: This bit is used for RS-485 Normal Multi-drop mode. It should be programmed before RS485NMM (UART_ALTCTL[8]) is programmed.</description>
              <bitOffset>8</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Receiver Enabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Receiver Disabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RTSTRGLV</name>
              <description>nRTS Trigger Level for Auto-flow Control\nNote: This field is used for auto nRTS flow control.</description>
              <bitOffset>16</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>4</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>nRTS Trigger Level is 1 byte</description>
                    <value>#0000</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>nRTS Trigger Level is 4 bytes</description>
                    <value>#0001</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>2</name>
                    <description>nRTS Trigger Level is 8 bytes</description>
                    <value>#0010</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>3</name>
                    <description>nRTS Trigger Level is 14 bytes</description>
                    <value>#0011</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>UARTx_LINE</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>UARTx_LINE</displayName>
          <description>UART Line Control Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0xC</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>WLS</name>
              <description>Word Length Selection\nThis field sets UART word length.</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>2</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>5 bits</description>
                    <value>#00</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>6 bits</description>
                    <value>#01</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>2</name>
                    <description>7 bits</description>
                    <value>#10</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>3</name>
                    <description>8 bits</description>
                    <value>#11</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>NSB</name>
              <description>Number of 'STOP Bit'</description>
              <bitOffset>2</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>One 'STOP bit' is generated in the transmitted data</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>When select 5-bit word length, 1.5 'STOP bit' is generated in the transmitted data. When select 6-, 7- and 8-bit word length, 2 'STOP bit' is generated in the transmitted data</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>PBE</name>
              <description>PARITY Bit Enable Bit\nNote: PARITY bit is generated on each outgoing character and is checked on each incoming data.</description>
              <bitOffset>3</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>PARITY bit generated Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>PARITY bit generated Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>EPE</name>
              <description>Even Parity Enable Bit\nNote: This bit has effect only when PBE (UART_LINE[3]) is set.</description>
              <bitOffset>4</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Odd number of logic 1's is transmitted and checked in each word</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Even number of logic 1's is transmitted and checked in each word</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SPE</name>
              <description>Stick Parity Enable Bit\nNote: If PBE (UART_LINE[3]) and EPE (UART_LINE[4]) are logic 1, the PARITY bit is transmitted and checked as logic 0. If PBE (UART_LINE[3]) is 1 and EPE (UART_LINE[4]) is 0 then the PARITY bit is transmitted and checked as 1.</description>
              <bitOffset>5</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Stick parity Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Stick parity Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BCB</name>
              <description>Break Control Bit\nNote: When this bit is set to logic 1, the transmitted serial data output (TX) is forced to the Spacing State (logic 0). This bit acts only on TX line and has no effect on the transmitter logic.</description>
              <bitOffset>6</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Break Control Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Break Control Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>PSS</name>
              <description>PARITY Bit Source Selection\nThe PARITY bit can be selected to be generated and checked automatically or by software.\nNote 1: This bit has effect only when PBE (UART_LINE[3]) is set.\nNote 2: If PSS is 0, the PARITY bit is transmitted and checked automatically. If PSS is 1, the transmitted PARITY bit value can be determined by writing PARITY (UART_DAT[8]) and the PARITY bit can be read by reading PARITY (UART_DAT[8]).</description>
              <bitOffset>7</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>PARITY bit is generated by EPE (UART_LINE[4]) and SPE (UART_LINE[5]) setting and checked automatically</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>PARITY bit generated and checked by software</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TXDINV</name>
              <description>TX Data Inverted\nNote 1: Before setting this bit, TXRXDIS (UART_FUNCSEL[3]) should be set then waited for TXRXACT (UART_FIFOSTS[31]) is cleared. When the configuration is done, cleared TXRXDIS (UART_FUNCSEL[3]) to activate UART controller.\nNote 2: This bit is valid when FUNCSEL (UART_FUNCSEL[2:0]) is select UART, LIN or RS485 function.</description>
              <bitOffset>8</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Transmitted data signal inverted Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Transmitted data signal inverted Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RXDINV</name>
              <description>RX Data Inverted\nNote 1: Before setting this bit, TXRXDIS (UART_FUNCSEL[3]) should be set then waited for TXRXACT (UART_FIFOSTS[31]) is cleared. When the configuration is done, cleared TXRXDIS (UART_FUNCSEL[3]) to activate UART controller.\nNote 2: This bit is valid when FUNCSEL (UART_FUNCSEL[2:0]) is select UART, LIN or RS485 function.</description>
              <bitOffset>9</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Received data signal inverted Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Received data signal inverted Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>UARTx_MODEM</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>UARTx_MODEM</displayName>
          <description>UART Modem Control Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x10</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000200</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>RTS</name>
              <description>nRTS Signal Control\nThis bit is direct control internal nRTS (Request-to-send) signal active or not, and then drive the nRTS pin output with RTSACTLV bit configuration.\nNote 1: The nRTS signal control bit is not effective when nRTS auto-flow control is enabled in UART function mode.\nNote 2: The nRTS signal control bit is not effective when RS-485 auto direction mode (AUD) is enabled in RS-485 function mode.\nNote 3: Single-wire mode is support this feature.</description>
              <bitOffset>1</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>nRTS signal is active</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>nRTS signal is inactive</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RTSACTLV</name>
              <description>nRTS Pin Active Level\nThis bit defines the active level state of nRTS pin output.\nNote 1: Refer to Figure 6.2113 and Figure 6.2114 for UART function mode.\nNote 2: Refer to Figure 6.2124 and Figure 6.2125 for RS-485 function mode.\nNote 3: Before setting this bit, TXRXDIS (UART_FUNCSEL[3]) should be set then waited for TXRXACT (UART_FIFOSTS[31]) is cleared. When the configuration is done, cleared TXRXDIS (UART_FUNCSEL[3]) to activate UART controller.</description>
              <bitOffset>9</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>nRTS pin output is high level active</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>nRTS pin output is low level active. (Default)</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RTSSTS</name>
              <description>nRTS Pin Status (Read Only)\nThis bit mirror from nRTS pin output of voltage logic status.</description>
              <bitOffset>13</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>nRTS pin output is low level voltage logic state</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>nRTS pin output is high level voltage logic state</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>UARTx_MODEMSTS</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>UARTx_MODEMSTS</displayName>
          <description>UART Modem Status Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x14</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000110</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>CTSDETF</name>
              <description>Detect nCTS State Change Flag\nThis bit is set whenever nCTS input has change state, and it will generate Modem interrupt to CPU when MODEMIEN (UART_INTEN[3]) is set to 1.\nNote: This bit can be cleared by writing '1' to it.</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>nCTS input has not change state</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>nCTS input has change state</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>CTSSTS</name>
              <description>nCTS Pin Status (Read Only)\nThis bit mirror from nCTS pin input of voltage logic status.\nNote: This bit echoes when UART controller peripheral clock is enabled, and nCTS multi-function port is selected.</description>
              <bitOffset>4</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>nCTS pin input is low level voltage logic state</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>nCTS pin input is high level voltage logic state</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>CTSACTLV</name>
              <description>nCTS Pin Active Level\nThis bit defines the active level state of nCTS pin input.\nNote: Before setting this bit, TXRXDIS (UART_FUNCSEL[3]) should be set then waited for TXRXACT (UART_FIFOSTS[31]) is cleared. When the configuration is done, cleared TXRXDIS (UART_FUNCSEL[3]) to activate UART controller.</description>
              <bitOffset>8</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>nCTS pin input is high level active</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>nCTS pin input is low level active. (Default)</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>UARTx_FIFOSTS</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>UARTx_FIFOSTS</displayName>
          <description>UART FIFO Status Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x18</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0xB0404000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>RXOVIF</name>
              <description>RX Overflow Error Interrupt Flag\nThis bit is set when RX FIFO overflow.\nIf the number of bytes of received data is greater than RX_FIFO (UART_DAT) size 16 bytes, this bit will be set.\nNote: This bit can be cleared by writing '1' to it.</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>RX FIFO is not overflow</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>RX FIFO is overflow</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ABRDIF</name>
              <description>Auto-baud Rate Detect Interrupt Flag\nThis bit is set to logic '1' when auto-baud rate detect function is finished.\nNote: This bit can be cleared by writing '1' to it.</description>
              <bitOffset>1</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Auto-baud rate detect function is not finished</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Auto-baud rate detect function is finished</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ABRDTOIF</name>
              <description>Auto-baud Rate Detect Time-out Interrupt Flag\nThis bit is set to logic '1' in Auto-baud Rate Detect mode when the baud rate counter is overflow.\nNote: This bit can be cleared by writing '1' to it.</description>
              <bitOffset>2</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Auto-baud rate counter is underflow</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Auto-baud rate counter is overflow</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ADDRDETF</name>
              <description>RS-485 Address Byte Detect Flag\nNote 1: This field is used for RS-485 function mode and ADDRDEN (UART_ALTCTL[15]) is set to 1 to enable Address detection mode.\nNote 2: This bit can be cleared by writing '1' to it.</description>
              <bitOffset>3</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Receiver detects a data that is not an address bit (bit 9 ='0')</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Receiver detects a data that is an address bit (bit 9 ='1')</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>PEF</name>
              <description>Parity Error Flag\nThis bit is set to logic 1 whenever the received character does not have a valid 'PARITY bit'.\nNote: This bit can be cleared by writing '1' to it.</description>
              <bitOffset>4</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No parity error is generated</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Parity error is generated</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>FEF</name>
              <description>Framing Error Flag\nThis bit is set to logic 1 whenever the received character does not have a valid 'STOP bit' (that is, the STOP bit following the last data bit or PARITY bit is detected as logic 0).\nNote: This bit can be cleared by writing '1' to it.</description>
              <bitOffset>5</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No framing error is generated</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Framing error is generated</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BIF</name>
              <description>Break Interrupt Flag\nThis bit is set to logic 1 whenever the received data input (RX) is held in the 'spacing state' (logic 0) for longer than a full word transmission time (that is, the total time of 'START bit' + data bits + parity + STOP bits).\nNote: This bit can be cleared by writing '1' to it.</description>
              <bitOffset>6</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No Break interrupt is generated</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Break interrupt is generated</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RXPTR</name>
              <description>RX FIFO Pointer (Read Only)\nThis field indicates the RX FIFO Buffer Pointer. When UART receives one byte from external device, RXPTR increases one. When one byte of RX FIFO is read by CPU, RXPTR decreases one.\nThe Maximum value shown in RXPTR is 15. When the using level of RX FIFO Buffer equal to 16, the RXFULL bit is set to 1 and RXPTR will show 0. As one byte of RX FIFO is read by CPU, the RXFULL bit is cleared to 0 and RXPTR will show 15.</description>
              <bitOffset>8</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>6</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RXEMPTY</name>
              <description>Receiver FIFO Empty (Read Only)\nThis bit initiate RX FIFO empty or not.\nNote: When the last byte of RX FIFO has been read by CPU, hardware sets this bit high. It will be cleared when UART receives any new data.</description>
              <bitOffset>14</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>RX FIFO is not empty</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>RX FIFO is empty</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RXFULL</name>
              <description>Receiver FIFO Full (Read Only)\nThis bit initiates RX FIFO full or not.\nNote: This bit is set when the number of usage in RX FIFO Buffer is equal to 16, otherwise it is cleared by hardware.</description>
              <bitOffset>15</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>RX FIFO is not full</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>RX FIFO is full</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TXPTR</name>
              <description>TX FIFO Pointer (Read Only)\nThis field indicates the TX FIFO Buffer Pointer. When CPU writes one byte into UART_DAT, TXPTR increases one. When one byte of TX FIFO is transferred to Transmitter Shift Register, TXPTR decreases one.\nThe Maximum value shown in TXPTR is 15. When the using level of TX FIFO Buffer equal to 16, the TXFULL bit is set to 1 and TXPTR will show 0. As one byte of TX FIFO is transferred to Transmitter Shift Register, the TXFULL bit is cleared to 0 and TXPTR will show 15.</description>
              <bitOffset>16</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>6</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TXEMPTY</name>
              <description>Transmitter FIFO Empty (Read Only)\nThis bit indicates TX FIFO empty or not.\nNote: When the last byte of TX FIFO has been transferred to Transmitter Shift Register, hardware sets this bit high. It will be cleared when writing data into UART_DAT (TX FIFO not empty).</description>
              <bitOffset>22</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>TX FIFO is not empty</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>TX FIFO is empty</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TXFULL</name>
              <description>Transmitter FIFO Full (Read Only)\nThis bit indicates TX FIFO full or not.\nNote: This bit is set when the number of usage in TX FIFO Buffer is equal to 16, otherwise it is cleared by hardware.</description>
              <bitOffset>23</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>TX FIFO is not full</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>TX FIFO is full</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TXOVIF</name>
              <description>TX Overflow Error Interrupt Flag\nIf TX FIFO (UART_DAT) is full, an additional write to UART_DAT will cause this bit to logic 1.\nNote: This bit can be cleared by writing '1' to it.</description>
              <bitOffset>24</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>TX FIFO is not overflow</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>TX FIFO is overflow</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TXEMPTYF</name>
              <description>Transmitter Empty Flag (Read Only)\nThis bit is set by hardware when TX FIFO (UART_DAT) is empty and the STOP bit of the last byte has been transmitted.\nNote: This bit is cleared automatically when TX FIFO is not empty or the last byte transmission has not completed.</description>
              <bitOffset>28</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>TX FIFO is not empty or the STOP bit of the last byte has been not transmitted</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>TX FIFO is empty and the STOP bit of the last byte has been transmitted</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RXIDLE</name>
              <description>RX Idle Status (Read Only)\nThis bit is set by hardware when RX is idle.</description>
              <bitOffset>29</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>RX is busy</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>RX is idle. (Default)</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TXRXACT</name>
              <description>TX and RX Active Status (Read Only)\nThis bit indicates TX and RX are active or inactive.\nNote: When TXRXDIS (UART_FUNCSEL[3]) is set and both TX and RX are in idle state, this bit is cleared. The UART controller cannot transmit or receive data at this moment. Otherwise this bit is set.</description>
              <bitOffset>31</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>TX and RX are inactive</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>TX and RX are active. (Default)</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>UARTx_INTSTS</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>UARTx_INTSTS</displayName>
          <description>UART Interrupt Status Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x1C</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00400002</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>RDAIF</name>
              <description>Receive Data Available Interrupt Flag (Read Only)\nWhen the number of bytes in the RX FIFO equals the RFITL then the RDAIF(UART_INTSTS[0]) will be set. If RDAIEN (UART_INTEN[0]) is enabled, the RDA interrupt will be generated.\nNote: This bit is read only and will be cleared when the number of unread bytes of RX FIFO drops below the threshold level (RFITL(UART_FIFO[7:4]).</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No RDA interrupt flag is generated</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>RDA interrupt flag is generated</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>THREIF</name>
              <description>Transmit Holding Register Empty Interrupt Flag (Read Only)\nThis bit is set when the last data of TX FIFO is transferred to Transmitter Shift Register. If THREIEN (UART_INTEN[1]) is enabled, the THRE interrupt will be generated.\nNote: This bit is read only and will be cleared when writing data into UART_DAT (TX FIFO is not empty).</description>
              <bitOffset>1</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No THRE interrupt flag is generated</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>THRE interrupt flag is generated</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RLSIF</name>
              <description>Receive Line Interrupt Flag (Read Only)\nThis bit is set when the RX receive data have parity error, frame error or break error (at least one of 3 bits, BIF(UART_FIFOSTS[6]), FEF(UART_FIFOSTS[5]) and PEF(UART_FIFOSTS[4]), is set). If RLSIEN (UART_INTEN[2]) is enabled, the RLS interrupt will be generated.\nNote 2: This bit is read only and reset to 0 when all bits of BIF (UART_FIFOSTS[6]), FEF(UART_FIFOSTS[5]) and PEF(UART_FIFOSTS[4]) are cleared.\nNote 3: In RS-485 function mode, this bit is read only and reset to 0 when all bits of BIF (UART_FIFOSTS[6]), FEF(UART_FIFOSTS[5]), PEF(UART_FIFOSTS[4]) and ADDRDETF (UART_FIFOSTS[3]) are cleared.\nNote 4: This bit is valid only when RXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[15]) and TXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[14]) are both 0.</description>
              <bitOffset>2</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No RLS interrupt flag is generated</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>RLS interrupt flag is generated</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>MODEMIF</name>
              <description>MODEM Interrupt Flag (Read Only)\nNote 1: This bit is read only and reset to 0 when bit CTSDETF is cleared by a write 1 on CTSDETF(UART_MODEMSTS[0]).\nNote 2: This bit is valid only when RXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[15]) and TXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[14]) are both 0.</description>
              <bitOffset>3</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No Modem interrupt flag is generated</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Modem interrupt flag is generated</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RXTOIF</name>
              <description>RX Time-out Interrupt Flag (Read Only)\nThis bit is set when the RX FIFO is not empty and no activities occurred in the RX FIFO and the time-out counter equal to TOIC (UART_TOUT[7:0]). If RXTOIEN (UART_INTEN[4]) is enabled, the RX time-out interrupt will be generated.\nNote 1: This bit can be cleared by writing '1' to it.\nNote 2: If BITOMEN (UART_TOUT[31]) is reset to 0, user can read UART_DAT (RX is in active) to clear this bit.\nNote 3: This bit is valid only when RXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[15]) and TXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[14]) are both 0.</description>
              <bitOffset>4</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No RX time-out interrupt flag is generated</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>RX time-out interrupt flag is generated</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BUFERRIF</name>
              <description>Buffer Error Interrupt Flag (Read Only)\nThis bit is set when the TX FIFO or RX FIFO overflows (TXOVIF (UART_FIFOSTS[24]) or RXOVIF (UART_FIFOSTS[0]) is set). When BUFERRIF (UART_INTSTS[5]) is set, the transfer is not correct. If BUFERRIEN (UART_INTEN[5]) is enabled, the buffer error interrupt will be generated.\nNote 1: This bit is cleared if both of RXOVIF(UART_FIFOSTS[0]) and TXOVIF(UART_FIFOSTS[24]) are cleared to 0 by writing 1 to RXOVIF(UART_FIFOSTS[0]) and TXOVIF(UART_FIFOSTS[24]).\nNote 2: This bit is valid only when RXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[15]) and TXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[14]) are both 0.</description>
              <bitOffset>5</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No buffer error interrupt flag is generated</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Buffer error interrupt flag is generated</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>WKIF</name>
              <description>UART Wake-up Interrupt Flag (Read Only)\nThis bit is set when LINWKF (UART_LINWKCTL[29]), TOUTWKF (UART_WKSTS[4]), RS485WKF (UART_WKSTS[3]), RFRTWKF (UART_WKSTS[2]), DATWKF (UART_WKSTS[1]) or CTSWKF(UART_WKSTS[0]) is set to 1.\nNote: This bit is cleared if all of LINWKF, TOUTWKF, RS485WKF, RFRTWKF, DATWKF and CTSWKF are cleared to 0 by writing 1 to the corresponding interrupt flag.</description>
              <bitOffset>6</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No UART wake-up interrupt flag is generated</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>UART wake-up interrupt flag is generated</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>LINIF</name>
              <description>LIN Bus Interrupt Flag\nNote: This bit is cleared when SLVHDETF (UART_LINSTS[0]), SLVHEF (UART_LINSTS[1]), SLVIDPEF (UART_LINSTS[2]), SLVHTOF (UART_LINSTS[4]), RTOUTF (UART_LINSTS[5]), BRKDETF (UART_LINSTS[8]), and BITEF (UART_LINSTS[9]) all are cleared and software writing '1' to LINIF (UART_INTSTS[7]).</description>
              <bitOffset>7</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>None of SLVHDETF, SLVHEF, SLVIDPEF, SLVHTOF, RTOUTF, BITEF, and BRKDETF is generated</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>At least one of SLVHDETF, SLVHEF, SLVIDPEF, SLVHTOF, RTOUTF, BITEF, and BRKDETF is generated</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RDAINT</name>
              <description>Receive Data Available Interrupt Indicator (Read Only)\nThis bit is set if RDAIEN (UART_INTEN[0]) and RDAIF (UART_INTSTS[0]) are both set to 1.</description>
              <bitOffset>8</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No RDA interrupt is generated</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>RDA interrupt is generated</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>THREINT</name>
              <description>Transmit Holding Register Empty Interrupt Indicator (Read Only)\nThis bit is set if THREIEN (UART_INTEN[1]) and THREIF(UART_INTSTS[1]) are both set to 1.</description>
              <bitOffset>9</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No THRE interrupt is generated</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>THRE interrupt is generated</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RLSINT</name>
              <description>Receive Line Status Interrupt Indicator (Read Only) \nThis bit is set if RLSIEN (UART_INTEN[2]) and RLSIF(UART_INTSTS[2]) are both set to 1.\nNote: This bit is valid only when RXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[15]) and TXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[14]) are both 0.</description>
              <bitOffset>10</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No RLS interrupt is generated</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>RLS interrupt is generated</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>MODEMINT</name>
              <description>MODEM Status Interrupt Indicator (Read Only)\nThis bit is set if MODEMIEN(UART_INTEN[3]) and MODEMIF(UART_INTSTS[3]) are both set to 1\nNote: This bit is valid only when RXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[15]) and TXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[14]) are both 0.</description>
              <bitOffset>11</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No Modem interrupt is generated</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Modem interrupt is generated</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RXTOINT</name>
              <description>RX Time-out Interrupt Indicator (Read Only)\nThis bit is set if RXTOIEN (UART_INTEN[4]) and RXTOIF(UART_INTSTS[4]) are both set to 1.\nNote: This bit is valid only when RXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[15]) and TXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[14]) are both 0.</description>
              <bitOffset>12</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No RX time-out interrupt is generated</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>RX time-out interrupt is generated</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BUFERRINT</name>
              <description>Buffer Error Interrupt Indicator (Read Only)\nThis bit is set if BUFERRIEN(UART_INTEN[5]) and BUFERRIF(UART_ INTSTS[5]) are both set to 1.\nNote: This bit is valid only when RXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[15]) and TXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[14]) are both 0.</description>
              <bitOffset>13</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No buffer error interrupt is generated</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Buffer error interrupt is generated</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>WKINT</name>
              <description>UART Wake-up Interrupt Indicator (Read Only)\nThis bit is set if WKIEN (UART_INTEN[6]) and WKIF (UART_INTSTS[6]) are both set to 1.</description>
              <bitOffset>14</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No UART wake-up interrupt is generated</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>UART wake-up interrupt is generated</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>LININT</name>
              <description>LIN Bus Interrupt Indicator (Read Only)\nThis bit is set if LINIEN (UART_INTEN[8]) and LINIF(UART_INTSTS[7]) are both set to 1.</description>
              <bitOffset>15</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No LIN Bus interrupt is generated</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>The LIN Bus interrupt is generated</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SWBEIF</name>
              <description>Single-wire Bit Error Detection Interrupt Flag\nThis bit is set when the single wire bus state not equals to UART controller TX state in Single-wire mode.\nNote 1: This bit is active when FUNCSEL (UART_FUNCSEL[2:0]) is select UART Single-wire mode.\nNote 2: This bit can be cleared by writing '1' to it.</description>
              <bitOffset>16</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No single-wire bit error detection interrupt flag is generated</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Single-wire bit error detection interrupt flag is generated</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>HWRLSIF</name>
              <description>PDMA Mode Receive Line Status Flag (Read Only)\nThis bit is set when the RX receive data have parity error, frame error or break error (at least one of 3 bits, BIF (UART_FIFOSTS[6]), FEF (UART_FIFOSTS[5]) and PEF (UART_FIFOSTS[4]) is set). If RLSIEN (UART_INTEN[2]) is enabled, the RLS interrupt will be generated.\nNote 2: In UART function mode, this bit is read only and reset to 0 when all bits of BIF(UART_FIFOSTS[6]), FEF(UART_FIFOSTS[5]) and PEF(UART_FIFOSTS[4]) are cleared. \nNote 3: In RS-485 function mode, this bit is read only and reset to 0 when all bits of BIF(UART_FIFOSTS[6]), FEF(UART_FIFOSTS[5]), PEF(UART_FIFOSTS[4]) and ADDRDETF (UART_FIFOSTS[3]) are cleared.</description>
              <bitOffset>18</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No RLS interrupt flag is generated when RXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[15]) or TXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[14]) is 1</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>RLS interrupt flag is generated when RXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[15]) or TXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[14]) is 1</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>HWMODIF</name>
              <description>PDMA Mode MODEM Interrupt Flag (Read Only)\nNote: This bit is read only and reset to 0 when the bit CTSDETF (UART_MODEMSTS[0]) is cleared by writing 1 on CTSDETF (UART_MODEMSTS[0]).</description>
              <bitOffset>19</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No Modem interrupt flag is generated when RXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[15]) or TXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[14]) is 1</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Modem interrupt flag is generated when RXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[15]) or TXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[14]) is 1</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>HWTOIF</name>
              <description>PDMA Mode RX Time-out Interrupt Flag (Read Only)\nThis bit is set when the RX FIFO is not empty and no activities occurred in the RX FIFO and the time-out counter equal to TOIC (UART_TOUT[7:0]). If RXTOIEN (UART_INTEN[4]) is enabled, the RX time-out interrupt will be generated. \nNote: This bit is read only and user can read UART_DAT (RX is in active) to clear it.</description>
              <bitOffset>20</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No RX time-out interrupt flag is generated when RXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[15]) or TXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[14]) is 1</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>RX time-out interrupt flag is generated when RXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[15]) or TXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[14]) is 1</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>HWBUFEIF</name>
              <description>PDMA Mode Buffer Error Interrupt Flag (Read Only)\nThis bit is set when the TX or RX FIFO overflows (TXOVIF (UART_FIFOSTS[24]) or RXOVIF (UART_FIFOSTS[0]) is set). When BUFERRIF (UART_INTSTS[5]) is set, the transfer maybe is not correct. If BUFERRIEN (UART_INTEN[5]) is enabled, the buffer error interrupt will be generated.\nNote: This bit is cleared when both TXOVIF (UART_FIFOSTS[24]]) and RXOVIF  (UART_FIFOSTS[0]) are cleared.</description>
              <bitOffset>21</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No buffer error interrupt flag is generated when RXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[15]) or TXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[14]) is 1</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Buffer error interrupt flag is generated when RXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[15]) or TXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[14]) is 1</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TXENDIF</name>
              <description>Transmitter Empty Interrupt Flag (Read Only)\nThis bit is set when TX FIFO (UART_DAT) is empty and the STOP bit of the last byte has been transmitted (TXEMPTYF (UART_FIFOSTS[28]) is set). If TXENDIEN (UART_INTEN[22]) is enabled, the Transmitter Empty interrupt will be generated.\nNote: This bit is cleared automatically when TX FIFO is not empty or the last byte transmission has not completed.</description>
              <bitOffset>22</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No transmitter empty interrupt flag is generated</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Transmitter empty interrupt flag is generated</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SWBEINT</name>
              <description>Single-wire Bit Error Detect Interrupt Indicator (Read Only)\nThis bit is set if SWBEIEN (UART_INTEN[16]) and SWBEIF (UART_INTSTS[16]) are both set to 1.</description>
              <bitOffset>24</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No Single-wire Bit Error Detection Interrupt generated</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Single-wire Bit Error Detection Interrupt generated</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>HWRLSINT</name>
              <description>PDMA Mode Receive Line Status Interrupt Indicator (Read Only)\nThis bit is set if RLSIEN (UART_INTEN[2]) and HWRLSIF(UART_INTSTS[18]) are both set to 1.</description>
              <bitOffset>26</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No RLS interrupt is generated when RXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[15]) or TXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[14]) is 1</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>RLS interrupt is generated when RXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[15]) or TXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[14]) is 1</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>HWMODINT</name>
              <description>PDMA Mode MODEM Status Interrupt Indicator (Read Only)\nThis bit is set if MODEMIEN (UART_INTEN[3]) and HWMODIF(UART_INTSTS[19]) are both set to 1.</description>
              <bitOffset>27</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No Modem interrupt is generated when RXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[15]) or TXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[14]) is 1</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Modem interrupt is generated when RXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[15]) or TXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[14]) is 1</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>HWTOINT</name>
              <description>PDMA Mode RX Time-out Interrupt Indicator (Read Only)\nThis bit is set if RXTOIEN (UART_INTEN[4]) and HWTOIF(UART_INTSTS[20]) are both set to 1.</description>
              <bitOffset>28</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No RX time-out interrupt is generated when RXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[15]) or TXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[14]) is 1</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>RX time-out interrupt is generated when RXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[15]) or TXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[14]) is 1</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>HWBUFEINT</name>
              <description>PDMA Mode Buffer Error Interrupt Indicator (Read Only)\nThis bit is set if BUFERRIEN (UART_INTEN[5]) and HWBUFEIF (UART_INTSTS[21]) are both set to 1.</description>
              <bitOffset>29</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No buffer error interrupt is generated when RXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[15]) or TXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[14]) is 1</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Buffer error interrupt is generated when RXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[15]) or TXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[14]) is 1</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TXENDINT</name>
              <description>Transmitter Empty Interrupt Indicator (Read Only) \nThis bit is set if TXENDIEN (UART_INTEN[22]) and TXENDIF(UART_INTSTS[22]) are both set to 1.</description>
              <bitOffset>30</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No Transmitter Empty interrupt is generated</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Transmitter Empty interrupt is generated</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ABRINT</name>
              <description>Auto-baud Rate Interrupt Indicator (Read Only)\nThis bit is set if ABRIEN (UART_INTEN[18]) and ABRIF (UART_ALTCTL[17]) are both set to 1.</description>
              <bitOffset>31</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No Auto-baud Rate interrupt is generated</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>The Auto-baud Rate interrupt is generated</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>UARTx_TOUT</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>UARTx_TOUT</displayName>
          <description>UART Time-out Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x20</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>TOIC</name>
              <description>Time-out Interrupt Comparator</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>8</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>DLY</name>
              <description>TX Delay Time Value \nThis field is used to program the transfer delay time between the last STOP bit and next START bit. The unit is bit time.</description>
              <bitOffset>8</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>8</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BITOMEN</name>
              <description>Bus Idle Time-out Mode Enable Bit\nIf BITOMEN (UART_TOUT[31]) is enabled, the reset conditions of the time-out counter and RXTOIF (UART_INTSTS[4]) will be changed to detect the bus idle.\nWhen BITOMEN (UART_TOUT[31]) is disabled, the time-out counter and RXTOIF (UART_INTSTS[4]) maintain reset value whenever the RX FIFO is empty. In addition, Any RX FIFO activities, such as a new incoming data or user reading data, will clear RXTOIF (UART_INTSTS[4]) and reset RX time-out counter.. \nOn the other hand, when BITOMEN (UART_TOUT[31]) is enabled, the RX FIFO empty state will not reset the time-out counter and RXTOIF (UART_INTSTS[4]), and RX FIFO activities, such as a new incoming data or user reading data, will not clear RXTOIF (UART_INTSTS[4]). However, the time-out counter needs to receive a first incoming data before it can start counting. After receiving the first incoming data, the time-out counter will count even if the RX FIFO is empty.</description>
              <bitOffset>31</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Bus idle time-out mode Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Bus idle time-out mode Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>UARTx_BAUD</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>UARTx_BAUD</displayName>
          <description>UART Baud Rate Divider Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x24</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x0F0000FF</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>BRD</name>
              <description>Baud Rate Divider\nThe field indicates the baud rate divider. This filed is used in baud rate calculation. The detail description is shown in Table 6.214.</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>16</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BRFD</name>
              <description>Baud Rate Fractional Divider\nThis field is the fractional part of the baud rate divisor.  \nWhen BRFDEN (UART_BAUD[30]) is set, the Baud Rate Equation goes to UART_CLK / ((BRD+2) + (BRFD/256)). </description>
              <bitOffset>16</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>8</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>EDIVM1</name>
              <description>Extra Divider for BAUD Rate Mode 1\nThis field is used for baud rate calculation in mode 1 and has no effect for baud rate calculation in mode 0 and mode 2. The detail description is shown in Table 6.214.</description>
              <bitOffset>24</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>4</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BAUDM0</name>
              <description>BAUD Rate Mode Selection Bit 0\nThis bit is baud rate mode selection bit 0. UART provides three baud rate calculation modes. This bit combines with BAUDM1 (UART_BAUD[29]) to select baud rate calculation mode. The detail description is shown in Table 6.214.</description>
              <bitOffset>28</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BAUDM1</name>
              <description>BAUD Rate Mode Selection Bit 1\nThis bit is baud rate mode selection bit 1. UART provides three baud rate calculation modes. This bit combines with BAUDM0 (UART_BAUD[28]) to select baud rate calculation mode. The detail description is shown in Table 6.214.\nNote: In IrDA mode must be operated in mode 0.</description>
              <bitOffset>29</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BRFDEN</name>
              <description>Baud Rate Fractional Divider Enable Bit\nNote: This bit has effect only at baud rate mode 2 (BAUDM1 (UART_BAUD[29]) and BAUDM0 (UART_BAUD[28]) must be 1).</description>
              <bitOffset>30</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Baud Rate Fractional Divider Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Baud Rate Fractional Divider Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>UARTx_IRDA</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>UARTx_IRDA</displayName>
          <description>UART IrDA Control Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x28</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000040</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>TXEN</name>
              <description>IrDA Receiver/Transmitter Selection Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>1</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>IrDA Transmitter Disabled and Receiver Enabled. (Default)</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>IrDA Transmitter Enabled and Receiver Disabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TXINV</name>
              <description>IrDA Inverse Transmitting Output Signal \nNote 1: Before setting this bit, TXRXDIS (UART_FUNCSEL[3]) should be set then waited for TXRXACT (UART_FIFOSTS[31]) is cleared. When the configuration is done, cleared TXRXDIS (UART_FUNCSEL[3]) to activate UART controller.\nNote 2: This bit is valid when FUNCSEL (UART_FUNCSEL[2:0]) is select IrDA function.</description>
              <bitOffset>5</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>None inverse transmitting signal. (Default)</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Inverse transmitting output signal</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RXINV</name>
              <description>IrDA Inverse Receive Input Signal \nNote 1: Before setting this bit, TXRXDIS (UART_FUNCSEL[3]) should be set then waited for TXRXACT (UART_FIFOSTS[31]) is cleared. When the configuration is done, cleared TXRXDIS (UART_FUNCSEL[3]) to activate UART controller.\nNote 2: This bit is valid when FUNCSEL (UART_FUNCSEL[2:0]) is select IrDA function.</description>
              <bitOffset>6</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>None inverse receiving input signal</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Inverse receiving input signal. (Default)</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>UARTx_ALTCTL</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>UARTx_ALTCTL</displayName>
          <description>UART Alternate Control/Status Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x2C</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x0000000C</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>BRKFL</name>
              <description>UART LIN Break Field Length\nThis field indicates a 4-bit LIN TX break field count.\nNote 1: This break field length is BRKFL + 1.</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>4</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>LINRXEN</name>
              <description>LIN RX Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>6</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>LIN RX mode Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>LIN RX mode Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>LINTXEN</name>
              <description>LIN TX Break Mode Enable Bit\nNote: When TX break field transfer operation finished, this bit will be cleared automatically.</description>
              <bitOffset>7</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>LIN TX Break mode Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>LIN TX Break mode Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RS485NMM</name>
              <description>RS-485 Normal Multi-drop Operation Mode\nNote: It cannot be active with RS-485_AAD operation mode.</description>
              <bitOffset>8</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>RS-485 Normal Multi-drop Operation mode (NMM) Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>RS-485 Normal Multi-drop Operation mode (NMM) Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RS485AAD</name>
              <description>RS-485 Auto Address Detection Operation Mode\nNote: It cannot be active with RS-485_NMM operation mode.</description>
              <bitOffset>9</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>RS-485 Auto Address Detection Operation mode (AAD) Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>RS-485 Auto Address Detection Operation mode (AAD) Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RS485AUD</name>
              <description>RS-485 Auto Direction Function\nNote: It can be active with RS-485_AAD or RS-485_NMM operation mode.</description>
              <bitOffset>10</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>RS-485 Auto Direction Operation function (AUD) Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>RS-485 Auto Direction Operation function (AUD) Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ADDRDEN</name>
              <description>RS-485 Address Detection Enable Bit\nThis bit is used to enable RS-485 Address Detection mode. \nNote: This bit is used for RS-485 any operation mode.</description>
              <bitOffset>15</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Address detection mode Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Address detection mode Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ABRIF</name>
              <description>Auto-baud Rate Interrupt Flag (Read Only) \nThis bit is set when auto-baud rate detection function finished or the auto-baud rate counter was overflow and if ABRIEN(UART_INTEN[18]) is set then the auto-baud rate interrupt will be generated. \nNote: This bit is read only, but it can be cleared by writing '1' to ABRDTOIF (UART_FIFOSTS[2]) and ABRDIF(UART_FIFOSTS[1]).</description>
              <bitOffset>17</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No auto-baud rate interrupt flag is generated</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Auto-baud rate interrupt flag is generated</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ABRDEN</name>
              <description>Auto-baud Rate Detect Enable Bit\nNote: This bit is cleared automatically after auto-baud detection is finished.</description>
              <bitOffset>18</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Auto-baud rate detect function Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Auto-baud rate detect function Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ABRDBITS</name>
              <description>Auto-baud Rate Detect Bit Length \nNote: The calculation of bit number includes the START bit.</description>
              <bitOffset>19</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>2</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>1-bit time from START bit to the 1st rising edge. The input pattern shall be 0x01</description>
                    <value>#00</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>2-bit time from START bit to the 1st rising edge. The input pattern shall be 0x02</description>
                    <value>#01</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>2</name>
                    <description>4-bit time from START bit to the 1st rising edge. The input pattern shall be 0x08</description>
                    <value>#10</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>3</name>
                    <description>8-bit time from START bit to the 1st rising edge. The input pattern shall be 0x80</description>
                    <value>#11</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ADDRMV</name>
              <description>Address Match Value \nThis field contains the RS-485 address match values.\nNote: This field is used for RS-485 auto address detection mode.</description>
              <bitOffset>24</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>8</bitWidth>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>UARTx_FUNCSEL</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>UARTx_FUNCSEL</displayName>
          <description>UART Function Select Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x30</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>FUNCSEL</name>
              <description>Function Select</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>3</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>UART function</description>
                    <value>#000</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>LIN function</description>
                    <value>#001</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>2</name>
                    <description>IrDA function</description>
                    <value>#010</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>3</name>
                    <description>RS-485 function</description>
                    <value>#011</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>4</name>
                    <description>UART Single-wire function</description>
                    <value>#100</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TXRXDIS</name>
              <description>TX and RX Disable Bit\nSetting this bit can disable TX and RX.\nNote: The TX and RX will not be disabled immediately when this bit is set. The TX and RX complete current task before TX and RX are disabled. When TX and RX are disabled, the TXRXACT (UART_FIFOSTS[31]) is cleared.</description>
              <bitOffset>3</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>TX and RX Enabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>TX and RX Disabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>DGE</name>
              <description>Deglitch Enable Bit\nNote 1: When this bit is set to logic 1, any pulse width less than about 150 ns will be considered a glitch and will be removed in the serial data input (RX). This bit acts only on RX line and has no effect on the transmitter logic.\nNote 2: It is recommended to set this bit only when operating at baud rate under 2.5 Mbps.</description>
              <bitOffset>6</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Deglitch Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Deglitch Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TXRXSWP</name>
              <description>TX and RX Swap Enable Bit\nSetting this bit Swaps TX pin and RX pin.</description>
              <bitOffset>7</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>TX and RX Swap Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>TX and RX Swap Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>UARTx_LINCTL</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>UARTx_LINCTL</displayName>
          <description>UART LIN Control Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x34</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x000C0000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>SLVEN</name>
              <description>LIN Slave Mode Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>LIN slave mode Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>LIN slave mode Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SLVHDEN</name>
              <description>LIN Slave Header Detection Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>1</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>LIN slave header detection Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>LIN slave header detection Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SLVAREN</name>
              <description>LIN Slave Automatic Resynchronization Mode Enable Bit\nNote 2: When operation in Automatic Resynchronization mode, the baud rate setting must be mode 2 (BAUDM1 (UART_BAUD[29]) and BAUDM0 (UART_BAUD[28]) must be 1).\nNote 3: The control and interactions of this field are explained in 6.21.5.10 (Slave mode with automatic resynchronization).\nNote 4: The minimum detection range of the target BRD (UART_BAUD[15:0]) is 0x30.</description>
              <bitOffset>2</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>LIN automatic resynchronization Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>LIN automatic resynchronization Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SLVDUEN</name>
              <description>LIN Slave Divider Update Method Enable Bit\nNote 2: This bit used for LIN Slave Automatic Resynchronization mode. (for Non-Automatic Resynchronization mode, this bit should be kept cleared) \nNote 3: The control and interactions of this field are explained in 6.21.5.10 (Slave mode with automatic resynchronization).</description>
              <bitOffset>3</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>UART_BAUD updated is written by software (if no automatic resynchronization update occurs at the same time)</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>UART_BAUD is updated at the next received character. User must set the bit before checksum reception</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>MUTE</name>
              <description>LIN Mute Mode Enable Bit\nNote: The exit from mute mode condition and each control and interactions of this field are explained in 6.21.5.10 (LIN slave mode).</description>
              <bitOffset>4</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>LIN mute mode Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>LIN mute mode Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RTOUTEN</name>
              <description>LIN Response Time-out Detection Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>5</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>LIN response time-out detection Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>LIN response time-out detection Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SENDH</name>
              <description>LIN TX Send Header Enable Bit\nThe LIN TX header can be 'break field' or 'break and sync field' or 'break, sync and frame ID field', it is depend on setting HSEL (UART_LINCTL[23:22]).\nNote 1: This bit is shadow bit of LINTXEN (UART_ALTCTL[7]); user can read/write it by setting LINTXEN (UART_ALTCTL[7]) or SENDH (UART_LINCTL[8]).\nNote 2: When transmitter header field (it may be 'break' or 'break + sync' or 'break + sync + frame ID' selected by HSEL (UART_LINCTL[23:22]) field) transfer operation finished, this bit will be cleared automatically.</description>
              <bitOffset>8</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Send LIN TX header Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Send LIN TX header Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>IDPEN</name>
              <description>LIN ID Parity Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>9</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>LIN frame ID parity Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>LIN frame ID parity Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BRKDETEN</name>
              <description>LIN Break Detection Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>10</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>LIN break detection Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>LIN break detection Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>LINRXOFF</name>
              <description>LIN Receiver Disable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>11</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>LIN receiver Enabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>LIN receiver Disabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BITERREN</name>
              <description>Bit Error Detect Enable Bit </description>
              <bitOffset>12</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Bit error detection function Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Bit error detection function Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BRKFL</name>
              <description>LIN Break Field Length \nThis field indicates a 4-bit LIN TX break field count.\nNote 1: These registers are shadow registers of BRKFL (UART_ALTCTL[3:0]). User can read/write it by setting BRKFL (UART_ALTCTL[3:0]) or BRKFL (UART_LINCTL[19:16]).\nNote 2: This break field length is BRKFL + 1.</description>
              <bitOffset>16</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>4</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BSL</name>
              <description>LIN Break/Sync Delimiter Length \nNote: This bit used for LIN master to sending header field.</description>
              <bitOffset>20</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>2</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>The LIN break/sync delimiter length is 1-bit time</description>
                    <value>#00</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>The LIN break/sync delimiter length is 2-bit time</description>
                    <value>#01</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>2</name>
                    <description>The LIN break/sync delimiter length is 3-bit time</description>
                    <value>#10</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>3</name>
                    <description>The LIN break/sync delimiter length is 4-bit time</description>
                    <value>#11</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>HSEL</name>
              <description>LIN Header Select </description>
              <bitOffset>22</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>2</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>The LIN header includes 'break field'</description>
                    <value>#00</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>The LIN header includes 'break field' and 'sync field'</description>
                    <value>#01</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>2</name>
                    <description>The LIN header includes 'break field', 'sync field' and 'frame ID field'</description>
                    <value>#10</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>3</name>
                    <description>Reserved.</description>
                    <value>#11</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>PID</name>
              <description>LIN PID Bits\nIf the parity generated by hardware, user fill ID0~ID5 (PID[29:24]), hardware will calculate P0 (PID[30]) and P1 (PID[31]), otherwise user must filled frame ID and parity in this field.\nNote 1: User can fill any 8-bit value to this field and the bit 24 indicates ID0 (LSB first).\nNote 2: This field can be used for LIN master mode or slave mode.</description>
              <bitOffset>24</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>8</bitWidth>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>UARTx_LINSTS</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>UARTx_LINSTS</displayName>
          <description>UART LIN Status Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x38</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>SLVHDETF</name>
              <description>LIN Slave Header Detection Flag\nThis bit is set by hardware when a LIN header is detected in LIN slave mode and be cleared by writing 1 to it.\nNote 3: When enable ID parity check IDPEN (UART_LINCTL[9]), if hardware detect complete header ('break + sync + frame ID'), the SLVHDETF will be set whether the frame ID correct or not.</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>LIN header not detected</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>LIN header detected (break + sync + frame ID)</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SLVHEF</name>
              <description>LIN Slave Header Error Flag\nThis bit is set by hardware when a LIN header error is detected in LIN slave mode and be cleared by writing 1 to it. The header errors include 'break delimiter is too short (less than 0.5 bit time)', 'frame error in sync field or Identifier field', 'sync field data is not 0x55 in Non-Automatic Resynchronization mode', 'sync field deviation error with Automatic Resynchronization mode', 'sync field measure time-out with Automatic Resynchronization mode' and 'LIN header reception time-out'.</description>
              <bitOffset>1</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>LIN header error not detected</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>LIN header error detected</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SLVIDPEF</name>
              <description>LIN Slave ID Parity Error Flag \nThis bit is set by hardware when receipted frame ID parity is not correct.</description>
              <bitOffset>2</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No active</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Receipted frame ID parity is not correct</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SLVSYNCF</name>
              <description>LIN Slave Sync Field\nThis bit indicates that the LIN sync field is being analyzed in Automatic Resynchronization mode. When the receiver header have some error been detect, user must reset the internal circuit to re-search new frame header by writing 1 to this bit.\nNote 2: This bit can be cleared by writing 1 to it.\nNote 3: When writing 1 to it, hardware will reload the initial baud rate and re-search a new frame header.</description>
              <bitOffset>3</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>The current character is not at LIN sync state</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>The current character is at LIN sync state</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SLVHTOF</name>
              <description>LIN Slave Header Time-out Flag\nThis bit is set by hardware when a LIN header reception time-out is detected in LIN slave mode and be cleared by writing 1 to it. When this bit is set, SLVHEF (UART_LINSTS[1]) will also be set.</description>
              <bitOffset>4</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>LIN header time-out not detected</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>LIN header time-out detected</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RTOUTF</name>
              <description>LIN Response Time-out Flag\nThis bit is set when no LIN response received and the time-out counter equal to or bigger than LINRTOIC (UART_LINRTOUT[23:0]). If LINIEN (UART_INTEN[8]) is enabled, the LIN Bus Interrupt will be generated.\nNote 1: This bit can be cleared by writing 1 to it.</description>
              <bitOffset>5</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>LIN response time-out not detected</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>LIN response time-out detected</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BRKDETF</name>
              <description>LIN Break Detection Flag\nThis bit is set by hardware when a break is detected and be cleared by writing 1 to it through software.</description>
              <bitOffset>8</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>LIN break not detected</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>LIN break detected</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BITEF</name>
              <description>Bit Error Detect Status Flag \nAt TX transfer state, hardware will monitor the bus state, if the input pin (UART_RXD) state not equals to the output pin (UART_TXD) state, BITEF (UART_LINSTS[9]) will be set.</description>
              <bitOffset>9</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Bit error not detected</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Bit error detected</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>UARTx_BRCOMP</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>UARTx_BRCOMP</displayName>
          <description>UART Baud Rate Compensation Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x3C</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>BRCOMP</name>
              <description>Baud Rate Compensation Patten\nThese 9-bits are used to define the relative bit is compensated or not. \nBRCOMP[7:0] is used to define the compensation of DAT (UART_DAT[7:0]) and BRCOM[8] is used to define PARITY (UART_DAT[8]).</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>9</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BRCOMPDEC</name>
              <description>Baud Rate Compensation Decrease</description>
              <bitOffset>31</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Positive (increase one module clock) compensation for each compensated bit</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Negative (decrease one module clock) compensation for each compensated bit</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>UARTx_WKCTL</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>UARTx_WKCTL</displayName>
          <description>UART Wake-up Control Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x40</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>WKCTSEN</name>
              <description>nCTS Wake-up Enable Bit\nNote: When the system is in Power-down mode, an external nCTS change will wake up system from Power-down mode.</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>nCTS Wake-up system function Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>nCTS Wake-up system function Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>WKDATEN</name>
              <description>Incoming Data Wake-up Enable Bit\nNote: When the system is in Power-down mode, incoming data will wake-up system from Power-down mode.</description>
              <bitOffset>1</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Incoming data wake-up system function Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Incoming data wake-up system function Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>WKRFRTEN</name>
              <description>Received Data FIFO Reached Threshold Wake-up Enable Bit\nNote: When the system is in Power-down mode, Received Data FIFO reached threshold will wake-up system from Power-down mode.</description>
              <bitOffset>2</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Received Data FIFO reached threshold wake-up system function Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Received Data FIFO reached threshold wake-up system function Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>WKRS485EN</name>
              <description>RS-485 Address Match Wake-up Enable Bit\nNote 1: When the system is in Power-down mode, RS-485 Address Match will wake-up system from Power-down mode.\nNote 2: This bit is used for RS-485 Auto Address Detection (AAD) mode in RS-485 function mode and ADDRDEN (UART_ALTCTL[15]) is set to 1.</description>
              <bitOffset>3</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>RS-485 Address Match (AAD mode) wake-up system function Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>RS-485 Address Match (AAD mode) wake-up system function Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>WKTOUTEN</name>
              <description>Received Data FIFO Reached Threshold Time-out Wake-up Enable Bit\nNote 1: When the system is in Power-down mode, Received Data FIFO reached threshold time-out will wake up system from Power-down mode.\nNote 2: It is suggested the function is enabled when the WKRFRTEN (UART_WKCTL[2]) is set to 1.</description>
              <bitOffset>4</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Received Data FIFO reached threshold time-out wake-up system function Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Received Data FIFO reached threshold time-out wake-up system function Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>UARTx_WKSTS</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>UARTx_WKSTS</displayName>
          <description>UART Wake-up Status Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x44</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>CTSWKF</name>
              <description>nCTS Wake-up Flag\nThis bit is set if chip wake-up from power-down state by nCTS wake-up.\nNote 1: If WKCTSEN (UART_WKCTL[0]) is enabled, the nCTS wake-up cause this bit is set to '1'.\nNote 2: This bit can be cleared by writing '1' to it.</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Chip stays in power-down state</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Chip wake-up from power-down state by nCTS wake-up</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>DATWKF</name>
              <description>Incoming Data Wake-up Flag\nThis bit is set if chip wake-up from power-down state by data wake-up.\nNote 1: If WKDATEN (UART_WKCTL[1]) is enabled, the Incoming Data wake-up cause this bit is set to '1'.\nNote 2: This bit can be cleared by writing '1' to it.</description>
              <bitOffset>1</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Chip stays in power-down state</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Chip wake-up from power-down state by Incoming Data wake-up</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RFRTWKF</name>
              <description>Received Data FIFO Reached Threshold Wake-up Flag\nThis bit is set if chip wake-up from power-down state by Received Data FIFO reached threshold wake-up.\nNote 1: If WKRFRTEN (UART_WKCTL[2]) is enabled, the Received Data FIFO Reached Threshold wake-up cause this bit is set to '1'.\nNote 2: This bit can be cleared by writing '1' to it.</description>
              <bitOffset>2</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Chip stays in power-down state</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Chip wake-up from power-down state by Received Data FIFO Reached Threshold wake-up</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RS485WKF</name>
              <description>RS-485 Address Match Wake-up Flag\nThis bit is set if chip wake-up from power-down state by RS-485 Address Match (AAD mode).\nNote 1: If WKRS485EN (UART_WKCTL[3]) is enabled, the RS-485 Address Match (AAD mode) wake-up cause this bit is set to '1'.\nNote 2: This bit can be cleared by writing '1' to it.</description>
              <bitOffset>3</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Chip stays in power-down state</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Chip wake-up from power-down state by RS-485 Address Match (AAD mode) wake-up</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TOUTWKF</name>
              <description>Received Data FIFO Threshold Time-out Wake-up Flag\nThis bit is set if chip wake-up from power-down state by Received Data FIFO Threshold Time-out\nwake-up.\nNote 1: If WKTOUTEN (UART_WKCTL[4]) is enabled, the Received Data FIFO reached threshold time-out wake-up cause this bit is set to '1'.\nNote 2: This bit can be cleared by writing '1' to it.</description>
              <bitOffset>4</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Chip stays in power-down state</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Chip wake-up from power-down state by Received Data FIFO reached threshold time-out wake-up</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>UARTx_DWKCOMP</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>UARTx_DWKCOMP</displayName>
          <description>UART Incoming Data Wake-up Compensation Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x48</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>STCOMP</name>
              <description>START Bit Compensation Value\nThese bits field indicate how many clock cycle selected by UART_CLK do the UART controller can get the 1st bit (START bit) when the device is wake-up from Power-down mode.\nNote: It is valid only when WKDATEN (UART_WKCTL[1]) is set.</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>16</bitWidth>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>UARTx_RS485DD</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>UARTx_RS485DD</displayName>
          <description>UART RS485 Transceiver Deactivate Delay Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x4C</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>RTSDDLY</name>
              <description>RS485 Transceiver Deactivate Delay Value\nThese bits field indicate how many clock cycles selected by UART_CLK do the UART controller delay the RS485 transceiver state trancing when the state trancing of RS485 transceiver is from TX to RX state. These bits field have no effect when the state trancing of RS485 transceiver is from RX to TX state.\nNote: It is valid only when RS485AUD (UART_ALTCTL[10]) is set.</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>16</bitWidth>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>UARTx_LINRTOUT</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>UARTx_LINRTOUT</displayName>
          <description>UART LIN Response Time-out Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x50</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00FFFFFF</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>LINRTOIC</name>
              <description>LIN Response Time-out Comparator</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>24</bitWidth>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>UARTx_LINWKCTL</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>UARTx_LINWKCTL</displayName>
          <description>UART LIN Wake-up Control Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x54</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x000000C0</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>LINWKC</name>
              <description>LIN Send Wake-up Signal Length Counter</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>24</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SENDLINW</name>
              <description>LIN Send Wake-up Enable Bit\nNote 1: When this bit is set, the UART will send LIN wake-up automatically. When LIN wake-up transfer operation finished, this bit will be cleared automatically.</description>
              <bitOffset>24</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Send LIN Wake-up Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Send LIN Wake-up Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>LINWKEN</name>
              <description>LIN Wake-up Enable Bit\nNote 1: When the system is in Power-down mode, LIN wake-up event will wake up system from Power-down mode.</description>
              <bitOffset>28</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>LIN wake-up system function Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>LIN wake-up system function Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>LINWKF</name>
              <description>LIN Wake-up Flag\nThis bit is set if chip wake-up from power-down state by LIN wake-up.\nNote 1: If LINWKEN (UART_LINWKCTL[28]) is enabled, the LIN wake-up event will cause this bit set to '1'.\nNote 2: This bit can be cleared by writing '1' to it.</description>
              <bitOffset>29</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Chip stays in power-down state</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Chip wake-up from power-down state by LIN wake-up</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
      </registers>
    </peripheral>
    <peripheral>
      <name>UART7</name>
      <description>UART Register Map</description>
      <groupName>UART</groupName>
      <baseAddress>0x40077000</baseAddress>
      <!-- d -> no access permission -->
      <addressBlock>
        <offset>0x0</offset>
        <size>0x58</size>
        <usage>registers</usage>
      </addressBlock>
      <registers>
       <register>
          <name>UARTx_DAT</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>UARTx_DAT</displayName>
          <description>UART Receive/Transmit Buffer Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x0</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0x00000000</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>DAT</name>
              <description>Data Receive/Transmit Buffer\nWrite Operation:\nBy writing one byte to this register, the data byte will be stored in transmitter FIFO. The UART controller will send out the data stored in transmitter FIFO top location through the UART_TXD.\nRead Operation:\nBy reading this register, the UART controller will return an 8-bit data received from receiver FIFO.</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>8</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>PARITY</name>
              <description>PARITY Bit Receive/Transmit Buffer\nWrite Operation:\nBy writing to this bit, the PARITY bit will be stored in transmitter FIFO. If PBE (UART_LINE[3]) and PSS (UART_LINE[7]) are set, the UART controller will send out this bit follow the DAT (UART_DAT[7:0]) through the UART_TXD.\nRead Operation:\nIf PBE (UART_LINE[3]) and PSS (UART_LINE[7]) are enabled, the PARITY bit can be read by this bit.\nNote: This bit has effect only when PBE (UART_LINE[3]) and PSS (UART_LINE[7]) are set.</description>
              <bitOffset>8</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>UARTx_INTEN</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>UARTx_INTEN</displayName>
          <description>UART Interrupt Enable Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x4</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>RDAIEN</name>
              <description>Receive Data Available Interrupt Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Receive data available interrupt Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Receive data available interrupt Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>THREIEN</name>
              <description>Transmit Holding Register Empty Interrupt Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>1</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Transmit holding register empty interrupt Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Transmit holding register empty interrupt Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RLSIEN</name>
              <description>Receive Line Status Interrupt Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>2</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Receive Line Status interrupt Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Receive Line Status interrupt Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>MODEMIEN</name>
              <description>Modem Status Interrupt Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>3</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Modem status interrupt Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Modem status interrupt Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RXTOIEN</name>
              <description>RX Time-out Interrupt Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>4</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>RX time-out interrupt Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>RX time-out interrupt Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BUFERRIEN</name>
              <description>Buffer Error Interrupt Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>5</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Buffer error interrupt Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Buffer error interrupt Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>WKIEN</name>
              <description>Wake-up Interrupt Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>6</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Wake-up Interrupt Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Wake-up Interrupt Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>LINIEN</name>
              <description>LIN Bus Interrupt Enable Bit\nNote: This bit is used for LIN function mode.</description>
              <bitOffset>8</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>LIN bus interrupt Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>LIN bus interrupt Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TOCNTEN</name>
              <description>Receive Buffer Time-out Counter Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>11</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Receive Buffer Time-out counter Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Receive Buffer Time-out counter Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ATORTSEN</name>
              <description>nRTS Auto-flow Control Enable Bit\nNote: When nRTS auto-flow is enabled, if the number of bytes in the RX FIFO equals the RTSTRGLV (UART_FIFO[19:16]), the UART will de-assert nRTS signal.</description>
              <bitOffset>12</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>nRTS auto-flow control Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>nRTS auto-flow control Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ATOCTSEN</name>
              <description>nCTS Auto-flow Control Enable Bit\nNote: When nCTS auto-flow is enabled, the UART will send data to external device if nCTS input assert (UART will not send data to device until nCTS is asserted).</description>
              <bitOffset>13</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>nCTS auto-flow control Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>nCTS auto-flow control Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TXPDMAEN</name>
              <description>TX PDMA Enable Bit\nNote: If RLSIEN (UART_INTEN[2]) is enabled and HWRLSINT (UART_INTSTS[26]) is set to 1, the RLS (Receive Line Status) Interrupt is caused. If RLS interrupt is caused by Break Error Flag BIF(UART_FIFOSTS[6]), Frame Error Flag FEF(UART_FIFO[5]) or Parity Error Flag PEF(UART_FIFOSTS[4]), UART PDMA transmit request operation is stopped. Clear Break Error Flag BIF or Frame Error Flag FEF or Parity Error Flag PEF by writing '1' to corresponding BIF, FEF and PEF to make UART PDMA transmit request operation continue.</description>
              <bitOffset>14</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>TX PDMA Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>TX PDMA Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RXPDMAEN</name>
              <description>RX PDMA Enable Bit\nThis bit can enable or disable RX PDMA service.\nNote: If RLSIEN (UART_INTEN[2]) is enabled and HWRLSINT (UART_INTSTS[26]) is set to 1, the RLS (Receive Line Status) Interrupt is caused. If RLS interrupt is caused by Break Error Flag BIF(UART_FIFOSTS[6]), Frame Error Flag FEF(UART_FIFO[5]) or Parity Error Flag PEF(UART_FIFOSTS[4]), UART PDMA receive request operation is stopped. Clear Break Error Flag BIF or Frame Error Flag FEF or Parity Error Flag PEF by writing '1' to corresponding BIF, FEF and PEF to make UART PDMA receive request operation continue.</description>
              <bitOffset>15</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>RX PDMA Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>RX PDMA Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SWBEIEN</name>
              <description>Single-wire Bit Error Detection Interrupt Enable Bit\nSet this bit, the Single-wire Half Duplex Bit Error Detection Interrupt SWBEINT(UART_INTSTS[24]) is generated when Single-wire Bit Error Detection SWBEIF(UART_INTSTS[16]) is set.\nNote: This bit is valid when FUNCSEL (UART_FUNCSEL[2:0]) is select UART Single-wire mode.</description>
              <bitOffset>16</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Single-wire Bit Error Detect Interrupt Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Single-wire Bit Error Detect Interrupt Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ABRIEN</name>
              <description>Auto-baud Rate Interrupt Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>18</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Auto-baud rate interrupt Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Auto-baud rate interrupt Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TXENDIEN</name>
              <description>Transmitter Empty Interrupt Enable Bit\nIf TXENDIEN (UART_INTEN[22]) is enabled, the Transmitter Empty interrupt TXENDINT (UART_INTSTS[30]) will be generated when TXENDIF (UART_INTSTS[22]) is set (TX FIFO (UART_DAT) is empty and the STOP bit of the last byte has been transmitted).</description>
              <bitOffset>22</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Transmitter empty interrupt Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Transmitter empty interrupt Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>UARTx_FIFO</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>UARTx_FIFO</displayName>
          <description>UART FIFO Control Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x8</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000101</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>RXRST</name>
              <description>RX Field Software Reset\nWhen RXRST (UART_FIFO[1]) is set, all the byte in the receiver FIFO and RX internal state machine are cleared.\nNote 1: This bit will automatically clear at least 3 UART peripheral clock cycles.\nNote 2: Before setting this bit, it should wait for the RXIDLE (UART_FIFOSTS[29]) be set.</description>
              <bitOffset>1</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No effect</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Reset the RX internal state machine and pointers</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TXRST</name>
              <description>TX Field Software Reset\nWhen TXRST (UART_FIFO[2]) is set, all the byte in the transmit FIFO and TX internal state machine are cleared.\nNote 1: This bit will automatically clear at least 3 UART peripheral clock cycles.\nNote 2: Before setting this bit, it should wait for the TXEMPTYF (UART_FIFOSTS[28]) be set.</description>
              <bitOffset>2</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No effect</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Reset the TX internal state machine and pointers</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RFITL</name>
              <description>RX FIFO Interrupt Trigger Level\nWhen the number of bytes in the receive FIFO equals the RFITL, the RDAIF (UART_INTSTS[0]) will be set (if RDAIEN (UART_INTEN[0]) enabled, and an interrupt will be generated).</description>
              <bitOffset>4</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>4</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>RX FIFO Interrupt Trigger Level is 1 byte</description>
                    <value>#0000</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>RX FIFO Interrupt Trigger Level is 4 bytes</description>
                    <value>#0001</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>2</name>
                    <description>RX FIFO Interrupt Trigger Level is 8 bytes</description>
                    <value>#0010</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>3</name>
                    <description>RX FIFO Interrupt Trigger Level is 14 bytes</description>
                    <value>#0011</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RXOFF</name>
              <description>Receiver Disable Bit\nThe receiver is disabled or not (set 1 to disable receiver).\nNote: This bit is used for RS-485 Normal Multi-drop mode. It should be programmed before RS485NMM (UART_ALTCTL[8]) is programmed.</description>
              <bitOffset>8</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Receiver Enabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Receiver Disabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RTSTRGLV</name>
              <description>nRTS Trigger Level for Auto-flow Control\nNote: This field is used for auto nRTS flow control.</description>
              <bitOffset>16</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>4</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>nRTS Trigger Level is 1 byte</description>
                    <value>#0000</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>nRTS Trigger Level is 4 bytes</description>
                    <value>#0001</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>2</name>
                    <description>nRTS Trigger Level is 8 bytes</description>
                    <value>#0010</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>3</name>
                    <description>nRTS Trigger Level is 14 bytes</description>
                    <value>#0011</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>UARTx_LINE</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>UARTx_LINE</displayName>
          <description>UART Line Control Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0xC</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>WLS</name>
              <description>Word Length Selection\nThis field sets UART word length.</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>2</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>5 bits</description>
                    <value>#00</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>6 bits</description>
                    <value>#01</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>2</name>
                    <description>7 bits</description>
                    <value>#10</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>3</name>
                    <description>8 bits</description>
                    <value>#11</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>NSB</name>
              <description>Number of 'STOP Bit'</description>
              <bitOffset>2</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>One 'STOP bit' is generated in the transmitted data</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>When select 5-bit word length, 1.5 'STOP bit' is generated in the transmitted data. When select 6-, 7- and 8-bit word length, 2 'STOP bit' is generated in the transmitted data</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>PBE</name>
              <description>PARITY Bit Enable Bit\nNote: PARITY bit is generated on each outgoing character and is checked on each incoming data.</description>
              <bitOffset>3</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>PARITY bit generated Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>PARITY bit generated Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>EPE</name>
              <description>Even Parity Enable Bit\nNote: This bit has effect only when PBE (UART_LINE[3]) is set.</description>
              <bitOffset>4</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Odd number of logic 1's is transmitted and checked in each word</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Even number of logic 1's is transmitted and checked in each word</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SPE</name>
              <description>Stick Parity Enable Bit\nNote: If PBE (UART_LINE[3]) and EPE (UART_LINE[4]) are logic 1, the PARITY bit is transmitted and checked as logic 0. If PBE (UART_LINE[3]) is 1 and EPE (UART_LINE[4]) is 0 then the PARITY bit is transmitted and checked as 1.</description>
              <bitOffset>5</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Stick parity Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Stick parity Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BCB</name>
              <description>Break Control Bit\nNote: When this bit is set to logic 1, the transmitted serial data output (TX) is forced to the Spacing State (logic 0). This bit acts only on TX line and has no effect on the transmitter logic.</description>
              <bitOffset>6</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Break Control Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Break Control Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>PSS</name>
              <description>PARITY Bit Source Selection\nThe PARITY bit can be selected to be generated and checked automatically or by software.\nNote 1: This bit has effect only when PBE (UART_LINE[3]) is set.\nNote 2: If PSS is 0, the PARITY bit is transmitted and checked automatically. If PSS is 1, the transmitted PARITY bit value can be determined by writing PARITY (UART_DAT[8]) and the PARITY bit can be read by reading PARITY (UART_DAT[8]).</description>
              <bitOffset>7</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>PARITY bit is generated by EPE (UART_LINE[4]) and SPE (UART_LINE[5]) setting and checked automatically</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>PARITY bit generated and checked by software</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TXDINV</name>
              <description>TX Data Inverted\nNote 1: Before setting this bit, TXRXDIS (UART_FUNCSEL[3]) should be set then waited for TXRXACT (UART_FIFOSTS[31]) is cleared. When the configuration is done, cleared TXRXDIS (UART_FUNCSEL[3]) to activate UART controller.\nNote 2: This bit is valid when FUNCSEL (UART_FUNCSEL[2:0]) is select UART, LIN or RS485 function.</description>
              <bitOffset>8</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Transmitted data signal inverted Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Transmitted data signal inverted Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RXDINV</name>
              <description>RX Data Inverted\nNote 1: Before setting this bit, TXRXDIS (UART_FUNCSEL[3]) should be set then waited for TXRXACT (UART_FIFOSTS[31]) is cleared. When the configuration is done, cleared TXRXDIS (UART_FUNCSEL[3]) to activate UART controller.\nNote 2: This bit is valid when FUNCSEL (UART_FUNCSEL[2:0]) is select UART, LIN or RS485 function.</description>
              <bitOffset>9</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Received data signal inverted Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Received data signal inverted Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>UARTx_MODEM</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>UARTx_MODEM</displayName>
          <description>UART Modem Control Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x10</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000200</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>RTS</name>
              <description>nRTS Signal Control\nThis bit is direct control internal nRTS (Request-to-send) signal active or not, and then drive the nRTS pin output with RTSACTLV bit configuration.\nNote 1: The nRTS signal control bit is not effective when nRTS auto-flow control is enabled in UART function mode.\nNote 2: The nRTS signal control bit is not effective when RS-485 auto direction mode (AUD) is enabled in RS-485 function mode.\nNote 3: Single-wire mode is support this feature.</description>
              <bitOffset>1</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>nRTS signal is active</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>nRTS signal is inactive</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RTSACTLV</name>
              <description>nRTS Pin Active Level\nThis bit defines the active level state of nRTS pin output.\nNote 1: Refer to Figure 6.2113 and Figure 6.2114 for UART function mode.\nNote 2: Refer to Figure 6.2124 and Figure 6.2125 for RS-485 function mode.\nNote 3: Before setting this bit, TXRXDIS (UART_FUNCSEL[3]) should be set then waited for TXRXACT (UART_FIFOSTS[31]) is cleared. When the configuration is done, cleared TXRXDIS (UART_FUNCSEL[3]) to activate UART controller.</description>
              <bitOffset>9</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>nRTS pin output is high level active</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>nRTS pin output is low level active. (Default)</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RTSSTS</name>
              <description>nRTS Pin Status (Read Only)\nThis bit mirror from nRTS pin output of voltage logic status.</description>
              <bitOffset>13</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>nRTS pin output is low level voltage logic state</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>nRTS pin output is high level voltage logic state</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>UARTx_MODEMSTS</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>UARTx_MODEMSTS</displayName>
          <description>UART Modem Status Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x14</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000110</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>CTSDETF</name>
              <description>Detect nCTS State Change Flag\nThis bit is set whenever nCTS input has change state, and it will generate Modem interrupt to CPU when MODEMIEN (UART_INTEN[3]) is set to 1.\nNote: This bit can be cleared by writing '1' to it.</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>nCTS input has not change state</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>nCTS input has change state</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>CTSSTS</name>
              <description>nCTS Pin Status (Read Only)\nThis bit mirror from nCTS pin input of voltage logic status.\nNote: This bit echoes when UART controller peripheral clock is enabled, and nCTS multi-function port is selected.</description>
              <bitOffset>4</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>nCTS pin input is low level voltage logic state</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>nCTS pin input is high level voltage logic state</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>CTSACTLV</name>
              <description>nCTS Pin Active Level\nThis bit defines the active level state of nCTS pin input.\nNote: Before setting this bit, TXRXDIS (UART_FUNCSEL[3]) should be set then waited for TXRXACT (UART_FIFOSTS[31]) is cleared. When the configuration is done, cleared TXRXDIS (UART_FUNCSEL[3]) to activate UART controller.</description>
              <bitOffset>8</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>nCTS pin input is high level active</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>nCTS pin input is low level active. (Default)</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>UARTx_FIFOSTS</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>UARTx_FIFOSTS</displayName>
          <description>UART FIFO Status Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x18</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0xB0404000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>RXOVIF</name>
              <description>RX Overflow Error Interrupt Flag\nThis bit is set when RX FIFO overflow.\nIf the number of bytes of received data is greater than RX_FIFO (UART_DAT) size 16 bytes, this bit will be set.\nNote: This bit can be cleared by writing '1' to it.</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>RX FIFO is not overflow</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>RX FIFO is overflow</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ABRDIF</name>
              <description>Auto-baud Rate Detect Interrupt Flag\nThis bit is set to logic '1' when auto-baud rate detect function is finished.\nNote: This bit can be cleared by writing '1' to it.</description>
              <bitOffset>1</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Auto-baud rate detect function is not finished</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Auto-baud rate detect function is finished</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ABRDTOIF</name>
              <description>Auto-baud Rate Detect Time-out Interrupt Flag\nThis bit is set to logic '1' in Auto-baud Rate Detect mode when the baud rate counter is overflow.\nNote: This bit can be cleared by writing '1' to it.</description>
              <bitOffset>2</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Auto-baud rate counter is underflow</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Auto-baud rate counter is overflow</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ADDRDETF</name>
              <description>RS-485 Address Byte Detect Flag\nNote 1: This field is used for RS-485 function mode and ADDRDEN (UART_ALTCTL[15]) is set to 1 to enable Address detection mode.\nNote 2: This bit can be cleared by writing '1' to it.</description>
              <bitOffset>3</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Receiver detects a data that is not an address bit (bit 9 ='0')</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Receiver detects a data that is an address bit (bit 9 ='1')</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>PEF</name>
              <description>Parity Error Flag\nThis bit is set to logic 1 whenever the received character does not have a valid 'PARITY bit'.\nNote: This bit can be cleared by writing '1' to it.</description>
              <bitOffset>4</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No parity error is generated</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Parity error is generated</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>FEF</name>
              <description>Framing Error Flag\nThis bit is set to logic 1 whenever the received character does not have a valid 'STOP bit' (that is, the STOP bit following the last data bit or PARITY bit is detected as logic 0).\nNote: This bit can be cleared by writing '1' to it.</description>
              <bitOffset>5</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No framing error is generated</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Framing error is generated</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BIF</name>
              <description>Break Interrupt Flag\nThis bit is set to logic 1 whenever the received data input (RX) is held in the 'spacing state' (logic 0) for longer than a full word transmission time (that is, the total time of 'START bit' + data bits + parity + STOP bits).\nNote: This bit can be cleared by writing '1' to it.</description>
              <bitOffset>6</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No Break interrupt is generated</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Break interrupt is generated</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RXPTR</name>
              <description>RX FIFO Pointer (Read Only)\nThis field indicates the RX FIFO Buffer Pointer. When UART receives one byte from external device, RXPTR increases one. When one byte of RX FIFO is read by CPU, RXPTR decreases one.\nThe Maximum value shown in RXPTR is 15. When the using level of RX FIFO Buffer equal to 16, the RXFULL bit is set to 1 and RXPTR will show 0. As one byte of RX FIFO is read by CPU, the RXFULL bit is cleared to 0 and RXPTR will show 15.</description>
              <bitOffset>8</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>6</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RXEMPTY</name>
              <description>Receiver FIFO Empty (Read Only)\nThis bit initiate RX FIFO empty or not.\nNote: When the last byte of RX FIFO has been read by CPU, hardware sets this bit high. It will be cleared when UART receives any new data.</description>
              <bitOffset>14</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>RX FIFO is not empty</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>RX FIFO is empty</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RXFULL</name>
              <description>Receiver FIFO Full (Read Only)\nThis bit initiates RX FIFO full or not.\nNote: This bit is set when the number of usage in RX FIFO Buffer is equal to 16, otherwise it is cleared by hardware.</description>
              <bitOffset>15</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>RX FIFO is not full</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>RX FIFO is full</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TXPTR</name>
              <description>TX FIFO Pointer (Read Only)\nThis field indicates the TX FIFO Buffer Pointer. When CPU writes one byte into UART_DAT, TXPTR increases one. When one byte of TX FIFO is transferred to Transmitter Shift Register, TXPTR decreases one.\nThe Maximum value shown in TXPTR is 15. When the using level of TX FIFO Buffer equal to 16, the TXFULL bit is set to 1 and TXPTR will show 0. As one byte of TX FIFO is transferred to Transmitter Shift Register, the TXFULL bit is cleared to 0 and TXPTR will show 15.</description>
              <bitOffset>16</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>6</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TXEMPTY</name>
              <description>Transmitter FIFO Empty (Read Only)\nThis bit indicates TX FIFO empty or not.\nNote: When the last byte of TX FIFO has been transferred to Transmitter Shift Register, hardware sets this bit high. It will be cleared when writing data into UART_DAT (TX FIFO not empty).</description>
              <bitOffset>22</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>TX FIFO is not empty</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>TX FIFO is empty</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TXFULL</name>
              <description>Transmitter FIFO Full (Read Only)\nThis bit indicates TX FIFO full or not.\nNote: This bit is set when the number of usage in TX FIFO Buffer is equal to 16, otherwise it is cleared by hardware.</description>
              <bitOffset>23</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>TX FIFO is not full</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>TX FIFO is full</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TXOVIF</name>
              <description>TX Overflow Error Interrupt Flag\nIf TX FIFO (UART_DAT) is full, an additional write to UART_DAT will cause this bit to logic 1.\nNote: This bit can be cleared by writing '1' to it.</description>
              <bitOffset>24</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>TX FIFO is not overflow</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>TX FIFO is overflow</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TXEMPTYF</name>
              <description>Transmitter Empty Flag (Read Only)\nThis bit is set by hardware when TX FIFO (UART_DAT) is empty and the STOP bit of the last byte has been transmitted.\nNote: This bit is cleared automatically when TX FIFO is not empty or the last byte transmission has not completed.</description>
              <bitOffset>28</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>TX FIFO is not empty or the STOP bit of the last byte has been not transmitted</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>TX FIFO is empty and the STOP bit of the last byte has been transmitted</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RXIDLE</name>
              <description>RX Idle Status (Read Only)\nThis bit is set by hardware when RX is idle.</description>
              <bitOffset>29</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>RX is busy</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>RX is idle. (Default)</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TXRXACT</name>
              <description>TX and RX Active Status (Read Only)\nThis bit indicates TX and RX are active or inactive.\nNote: When TXRXDIS (UART_FUNCSEL[3]) is set and both TX and RX are in idle state, this bit is cleared. The UART controller cannot transmit or receive data at this moment. Otherwise this bit is set.</description>
              <bitOffset>31</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>TX and RX are inactive</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>TX and RX are active. (Default)</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>UARTx_INTSTS</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>UARTx_INTSTS</displayName>
          <description>UART Interrupt Status Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x1C</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00400002</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>RDAIF</name>
              <description>Receive Data Available Interrupt Flag (Read Only)\nWhen the number of bytes in the RX FIFO equals the RFITL then the RDAIF(UART_INTSTS[0]) will be set. If RDAIEN (UART_INTEN[0]) is enabled, the RDA interrupt will be generated.\nNote: This bit is read only and will be cleared when the number of unread bytes of RX FIFO drops below the threshold level (RFITL(UART_FIFO[7:4]).</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No RDA interrupt flag is generated</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>RDA interrupt flag is generated</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>THREIF</name>
              <description>Transmit Holding Register Empty Interrupt Flag (Read Only)\nThis bit is set when the last data of TX FIFO is transferred to Transmitter Shift Register. If THREIEN (UART_INTEN[1]) is enabled, the THRE interrupt will be generated.\nNote: This bit is read only and will be cleared when writing data into UART_DAT (TX FIFO is not empty).</description>
              <bitOffset>1</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No THRE interrupt flag is generated</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>THRE interrupt flag is generated</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RLSIF</name>
              <description>Receive Line Interrupt Flag (Read Only)\nThis bit is set when the RX receive data have parity error, frame error or break error (at least one of 3 bits, BIF(UART_FIFOSTS[6]), FEF(UART_FIFOSTS[5]) and PEF(UART_FIFOSTS[4]), is set). If RLSIEN (UART_INTEN[2]) is enabled, the RLS interrupt will be generated.\nNote 2: This bit is read only and reset to 0 when all bits of BIF (UART_FIFOSTS[6]), FEF(UART_FIFOSTS[5]) and PEF(UART_FIFOSTS[4]) are cleared.\nNote 3: In RS-485 function mode, this bit is read only and reset to 0 when all bits of BIF (UART_FIFOSTS[6]), FEF(UART_FIFOSTS[5]), PEF(UART_FIFOSTS[4]) and ADDRDETF (UART_FIFOSTS[3]) are cleared.\nNote 4: This bit is valid only when RXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[15]) and TXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[14]) are both 0.</description>
              <bitOffset>2</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No RLS interrupt flag is generated</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>RLS interrupt flag is generated</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>MODEMIF</name>
              <description>MODEM Interrupt Flag (Read Only)\nNote 1: This bit is read only and reset to 0 when bit CTSDETF is cleared by a write 1 on CTSDETF(UART_MODEMSTS[0]).\nNote 2: This bit is valid only when RXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[15]) and TXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[14]) are both 0.</description>
              <bitOffset>3</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No Modem interrupt flag is generated</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Modem interrupt flag is generated</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RXTOIF</name>
              <description>RX Time-out Interrupt Flag (Read Only)\nThis bit is set when the RX FIFO is not empty and no activities occurred in the RX FIFO and the time-out counter equal to TOIC (UART_TOUT[7:0]). If RXTOIEN (UART_INTEN[4]) is enabled, the RX time-out interrupt will be generated.\nNote 1: This bit can be cleared by writing '1' to it.\nNote 2: If BITOMEN (UART_TOUT[31]) is reset to 0, user can read UART_DAT (RX is in active) to clear this bit.\nNote 3: This bit is valid only when RXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[15]) and TXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[14]) are both 0.</description>
              <bitOffset>4</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No RX time-out interrupt flag is generated</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>RX time-out interrupt flag is generated</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BUFERRIF</name>
              <description>Buffer Error Interrupt Flag (Read Only)\nThis bit is set when the TX FIFO or RX FIFO overflows (TXOVIF (UART_FIFOSTS[24]) or RXOVIF (UART_FIFOSTS[0]) is set). When BUFERRIF (UART_INTSTS[5]) is set, the transfer is not correct. If BUFERRIEN (UART_INTEN[5]) is enabled, the buffer error interrupt will be generated.\nNote 1: This bit is cleared if both of RXOVIF(UART_FIFOSTS[0]) and TXOVIF(UART_FIFOSTS[24]) are cleared to 0 by writing 1 to RXOVIF(UART_FIFOSTS[0]) and TXOVIF(UART_FIFOSTS[24]).\nNote 2: This bit is valid only when RXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[15]) and TXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[14]) are both 0.</description>
              <bitOffset>5</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No buffer error interrupt flag is generated</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Buffer error interrupt flag is generated</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>WKIF</name>
              <description>UART Wake-up Interrupt Flag (Read Only)\nThis bit is set when LINWKF (UART_LINWKCTL[29]), TOUTWKF (UART_WKSTS[4]), RS485WKF (UART_WKSTS[3]), RFRTWKF (UART_WKSTS[2]), DATWKF (UART_WKSTS[1]) or CTSWKF(UART_WKSTS[0]) is set to 1.\nNote: This bit is cleared if all of LINWKF, TOUTWKF, RS485WKF, RFRTWKF, DATWKF and CTSWKF are cleared to 0 by writing 1 to the corresponding interrupt flag.</description>
              <bitOffset>6</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No UART wake-up interrupt flag is generated</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>UART wake-up interrupt flag is generated</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>LINIF</name>
              <description>LIN Bus Interrupt Flag\nNote: This bit is cleared when SLVHDETF (UART_LINSTS[0]), SLVHEF (UART_LINSTS[1]), SLVIDPEF (UART_LINSTS[2]), SLVHTOF (UART_LINSTS[4]), RTOUTF (UART_LINSTS[5]), BRKDETF (UART_LINSTS[8]), and BITEF (UART_LINSTS[9]) all are cleared and software writing '1' to LINIF (UART_INTSTS[7]).</description>
              <bitOffset>7</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>None of SLVHDETF, SLVHEF, SLVIDPEF, SLVHTOF, RTOUTF, BITEF, and BRKDETF is generated</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>At least one of SLVHDETF, SLVHEF, SLVIDPEF, SLVHTOF, RTOUTF, BITEF, and BRKDETF is generated</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RDAINT</name>
              <description>Receive Data Available Interrupt Indicator (Read Only)\nThis bit is set if RDAIEN (UART_INTEN[0]) and RDAIF (UART_INTSTS[0]) are both set to 1.</description>
              <bitOffset>8</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No RDA interrupt is generated</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>RDA interrupt is generated</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>THREINT</name>
              <description>Transmit Holding Register Empty Interrupt Indicator (Read Only)\nThis bit is set if THREIEN (UART_INTEN[1]) and THREIF(UART_INTSTS[1]) are both set to 1.</description>
              <bitOffset>9</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No THRE interrupt is generated</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>THRE interrupt is generated</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RLSINT</name>
              <description>Receive Line Status Interrupt Indicator (Read Only) \nThis bit is set if RLSIEN (UART_INTEN[2]) and RLSIF(UART_INTSTS[2]) are both set to 1.\nNote: This bit is valid only when RXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[15]) and TXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[14]) are both 0.</description>
              <bitOffset>10</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No RLS interrupt is generated</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>RLS interrupt is generated</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>MODEMINT</name>
              <description>MODEM Status Interrupt Indicator (Read Only)\nThis bit is set if MODEMIEN(UART_INTEN[3]) and MODEMIF(UART_INTSTS[3]) are both set to 1\nNote: This bit is valid only when RXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[15]) and TXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[14]) are both 0.</description>
              <bitOffset>11</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No Modem interrupt is generated</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Modem interrupt is generated</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RXTOINT</name>
              <description>RX Time-out Interrupt Indicator (Read Only)\nThis bit is set if RXTOIEN (UART_INTEN[4]) and RXTOIF(UART_INTSTS[4]) are both set to 1.\nNote: This bit is valid only when RXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[15]) and TXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[14]) are both 0.</description>
              <bitOffset>12</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No RX time-out interrupt is generated</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>RX time-out interrupt is generated</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BUFERRINT</name>
              <description>Buffer Error Interrupt Indicator (Read Only)\nThis bit is set if BUFERRIEN(UART_INTEN[5]) and BUFERRIF(UART_ INTSTS[5]) are both set to 1.\nNote: This bit is valid only when RXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[15]) and TXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[14]) are both 0.</description>
              <bitOffset>13</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No buffer error interrupt is generated</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Buffer error interrupt is generated</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>WKINT</name>
              <description>UART Wake-up Interrupt Indicator (Read Only)\nThis bit is set if WKIEN (UART_INTEN[6]) and WKIF (UART_INTSTS[6]) are both set to 1.</description>
              <bitOffset>14</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No UART wake-up interrupt is generated</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>UART wake-up interrupt is generated</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>LININT</name>
              <description>LIN Bus Interrupt Indicator (Read Only)\nThis bit is set if LINIEN (UART_INTEN[8]) and LINIF(UART_INTSTS[7]) are both set to 1.</description>
              <bitOffset>15</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No LIN Bus interrupt is generated</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>The LIN Bus interrupt is generated</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SWBEIF</name>
              <description>Single-wire Bit Error Detection Interrupt Flag\nThis bit is set when the single wire bus state not equals to UART controller TX state in Single-wire mode.\nNote 1: This bit is active when FUNCSEL (UART_FUNCSEL[2:0]) is select UART Single-wire mode.\nNote 2: This bit can be cleared by writing '1' to it.</description>
              <bitOffset>16</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No single-wire bit error detection interrupt flag is generated</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Single-wire bit error detection interrupt flag is generated</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>HWRLSIF</name>
              <description>PDMA Mode Receive Line Status Flag (Read Only)\nThis bit is set when the RX receive data have parity error, frame error or break error (at least one of 3 bits, BIF (UART_FIFOSTS[6]), FEF (UART_FIFOSTS[5]) and PEF (UART_FIFOSTS[4]) is set). If RLSIEN (UART_INTEN[2]) is enabled, the RLS interrupt will be generated.\nNote 2: In UART function mode, this bit is read only and reset to 0 when all bits of BIF(UART_FIFOSTS[6]), FEF(UART_FIFOSTS[5]) and PEF(UART_FIFOSTS[4]) are cleared. \nNote 3: In RS-485 function mode, this bit is read only and reset to 0 when all bits of BIF(UART_FIFOSTS[6]), FEF(UART_FIFOSTS[5]), PEF(UART_FIFOSTS[4]) and ADDRDETF (UART_FIFOSTS[3]) are cleared.</description>
              <bitOffset>18</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No RLS interrupt flag is generated when RXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[15]) or TXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[14]) is 1</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>RLS interrupt flag is generated when RXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[15]) or TXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[14]) is 1</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>HWMODIF</name>
              <description>PDMA Mode MODEM Interrupt Flag (Read Only)\nNote: This bit is read only and reset to 0 when the bit CTSDETF (UART_MODEMSTS[0]) is cleared by writing 1 on CTSDETF (UART_MODEMSTS[0]).</description>
              <bitOffset>19</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No Modem interrupt flag is generated when RXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[15]) or TXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[14]) is 1</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Modem interrupt flag is generated when RXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[15]) or TXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[14]) is 1</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>HWTOIF</name>
              <description>PDMA Mode RX Time-out Interrupt Flag (Read Only)\nThis bit is set when the RX FIFO is not empty and no activities occurred in the RX FIFO and the time-out counter equal to TOIC (UART_TOUT[7:0]). If RXTOIEN (UART_INTEN[4]) is enabled, the RX time-out interrupt will be generated. \nNote: This bit is read only and user can read UART_DAT (RX is in active) to clear it.</description>
              <bitOffset>20</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No RX time-out interrupt flag is generated when RXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[15]) or TXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[14]) is 1</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>RX time-out interrupt flag is generated when RXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[15]) or TXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[14]) is 1</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>HWBUFEIF</name>
              <description>PDMA Mode Buffer Error Interrupt Flag (Read Only)\nThis bit is set when the TX or RX FIFO overflows (TXOVIF (UART_FIFOSTS[24]) or RXOVIF (UART_FIFOSTS[0]) is set). When BUFERRIF (UART_INTSTS[5]) is set, the transfer maybe is not correct. If BUFERRIEN (UART_INTEN[5]) is enabled, the buffer error interrupt will be generated.\nNote: This bit is cleared when both TXOVIF (UART_FIFOSTS[24]]) and RXOVIF  (UART_FIFOSTS[0]) are cleared.</description>
              <bitOffset>21</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No buffer error interrupt flag is generated when RXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[15]) or TXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[14]) is 1</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Buffer error interrupt flag is generated when RXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[15]) or TXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[14]) is 1</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TXENDIF</name>
              <description>Transmitter Empty Interrupt Flag (Read Only)\nThis bit is set when TX FIFO (UART_DAT) is empty and the STOP bit of the last byte has been transmitted (TXEMPTYF (UART_FIFOSTS[28]) is set). If TXENDIEN (UART_INTEN[22]) is enabled, the Transmitter Empty interrupt will be generated.\nNote: This bit is cleared automatically when TX FIFO is not empty or the last byte transmission has not completed.</description>
              <bitOffset>22</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No transmitter empty interrupt flag is generated</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Transmitter empty interrupt flag is generated</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SWBEINT</name>
              <description>Single-wire Bit Error Detect Interrupt Indicator (Read Only)\nThis bit is set if SWBEIEN (UART_INTEN[16]) and SWBEIF (UART_INTSTS[16]) are both set to 1.</description>
              <bitOffset>24</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No Single-wire Bit Error Detection Interrupt generated</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Single-wire Bit Error Detection Interrupt generated</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>HWRLSINT</name>
              <description>PDMA Mode Receive Line Status Interrupt Indicator (Read Only)\nThis bit is set if RLSIEN (UART_INTEN[2]) and HWRLSIF(UART_INTSTS[18]) are both set to 1.</description>
              <bitOffset>26</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No RLS interrupt is generated when RXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[15]) or TXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[14]) is 1</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>RLS interrupt is generated when RXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[15]) or TXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[14]) is 1</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>HWMODINT</name>
              <description>PDMA Mode MODEM Status Interrupt Indicator (Read Only)\nThis bit is set if MODEMIEN (UART_INTEN[3]) and HWMODIF(UART_INTSTS[19]) are both set to 1.</description>
              <bitOffset>27</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No Modem interrupt is generated when RXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[15]) or TXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[14]) is 1</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Modem interrupt is generated when RXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[15]) or TXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[14]) is 1</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>HWTOINT</name>
              <description>PDMA Mode RX Time-out Interrupt Indicator (Read Only)\nThis bit is set if RXTOIEN (UART_INTEN[4]) and HWTOIF(UART_INTSTS[20]) are both set to 1.</description>
              <bitOffset>28</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No RX time-out interrupt is generated when RXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[15]) or TXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[14]) is 1</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>RX time-out interrupt is generated when RXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[15]) or TXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[14]) is 1</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>HWBUFEINT</name>
              <description>PDMA Mode Buffer Error Interrupt Indicator (Read Only)\nThis bit is set if BUFERRIEN (UART_INTEN[5]) and HWBUFEIF (UART_INTSTS[21]) are both set to 1.</description>
              <bitOffset>29</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No buffer error interrupt is generated when RXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[15]) or TXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[14]) is 1</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Buffer error interrupt is generated when RXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[15]) or TXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[14]) is 1</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TXENDINT</name>
              <description>Transmitter Empty Interrupt Indicator (Read Only) \nThis bit is set if TXENDIEN (UART_INTEN[22]) and TXENDIF(UART_INTSTS[22]) are both set to 1.</description>
              <bitOffset>30</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No Transmitter Empty interrupt is generated</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Transmitter Empty interrupt is generated</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ABRINT</name>
              <description>Auto-baud Rate Interrupt Indicator (Read Only)\nThis bit is set if ABRIEN (UART_INTEN[18]) and ABRIF (UART_ALTCTL[17]) are both set to 1.</description>
              <bitOffset>31</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No Auto-baud Rate interrupt is generated</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>The Auto-baud Rate interrupt is generated</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>UARTx_TOUT</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>UARTx_TOUT</displayName>
          <description>UART Time-out Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x20</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>TOIC</name>
              <description>Time-out Interrupt Comparator</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>8</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>DLY</name>
              <description>TX Delay Time Value \nThis field is used to program the transfer delay time between the last STOP bit and next START bit. The unit is bit time.</description>
              <bitOffset>8</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>8</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BITOMEN</name>
              <description>Bus Idle Time-out Mode Enable Bit\nIf BITOMEN (UART_TOUT[31]) is enabled, the reset conditions of the time-out counter and RXTOIF (UART_INTSTS[4]) will be changed to detect the bus idle.\nWhen BITOMEN (UART_TOUT[31]) is disabled, the time-out counter and RXTOIF (UART_INTSTS[4]) maintain reset value whenever the RX FIFO is empty. In addition, Any RX FIFO activities, such as a new incoming data or user reading data, will clear RXTOIF (UART_INTSTS[4]) and reset RX time-out counter.. \nOn the other hand, when BITOMEN (UART_TOUT[31]) is enabled, the RX FIFO empty state will not reset the time-out counter and RXTOIF (UART_INTSTS[4]), and RX FIFO activities, such as a new incoming data or user reading data, will not clear RXTOIF (UART_INTSTS[4]). However, the time-out counter needs to receive a first incoming data before it can start counting. After receiving the first incoming data, the time-out counter will count even if the RX FIFO is empty.</description>
              <bitOffset>31</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Bus idle time-out mode Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Bus idle time-out mode Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>UARTx_BAUD</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>UARTx_BAUD</displayName>
          <description>UART Baud Rate Divider Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x24</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x0F0000FF</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>BRD</name>
              <description>Baud Rate Divider\nThe field indicates the baud rate divider. This filed is used in baud rate calculation. The detail description is shown in Table 6.214.</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>16</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BRFD</name>
              <description>Baud Rate Fractional Divider\nThis field is the fractional part of the baud rate divisor.  \nWhen BRFDEN (UART_BAUD[30]) is set, the Baud Rate Equation goes to UART_CLK / ((BRD+2) + (BRFD/256)). </description>
              <bitOffset>16</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>8</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>EDIVM1</name>
              <description>Extra Divider for BAUD Rate Mode 1\nThis field is used for baud rate calculation in mode 1 and has no effect for baud rate calculation in mode 0 and mode 2. The detail description is shown in Table 6.214.</description>
              <bitOffset>24</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>4</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BAUDM0</name>
              <description>BAUD Rate Mode Selection Bit 0\nThis bit is baud rate mode selection bit 0. UART provides three baud rate calculation modes. This bit combines with BAUDM1 (UART_BAUD[29]) to select baud rate calculation mode. The detail description is shown in Table 6.214.</description>
              <bitOffset>28</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BAUDM1</name>
              <description>BAUD Rate Mode Selection Bit 1\nThis bit is baud rate mode selection bit 1. UART provides three baud rate calculation modes. This bit combines with BAUDM0 (UART_BAUD[28]) to select baud rate calculation mode. The detail description is shown in Table 6.214.\nNote: In IrDA mode must be operated in mode 0.</description>
              <bitOffset>29</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BRFDEN</name>
              <description>Baud Rate Fractional Divider Enable Bit\nNote: This bit has effect only at baud rate mode 2 (BAUDM1 (UART_BAUD[29]) and BAUDM0 (UART_BAUD[28]) must be 1).</description>
              <bitOffset>30</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Baud Rate Fractional Divider Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Baud Rate Fractional Divider Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>UARTx_IRDA</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>UARTx_IRDA</displayName>
          <description>UART IrDA Control Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x28</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000040</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>TXEN</name>
              <description>IrDA Receiver/Transmitter Selection Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>1</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>IrDA Transmitter Disabled and Receiver Enabled. (Default)</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>IrDA Transmitter Enabled and Receiver Disabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TXINV</name>
              <description>IrDA Inverse Transmitting Output Signal \nNote 1: Before setting this bit, TXRXDIS (UART_FUNCSEL[3]) should be set then waited for TXRXACT (UART_FIFOSTS[31]) is cleared. When the configuration is done, cleared TXRXDIS (UART_FUNCSEL[3]) to activate UART controller.\nNote 2: This bit is valid when FUNCSEL (UART_FUNCSEL[2:0]) is select IrDA function.</description>
              <bitOffset>5</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>None inverse transmitting signal. (Default)</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Inverse transmitting output signal</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RXINV</name>
              <description>IrDA Inverse Receive Input Signal \nNote 1: Before setting this bit, TXRXDIS (UART_FUNCSEL[3]) should be set then waited for TXRXACT (UART_FIFOSTS[31]) is cleared. When the configuration is done, cleared TXRXDIS (UART_FUNCSEL[3]) to activate UART controller.\nNote 2: This bit is valid when FUNCSEL (UART_FUNCSEL[2:0]) is select IrDA function.</description>
              <bitOffset>6</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>None inverse receiving input signal</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Inverse receiving input signal. (Default)</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>UARTx_ALTCTL</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>UARTx_ALTCTL</displayName>
          <description>UART Alternate Control/Status Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x2C</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x0000000C</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>BRKFL</name>
              <description>UART LIN Break Field Length\nThis field indicates a 4-bit LIN TX break field count.\nNote 1: This break field length is BRKFL + 1.</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>4</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>LINRXEN</name>
              <description>LIN RX Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>6</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>LIN RX mode Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>LIN RX mode Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>LINTXEN</name>
              <description>LIN TX Break Mode Enable Bit\nNote: When TX break field transfer operation finished, this bit will be cleared automatically.</description>
              <bitOffset>7</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>LIN TX Break mode Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>LIN TX Break mode Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RS485NMM</name>
              <description>RS-485 Normal Multi-drop Operation Mode\nNote: It cannot be active with RS-485_AAD operation mode.</description>
              <bitOffset>8</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>RS-485 Normal Multi-drop Operation mode (NMM) Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>RS-485 Normal Multi-drop Operation mode (NMM) Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RS485AAD</name>
              <description>RS-485 Auto Address Detection Operation Mode\nNote: It cannot be active with RS-485_NMM operation mode.</description>
              <bitOffset>9</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>RS-485 Auto Address Detection Operation mode (AAD) Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>RS-485 Auto Address Detection Operation mode (AAD) Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RS485AUD</name>
              <description>RS-485 Auto Direction Function\nNote: It can be active with RS-485_AAD or RS-485_NMM operation mode.</description>
              <bitOffset>10</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>RS-485 Auto Direction Operation function (AUD) Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>RS-485 Auto Direction Operation function (AUD) Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ADDRDEN</name>
              <description>RS-485 Address Detection Enable Bit\nThis bit is used to enable RS-485 Address Detection mode. \nNote: This bit is used for RS-485 any operation mode.</description>
              <bitOffset>15</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Address detection mode Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Address detection mode Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ABRIF</name>
              <description>Auto-baud Rate Interrupt Flag (Read Only) \nThis bit is set when auto-baud rate detection function finished or the auto-baud rate counter was overflow and if ABRIEN(UART_INTEN[18]) is set then the auto-baud rate interrupt will be generated. \nNote: This bit is read only, but it can be cleared by writing '1' to ABRDTOIF (UART_FIFOSTS[2]) and ABRDIF(UART_FIFOSTS[1]).</description>
              <bitOffset>17</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No auto-baud rate interrupt flag is generated</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Auto-baud rate interrupt flag is generated</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ABRDEN</name>
              <description>Auto-baud Rate Detect Enable Bit\nNote: This bit is cleared automatically after auto-baud detection is finished.</description>
              <bitOffset>18</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Auto-baud rate detect function Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Auto-baud rate detect function Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ABRDBITS</name>
              <description>Auto-baud Rate Detect Bit Length \nNote: The calculation of bit number includes the START bit.</description>
              <bitOffset>19</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>2</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>1-bit time from START bit to the 1st rising edge. The input pattern shall be 0x01</description>
                    <value>#00</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>2-bit time from START bit to the 1st rising edge. The input pattern shall be 0x02</description>
                    <value>#01</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>2</name>
                    <description>4-bit time from START bit to the 1st rising edge. The input pattern shall be 0x08</description>
                    <value>#10</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>3</name>
                    <description>8-bit time from START bit to the 1st rising edge. The input pattern shall be 0x80</description>
                    <value>#11</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ADDRMV</name>
              <description>Address Match Value \nThis field contains the RS-485 address match values.\nNote: This field is used for RS-485 auto address detection mode.</description>
              <bitOffset>24</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>8</bitWidth>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>UARTx_FUNCSEL</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>UARTx_FUNCSEL</displayName>
          <description>UART Function Select Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x30</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>FUNCSEL</name>
              <description>Function Select</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>3</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>UART function</description>
                    <value>#000</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>LIN function</description>
                    <value>#001</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>2</name>
                    <description>IrDA function</description>
                    <value>#010</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>3</name>
                    <description>RS-485 function</description>
                    <value>#011</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>4</name>
                    <description>UART Single-wire function</description>
                    <value>#100</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TXRXDIS</name>
              <description>TX and RX Disable Bit\nSetting this bit can disable TX and RX.\nNote: The TX and RX will not be disabled immediately when this bit is set. The TX and RX complete current task before TX and RX are disabled. When TX and RX are disabled, the TXRXACT (UART_FIFOSTS[31]) is cleared.</description>
              <bitOffset>3</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>TX and RX Enabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>TX and RX Disabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>DGE</name>
              <description>Deglitch Enable Bit\nNote 1: When this bit is set to logic 1, any pulse width less than about 150 ns will be considered a glitch and will be removed in the serial data input (RX). This bit acts only on RX line and has no effect on the transmitter logic.\nNote 2: It is recommended to set this bit only when operating at baud rate under 2.5 Mbps.</description>
              <bitOffset>6</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Deglitch Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Deglitch Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TXRXSWP</name>
              <description>TX and RX Swap Enable Bit\nSetting this bit Swaps TX pin and RX pin.</description>
              <bitOffset>7</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>TX and RX Swap Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>TX and RX Swap Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>UARTx_LINCTL</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>UARTx_LINCTL</displayName>
          <description>UART LIN Control Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x34</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x000C0000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>SLVEN</name>
              <description>LIN Slave Mode Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>LIN slave mode Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>LIN slave mode Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SLVHDEN</name>
              <description>LIN Slave Header Detection Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>1</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>LIN slave header detection Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>LIN slave header detection Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SLVAREN</name>
              <description>LIN Slave Automatic Resynchronization Mode Enable Bit\nNote 2: When operation in Automatic Resynchronization mode, the baud rate setting must be mode 2 (BAUDM1 (UART_BAUD[29]) and BAUDM0 (UART_BAUD[28]) must be 1).\nNote 3: The control and interactions of this field are explained in 6.21.5.10 (Slave mode with automatic resynchronization).\nNote 4: The minimum detection range of the target BRD (UART_BAUD[15:0]) is 0x30.</description>
              <bitOffset>2</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>LIN automatic resynchronization Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>LIN automatic resynchronization Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SLVDUEN</name>
              <description>LIN Slave Divider Update Method Enable Bit\nNote 2: This bit used for LIN Slave Automatic Resynchronization mode. (for Non-Automatic Resynchronization mode, this bit should be kept cleared) \nNote 3: The control and interactions of this field are explained in 6.21.5.10 (Slave mode with automatic resynchronization).</description>
              <bitOffset>3</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>UART_BAUD updated is written by software (if no automatic resynchronization update occurs at the same time)</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>UART_BAUD is updated at the next received character. User must set the bit before checksum reception</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>MUTE</name>
              <description>LIN Mute Mode Enable Bit\nNote: The exit from mute mode condition and each control and interactions of this field are explained in 6.21.5.10 (LIN slave mode).</description>
              <bitOffset>4</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>LIN mute mode Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>LIN mute mode Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RTOUTEN</name>
              <description>LIN Response Time-out Detection Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>5</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>LIN response time-out detection Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>LIN response time-out detection Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SENDH</name>
              <description>LIN TX Send Header Enable Bit\nThe LIN TX header can be 'break field' or 'break and sync field' or 'break, sync and frame ID field', it is depend on setting HSEL (UART_LINCTL[23:22]).\nNote 1: This bit is shadow bit of LINTXEN (UART_ALTCTL[7]); user can read/write it by setting LINTXEN (UART_ALTCTL[7]) or SENDH (UART_LINCTL[8]).\nNote 2: When transmitter header field (it may be 'break' or 'break + sync' or 'break + sync + frame ID' selected by HSEL (UART_LINCTL[23:22]) field) transfer operation finished, this bit will be cleared automatically.</description>
              <bitOffset>8</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Send LIN TX header Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Send LIN TX header Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>IDPEN</name>
              <description>LIN ID Parity Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>9</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>LIN frame ID parity Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>LIN frame ID parity Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BRKDETEN</name>
              <description>LIN Break Detection Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>10</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>LIN break detection Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>LIN break detection Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>LINRXOFF</name>
              <description>LIN Receiver Disable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>11</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>LIN receiver Enabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>LIN receiver Disabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BITERREN</name>
              <description>Bit Error Detect Enable Bit </description>
              <bitOffset>12</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Bit error detection function Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Bit error detection function Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BRKFL</name>
              <description>LIN Break Field Length \nThis field indicates a 4-bit LIN TX break field count.\nNote 1: These registers are shadow registers of BRKFL (UART_ALTCTL[3:0]). User can read/write it by setting BRKFL (UART_ALTCTL[3:0]) or BRKFL (UART_LINCTL[19:16]).\nNote 2: This break field length is BRKFL + 1.</description>
              <bitOffset>16</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>4</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BSL</name>
              <description>LIN Break/Sync Delimiter Length \nNote: This bit used for LIN master to sending header field.</description>
              <bitOffset>20</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>2</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>The LIN break/sync delimiter length is 1-bit time</description>
                    <value>#00</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>The LIN break/sync delimiter length is 2-bit time</description>
                    <value>#01</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>2</name>
                    <description>The LIN break/sync delimiter length is 3-bit time</description>
                    <value>#10</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>3</name>
                    <description>The LIN break/sync delimiter length is 4-bit time</description>
                    <value>#11</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>HSEL</name>
              <description>LIN Header Select </description>
              <bitOffset>22</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>2</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>The LIN header includes 'break field'</description>
                    <value>#00</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>The LIN header includes 'break field' and 'sync field'</description>
                    <value>#01</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>2</name>
                    <description>The LIN header includes 'break field', 'sync field' and 'frame ID field'</description>
                    <value>#10</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>3</name>
                    <description>Reserved.</description>
                    <value>#11</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>PID</name>
              <description>LIN PID Bits\nIf the parity generated by hardware, user fill ID0~ID5 (PID[29:24]), hardware will calculate P0 (PID[30]) and P1 (PID[31]), otherwise user must filled frame ID and parity in this field.\nNote 1: User can fill any 8-bit value to this field and the bit 24 indicates ID0 (LSB first).\nNote 2: This field can be used for LIN master mode or slave mode.</description>
              <bitOffset>24</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>8</bitWidth>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>UARTx_LINSTS</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>UARTx_LINSTS</displayName>
          <description>UART LIN Status Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x38</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>SLVHDETF</name>
              <description>LIN Slave Header Detection Flag\nThis bit is set by hardware when a LIN header is detected in LIN slave mode and be cleared by writing 1 to it.\nNote 3: When enable ID parity check IDPEN (UART_LINCTL[9]), if hardware detect complete header ('break + sync + frame ID'), the SLVHDETF will be set whether the frame ID correct or not.</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>LIN header not detected</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>LIN header detected (break + sync + frame ID)</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SLVHEF</name>
              <description>LIN Slave Header Error Flag\nThis bit is set by hardware when a LIN header error is detected in LIN slave mode and be cleared by writing 1 to it. The header errors include 'break delimiter is too short (less than 0.5 bit time)', 'frame error in sync field or Identifier field', 'sync field data is not 0x55 in Non-Automatic Resynchronization mode', 'sync field deviation error with Automatic Resynchronization mode', 'sync field measure time-out with Automatic Resynchronization mode' and 'LIN header reception time-out'.</description>
              <bitOffset>1</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>LIN header error not detected</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>LIN header error detected</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SLVIDPEF</name>
              <description>LIN Slave ID Parity Error Flag \nThis bit is set by hardware when receipted frame ID parity is not correct.</description>
              <bitOffset>2</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No active</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Receipted frame ID parity is not correct</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SLVSYNCF</name>
              <description>LIN Slave Sync Field\nThis bit indicates that the LIN sync field is being analyzed in Automatic Resynchronization mode. When the receiver header have some error been detect, user must reset the internal circuit to re-search new frame header by writing 1 to this bit.\nNote 2: This bit can be cleared by writing 1 to it.\nNote 3: When writing 1 to it, hardware will reload the initial baud rate and re-search a new frame header.</description>
              <bitOffset>3</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>The current character is not at LIN sync state</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>The current character is at LIN sync state</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SLVHTOF</name>
              <description>LIN Slave Header Time-out Flag\nThis bit is set by hardware when a LIN header reception time-out is detected in LIN slave mode and be cleared by writing 1 to it. When this bit is set, SLVHEF (UART_LINSTS[1]) will also be set.</description>
              <bitOffset>4</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>LIN header time-out not detected</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>LIN header time-out detected</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RTOUTF</name>
              <description>LIN Response Time-out Flag\nThis bit is set when no LIN response received and the time-out counter equal to or bigger than LINRTOIC (UART_LINRTOUT[23:0]). If LINIEN (UART_INTEN[8]) is enabled, the LIN Bus Interrupt will be generated.\nNote 1: This bit can be cleared by writing 1 to it.</description>
              <bitOffset>5</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>LIN response time-out not detected</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>LIN response time-out detected</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BRKDETF</name>
              <description>LIN Break Detection Flag\nThis bit is set by hardware when a break is detected and be cleared by writing 1 to it through software.</description>
              <bitOffset>8</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>LIN break not detected</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>LIN break detected</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BITEF</name>
              <description>Bit Error Detect Status Flag \nAt TX transfer state, hardware will monitor the bus state, if the input pin (UART_RXD) state not equals to the output pin (UART_TXD) state, BITEF (UART_LINSTS[9]) will be set.</description>
              <bitOffset>9</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Bit error not detected</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Bit error detected</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>UARTx_BRCOMP</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>UARTx_BRCOMP</displayName>
          <description>UART Baud Rate Compensation Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x3C</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>BRCOMP</name>
              <description>Baud Rate Compensation Patten\nThese 9-bits are used to define the relative bit is compensated or not. \nBRCOMP[7:0] is used to define the compensation of DAT (UART_DAT[7:0]) and BRCOM[8] is used to define PARITY (UART_DAT[8]).</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>9</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BRCOMPDEC</name>
              <description>Baud Rate Compensation Decrease</description>
              <bitOffset>31</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Positive (increase one module clock) compensation for each compensated bit</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Negative (decrease one module clock) compensation for each compensated bit</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>UARTx_WKCTL</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>UARTx_WKCTL</displayName>
          <description>UART Wake-up Control Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x40</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>WKCTSEN</name>
              <description>nCTS Wake-up Enable Bit\nNote: When the system is in Power-down mode, an external nCTS change will wake up system from Power-down mode.</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>nCTS Wake-up system function Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>nCTS Wake-up system function Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>WKDATEN</name>
              <description>Incoming Data Wake-up Enable Bit\nNote: When the system is in Power-down mode, incoming data will wake-up system from Power-down mode.</description>
              <bitOffset>1</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Incoming data wake-up system function Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Incoming data wake-up system function Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>WKRFRTEN</name>
              <description>Received Data FIFO Reached Threshold Wake-up Enable Bit\nNote: When the system is in Power-down mode, Received Data FIFO reached threshold will wake-up system from Power-down mode.</description>
              <bitOffset>2</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Received Data FIFO reached threshold wake-up system function Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Received Data FIFO reached threshold wake-up system function Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>WKRS485EN</name>
              <description>RS-485 Address Match Wake-up Enable Bit\nNote 1: When the system is in Power-down mode, RS-485 Address Match will wake-up system from Power-down mode.\nNote 2: This bit is used for RS-485 Auto Address Detection (AAD) mode in RS-485 function mode and ADDRDEN (UART_ALTCTL[15]) is set to 1.</description>
              <bitOffset>3</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>RS-485 Address Match (AAD mode) wake-up system function Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>RS-485 Address Match (AAD mode) wake-up system function Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>WKTOUTEN</name>
              <description>Received Data FIFO Reached Threshold Time-out Wake-up Enable Bit\nNote 1: When the system is in Power-down mode, Received Data FIFO reached threshold time-out will wake up system from Power-down mode.\nNote 2: It is suggested the function is enabled when the WKRFRTEN (UART_WKCTL[2]) is set to 1.</description>
              <bitOffset>4</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Received Data FIFO reached threshold time-out wake-up system function Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Received Data FIFO reached threshold time-out wake-up system function Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>UARTx_WKSTS</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>UARTx_WKSTS</displayName>
          <description>UART Wake-up Status Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x44</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>CTSWKF</name>
              <description>nCTS Wake-up Flag\nThis bit is set if chip wake-up from power-down state by nCTS wake-up.\nNote 1: If WKCTSEN (UART_WKCTL[0]) is enabled, the nCTS wake-up cause this bit is set to '1'.\nNote 2: This bit can be cleared by writing '1' to it.</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Chip stays in power-down state</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Chip wake-up from power-down state by nCTS wake-up</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>DATWKF</name>
              <description>Incoming Data Wake-up Flag\nThis bit is set if chip wake-up from power-down state by data wake-up.\nNote 1: If WKDATEN (UART_WKCTL[1]) is enabled, the Incoming Data wake-up cause this bit is set to '1'.\nNote 2: This bit can be cleared by writing '1' to it.</description>
              <bitOffset>1</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Chip stays in power-down state</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Chip wake-up from power-down state by Incoming Data wake-up</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RFRTWKF</name>
              <description>Received Data FIFO Reached Threshold Wake-up Flag\nThis bit is set if chip wake-up from power-down state by Received Data FIFO reached threshold wake-up.\nNote 1: If WKRFRTEN (UART_WKCTL[2]) is enabled, the Received Data FIFO Reached Threshold wake-up cause this bit is set to '1'.\nNote 2: This bit can be cleared by writing '1' to it.</description>
              <bitOffset>2</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Chip stays in power-down state</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Chip wake-up from power-down state by Received Data FIFO Reached Threshold wake-up</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RS485WKF</name>
              <description>RS-485 Address Match Wake-up Flag\nThis bit is set if chip wake-up from power-down state by RS-485 Address Match (AAD mode).\nNote 1: If WKRS485EN (UART_WKCTL[3]) is enabled, the RS-485 Address Match (AAD mode) wake-up cause this bit is set to '1'.\nNote 2: This bit can be cleared by writing '1' to it.</description>
              <bitOffset>3</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Chip stays in power-down state</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Chip wake-up from power-down state by RS-485 Address Match (AAD mode) wake-up</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TOUTWKF</name>
              <description>Received Data FIFO Threshold Time-out Wake-up Flag\nThis bit is set if chip wake-up from power-down state by Received Data FIFO Threshold Time-out\nwake-up.\nNote 1: If WKTOUTEN (UART_WKCTL[4]) is enabled, the Received Data FIFO reached threshold time-out wake-up cause this bit is set to '1'.\nNote 2: This bit can be cleared by writing '1' to it.</description>
              <bitOffset>4</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Chip stays in power-down state</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Chip wake-up from power-down state by Received Data FIFO reached threshold time-out wake-up</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>UARTx_DWKCOMP</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>UARTx_DWKCOMP</displayName>
          <description>UART Incoming Data Wake-up Compensation Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x48</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>STCOMP</name>
              <description>START Bit Compensation Value\nThese bits field indicate how many clock cycle selected by UART_CLK do the UART controller can get the 1st bit (START bit) when the device is wake-up from Power-down mode.\nNote: It is valid only when WKDATEN (UART_WKCTL[1]) is set.</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>16</bitWidth>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>UARTx_RS485DD</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>UARTx_RS485DD</displayName>
          <description>UART RS485 Transceiver Deactivate Delay Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x4C</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>RTSDDLY</name>
              <description>RS485 Transceiver Deactivate Delay Value\nThese bits field indicate how many clock cycles selected by UART_CLK do the UART controller delay the RS485 transceiver state trancing when the state trancing of RS485 transceiver is from TX to RX state. These bits field have no effect when the state trancing of RS485 transceiver is from RX to TX state.\nNote: It is valid only when RS485AUD (UART_ALTCTL[10]) is set.</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>16</bitWidth>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>UARTx_LINRTOUT</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>UARTx_LINRTOUT</displayName>
          <description>UART LIN Response Time-out Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x50</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00FFFFFF</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>LINRTOIC</name>
              <description>LIN Response Time-out Comparator</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>24</bitWidth>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>UARTx_LINWKCTL</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>UARTx_LINWKCTL</displayName>
          <description>UART LIN Wake-up Control Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x54</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x000000C0</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>LINWKC</name>
              <description>LIN Send Wake-up Signal Length Counter</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>24</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SENDLINW</name>
              <description>LIN Send Wake-up Enable Bit\nNote 1: When this bit is set, the UART will send LIN wake-up automatically. When LIN wake-up transfer operation finished, this bit will be cleared automatically.</description>
              <bitOffset>24</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Send LIN Wake-up Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Send LIN Wake-up Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>LINWKEN</name>
              <description>LIN Wake-up Enable Bit\nNote 1: When the system is in Power-down mode, LIN wake-up event will wake up system from Power-down mode.</description>
              <bitOffset>28</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>LIN wake-up system function Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>LIN wake-up system function Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>LINWKF</name>
              <description>LIN Wake-up Flag\nThis bit is set if chip wake-up from power-down state by LIN wake-up.\nNote 1: If LINWKEN (UART_LINWKCTL[28]) is enabled, the LIN wake-up event will cause this bit set to '1'.\nNote 2: This bit can be cleared by writing '1' to it.</description>
              <bitOffset>29</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Chip stays in power-down state</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Chip wake-up from power-down state by LIN wake-up</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
      </registers>
    </peripheral>
    <peripheral>
      <name>UART8</name>
      <description>UART Register Map</description>
      <groupName>UART</groupName>
      <baseAddress>0x40078000</baseAddress>
      <!-- d -> no access permission -->
      <addressBlock>
        <offset>0x0</offset>
        <size>0x58</size>
        <usage>registers</usage>
      </addressBlock>
      <registers>
       <register>
          <name>UARTx_DAT</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>UARTx_DAT</displayName>
          <description>UART Receive/Transmit Buffer Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x0</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0x00000000</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>DAT</name>
              <description>Data Receive/Transmit Buffer\nWrite Operation:\nBy writing one byte to this register, the data byte will be stored in transmitter FIFO. The UART controller will send out the data stored in transmitter FIFO top location through the UART_TXD.\nRead Operation:\nBy reading this register, the UART controller will return an 8-bit data received from receiver FIFO.</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>8</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>PARITY</name>
              <description>PARITY Bit Receive/Transmit Buffer\nWrite Operation:\nBy writing to this bit, the PARITY bit will be stored in transmitter FIFO. If PBE (UART_LINE[3]) and PSS (UART_LINE[7]) are set, the UART controller will send out this bit follow the DAT (UART_DAT[7:0]) through the UART_TXD.\nRead Operation:\nIf PBE (UART_LINE[3]) and PSS (UART_LINE[7]) are enabled, the PARITY bit can be read by this bit.\nNote: This bit has effect only when PBE (UART_LINE[3]) and PSS (UART_LINE[7]) are set.</description>
              <bitOffset>8</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>UARTx_INTEN</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>UARTx_INTEN</displayName>
          <description>UART Interrupt Enable Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x4</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>RDAIEN</name>
              <description>Receive Data Available Interrupt Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Receive data available interrupt Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Receive data available interrupt Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>THREIEN</name>
              <description>Transmit Holding Register Empty Interrupt Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>1</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Transmit holding register empty interrupt Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Transmit holding register empty interrupt Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RLSIEN</name>
              <description>Receive Line Status Interrupt Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>2</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Receive Line Status interrupt Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Receive Line Status interrupt Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>MODEMIEN</name>
              <description>Modem Status Interrupt Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>3</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Modem status interrupt Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Modem status interrupt Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RXTOIEN</name>
              <description>RX Time-out Interrupt Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>4</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>RX time-out interrupt Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>RX time-out interrupt Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BUFERRIEN</name>
              <description>Buffer Error Interrupt Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>5</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Buffer error interrupt Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Buffer error interrupt Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>WKIEN</name>
              <description>Wake-up Interrupt Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>6</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Wake-up Interrupt Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Wake-up Interrupt Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>LINIEN</name>
              <description>LIN Bus Interrupt Enable Bit\nNote: This bit is used for LIN function mode.</description>
              <bitOffset>8</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>LIN bus interrupt Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>LIN bus interrupt Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TOCNTEN</name>
              <description>Receive Buffer Time-out Counter Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>11</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Receive Buffer Time-out counter Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Receive Buffer Time-out counter Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ATORTSEN</name>
              <description>nRTS Auto-flow Control Enable Bit\nNote: When nRTS auto-flow is enabled, if the number of bytes in the RX FIFO equals the RTSTRGLV (UART_FIFO[19:16]), the UART will de-assert nRTS signal.</description>
              <bitOffset>12</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>nRTS auto-flow control Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>nRTS auto-flow control Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ATOCTSEN</name>
              <description>nCTS Auto-flow Control Enable Bit\nNote: When nCTS auto-flow is enabled, the UART will send data to external device if nCTS input assert (UART will not send data to device until nCTS is asserted).</description>
              <bitOffset>13</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>nCTS auto-flow control Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>nCTS auto-flow control Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TXPDMAEN</name>
              <description>TX PDMA Enable Bit\nNote: If RLSIEN (UART_INTEN[2]) is enabled and HWRLSINT (UART_INTSTS[26]) is set to 1, the RLS (Receive Line Status) Interrupt is caused. If RLS interrupt is caused by Break Error Flag BIF(UART_FIFOSTS[6]), Frame Error Flag FEF(UART_FIFO[5]) or Parity Error Flag PEF(UART_FIFOSTS[4]), UART PDMA transmit request operation is stopped. Clear Break Error Flag BIF or Frame Error Flag FEF or Parity Error Flag PEF by writing '1' to corresponding BIF, FEF and PEF to make UART PDMA transmit request operation continue.</description>
              <bitOffset>14</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>TX PDMA Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>TX PDMA Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RXPDMAEN</name>
              <description>RX PDMA Enable Bit\nThis bit can enable or disable RX PDMA service.\nNote: If RLSIEN (UART_INTEN[2]) is enabled and HWRLSINT (UART_INTSTS[26]) is set to 1, the RLS (Receive Line Status) Interrupt is caused. If RLS interrupt is caused by Break Error Flag BIF(UART_FIFOSTS[6]), Frame Error Flag FEF(UART_FIFO[5]) or Parity Error Flag PEF(UART_FIFOSTS[4]), UART PDMA receive request operation is stopped. Clear Break Error Flag BIF or Frame Error Flag FEF or Parity Error Flag PEF by writing '1' to corresponding BIF, FEF and PEF to make UART PDMA receive request operation continue.</description>
              <bitOffset>15</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>RX PDMA Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>RX PDMA Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SWBEIEN</name>
              <description>Single-wire Bit Error Detection Interrupt Enable Bit\nSet this bit, the Single-wire Half Duplex Bit Error Detection Interrupt SWBEINT(UART_INTSTS[24]) is generated when Single-wire Bit Error Detection SWBEIF(UART_INTSTS[16]) is set.\nNote: This bit is valid when FUNCSEL (UART_FUNCSEL[2:0]) is select UART Single-wire mode.</description>
              <bitOffset>16</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Single-wire Bit Error Detect Interrupt Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Single-wire Bit Error Detect Interrupt Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ABRIEN</name>
              <description>Auto-baud Rate Interrupt Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>18</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Auto-baud rate interrupt Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Auto-baud rate interrupt Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TXENDIEN</name>
              <description>Transmitter Empty Interrupt Enable Bit\nIf TXENDIEN (UART_INTEN[22]) is enabled, the Transmitter Empty interrupt TXENDINT (UART_INTSTS[30]) will be generated when TXENDIF (UART_INTSTS[22]) is set (TX FIFO (UART_DAT) is empty and the STOP bit of the last byte has been transmitted).</description>
              <bitOffset>22</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Transmitter empty interrupt Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Transmitter empty interrupt Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>UARTx_FIFO</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>UARTx_FIFO</displayName>
          <description>UART FIFO Control Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x8</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000101</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>RXRST</name>
              <description>RX Field Software Reset\nWhen RXRST (UART_FIFO[1]) is set, all the byte in the receiver FIFO and RX internal state machine are cleared.\nNote 1: This bit will automatically clear at least 3 UART peripheral clock cycles.\nNote 2: Before setting this bit, it should wait for the RXIDLE (UART_FIFOSTS[29]) be set.</description>
              <bitOffset>1</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No effect</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Reset the RX internal state machine and pointers</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TXRST</name>
              <description>TX Field Software Reset\nWhen TXRST (UART_FIFO[2]) is set, all the byte in the transmit FIFO and TX internal state machine are cleared.\nNote 1: This bit will automatically clear at least 3 UART peripheral clock cycles.\nNote 2: Before setting this bit, it should wait for the TXEMPTYF (UART_FIFOSTS[28]) be set.</description>
              <bitOffset>2</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No effect</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Reset the TX internal state machine and pointers</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RFITL</name>
              <description>RX FIFO Interrupt Trigger Level\nWhen the number of bytes in the receive FIFO equals the RFITL, the RDAIF (UART_INTSTS[0]) will be set (if RDAIEN (UART_INTEN[0]) enabled, and an interrupt will be generated).</description>
              <bitOffset>4</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>4</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>RX FIFO Interrupt Trigger Level is 1 byte</description>
                    <value>#0000</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>RX FIFO Interrupt Trigger Level is 4 bytes</description>
                    <value>#0001</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>2</name>
                    <description>RX FIFO Interrupt Trigger Level is 8 bytes</description>
                    <value>#0010</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>3</name>
                    <description>RX FIFO Interrupt Trigger Level is 14 bytes</description>
                    <value>#0011</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RXOFF</name>
              <description>Receiver Disable Bit\nThe receiver is disabled or not (set 1 to disable receiver).\nNote: This bit is used for RS-485 Normal Multi-drop mode. It should be programmed before RS485NMM (UART_ALTCTL[8]) is programmed.</description>
              <bitOffset>8</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Receiver Enabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Receiver Disabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RTSTRGLV</name>
              <description>nRTS Trigger Level for Auto-flow Control\nNote: This field is used for auto nRTS flow control.</description>
              <bitOffset>16</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>4</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>nRTS Trigger Level is 1 byte</description>
                    <value>#0000</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>nRTS Trigger Level is 4 bytes</description>
                    <value>#0001</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>2</name>
                    <description>nRTS Trigger Level is 8 bytes</description>
                    <value>#0010</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>3</name>
                    <description>nRTS Trigger Level is 14 bytes</description>
                    <value>#0011</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>UARTx_LINE</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>UARTx_LINE</displayName>
          <description>UART Line Control Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0xC</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>WLS</name>
              <description>Word Length Selection\nThis field sets UART word length.</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>2</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>5 bits</description>
                    <value>#00</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>6 bits</description>
                    <value>#01</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>2</name>
                    <description>7 bits</description>
                    <value>#10</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>3</name>
                    <description>8 bits</description>
                    <value>#11</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>NSB</name>
              <description>Number of 'STOP Bit'</description>
              <bitOffset>2</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>One 'STOP bit' is generated in the transmitted data</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>When select 5-bit word length, 1.5 'STOP bit' is generated in the transmitted data. When select 6-, 7- and 8-bit word length, 2 'STOP bit' is generated in the transmitted data</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>PBE</name>
              <description>PARITY Bit Enable Bit\nNote: PARITY bit is generated on each outgoing character and is checked on each incoming data.</description>
              <bitOffset>3</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>PARITY bit generated Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>PARITY bit generated Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>EPE</name>
              <description>Even Parity Enable Bit\nNote: This bit has effect only when PBE (UART_LINE[3]) is set.</description>
              <bitOffset>4</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Odd number of logic 1's is transmitted and checked in each word</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Even number of logic 1's is transmitted and checked in each word</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SPE</name>
              <description>Stick Parity Enable Bit\nNote: If PBE (UART_LINE[3]) and EPE (UART_LINE[4]) are logic 1, the PARITY bit is transmitted and checked as logic 0. If PBE (UART_LINE[3]) is 1 and EPE (UART_LINE[4]) is 0 then the PARITY bit is transmitted and checked as 1.</description>
              <bitOffset>5</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Stick parity Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Stick parity Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BCB</name>
              <description>Break Control Bit\nNote: When this bit is set to logic 1, the transmitted serial data output (TX) is forced to the Spacing State (logic 0). This bit acts only on TX line and has no effect on the transmitter logic.</description>
              <bitOffset>6</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Break Control Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Break Control Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>PSS</name>
              <description>PARITY Bit Source Selection\nThe PARITY bit can be selected to be generated and checked automatically or by software.\nNote 1: This bit has effect only when PBE (UART_LINE[3]) is set.\nNote 2: If PSS is 0, the PARITY bit is transmitted and checked automatically. If PSS is 1, the transmitted PARITY bit value can be determined by writing PARITY (UART_DAT[8]) and the PARITY bit can be read by reading PARITY (UART_DAT[8]).</description>
              <bitOffset>7</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>PARITY bit is generated by EPE (UART_LINE[4]) and SPE (UART_LINE[5]) setting and checked automatically</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>PARITY bit generated and checked by software</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TXDINV</name>
              <description>TX Data Inverted\nNote 1: Before setting this bit, TXRXDIS (UART_FUNCSEL[3]) should be set then waited for TXRXACT (UART_FIFOSTS[31]) is cleared. When the configuration is done, cleared TXRXDIS (UART_FUNCSEL[3]) to activate UART controller.\nNote 2: This bit is valid when FUNCSEL (UART_FUNCSEL[2:0]) is select UART, LIN or RS485 function.</description>
              <bitOffset>8</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Transmitted data signal inverted Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Transmitted data signal inverted Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RXDINV</name>
              <description>RX Data Inverted\nNote 1: Before setting this bit, TXRXDIS (UART_FUNCSEL[3]) should be set then waited for TXRXACT (UART_FIFOSTS[31]) is cleared. When the configuration is done, cleared TXRXDIS (UART_FUNCSEL[3]) to activate UART controller.\nNote 2: This bit is valid when FUNCSEL (UART_FUNCSEL[2:0]) is select UART, LIN or RS485 function.</description>
              <bitOffset>9</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Received data signal inverted Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Received data signal inverted Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>UARTx_MODEM</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>UARTx_MODEM</displayName>
          <description>UART Modem Control Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x10</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000200</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>RTS</name>
              <description>nRTS Signal Control\nThis bit is direct control internal nRTS (Request-to-send) signal active or not, and then drive the nRTS pin output with RTSACTLV bit configuration.\nNote 1: The nRTS signal control bit is not effective when nRTS auto-flow control is enabled in UART function mode.\nNote 2: The nRTS signal control bit is not effective when RS-485 auto direction mode (AUD) is enabled in RS-485 function mode.\nNote 3: Single-wire mode is support this feature.</description>
              <bitOffset>1</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>nRTS signal is active</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>nRTS signal is inactive</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RTSACTLV</name>
              <description>nRTS Pin Active Level\nThis bit defines the active level state of nRTS pin output.\nNote 1: Refer to Figure 6.2113 and Figure 6.2114 for UART function mode.\nNote 2: Refer to Figure 6.2124 and Figure 6.2125 for RS-485 function mode.\nNote 3: Before setting this bit, TXRXDIS (UART_FUNCSEL[3]) should be set then waited for TXRXACT (UART_FIFOSTS[31]) is cleared. When the configuration is done, cleared TXRXDIS (UART_FUNCSEL[3]) to activate UART controller.</description>
              <bitOffset>9</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>nRTS pin output is high level active</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>nRTS pin output is low level active. (Default)</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RTSSTS</name>
              <description>nRTS Pin Status (Read Only)\nThis bit mirror from nRTS pin output of voltage logic status.</description>
              <bitOffset>13</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>nRTS pin output is low level voltage logic state</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>nRTS pin output is high level voltage logic state</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>UARTx_MODEMSTS</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>UARTx_MODEMSTS</displayName>
          <description>UART Modem Status Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x14</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000110</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>CTSDETF</name>
              <description>Detect nCTS State Change Flag\nThis bit is set whenever nCTS input has change state, and it will generate Modem interrupt to CPU when MODEMIEN (UART_INTEN[3]) is set to 1.\nNote: This bit can be cleared by writing '1' to it.</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>nCTS input has not change state</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>nCTS input has change state</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>CTSSTS</name>
              <description>nCTS Pin Status (Read Only)\nThis bit mirror from nCTS pin input of voltage logic status.\nNote: This bit echoes when UART controller peripheral clock is enabled, and nCTS multi-function port is selected.</description>
              <bitOffset>4</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>nCTS pin input is low level voltage logic state</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>nCTS pin input is high level voltage logic state</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>CTSACTLV</name>
              <description>nCTS Pin Active Level\nThis bit defines the active level state of nCTS pin input.\nNote: Before setting this bit, TXRXDIS (UART_FUNCSEL[3]) should be set then waited for TXRXACT (UART_FIFOSTS[31]) is cleared. When the configuration is done, cleared TXRXDIS (UART_FUNCSEL[3]) to activate UART controller.</description>
              <bitOffset>8</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>nCTS pin input is high level active</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>nCTS pin input is low level active. (Default)</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>UARTx_FIFOSTS</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>UARTx_FIFOSTS</displayName>
          <description>UART FIFO Status Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x18</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0xB0404000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>RXOVIF</name>
              <description>RX Overflow Error Interrupt Flag\nThis bit is set when RX FIFO overflow.\nIf the number of bytes of received data is greater than RX_FIFO (UART_DAT) size 16 bytes, this bit will be set.\nNote: This bit can be cleared by writing '1' to it.</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>RX FIFO is not overflow</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>RX FIFO is overflow</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ABRDIF</name>
              <description>Auto-baud Rate Detect Interrupt Flag\nThis bit is set to logic '1' when auto-baud rate detect function is finished.\nNote: This bit can be cleared by writing '1' to it.</description>
              <bitOffset>1</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Auto-baud rate detect function is not finished</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Auto-baud rate detect function is finished</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ABRDTOIF</name>
              <description>Auto-baud Rate Detect Time-out Interrupt Flag\nThis bit is set to logic '1' in Auto-baud Rate Detect mode when the baud rate counter is overflow.\nNote: This bit can be cleared by writing '1' to it.</description>
              <bitOffset>2</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Auto-baud rate counter is underflow</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Auto-baud rate counter is overflow</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ADDRDETF</name>
              <description>RS-485 Address Byte Detect Flag\nNote 1: This field is used for RS-485 function mode and ADDRDEN (UART_ALTCTL[15]) is set to 1 to enable Address detection mode.\nNote 2: This bit can be cleared by writing '1' to it.</description>
              <bitOffset>3</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Receiver detects a data that is not an address bit (bit 9 ='0')</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Receiver detects a data that is an address bit (bit 9 ='1')</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>PEF</name>
              <description>Parity Error Flag\nThis bit is set to logic 1 whenever the received character does not have a valid 'PARITY bit'.\nNote: This bit can be cleared by writing '1' to it.</description>
              <bitOffset>4</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No parity error is generated</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Parity error is generated</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>FEF</name>
              <description>Framing Error Flag\nThis bit is set to logic 1 whenever the received character does not have a valid 'STOP bit' (that is, the STOP bit following the last data bit or PARITY bit is detected as logic 0).\nNote: This bit can be cleared by writing '1' to it.</description>
              <bitOffset>5</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No framing error is generated</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Framing error is generated</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BIF</name>
              <description>Break Interrupt Flag\nThis bit is set to logic 1 whenever the received data input (RX) is held in the 'spacing state' (logic 0) for longer than a full word transmission time (that is, the total time of 'START bit' + data bits + parity + STOP bits).\nNote: This bit can be cleared by writing '1' to it.</description>
              <bitOffset>6</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No Break interrupt is generated</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Break interrupt is generated</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RXPTR</name>
              <description>RX FIFO Pointer (Read Only)\nThis field indicates the RX FIFO Buffer Pointer. When UART receives one byte from external device, RXPTR increases one. When one byte of RX FIFO is read by CPU, RXPTR decreases one.\nThe Maximum value shown in RXPTR is 15. When the using level of RX FIFO Buffer equal to 16, the RXFULL bit is set to 1 and RXPTR will show 0. As one byte of RX FIFO is read by CPU, the RXFULL bit is cleared to 0 and RXPTR will show 15.</description>
              <bitOffset>8</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>6</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RXEMPTY</name>
              <description>Receiver FIFO Empty (Read Only)\nThis bit initiate RX FIFO empty or not.\nNote: When the last byte of RX FIFO has been read by CPU, hardware sets this bit high. It will be cleared when UART receives any new data.</description>
              <bitOffset>14</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>RX FIFO is not empty</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>RX FIFO is empty</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RXFULL</name>
              <description>Receiver FIFO Full (Read Only)\nThis bit initiates RX FIFO full or not.\nNote: This bit is set when the number of usage in RX FIFO Buffer is equal to 16, otherwise it is cleared by hardware.</description>
              <bitOffset>15</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>RX FIFO is not full</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>RX FIFO is full</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TXPTR</name>
              <description>TX FIFO Pointer (Read Only)\nThis field indicates the TX FIFO Buffer Pointer. When CPU writes one byte into UART_DAT, TXPTR increases one. When one byte of TX FIFO is transferred to Transmitter Shift Register, TXPTR decreases one.\nThe Maximum value shown in TXPTR is 15. When the using level of TX FIFO Buffer equal to 16, the TXFULL bit is set to 1 and TXPTR will show 0. As one byte of TX FIFO is transferred to Transmitter Shift Register, the TXFULL bit is cleared to 0 and TXPTR will show 15.</description>
              <bitOffset>16</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>6</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TXEMPTY</name>
              <description>Transmitter FIFO Empty (Read Only)\nThis bit indicates TX FIFO empty or not.\nNote: When the last byte of TX FIFO has been transferred to Transmitter Shift Register, hardware sets this bit high. It will be cleared when writing data into UART_DAT (TX FIFO not empty).</description>
              <bitOffset>22</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>TX FIFO is not empty</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>TX FIFO is empty</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TXFULL</name>
              <description>Transmitter FIFO Full (Read Only)\nThis bit indicates TX FIFO full or not.\nNote: This bit is set when the number of usage in TX FIFO Buffer is equal to 16, otherwise it is cleared by hardware.</description>
              <bitOffset>23</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>TX FIFO is not full</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>TX FIFO is full</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TXOVIF</name>
              <description>TX Overflow Error Interrupt Flag\nIf TX FIFO (UART_DAT) is full, an additional write to UART_DAT will cause this bit to logic 1.\nNote: This bit can be cleared by writing '1' to it.</description>
              <bitOffset>24</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>TX FIFO is not overflow</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>TX FIFO is overflow</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TXEMPTYF</name>
              <description>Transmitter Empty Flag (Read Only)\nThis bit is set by hardware when TX FIFO (UART_DAT) is empty and the STOP bit of the last byte has been transmitted.\nNote: This bit is cleared automatically when TX FIFO is not empty or the last byte transmission has not completed.</description>
              <bitOffset>28</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>TX FIFO is not empty or the STOP bit of the last byte has been not transmitted</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>TX FIFO is empty and the STOP bit of the last byte has been transmitted</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RXIDLE</name>
              <description>RX Idle Status (Read Only)\nThis bit is set by hardware when RX is idle.</description>
              <bitOffset>29</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>RX is busy</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>RX is idle. (Default)</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TXRXACT</name>
              <description>TX and RX Active Status (Read Only)\nThis bit indicates TX and RX are active or inactive.\nNote: When TXRXDIS (UART_FUNCSEL[3]) is set and both TX and RX are in idle state, this bit is cleared. The UART controller cannot transmit or receive data at this moment. Otherwise this bit is set.</description>
              <bitOffset>31</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>TX and RX are inactive</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>TX and RX are active. (Default)</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>UARTx_INTSTS</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>UARTx_INTSTS</displayName>
          <description>UART Interrupt Status Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x1C</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00400002</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>RDAIF</name>
              <description>Receive Data Available Interrupt Flag (Read Only)\nWhen the number of bytes in the RX FIFO equals the RFITL then the RDAIF(UART_INTSTS[0]) will be set. If RDAIEN (UART_INTEN[0]) is enabled, the RDA interrupt will be generated.\nNote: This bit is read only and will be cleared when the number of unread bytes of RX FIFO drops below the threshold level (RFITL(UART_FIFO[7:4]).</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No RDA interrupt flag is generated</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>RDA interrupt flag is generated</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>THREIF</name>
              <description>Transmit Holding Register Empty Interrupt Flag (Read Only)\nThis bit is set when the last data of TX FIFO is transferred to Transmitter Shift Register. If THREIEN (UART_INTEN[1]) is enabled, the THRE interrupt will be generated.\nNote: This bit is read only and will be cleared when writing data into UART_DAT (TX FIFO is not empty).</description>
              <bitOffset>1</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No THRE interrupt flag is generated</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>THRE interrupt flag is generated</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RLSIF</name>
              <description>Receive Line Interrupt Flag (Read Only)\nThis bit is set when the RX receive data have parity error, frame error or break error (at least one of 3 bits, BIF(UART_FIFOSTS[6]), FEF(UART_FIFOSTS[5]) and PEF(UART_FIFOSTS[4]), is set). If RLSIEN (UART_INTEN[2]) is enabled, the RLS interrupt will be generated.\nNote 2: This bit is read only and reset to 0 when all bits of BIF (UART_FIFOSTS[6]), FEF(UART_FIFOSTS[5]) and PEF(UART_FIFOSTS[4]) are cleared.\nNote 3: In RS-485 function mode, this bit is read only and reset to 0 when all bits of BIF (UART_FIFOSTS[6]), FEF(UART_FIFOSTS[5]), PEF(UART_FIFOSTS[4]) and ADDRDETF (UART_FIFOSTS[3]) are cleared.\nNote 4: This bit is valid only when RXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[15]) and TXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[14]) are both 0.</description>
              <bitOffset>2</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No RLS interrupt flag is generated</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>RLS interrupt flag is generated</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>MODEMIF</name>
              <description>MODEM Interrupt Flag (Read Only)\nNote 1: This bit is read only and reset to 0 when bit CTSDETF is cleared by a write 1 on CTSDETF(UART_MODEMSTS[0]).\nNote 2: This bit is valid only when RXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[15]) and TXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[14]) are both 0.</description>
              <bitOffset>3</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No Modem interrupt flag is generated</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Modem interrupt flag is generated</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RXTOIF</name>
              <description>RX Time-out Interrupt Flag (Read Only)\nThis bit is set when the RX FIFO is not empty and no activities occurred in the RX FIFO and the time-out counter equal to TOIC (UART_TOUT[7:0]). If RXTOIEN (UART_INTEN[4]) is enabled, the RX time-out interrupt will be generated.\nNote 1: This bit can be cleared by writing '1' to it.\nNote 2: If BITOMEN (UART_TOUT[31]) is reset to 0, user can read UART_DAT (RX is in active) to clear this bit.\nNote 3: This bit is valid only when RXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[15]) and TXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[14]) are both 0.</description>
              <bitOffset>4</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No RX time-out interrupt flag is generated</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>RX time-out interrupt flag is generated</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BUFERRIF</name>
              <description>Buffer Error Interrupt Flag (Read Only)\nThis bit is set when the TX FIFO or RX FIFO overflows (TXOVIF (UART_FIFOSTS[24]) or RXOVIF (UART_FIFOSTS[0]) is set). When BUFERRIF (UART_INTSTS[5]) is set, the transfer is not correct. If BUFERRIEN (UART_INTEN[5]) is enabled, the buffer error interrupt will be generated.\nNote 1: This bit is cleared if both of RXOVIF(UART_FIFOSTS[0]) and TXOVIF(UART_FIFOSTS[24]) are cleared to 0 by writing 1 to RXOVIF(UART_FIFOSTS[0]) and TXOVIF(UART_FIFOSTS[24]).\nNote 2: This bit is valid only when RXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[15]) and TXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[14]) are both 0.</description>
              <bitOffset>5</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No buffer error interrupt flag is generated</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Buffer error interrupt flag is generated</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>WKIF</name>
              <description>UART Wake-up Interrupt Flag (Read Only)\nThis bit is set when LINWKF (UART_LINWKCTL[29]), TOUTWKF (UART_WKSTS[4]), RS485WKF (UART_WKSTS[3]), RFRTWKF (UART_WKSTS[2]), DATWKF (UART_WKSTS[1]) or CTSWKF(UART_WKSTS[0]) is set to 1.\nNote: This bit is cleared if all of LINWKF, TOUTWKF, RS485WKF, RFRTWKF, DATWKF and CTSWKF are cleared to 0 by writing 1 to the corresponding interrupt flag.</description>
              <bitOffset>6</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No UART wake-up interrupt flag is generated</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>UART wake-up interrupt flag is generated</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>LINIF</name>
              <description>LIN Bus Interrupt Flag\nNote: This bit is cleared when SLVHDETF (UART_LINSTS[0]), SLVHEF (UART_LINSTS[1]), SLVIDPEF (UART_LINSTS[2]), SLVHTOF (UART_LINSTS[4]), RTOUTF (UART_LINSTS[5]), BRKDETF (UART_LINSTS[8]), and BITEF (UART_LINSTS[9]) all are cleared and software writing '1' to LINIF (UART_INTSTS[7]).</description>
              <bitOffset>7</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>None of SLVHDETF, SLVHEF, SLVIDPEF, SLVHTOF, RTOUTF, BITEF, and BRKDETF is generated</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>At least one of SLVHDETF, SLVHEF, SLVIDPEF, SLVHTOF, RTOUTF, BITEF, and BRKDETF is generated</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RDAINT</name>
              <description>Receive Data Available Interrupt Indicator (Read Only)\nThis bit is set if RDAIEN (UART_INTEN[0]) and RDAIF (UART_INTSTS[0]) are both set to 1.</description>
              <bitOffset>8</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No RDA interrupt is generated</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>RDA interrupt is generated</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>THREINT</name>
              <description>Transmit Holding Register Empty Interrupt Indicator (Read Only)\nThis bit is set if THREIEN (UART_INTEN[1]) and THREIF(UART_INTSTS[1]) are both set to 1.</description>
              <bitOffset>9</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No THRE interrupt is generated</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>THRE interrupt is generated</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RLSINT</name>
              <description>Receive Line Status Interrupt Indicator (Read Only) \nThis bit is set if RLSIEN (UART_INTEN[2]) and RLSIF(UART_INTSTS[2]) are both set to 1.\nNote: This bit is valid only when RXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[15]) and TXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[14]) are both 0.</description>
              <bitOffset>10</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No RLS interrupt is generated</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>RLS interrupt is generated</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>MODEMINT</name>
              <description>MODEM Status Interrupt Indicator (Read Only)\nThis bit is set if MODEMIEN(UART_INTEN[3]) and MODEMIF(UART_INTSTS[3]) are both set to 1\nNote: This bit is valid only when RXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[15]) and TXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[14]) are both 0.</description>
              <bitOffset>11</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No Modem interrupt is generated</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Modem interrupt is generated</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RXTOINT</name>
              <description>RX Time-out Interrupt Indicator (Read Only)\nThis bit is set if RXTOIEN (UART_INTEN[4]) and RXTOIF(UART_INTSTS[4]) are both set to 1.\nNote: This bit is valid only when RXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[15]) and TXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[14]) are both 0.</description>
              <bitOffset>12</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No RX time-out interrupt is generated</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>RX time-out interrupt is generated</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BUFERRINT</name>
              <description>Buffer Error Interrupt Indicator (Read Only)\nThis bit is set if BUFERRIEN(UART_INTEN[5]) and BUFERRIF(UART_ INTSTS[5]) are both set to 1.\nNote: This bit is valid only when RXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[15]) and TXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[14]) are both 0.</description>
              <bitOffset>13</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No buffer error interrupt is generated</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Buffer error interrupt is generated</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>WKINT</name>
              <description>UART Wake-up Interrupt Indicator (Read Only)\nThis bit is set if WKIEN (UART_INTEN[6]) and WKIF (UART_INTSTS[6]) are both set to 1.</description>
              <bitOffset>14</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No UART wake-up interrupt is generated</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>UART wake-up interrupt is generated</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>LININT</name>
              <description>LIN Bus Interrupt Indicator (Read Only)\nThis bit is set if LINIEN (UART_INTEN[8]) and LINIF(UART_INTSTS[7]) are both set to 1.</description>
              <bitOffset>15</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No LIN Bus interrupt is generated</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>The LIN Bus interrupt is generated</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SWBEIF</name>
              <description>Single-wire Bit Error Detection Interrupt Flag\nThis bit is set when the single wire bus state not equals to UART controller TX state in Single-wire mode.\nNote 1: This bit is active when FUNCSEL (UART_FUNCSEL[2:0]) is select UART Single-wire mode.\nNote 2: This bit can be cleared by writing '1' to it.</description>
              <bitOffset>16</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No single-wire bit error detection interrupt flag is generated</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Single-wire bit error detection interrupt flag is generated</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>HWRLSIF</name>
              <description>PDMA Mode Receive Line Status Flag (Read Only)\nThis bit is set when the RX receive data have parity error, frame error or break error (at least one of 3 bits, BIF (UART_FIFOSTS[6]), FEF (UART_FIFOSTS[5]) and PEF (UART_FIFOSTS[4]) is set). If RLSIEN (UART_INTEN[2]) is enabled, the RLS interrupt will be generated.\nNote 2: In UART function mode, this bit is read only and reset to 0 when all bits of BIF(UART_FIFOSTS[6]), FEF(UART_FIFOSTS[5]) and PEF(UART_FIFOSTS[4]) are cleared. \nNote 3: In RS-485 function mode, this bit is read only and reset to 0 when all bits of BIF(UART_FIFOSTS[6]), FEF(UART_FIFOSTS[5]), PEF(UART_FIFOSTS[4]) and ADDRDETF (UART_FIFOSTS[3]) are cleared.</description>
              <bitOffset>18</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No RLS interrupt flag is generated when RXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[15]) or TXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[14]) is 1</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>RLS interrupt flag is generated when RXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[15]) or TXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[14]) is 1</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>HWMODIF</name>
              <description>PDMA Mode MODEM Interrupt Flag (Read Only)\nNote: This bit is read only and reset to 0 when the bit CTSDETF (UART_MODEMSTS[0]) is cleared by writing 1 on CTSDETF (UART_MODEMSTS[0]).</description>
              <bitOffset>19</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No Modem interrupt flag is generated when RXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[15]) or TXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[14]) is 1</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Modem interrupt flag is generated when RXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[15]) or TXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[14]) is 1</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>HWTOIF</name>
              <description>PDMA Mode RX Time-out Interrupt Flag (Read Only)\nThis bit is set when the RX FIFO is not empty and no activities occurred in the RX FIFO and the time-out counter equal to TOIC (UART_TOUT[7:0]). If RXTOIEN (UART_INTEN[4]) is enabled, the RX time-out interrupt will be generated. \nNote: This bit is read only and user can read UART_DAT (RX is in active) to clear it.</description>
              <bitOffset>20</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No RX time-out interrupt flag is generated when RXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[15]) or TXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[14]) is 1</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>RX time-out interrupt flag is generated when RXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[15]) or TXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[14]) is 1</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>HWBUFEIF</name>
              <description>PDMA Mode Buffer Error Interrupt Flag (Read Only)\nThis bit is set when the TX or RX FIFO overflows (TXOVIF (UART_FIFOSTS[24]) or RXOVIF (UART_FIFOSTS[0]) is set). When BUFERRIF (UART_INTSTS[5]) is set, the transfer maybe is not correct. If BUFERRIEN (UART_INTEN[5]) is enabled, the buffer error interrupt will be generated.\nNote: This bit is cleared when both TXOVIF (UART_FIFOSTS[24]]) and RXOVIF  (UART_FIFOSTS[0]) are cleared.</description>
              <bitOffset>21</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No buffer error interrupt flag is generated when RXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[15]) or TXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[14]) is 1</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Buffer error interrupt flag is generated when RXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[15]) or TXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[14]) is 1</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TXENDIF</name>
              <description>Transmitter Empty Interrupt Flag (Read Only)\nThis bit is set when TX FIFO (UART_DAT) is empty and the STOP bit of the last byte has been transmitted (TXEMPTYF (UART_FIFOSTS[28]) is set). If TXENDIEN (UART_INTEN[22]) is enabled, the Transmitter Empty interrupt will be generated.\nNote: This bit is cleared automatically when TX FIFO is not empty or the last byte transmission has not completed.</description>
              <bitOffset>22</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No transmitter empty interrupt flag is generated</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Transmitter empty interrupt flag is generated</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SWBEINT</name>
              <description>Single-wire Bit Error Detect Interrupt Indicator (Read Only)\nThis bit is set if SWBEIEN (UART_INTEN[16]) and SWBEIF (UART_INTSTS[16]) are both set to 1.</description>
              <bitOffset>24</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No Single-wire Bit Error Detection Interrupt generated</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Single-wire Bit Error Detection Interrupt generated</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>HWRLSINT</name>
              <description>PDMA Mode Receive Line Status Interrupt Indicator (Read Only)\nThis bit is set if RLSIEN (UART_INTEN[2]) and HWRLSIF(UART_INTSTS[18]) are both set to 1.</description>
              <bitOffset>26</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No RLS interrupt is generated when RXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[15]) or TXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[14]) is 1</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>RLS interrupt is generated when RXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[15]) or TXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[14]) is 1</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>HWMODINT</name>
              <description>PDMA Mode MODEM Status Interrupt Indicator (Read Only)\nThis bit is set if MODEMIEN (UART_INTEN[3]) and HWMODIF(UART_INTSTS[19]) are both set to 1.</description>
              <bitOffset>27</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No Modem interrupt is generated when RXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[15]) or TXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[14]) is 1</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Modem interrupt is generated when RXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[15]) or TXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[14]) is 1</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>HWTOINT</name>
              <description>PDMA Mode RX Time-out Interrupt Indicator (Read Only)\nThis bit is set if RXTOIEN (UART_INTEN[4]) and HWTOIF(UART_INTSTS[20]) are both set to 1.</description>
              <bitOffset>28</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No RX time-out interrupt is generated when RXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[15]) or TXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[14]) is 1</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>RX time-out interrupt is generated when RXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[15]) or TXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[14]) is 1</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>HWBUFEINT</name>
              <description>PDMA Mode Buffer Error Interrupt Indicator (Read Only)\nThis bit is set if BUFERRIEN (UART_INTEN[5]) and HWBUFEIF (UART_INTSTS[21]) are both set to 1.</description>
              <bitOffset>29</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No buffer error interrupt is generated when RXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[15]) or TXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[14]) is 1</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Buffer error interrupt is generated when RXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[15]) or TXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[14]) is 1</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TXENDINT</name>
              <description>Transmitter Empty Interrupt Indicator (Read Only) \nThis bit is set if TXENDIEN (UART_INTEN[22]) and TXENDIF(UART_INTSTS[22]) are both set to 1.</description>
              <bitOffset>30</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No Transmitter Empty interrupt is generated</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Transmitter Empty interrupt is generated</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ABRINT</name>
              <description>Auto-baud Rate Interrupt Indicator (Read Only)\nThis bit is set if ABRIEN (UART_INTEN[18]) and ABRIF (UART_ALTCTL[17]) are both set to 1.</description>
              <bitOffset>31</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No Auto-baud Rate interrupt is generated</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>The Auto-baud Rate interrupt is generated</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>UARTx_TOUT</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>UARTx_TOUT</displayName>
          <description>UART Time-out Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x20</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>TOIC</name>
              <description>Time-out Interrupt Comparator</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>8</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>DLY</name>
              <description>TX Delay Time Value \nThis field is used to program the transfer delay time between the last STOP bit and next START bit. The unit is bit time.</description>
              <bitOffset>8</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>8</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BITOMEN</name>
              <description>Bus Idle Time-out Mode Enable Bit\nIf BITOMEN (UART_TOUT[31]) is enabled, the reset conditions of the time-out counter and RXTOIF (UART_INTSTS[4]) will be changed to detect the bus idle.\nWhen BITOMEN (UART_TOUT[31]) is disabled, the time-out counter and RXTOIF (UART_INTSTS[4]) maintain reset value whenever the RX FIFO is empty. In addition, Any RX FIFO activities, such as a new incoming data or user reading data, will clear RXTOIF (UART_INTSTS[4]) and reset RX time-out counter.. \nOn the other hand, when BITOMEN (UART_TOUT[31]) is enabled, the RX FIFO empty state will not reset the time-out counter and RXTOIF (UART_INTSTS[4]), and RX FIFO activities, such as a new incoming data or user reading data, will not clear RXTOIF (UART_INTSTS[4]). However, the time-out counter needs to receive a first incoming data before it can start counting. After receiving the first incoming data, the time-out counter will count even if the RX FIFO is empty.</description>
              <bitOffset>31</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Bus idle time-out mode Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Bus idle time-out mode Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>UARTx_BAUD</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>UARTx_BAUD</displayName>
          <description>UART Baud Rate Divider Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x24</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x0F0000FF</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>BRD</name>
              <description>Baud Rate Divider\nThe field indicates the baud rate divider. This filed is used in baud rate calculation. The detail description is shown in Table 6.214.</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>16</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BRFD</name>
              <description>Baud Rate Fractional Divider\nThis field is the fractional part of the baud rate divisor.  \nWhen BRFDEN (UART_BAUD[30]) is set, the Baud Rate Equation goes to UART_CLK / ((BRD+2) + (BRFD/256)). </description>
              <bitOffset>16</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>8</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>EDIVM1</name>
              <description>Extra Divider for BAUD Rate Mode 1\nThis field is used for baud rate calculation in mode 1 and has no effect for baud rate calculation in mode 0 and mode 2. The detail description is shown in Table 6.214.</description>
              <bitOffset>24</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>4</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BAUDM0</name>
              <description>BAUD Rate Mode Selection Bit 0\nThis bit is baud rate mode selection bit 0. UART provides three baud rate calculation modes. This bit combines with BAUDM1 (UART_BAUD[29]) to select baud rate calculation mode. The detail description is shown in Table 6.214.</description>
              <bitOffset>28</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BAUDM1</name>
              <description>BAUD Rate Mode Selection Bit 1\nThis bit is baud rate mode selection bit 1. UART provides three baud rate calculation modes. This bit combines with BAUDM0 (UART_BAUD[28]) to select baud rate calculation mode. The detail description is shown in Table 6.214.\nNote: In IrDA mode must be operated in mode 0.</description>
              <bitOffset>29</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BRFDEN</name>
              <description>Baud Rate Fractional Divider Enable Bit\nNote: This bit has effect only at baud rate mode 2 (BAUDM1 (UART_BAUD[29]) and BAUDM0 (UART_BAUD[28]) must be 1).</description>
              <bitOffset>30</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Baud Rate Fractional Divider Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Baud Rate Fractional Divider Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>UARTx_IRDA</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>UARTx_IRDA</displayName>
          <description>UART IrDA Control Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x28</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000040</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>TXEN</name>
              <description>IrDA Receiver/Transmitter Selection Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>1</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>IrDA Transmitter Disabled and Receiver Enabled. (Default)</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>IrDA Transmitter Enabled and Receiver Disabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TXINV</name>
              <description>IrDA Inverse Transmitting Output Signal \nNote 1: Before setting this bit, TXRXDIS (UART_FUNCSEL[3]) should be set then waited for TXRXACT (UART_FIFOSTS[31]) is cleared. When the configuration is done, cleared TXRXDIS (UART_FUNCSEL[3]) to activate UART controller.\nNote 2: This bit is valid when FUNCSEL (UART_FUNCSEL[2:0]) is select IrDA function.</description>
              <bitOffset>5</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>None inverse transmitting signal. (Default)</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Inverse transmitting output signal</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RXINV</name>
              <description>IrDA Inverse Receive Input Signal \nNote 1: Before setting this bit, TXRXDIS (UART_FUNCSEL[3]) should be set then waited for TXRXACT (UART_FIFOSTS[31]) is cleared. When the configuration is done, cleared TXRXDIS (UART_FUNCSEL[3]) to activate UART controller.\nNote 2: This bit is valid when FUNCSEL (UART_FUNCSEL[2:0]) is select IrDA function.</description>
              <bitOffset>6</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>None inverse receiving input signal</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Inverse receiving input signal. (Default)</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>UARTx_ALTCTL</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>UARTx_ALTCTL</displayName>
          <description>UART Alternate Control/Status Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x2C</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x0000000C</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>BRKFL</name>
              <description>UART LIN Break Field Length\nThis field indicates a 4-bit LIN TX break field count.\nNote 1: This break field length is BRKFL + 1.</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>4</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>LINRXEN</name>
              <description>LIN RX Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>6</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>LIN RX mode Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>LIN RX mode Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>LINTXEN</name>
              <description>LIN TX Break Mode Enable Bit\nNote: When TX break field transfer operation finished, this bit will be cleared automatically.</description>
              <bitOffset>7</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>LIN TX Break mode Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>LIN TX Break mode Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RS485NMM</name>
              <description>RS-485 Normal Multi-drop Operation Mode\nNote: It cannot be active with RS-485_AAD operation mode.</description>
              <bitOffset>8</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>RS-485 Normal Multi-drop Operation mode (NMM) Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>RS-485 Normal Multi-drop Operation mode (NMM) Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RS485AAD</name>
              <description>RS-485 Auto Address Detection Operation Mode\nNote: It cannot be active with RS-485_NMM operation mode.</description>
              <bitOffset>9</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>RS-485 Auto Address Detection Operation mode (AAD) Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>RS-485 Auto Address Detection Operation mode (AAD) Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RS485AUD</name>
              <description>RS-485 Auto Direction Function\nNote: It can be active with RS-485_AAD or RS-485_NMM operation mode.</description>
              <bitOffset>10</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>RS-485 Auto Direction Operation function (AUD) Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>RS-485 Auto Direction Operation function (AUD) Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ADDRDEN</name>
              <description>RS-485 Address Detection Enable Bit\nThis bit is used to enable RS-485 Address Detection mode. \nNote: This bit is used for RS-485 any operation mode.</description>
              <bitOffset>15</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Address detection mode Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Address detection mode Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ABRIF</name>
              <description>Auto-baud Rate Interrupt Flag (Read Only) \nThis bit is set when auto-baud rate detection function finished or the auto-baud rate counter was overflow and if ABRIEN(UART_INTEN[18]) is set then the auto-baud rate interrupt will be generated. \nNote: This bit is read only, but it can be cleared by writing '1' to ABRDTOIF (UART_FIFOSTS[2]) and ABRDIF(UART_FIFOSTS[1]).</description>
              <bitOffset>17</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No auto-baud rate interrupt flag is generated</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Auto-baud rate interrupt flag is generated</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ABRDEN</name>
              <description>Auto-baud Rate Detect Enable Bit\nNote: This bit is cleared automatically after auto-baud detection is finished.</description>
              <bitOffset>18</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Auto-baud rate detect function Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Auto-baud rate detect function Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ABRDBITS</name>
              <description>Auto-baud Rate Detect Bit Length \nNote: The calculation of bit number includes the START bit.</description>
              <bitOffset>19</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>2</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>1-bit time from START bit to the 1st rising edge. The input pattern shall be 0x01</description>
                    <value>#00</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>2-bit time from START bit to the 1st rising edge. The input pattern shall be 0x02</description>
                    <value>#01</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>2</name>
                    <description>4-bit time from START bit to the 1st rising edge. The input pattern shall be 0x08</description>
                    <value>#10</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>3</name>
                    <description>8-bit time from START bit to the 1st rising edge. The input pattern shall be 0x80</description>
                    <value>#11</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ADDRMV</name>
              <description>Address Match Value \nThis field contains the RS-485 address match values.\nNote: This field is used for RS-485 auto address detection mode.</description>
              <bitOffset>24</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>8</bitWidth>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>UARTx_FUNCSEL</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>UARTx_FUNCSEL</displayName>
          <description>UART Function Select Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x30</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>FUNCSEL</name>
              <description>Function Select</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>3</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>UART function</description>
                    <value>#000</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>LIN function</description>
                    <value>#001</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>2</name>
                    <description>IrDA function</description>
                    <value>#010</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>3</name>
                    <description>RS-485 function</description>
                    <value>#011</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>4</name>
                    <description>UART Single-wire function</description>
                    <value>#100</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TXRXDIS</name>
              <description>TX and RX Disable Bit\nSetting this bit can disable TX and RX.\nNote: The TX and RX will not be disabled immediately when this bit is set. The TX and RX complete current task before TX and RX are disabled. When TX and RX are disabled, the TXRXACT (UART_FIFOSTS[31]) is cleared.</description>
              <bitOffset>3</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>TX and RX Enabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>TX and RX Disabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>DGE</name>
              <description>Deglitch Enable Bit\nNote 1: When this bit is set to logic 1, any pulse width less than about 150 ns will be considered a glitch and will be removed in the serial data input (RX). This bit acts only on RX line and has no effect on the transmitter logic.\nNote 2: It is recommended to set this bit only when operating at baud rate under 2.5 Mbps.</description>
              <bitOffset>6</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Deglitch Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Deglitch Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TXRXSWP</name>
              <description>TX and RX Swap Enable Bit\nSetting this bit Swaps TX pin and RX pin.</description>
              <bitOffset>7</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>TX and RX Swap Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>TX and RX Swap Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>UARTx_LINCTL</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>UARTx_LINCTL</displayName>
          <description>UART LIN Control Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x34</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x000C0000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>SLVEN</name>
              <description>LIN Slave Mode Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>LIN slave mode Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>LIN slave mode Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SLVHDEN</name>
              <description>LIN Slave Header Detection Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>1</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>LIN slave header detection Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>LIN slave header detection Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SLVAREN</name>
              <description>LIN Slave Automatic Resynchronization Mode Enable Bit\nNote 2: When operation in Automatic Resynchronization mode, the baud rate setting must be mode 2 (BAUDM1 (UART_BAUD[29]) and BAUDM0 (UART_BAUD[28]) must be 1).\nNote 3: The control and interactions of this field are explained in 6.21.5.10 (Slave mode with automatic resynchronization).\nNote 4: The minimum detection range of the target BRD (UART_BAUD[15:0]) is 0x30.</description>
              <bitOffset>2</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>LIN automatic resynchronization Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>LIN automatic resynchronization Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SLVDUEN</name>
              <description>LIN Slave Divider Update Method Enable Bit\nNote 2: This bit used for LIN Slave Automatic Resynchronization mode. (for Non-Automatic Resynchronization mode, this bit should be kept cleared) \nNote 3: The control and interactions of this field are explained in 6.21.5.10 (Slave mode with automatic resynchronization).</description>
              <bitOffset>3</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>UART_BAUD updated is written by software (if no automatic resynchronization update occurs at the same time)</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>UART_BAUD is updated at the next received character. User must set the bit before checksum reception</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>MUTE</name>
              <description>LIN Mute Mode Enable Bit\nNote: The exit from mute mode condition and each control and interactions of this field are explained in 6.21.5.10 (LIN slave mode).</description>
              <bitOffset>4</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>LIN mute mode Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>LIN mute mode Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RTOUTEN</name>
              <description>LIN Response Time-out Detection Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>5</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>LIN response time-out detection Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>LIN response time-out detection Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SENDH</name>
              <description>LIN TX Send Header Enable Bit\nThe LIN TX header can be 'break field' or 'break and sync field' or 'break, sync and frame ID field', it is depend on setting HSEL (UART_LINCTL[23:22]).\nNote 1: This bit is shadow bit of LINTXEN (UART_ALTCTL[7]); user can read/write it by setting LINTXEN (UART_ALTCTL[7]) or SENDH (UART_LINCTL[8]).\nNote 2: When transmitter header field (it may be 'break' or 'break + sync' or 'break + sync + frame ID' selected by HSEL (UART_LINCTL[23:22]) field) transfer operation finished, this bit will be cleared automatically.</description>
              <bitOffset>8</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Send LIN TX header Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Send LIN TX header Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>IDPEN</name>
              <description>LIN ID Parity Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>9</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>LIN frame ID parity Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>LIN frame ID parity Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BRKDETEN</name>
              <description>LIN Break Detection Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>10</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>LIN break detection Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>LIN break detection Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>LINRXOFF</name>
              <description>LIN Receiver Disable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>11</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>LIN receiver Enabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>LIN receiver Disabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BITERREN</name>
              <description>Bit Error Detect Enable Bit </description>
              <bitOffset>12</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Bit error detection function Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Bit error detection function Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BRKFL</name>
              <description>LIN Break Field Length \nThis field indicates a 4-bit LIN TX break field count.\nNote 1: These registers are shadow registers of BRKFL (UART_ALTCTL[3:0]). User can read/write it by setting BRKFL (UART_ALTCTL[3:0]) or BRKFL (UART_LINCTL[19:16]).\nNote 2: This break field length is BRKFL + 1.</description>
              <bitOffset>16</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>4</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BSL</name>
              <description>LIN Break/Sync Delimiter Length \nNote: This bit used for LIN master to sending header field.</description>
              <bitOffset>20</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>2</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>The LIN break/sync delimiter length is 1-bit time</description>
                    <value>#00</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>The LIN break/sync delimiter length is 2-bit time</description>
                    <value>#01</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>2</name>
                    <description>The LIN break/sync delimiter length is 3-bit time</description>
                    <value>#10</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>3</name>
                    <description>The LIN break/sync delimiter length is 4-bit time</description>
                    <value>#11</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>HSEL</name>
              <description>LIN Header Select </description>
              <bitOffset>22</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>2</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>The LIN header includes 'break field'</description>
                    <value>#00</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>The LIN header includes 'break field' and 'sync field'</description>
                    <value>#01</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>2</name>
                    <description>The LIN header includes 'break field', 'sync field' and 'frame ID field'</description>
                    <value>#10</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>3</name>
                    <description>Reserved.</description>
                    <value>#11</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>PID</name>
              <description>LIN PID Bits\nIf the parity generated by hardware, user fill ID0~ID5 (PID[29:24]), hardware will calculate P0 (PID[30]) and P1 (PID[31]), otherwise user must filled frame ID and parity in this field.\nNote 1: User can fill any 8-bit value to this field and the bit 24 indicates ID0 (LSB first).\nNote 2: This field can be used for LIN master mode or slave mode.</description>
              <bitOffset>24</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>8</bitWidth>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>UARTx_LINSTS</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>UARTx_LINSTS</displayName>
          <description>UART LIN Status Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x38</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>SLVHDETF</name>
              <description>LIN Slave Header Detection Flag\nThis bit is set by hardware when a LIN header is detected in LIN slave mode and be cleared by writing 1 to it.\nNote 3: When enable ID parity check IDPEN (UART_LINCTL[9]), if hardware detect complete header ('break + sync + frame ID'), the SLVHDETF will be set whether the frame ID correct or not.</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>LIN header not detected</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>LIN header detected (break + sync + frame ID)</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SLVHEF</name>
              <description>LIN Slave Header Error Flag\nThis bit is set by hardware when a LIN header error is detected in LIN slave mode and be cleared by writing 1 to it. The header errors include 'break delimiter is too short (less than 0.5 bit time)', 'frame error in sync field or Identifier field', 'sync field data is not 0x55 in Non-Automatic Resynchronization mode', 'sync field deviation error with Automatic Resynchronization mode', 'sync field measure time-out with Automatic Resynchronization mode' and 'LIN header reception time-out'.</description>
              <bitOffset>1</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>LIN header error not detected</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>LIN header error detected</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SLVIDPEF</name>
              <description>LIN Slave ID Parity Error Flag \nThis bit is set by hardware when receipted frame ID parity is not correct.</description>
              <bitOffset>2</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No active</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Receipted frame ID parity is not correct</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SLVSYNCF</name>
              <description>LIN Slave Sync Field\nThis bit indicates that the LIN sync field is being analyzed in Automatic Resynchronization mode. When the receiver header have some error been detect, user must reset the internal circuit to re-search new frame header by writing 1 to this bit.\nNote 2: This bit can be cleared by writing 1 to it.\nNote 3: When writing 1 to it, hardware will reload the initial baud rate and re-search a new frame header.</description>
              <bitOffset>3</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>The current character is not at LIN sync state</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>The current character is at LIN sync state</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SLVHTOF</name>
              <description>LIN Slave Header Time-out Flag\nThis bit is set by hardware when a LIN header reception time-out is detected in LIN slave mode and be cleared by writing 1 to it. When this bit is set, SLVHEF (UART_LINSTS[1]) will also be set.</description>
              <bitOffset>4</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>LIN header time-out not detected</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>LIN header time-out detected</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RTOUTF</name>
              <description>LIN Response Time-out Flag\nThis bit is set when no LIN response received and the time-out counter equal to or bigger than LINRTOIC (UART_LINRTOUT[23:0]). If LINIEN (UART_INTEN[8]) is enabled, the LIN Bus Interrupt will be generated.\nNote 1: This bit can be cleared by writing 1 to it.</description>
              <bitOffset>5</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>LIN response time-out not detected</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>LIN response time-out detected</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BRKDETF</name>
              <description>LIN Break Detection Flag\nThis bit is set by hardware when a break is detected and be cleared by writing 1 to it through software.</description>
              <bitOffset>8</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>LIN break not detected</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>LIN break detected</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BITEF</name>
              <description>Bit Error Detect Status Flag \nAt TX transfer state, hardware will monitor the bus state, if the input pin (UART_RXD) state not equals to the output pin (UART_TXD) state, BITEF (UART_LINSTS[9]) will be set.</description>
              <bitOffset>9</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Bit error not detected</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Bit error detected</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>UARTx_BRCOMP</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>UARTx_BRCOMP</displayName>
          <description>UART Baud Rate Compensation Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x3C</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>BRCOMP</name>
              <description>Baud Rate Compensation Patten\nThese 9-bits are used to define the relative bit is compensated or not. \nBRCOMP[7:0] is used to define the compensation of DAT (UART_DAT[7:0]) and BRCOM[8] is used to define PARITY (UART_DAT[8]).</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>9</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BRCOMPDEC</name>
              <description>Baud Rate Compensation Decrease</description>
              <bitOffset>31</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Positive (increase one module clock) compensation for each compensated bit</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Negative (decrease one module clock) compensation for each compensated bit</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>UARTx_WKCTL</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>UARTx_WKCTL</displayName>
          <description>UART Wake-up Control Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x40</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>WKCTSEN</name>
              <description>nCTS Wake-up Enable Bit\nNote: When the system is in Power-down mode, an external nCTS change will wake up system from Power-down mode.</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>nCTS Wake-up system function Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>nCTS Wake-up system function Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>WKDATEN</name>
              <description>Incoming Data Wake-up Enable Bit\nNote: When the system is in Power-down mode, incoming data will wake-up system from Power-down mode.</description>
              <bitOffset>1</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Incoming data wake-up system function Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Incoming data wake-up system function Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>WKRFRTEN</name>
              <description>Received Data FIFO Reached Threshold Wake-up Enable Bit\nNote: When the system is in Power-down mode, Received Data FIFO reached threshold will wake-up system from Power-down mode.</description>
              <bitOffset>2</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Received Data FIFO reached threshold wake-up system function Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Received Data FIFO reached threshold wake-up system function Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>WKRS485EN</name>
              <description>RS-485 Address Match Wake-up Enable Bit\nNote 1: When the system is in Power-down mode, RS-485 Address Match will wake-up system from Power-down mode.\nNote 2: This bit is used for RS-485 Auto Address Detection (AAD) mode in RS-485 function mode and ADDRDEN (UART_ALTCTL[15]) is set to 1.</description>
              <bitOffset>3</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>RS-485 Address Match (AAD mode) wake-up system function Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>RS-485 Address Match (AAD mode) wake-up system function Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>WKTOUTEN</name>
              <description>Received Data FIFO Reached Threshold Time-out Wake-up Enable Bit\nNote 1: When the system is in Power-down mode, Received Data FIFO reached threshold time-out will wake up system from Power-down mode.\nNote 2: It is suggested the function is enabled when the WKRFRTEN (UART_WKCTL[2]) is set to 1.</description>
              <bitOffset>4</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Received Data FIFO reached threshold time-out wake-up system function Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Received Data FIFO reached threshold time-out wake-up system function Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>UARTx_WKSTS</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>UARTx_WKSTS</displayName>
          <description>UART Wake-up Status Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x44</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>CTSWKF</name>
              <description>nCTS Wake-up Flag\nThis bit is set if chip wake-up from power-down state by nCTS wake-up.\nNote 1: If WKCTSEN (UART_WKCTL[0]) is enabled, the nCTS wake-up cause this bit is set to '1'.\nNote 2: This bit can be cleared by writing '1' to it.</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Chip stays in power-down state</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Chip wake-up from power-down state by nCTS wake-up</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>DATWKF</name>
              <description>Incoming Data Wake-up Flag\nThis bit is set if chip wake-up from power-down state by data wake-up.\nNote 1: If WKDATEN (UART_WKCTL[1]) is enabled, the Incoming Data wake-up cause this bit is set to '1'.\nNote 2: This bit can be cleared by writing '1' to it.</description>
              <bitOffset>1</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Chip stays in power-down state</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Chip wake-up from power-down state by Incoming Data wake-up</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RFRTWKF</name>
              <description>Received Data FIFO Reached Threshold Wake-up Flag\nThis bit is set if chip wake-up from power-down state by Received Data FIFO reached threshold wake-up.\nNote 1: If WKRFRTEN (UART_WKCTL[2]) is enabled, the Received Data FIFO Reached Threshold wake-up cause this bit is set to '1'.\nNote 2: This bit can be cleared by writing '1' to it.</description>
              <bitOffset>2</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Chip stays in power-down state</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Chip wake-up from power-down state by Received Data FIFO Reached Threshold wake-up</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RS485WKF</name>
              <description>RS-485 Address Match Wake-up Flag\nThis bit is set if chip wake-up from power-down state by RS-485 Address Match (AAD mode).\nNote 1: If WKRS485EN (UART_WKCTL[3]) is enabled, the RS-485 Address Match (AAD mode) wake-up cause this bit is set to '1'.\nNote 2: This bit can be cleared by writing '1' to it.</description>
              <bitOffset>3</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Chip stays in power-down state</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Chip wake-up from power-down state by RS-485 Address Match (AAD mode) wake-up</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TOUTWKF</name>
              <description>Received Data FIFO Threshold Time-out Wake-up Flag\nThis bit is set if chip wake-up from power-down state by Received Data FIFO Threshold Time-out\nwake-up.\nNote 1: If WKTOUTEN (UART_WKCTL[4]) is enabled, the Received Data FIFO reached threshold time-out wake-up cause this bit is set to '1'.\nNote 2: This bit can be cleared by writing '1' to it.</description>
              <bitOffset>4</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Chip stays in power-down state</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Chip wake-up from power-down state by Received Data FIFO reached threshold time-out wake-up</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>UARTx_DWKCOMP</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>UARTx_DWKCOMP</displayName>
          <description>UART Incoming Data Wake-up Compensation Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x48</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>STCOMP</name>
              <description>START Bit Compensation Value\nThese bits field indicate how many clock cycle selected by UART_CLK do the UART controller can get the 1st bit (START bit) when the device is wake-up from Power-down mode.\nNote: It is valid only when WKDATEN (UART_WKCTL[1]) is set.</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>16</bitWidth>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>UARTx_RS485DD</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>UARTx_RS485DD</displayName>
          <description>UART RS485 Transceiver Deactivate Delay Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x4C</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>RTSDDLY</name>
              <description>RS485 Transceiver Deactivate Delay Value\nThese bits field indicate how many clock cycles selected by UART_CLK do the UART controller delay the RS485 transceiver state trancing when the state trancing of RS485 transceiver is from TX to RX state. These bits field have no effect when the state trancing of RS485 transceiver is from RX to TX state.\nNote: It is valid only when RS485AUD (UART_ALTCTL[10]) is set.</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>16</bitWidth>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>UARTx_LINRTOUT</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>UARTx_LINRTOUT</displayName>
          <description>UART LIN Response Time-out Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x50</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00FFFFFF</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>LINRTOIC</name>
              <description>LIN Response Time-out Comparator</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>24</bitWidth>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>UARTx_LINWKCTL</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>UARTx_LINWKCTL</displayName>
          <description>UART LIN Wake-up Control Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x54</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x000000C0</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>LINWKC</name>
              <description>LIN Send Wake-up Signal Length Counter</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>24</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SENDLINW</name>
              <description>LIN Send Wake-up Enable Bit\nNote 1: When this bit is set, the UART will send LIN wake-up automatically. When LIN wake-up transfer operation finished, this bit will be cleared automatically.</description>
              <bitOffset>24</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Send LIN Wake-up Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Send LIN Wake-up Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>LINWKEN</name>
              <description>LIN Wake-up Enable Bit\nNote 1: When the system is in Power-down mode, LIN wake-up event will wake up system from Power-down mode.</description>
              <bitOffset>28</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>LIN wake-up system function Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>LIN wake-up system function Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>LINWKF</name>
              <description>LIN Wake-up Flag\nThis bit is set if chip wake-up from power-down state by LIN wake-up.\nNote 1: If LINWKEN (UART_LINWKCTL[28]) is enabled, the LIN wake-up event will cause this bit set to '1'.\nNote 2: This bit can be cleared by writing '1' to it.</description>
              <bitOffset>29</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Chip stays in power-down state</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Chip wake-up from power-down state by LIN wake-up</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
      </registers>
    </peripheral>
    <peripheral>
      <name>UART9</name>
      <description>UART Register Map</description>
      <groupName>UART</groupName>
      <baseAddress>0x40079000</baseAddress>
      <!-- d -> no access permission -->
      <addressBlock>
        <offset>0x0</offset>
        <size>0x58</size>
        <usage>registers</usage>
      </addressBlock>
      <registers>
       <register>
          <name>UARTx_DAT</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>UARTx_DAT</displayName>
          <description>UART Receive/Transmit Buffer Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x0</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0x00000000</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>DAT</name>
              <description>Data Receive/Transmit Buffer\nWrite Operation:\nBy writing one byte to this register, the data byte will be stored in transmitter FIFO. The UART controller will send out the data stored in transmitter FIFO top location through the UART_TXD.\nRead Operation:\nBy reading this register, the UART controller will return an 8-bit data received from receiver FIFO.</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>8</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>PARITY</name>
              <description>PARITY Bit Receive/Transmit Buffer\nWrite Operation:\nBy writing to this bit, the PARITY bit will be stored in transmitter FIFO. If PBE (UART_LINE[3]) and PSS (UART_LINE[7]) are set, the UART controller will send out this bit follow the DAT (UART_DAT[7:0]) through the UART_TXD.\nRead Operation:\nIf PBE (UART_LINE[3]) and PSS (UART_LINE[7]) are enabled, the PARITY bit can be read by this bit.\nNote: This bit has effect only when PBE (UART_LINE[3]) and PSS (UART_LINE[7]) are set.</description>
              <bitOffset>8</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>UARTx_INTEN</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>UARTx_INTEN</displayName>
          <description>UART Interrupt Enable Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x4</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>RDAIEN</name>
              <description>Receive Data Available Interrupt Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Receive data available interrupt Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Receive data available interrupt Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>THREIEN</name>
              <description>Transmit Holding Register Empty Interrupt Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>1</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Transmit holding register empty interrupt Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Transmit holding register empty interrupt Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RLSIEN</name>
              <description>Receive Line Status Interrupt Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>2</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Receive Line Status interrupt Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Receive Line Status interrupt Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>MODEMIEN</name>
              <description>Modem Status Interrupt Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>3</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Modem status interrupt Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Modem status interrupt Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RXTOIEN</name>
              <description>RX Time-out Interrupt Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>4</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>RX time-out interrupt Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>RX time-out interrupt Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BUFERRIEN</name>
              <description>Buffer Error Interrupt Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>5</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Buffer error interrupt Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Buffer error interrupt Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>WKIEN</name>
              <description>Wake-up Interrupt Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>6</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Wake-up Interrupt Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Wake-up Interrupt Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>LINIEN</name>
              <description>LIN Bus Interrupt Enable Bit\nNote: This bit is used for LIN function mode.</description>
              <bitOffset>8</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>LIN bus interrupt Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>LIN bus interrupt Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TOCNTEN</name>
              <description>Receive Buffer Time-out Counter Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>11</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Receive Buffer Time-out counter Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Receive Buffer Time-out counter Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ATORTSEN</name>
              <description>nRTS Auto-flow Control Enable Bit\nNote: When nRTS auto-flow is enabled, if the number of bytes in the RX FIFO equals the RTSTRGLV (UART_FIFO[19:16]), the UART will de-assert nRTS signal.</description>
              <bitOffset>12</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>nRTS auto-flow control Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>nRTS auto-flow control Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ATOCTSEN</name>
              <description>nCTS Auto-flow Control Enable Bit\nNote: When nCTS auto-flow is enabled, the UART will send data to external device if nCTS input assert (UART will not send data to device until nCTS is asserted).</description>
              <bitOffset>13</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>nCTS auto-flow control Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>nCTS auto-flow control Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TXPDMAEN</name>
              <description>TX PDMA Enable Bit\nNote: If RLSIEN (UART_INTEN[2]) is enabled and HWRLSINT (UART_INTSTS[26]) is set to 1, the RLS (Receive Line Status) Interrupt is caused. If RLS interrupt is caused by Break Error Flag BIF(UART_FIFOSTS[6]), Frame Error Flag FEF(UART_FIFO[5]) or Parity Error Flag PEF(UART_FIFOSTS[4]), UART PDMA transmit request operation is stopped. Clear Break Error Flag BIF or Frame Error Flag FEF or Parity Error Flag PEF by writing '1' to corresponding BIF, FEF and PEF to make UART PDMA transmit request operation continue.</description>
              <bitOffset>14</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>TX PDMA Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>TX PDMA Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RXPDMAEN</name>
              <description>RX PDMA Enable Bit\nThis bit can enable or disable RX PDMA service.\nNote: If RLSIEN (UART_INTEN[2]) is enabled and HWRLSINT (UART_INTSTS[26]) is set to 1, the RLS (Receive Line Status) Interrupt is caused. If RLS interrupt is caused by Break Error Flag BIF(UART_FIFOSTS[6]), Frame Error Flag FEF(UART_FIFO[5]) or Parity Error Flag PEF(UART_FIFOSTS[4]), UART PDMA receive request operation is stopped. Clear Break Error Flag BIF or Frame Error Flag FEF or Parity Error Flag PEF by writing '1' to corresponding BIF, FEF and PEF to make UART PDMA receive request operation continue.</description>
              <bitOffset>15</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>RX PDMA Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>RX PDMA Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SWBEIEN</name>
              <description>Single-wire Bit Error Detection Interrupt Enable Bit\nSet this bit, the Single-wire Half Duplex Bit Error Detection Interrupt SWBEINT(UART_INTSTS[24]) is generated when Single-wire Bit Error Detection SWBEIF(UART_INTSTS[16]) is set.\nNote: This bit is valid when FUNCSEL (UART_FUNCSEL[2:0]) is select UART Single-wire mode.</description>
              <bitOffset>16</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Single-wire Bit Error Detect Interrupt Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Single-wire Bit Error Detect Interrupt Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ABRIEN</name>
              <description>Auto-baud Rate Interrupt Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>18</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Auto-baud rate interrupt Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Auto-baud rate interrupt Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TXENDIEN</name>
              <description>Transmitter Empty Interrupt Enable Bit\nIf TXENDIEN (UART_INTEN[22]) is enabled, the Transmitter Empty interrupt TXENDINT (UART_INTSTS[30]) will be generated when TXENDIF (UART_INTSTS[22]) is set (TX FIFO (UART_DAT) is empty and the STOP bit of the last byte has been transmitted).</description>
              <bitOffset>22</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Transmitter empty interrupt Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Transmitter empty interrupt Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>UARTx_FIFO</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>UARTx_FIFO</displayName>
          <description>UART FIFO Control Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x8</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000101</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>RXRST</name>
              <description>RX Field Software Reset\nWhen RXRST (UART_FIFO[1]) is set, all the byte in the receiver FIFO and RX internal state machine are cleared.\nNote 1: This bit will automatically clear at least 3 UART peripheral clock cycles.\nNote 2: Before setting this bit, it should wait for the RXIDLE (UART_FIFOSTS[29]) be set.</description>
              <bitOffset>1</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No effect</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Reset the RX internal state machine and pointers</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TXRST</name>
              <description>TX Field Software Reset\nWhen TXRST (UART_FIFO[2]) is set, all the byte in the transmit FIFO and TX internal state machine are cleared.\nNote 1: This bit will automatically clear at least 3 UART peripheral clock cycles.\nNote 2: Before setting this bit, it should wait for the TXEMPTYF (UART_FIFOSTS[28]) be set.</description>
              <bitOffset>2</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No effect</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Reset the TX internal state machine and pointers</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RFITL</name>
              <description>RX FIFO Interrupt Trigger Level\nWhen the number of bytes in the receive FIFO equals the RFITL, the RDAIF (UART_INTSTS[0]) will be set (if RDAIEN (UART_INTEN[0]) enabled, and an interrupt will be generated).</description>
              <bitOffset>4</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>4</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>RX FIFO Interrupt Trigger Level is 1 byte</description>
                    <value>#0000</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>RX FIFO Interrupt Trigger Level is 4 bytes</description>
                    <value>#0001</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>2</name>
                    <description>RX FIFO Interrupt Trigger Level is 8 bytes</description>
                    <value>#0010</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>3</name>
                    <description>RX FIFO Interrupt Trigger Level is 14 bytes</description>
                    <value>#0011</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RXOFF</name>
              <description>Receiver Disable Bit\nThe receiver is disabled or not (set 1 to disable receiver).\nNote: This bit is used for RS-485 Normal Multi-drop mode. It should be programmed before RS485NMM (UART_ALTCTL[8]) is programmed.</description>
              <bitOffset>8</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Receiver Enabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Receiver Disabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RTSTRGLV</name>
              <description>nRTS Trigger Level for Auto-flow Control\nNote: This field is used for auto nRTS flow control.</description>
              <bitOffset>16</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>4</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>nRTS Trigger Level is 1 byte</description>
                    <value>#0000</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>nRTS Trigger Level is 4 bytes</description>
                    <value>#0001</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>2</name>
                    <description>nRTS Trigger Level is 8 bytes</description>
                    <value>#0010</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>3</name>
                    <description>nRTS Trigger Level is 14 bytes</description>
                    <value>#0011</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>UARTx_LINE</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>UARTx_LINE</displayName>
          <description>UART Line Control Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0xC</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>WLS</name>
              <description>Word Length Selection\nThis field sets UART word length.</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>2</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>5 bits</description>
                    <value>#00</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>6 bits</description>
                    <value>#01</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>2</name>
                    <description>7 bits</description>
                    <value>#10</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>3</name>
                    <description>8 bits</description>
                    <value>#11</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>NSB</name>
              <description>Number of 'STOP Bit'</description>
              <bitOffset>2</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>One 'STOP bit' is generated in the transmitted data</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>When select 5-bit word length, 1.5 'STOP bit' is generated in the transmitted data. When select 6-, 7- and 8-bit word length, 2 'STOP bit' is generated in the transmitted data</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>PBE</name>
              <description>PARITY Bit Enable Bit\nNote: PARITY bit is generated on each outgoing character and is checked on each incoming data.</description>
              <bitOffset>3</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>PARITY bit generated Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>PARITY bit generated Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>EPE</name>
              <description>Even Parity Enable Bit\nNote: This bit has effect only when PBE (UART_LINE[3]) is set.</description>
              <bitOffset>4</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Odd number of logic 1's is transmitted and checked in each word</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Even number of logic 1's is transmitted and checked in each word</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SPE</name>
              <description>Stick Parity Enable Bit\nNote: If PBE (UART_LINE[3]) and EPE (UART_LINE[4]) are logic 1, the PARITY bit is transmitted and checked as logic 0. If PBE (UART_LINE[3]) is 1 and EPE (UART_LINE[4]) is 0 then the PARITY bit is transmitted and checked as 1.</description>
              <bitOffset>5</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Stick parity Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Stick parity Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BCB</name>
              <description>Break Control Bit\nNote: When this bit is set to logic 1, the transmitted serial data output (TX) is forced to the Spacing State (logic 0). This bit acts only on TX line and has no effect on the transmitter logic.</description>
              <bitOffset>6</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Break Control Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Break Control Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>PSS</name>
              <description>PARITY Bit Source Selection\nThe PARITY bit can be selected to be generated and checked automatically or by software.\nNote 1: This bit has effect only when PBE (UART_LINE[3]) is set.\nNote 2: If PSS is 0, the PARITY bit is transmitted and checked automatically. If PSS is 1, the transmitted PARITY bit value can be determined by writing PARITY (UART_DAT[8]) and the PARITY bit can be read by reading PARITY (UART_DAT[8]).</description>
              <bitOffset>7</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>PARITY bit is generated by EPE (UART_LINE[4]) and SPE (UART_LINE[5]) setting and checked automatically</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>PARITY bit generated and checked by software</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TXDINV</name>
              <description>TX Data Inverted\nNote 1: Before setting this bit, TXRXDIS (UART_FUNCSEL[3]) should be set then waited for TXRXACT (UART_FIFOSTS[31]) is cleared. When the configuration is done, cleared TXRXDIS (UART_FUNCSEL[3]) to activate UART controller.\nNote 2: This bit is valid when FUNCSEL (UART_FUNCSEL[2:0]) is select UART, LIN or RS485 function.</description>
              <bitOffset>8</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Transmitted data signal inverted Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Transmitted data signal inverted Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RXDINV</name>
              <description>RX Data Inverted\nNote 1: Before setting this bit, TXRXDIS (UART_FUNCSEL[3]) should be set then waited for TXRXACT (UART_FIFOSTS[31]) is cleared. When the configuration is done, cleared TXRXDIS (UART_FUNCSEL[3]) to activate UART controller.\nNote 2: This bit is valid when FUNCSEL (UART_FUNCSEL[2:0]) is select UART, LIN or RS485 function.</description>
              <bitOffset>9</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Received data signal inverted Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Received data signal inverted Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>UARTx_MODEM</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>UARTx_MODEM</displayName>
          <description>UART Modem Control Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x10</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000200</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>RTS</name>
              <description>nRTS Signal Control\nThis bit is direct control internal nRTS (Request-to-send) signal active or not, and then drive the nRTS pin output with RTSACTLV bit configuration.\nNote 1: The nRTS signal control bit is not effective when nRTS auto-flow control is enabled in UART function mode.\nNote 2: The nRTS signal control bit is not effective when RS-485 auto direction mode (AUD) is enabled in RS-485 function mode.\nNote 3: Single-wire mode is support this feature.</description>
              <bitOffset>1</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>nRTS signal is active</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>nRTS signal is inactive</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RTSACTLV</name>
              <description>nRTS Pin Active Level\nThis bit defines the active level state of nRTS pin output.\nNote 1: Refer to Figure 6.2113 and Figure 6.2114 for UART function mode.\nNote 2: Refer to Figure 6.2124 and Figure 6.2125 for RS-485 function mode.\nNote 3: Before setting this bit, TXRXDIS (UART_FUNCSEL[3]) should be set then waited for TXRXACT (UART_FIFOSTS[31]) is cleared. When the configuration is done, cleared TXRXDIS (UART_FUNCSEL[3]) to activate UART controller.</description>
              <bitOffset>9</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>nRTS pin output is high level active</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>nRTS pin output is low level active. (Default)</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RTSSTS</name>
              <description>nRTS Pin Status (Read Only)\nThis bit mirror from nRTS pin output of voltage logic status.</description>
              <bitOffset>13</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>nRTS pin output is low level voltage logic state</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>nRTS pin output is high level voltage logic state</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>UARTx_MODEMSTS</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>UARTx_MODEMSTS</displayName>
          <description>UART Modem Status Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x14</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000110</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>CTSDETF</name>
              <description>Detect nCTS State Change Flag\nThis bit is set whenever nCTS input has change state, and it will generate Modem interrupt to CPU when MODEMIEN (UART_INTEN[3]) is set to 1.\nNote: This bit can be cleared by writing '1' to it.</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>nCTS input has not change state</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>nCTS input has change state</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>CTSSTS</name>
              <description>nCTS Pin Status (Read Only)\nThis bit mirror from nCTS pin input of voltage logic status.\nNote: This bit echoes when UART controller peripheral clock is enabled, and nCTS multi-function port is selected.</description>
              <bitOffset>4</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>nCTS pin input is low level voltage logic state</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>nCTS pin input is high level voltage logic state</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>CTSACTLV</name>
              <description>nCTS Pin Active Level\nThis bit defines the active level state of nCTS pin input.\nNote: Before setting this bit, TXRXDIS (UART_FUNCSEL[3]) should be set then waited for TXRXACT (UART_FIFOSTS[31]) is cleared. When the configuration is done, cleared TXRXDIS (UART_FUNCSEL[3]) to activate UART controller.</description>
              <bitOffset>8</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>nCTS pin input is high level active</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>nCTS pin input is low level active. (Default)</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>UARTx_FIFOSTS</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>UARTx_FIFOSTS</displayName>
          <description>UART FIFO Status Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x18</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0xB0404000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>RXOVIF</name>
              <description>RX Overflow Error Interrupt Flag\nThis bit is set when RX FIFO overflow.\nIf the number of bytes of received data is greater than RX_FIFO (UART_DAT) size 16 bytes, this bit will be set.\nNote: This bit can be cleared by writing '1' to it.</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>RX FIFO is not overflow</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>RX FIFO is overflow</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ABRDIF</name>
              <description>Auto-baud Rate Detect Interrupt Flag\nThis bit is set to logic '1' when auto-baud rate detect function is finished.\nNote: This bit can be cleared by writing '1' to it.</description>
              <bitOffset>1</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Auto-baud rate detect function is not finished</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Auto-baud rate detect function is finished</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ABRDTOIF</name>
              <description>Auto-baud Rate Detect Time-out Interrupt Flag\nThis bit is set to logic '1' in Auto-baud Rate Detect mode when the baud rate counter is overflow.\nNote: This bit can be cleared by writing '1' to it.</description>
              <bitOffset>2</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Auto-baud rate counter is underflow</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Auto-baud rate counter is overflow</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ADDRDETF</name>
              <description>RS-485 Address Byte Detect Flag\nNote 1: This field is used for RS-485 function mode and ADDRDEN (UART_ALTCTL[15]) is set to 1 to enable Address detection mode.\nNote 2: This bit can be cleared by writing '1' to it.</description>
              <bitOffset>3</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Receiver detects a data that is not an address bit (bit 9 ='0')</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Receiver detects a data that is an address bit (bit 9 ='1')</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>PEF</name>
              <description>Parity Error Flag\nThis bit is set to logic 1 whenever the received character does not have a valid 'PARITY bit'.\nNote: This bit can be cleared by writing '1' to it.</description>
              <bitOffset>4</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No parity error is generated</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Parity error is generated</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>FEF</name>
              <description>Framing Error Flag\nThis bit is set to logic 1 whenever the received character does not have a valid 'STOP bit' (that is, the STOP bit following the last data bit or PARITY bit is detected as logic 0).\nNote: This bit can be cleared by writing '1' to it.</description>
              <bitOffset>5</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No framing error is generated</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Framing error is generated</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BIF</name>
              <description>Break Interrupt Flag\nThis bit is set to logic 1 whenever the received data input (RX) is held in the 'spacing state' (logic 0) for longer than a full word transmission time (that is, the total time of 'START bit' + data bits + parity + STOP bits).\nNote: This bit can be cleared by writing '1' to it.</description>
              <bitOffset>6</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No Break interrupt is generated</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Break interrupt is generated</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RXPTR</name>
              <description>RX FIFO Pointer (Read Only)\nThis field indicates the RX FIFO Buffer Pointer. When UART receives one byte from external device, RXPTR increases one. When one byte of RX FIFO is read by CPU, RXPTR decreases one.\nThe Maximum value shown in RXPTR is 15. When the using level of RX FIFO Buffer equal to 16, the RXFULL bit is set to 1 and RXPTR will show 0. As one byte of RX FIFO is read by CPU, the RXFULL bit is cleared to 0 and RXPTR will show 15.</description>
              <bitOffset>8</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>6</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RXEMPTY</name>
              <description>Receiver FIFO Empty (Read Only)\nThis bit initiate RX FIFO empty or not.\nNote: When the last byte of RX FIFO has been read by CPU, hardware sets this bit high. It will be cleared when UART receives any new data.</description>
              <bitOffset>14</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>RX FIFO is not empty</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>RX FIFO is empty</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RXFULL</name>
              <description>Receiver FIFO Full (Read Only)\nThis bit initiates RX FIFO full or not.\nNote: This bit is set when the number of usage in RX FIFO Buffer is equal to 16, otherwise it is cleared by hardware.</description>
              <bitOffset>15</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>RX FIFO is not full</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>RX FIFO is full</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TXPTR</name>
              <description>TX FIFO Pointer (Read Only)\nThis field indicates the TX FIFO Buffer Pointer. When CPU writes one byte into UART_DAT, TXPTR increases one. When one byte of TX FIFO is transferred to Transmitter Shift Register, TXPTR decreases one.\nThe Maximum value shown in TXPTR is 15. When the using level of TX FIFO Buffer equal to 16, the TXFULL bit is set to 1 and TXPTR will show 0. As one byte of TX FIFO is transferred to Transmitter Shift Register, the TXFULL bit is cleared to 0 and TXPTR will show 15.</description>
              <bitOffset>16</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>6</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TXEMPTY</name>
              <description>Transmitter FIFO Empty (Read Only)\nThis bit indicates TX FIFO empty or not.\nNote: When the last byte of TX FIFO has been transferred to Transmitter Shift Register, hardware sets this bit high. It will be cleared when writing data into UART_DAT (TX FIFO not empty).</description>
              <bitOffset>22</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>TX FIFO is not empty</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>TX FIFO is empty</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TXFULL</name>
              <description>Transmitter FIFO Full (Read Only)\nThis bit indicates TX FIFO full or not.\nNote: This bit is set when the number of usage in TX FIFO Buffer is equal to 16, otherwise it is cleared by hardware.</description>
              <bitOffset>23</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>TX FIFO is not full</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>TX FIFO is full</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TXOVIF</name>
              <description>TX Overflow Error Interrupt Flag\nIf TX FIFO (UART_DAT) is full, an additional write to UART_DAT will cause this bit to logic 1.\nNote: This bit can be cleared by writing '1' to it.</description>
              <bitOffset>24</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>TX FIFO is not overflow</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>TX FIFO is overflow</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TXEMPTYF</name>
              <description>Transmitter Empty Flag (Read Only)\nThis bit is set by hardware when TX FIFO (UART_DAT) is empty and the STOP bit of the last byte has been transmitted.\nNote: This bit is cleared automatically when TX FIFO is not empty or the last byte transmission has not completed.</description>
              <bitOffset>28</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>TX FIFO is not empty or the STOP bit of the last byte has been not transmitted</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>TX FIFO is empty and the STOP bit of the last byte has been transmitted</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RXIDLE</name>
              <description>RX Idle Status (Read Only)\nThis bit is set by hardware when RX is idle.</description>
              <bitOffset>29</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>RX is busy</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>RX is idle. (Default)</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TXRXACT</name>
              <description>TX and RX Active Status (Read Only)\nThis bit indicates TX and RX are active or inactive.\nNote: When TXRXDIS (UART_FUNCSEL[3]) is set and both TX and RX are in idle state, this bit is cleared. The UART controller cannot transmit or receive data at this moment. Otherwise this bit is set.</description>
              <bitOffset>31</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>TX and RX are inactive</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>TX and RX are active. (Default)</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>UARTx_INTSTS</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>UARTx_INTSTS</displayName>
          <description>UART Interrupt Status Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x1C</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00400002</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>RDAIF</name>
              <description>Receive Data Available Interrupt Flag (Read Only)\nWhen the number of bytes in the RX FIFO equals the RFITL then the RDAIF(UART_INTSTS[0]) will be set. If RDAIEN (UART_INTEN[0]) is enabled, the RDA interrupt will be generated.\nNote: This bit is read only and will be cleared when the number of unread bytes of RX FIFO drops below the threshold level (RFITL(UART_FIFO[7:4]).</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No RDA interrupt flag is generated</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>RDA interrupt flag is generated</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>THREIF</name>
              <description>Transmit Holding Register Empty Interrupt Flag (Read Only)\nThis bit is set when the last data of TX FIFO is transferred to Transmitter Shift Register. If THREIEN (UART_INTEN[1]) is enabled, the THRE interrupt will be generated.\nNote: This bit is read only and will be cleared when writing data into UART_DAT (TX FIFO is not empty).</description>
              <bitOffset>1</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No THRE interrupt flag is generated</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>THRE interrupt flag is generated</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RLSIF</name>
              <description>Receive Line Interrupt Flag (Read Only)\nThis bit is set when the RX receive data have parity error, frame error or break error (at least one of 3 bits, BIF(UART_FIFOSTS[6]), FEF(UART_FIFOSTS[5]) and PEF(UART_FIFOSTS[4]), is set). If RLSIEN (UART_INTEN[2]) is enabled, the RLS interrupt will be generated.\nNote 2: This bit is read only and reset to 0 when all bits of BIF (UART_FIFOSTS[6]), FEF(UART_FIFOSTS[5]) and PEF(UART_FIFOSTS[4]) are cleared.\nNote 3: In RS-485 function mode, this bit is read only and reset to 0 when all bits of BIF (UART_FIFOSTS[6]), FEF(UART_FIFOSTS[5]), PEF(UART_FIFOSTS[4]) and ADDRDETF (UART_FIFOSTS[3]) are cleared.\nNote 4: This bit is valid only when RXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[15]) and TXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[14]) are both 0.</description>
              <bitOffset>2</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No RLS interrupt flag is generated</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>RLS interrupt flag is generated</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>MODEMIF</name>
              <description>MODEM Interrupt Flag (Read Only)\nNote 1: This bit is read only and reset to 0 when bit CTSDETF is cleared by a write 1 on CTSDETF(UART_MODEMSTS[0]).\nNote 2: This bit is valid only when RXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[15]) and TXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[14]) are both 0.</description>
              <bitOffset>3</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No Modem interrupt flag is generated</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Modem interrupt flag is generated</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RXTOIF</name>
              <description>RX Time-out Interrupt Flag (Read Only)\nThis bit is set when the RX FIFO is not empty and no activities occurred in the RX FIFO and the time-out counter equal to TOIC (UART_TOUT[7:0]). If RXTOIEN (UART_INTEN[4]) is enabled, the RX time-out interrupt will be generated.\nNote 1: This bit can be cleared by writing '1' to it.\nNote 2: If BITOMEN (UART_TOUT[31]) is reset to 0, user can read UART_DAT (RX is in active) to clear this bit.\nNote 3: This bit is valid only when RXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[15]) and TXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[14]) are both 0.</description>
              <bitOffset>4</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No RX time-out interrupt flag is generated</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>RX time-out interrupt flag is generated</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BUFERRIF</name>
              <description>Buffer Error Interrupt Flag (Read Only)\nThis bit is set when the TX FIFO or RX FIFO overflows (TXOVIF (UART_FIFOSTS[24]) or RXOVIF (UART_FIFOSTS[0]) is set). When BUFERRIF (UART_INTSTS[5]) is set, the transfer is not correct. If BUFERRIEN (UART_INTEN[5]) is enabled, the buffer error interrupt will be generated.\nNote 1: This bit is cleared if both of RXOVIF(UART_FIFOSTS[0]) and TXOVIF(UART_FIFOSTS[24]) are cleared to 0 by writing 1 to RXOVIF(UART_FIFOSTS[0]) and TXOVIF(UART_FIFOSTS[24]).\nNote 2: This bit is valid only when RXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[15]) and TXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[14]) are both 0.</description>
              <bitOffset>5</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No buffer error interrupt flag is generated</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Buffer error interrupt flag is generated</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>WKIF</name>
              <description>UART Wake-up Interrupt Flag (Read Only)\nThis bit is set when LINWKF (UART_LINWKCTL[29]), TOUTWKF (UART_WKSTS[4]), RS485WKF (UART_WKSTS[3]), RFRTWKF (UART_WKSTS[2]), DATWKF (UART_WKSTS[1]) or CTSWKF(UART_WKSTS[0]) is set to 1.\nNote: This bit is cleared if all of LINWKF, TOUTWKF, RS485WKF, RFRTWKF, DATWKF and CTSWKF are cleared to 0 by writing 1 to the corresponding interrupt flag.</description>
              <bitOffset>6</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No UART wake-up interrupt flag is generated</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>UART wake-up interrupt flag is generated</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>LINIF</name>
              <description>LIN Bus Interrupt Flag\nNote: This bit is cleared when SLVHDETF (UART_LINSTS[0]), SLVHEF (UART_LINSTS[1]), SLVIDPEF (UART_LINSTS[2]), SLVHTOF (UART_LINSTS[4]), RTOUTF (UART_LINSTS[5]), BRKDETF (UART_LINSTS[8]), and BITEF (UART_LINSTS[9]) all are cleared and software writing '1' to LINIF (UART_INTSTS[7]).</description>
              <bitOffset>7</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>None of SLVHDETF, SLVHEF, SLVIDPEF, SLVHTOF, RTOUTF, BITEF, and BRKDETF is generated</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>At least one of SLVHDETF, SLVHEF, SLVIDPEF, SLVHTOF, RTOUTF, BITEF, and BRKDETF is generated</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RDAINT</name>
              <description>Receive Data Available Interrupt Indicator (Read Only)\nThis bit is set if RDAIEN (UART_INTEN[0]) and RDAIF (UART_INTSTS[0]) are both set to 1.</description>
              <bitOffset>8</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No RDA interrupt is generated</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>RDA interrupt is generated</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>THREINT</name>
              <description>Transmit Holding Register Empty Interrupt Indicator (Read Only)\nThis bit is set if THREIEN (UART_INTEN[1]) and THREIF(UART_INTSTS[1]) are both set to 1.</description>
              <bitOffset>9</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No THRE interrupt is generated</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>THRE interrupt is generated</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RLSINT</name>
              <description>Receive Line Status Interrupt Indicator (Read Only) \nThis bit is set if RLSIEN (UART_INTEN[2]) and RLSIF(UART_INTSTS[2]) are both set to 1.\nNote: This bit is valid only when RXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[15]) and TXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[14]) are both 0.</description>
              <bitOffset>10</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No RLS interrupt is generated</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>RLS interrupt is generated</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>MODEMINT</name>
              <description>MODEM Status Interrupt Indicator (Read Only)\nThis bit is set if MODEMIEN(UART_INTEN[3]) and MODEMIF(UART_INTSTS[3]) are both set to 1\nNote: This bit is valid only when RXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[15]) and TXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[14]) are both 0.</description>
              <bitOffset>11</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No Modem interrupt is generated</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Modem interrupt is generated</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RXTOINT</name>
              <description>RX Time-out Interrupt Indicator (Read Only)\nThis bit is set if RXTOIEN (UART_INTEN[4]) and RXTOIF(UART_INTSTS[4]) are both set to 1.\nNote: This bit is valid only when RXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[15]) and TXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[14]) are both 0.</description>
              <bitOffset>12</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No RX time-out interrupt is generated</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>RX time-out interrupt is generated</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BUFERRINT</name>
              <description>Buffer Error Interrupt Indicator (Read Only)\nThis bit is set if BUFERRIEN(UART_INTEN[5]) and BUFERRIF(UART_ INTSTS[5]) are both set to 1.\nNote: This bit is valid only when RXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[15]) and TXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[14]) are both 0.</description>
              <bitOffset>13</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No buffer error interrupt is generated</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Buffer error interrupt is generated</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>WKINT</name>
              <description>UART Wake-up Interrupt Indicator (Read Only)\nThis bit is set if WKIEN (UART_INTEN[6]) and WKIF (UART_INTSTS[6]) are both set to 1.</description>
              <bitOffset>14</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No UART wake-up interrupt is generated</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>UART wake-up interrupt is generated</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>LININT</name>
              <description>LIN Bus Interrupt Indicator (Read Only)\nThis bit is set if LINIEN (UART_INTEN[8]) and LINIF(UART_INTSTS[7]) are both set to 1.</description>
              <bitOffset>15</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No LIN Bus interrupt is generated</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>The LIN Bus interrupt is generated</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SWBEIF</name>
              <description>Single-wire Bit Error Detection Interrupt Flag\nThis bit is set when the single wire bus state not equals to UART controller TX state in Single-wire mode.\nNote 1: This bit is active when FUNCSEL (UART_FUNCSEL[2:0]) is select UART Single-wire mode.\nNote 2: This bit can be cleared by writing '1' to it.</description>
              <bitOffset>16</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No single-wire bit error detection interrupt flag is generated</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Single-wire bit error detection interrupt flag is generated</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>HWRLSIF</name>
              <description>PDMA Mode Receive Line Status Flag (Read Only)\nThis bit is set when the RX receive data have parity error, frame error or break error (at least one of 3 bits, BIF (UART_FIFOSTS[6]), FEF (UART_FIFOSTS[5]) and PEF (UART_FIFOSTS[4]) is set). If RLSIEN (UART_INTEN[2]) is enabled, the RLS interrupt will be generated.\nNote 2: In UART function mode, this bit is read only and reset to 0 when all bits of BIF(UART_FIFOSTS[6]), FEF(UART_FIFOSTS[5]) and PEF(UART_FIFOSTS[4]) are cleared. \nNote 3: In RS-485 function mode, this bit is read only and reset to 0 when all bits of BIF(UART_FIFOSTS[6]), FEF(UART_FIFOSTS[5]), PEF(UART_FIFOSTS[4]) and ADDRDETF (UART_FIFOSTS[3]) are cleared.</description>
              <bitOffset>18</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No RLS interrupt flag is generated when RXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[15]) or TXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[14]) is 1</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>RLS interrupt flag is generated when RXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[15]) or TXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[14]) is 1</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>HWMODIF</name>
              <description>PDMA Mode MODEM Interrupt Flag (Read Only)\nNote: This bit is read only and reset to 0 when the bit CTSDETF (UART_MODEMSTS[0]) is cleared by writing 1 on CTSDETF (UART_MODEMSTS[0]).</description>
              <bitOffset>19</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No Modem interrupt flag is generated when RXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[15]) or TXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[14]) is 1</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Modem interrupt flag is generated when RXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[15]) or TXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[14]) is 1</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>HWTOIF</name>
              <description>PDMA Mode RX Time-out Interrupt Flag (Read Only)\nThis bit is set when the RX FIFO is not empty and no activities occurred in the RX FIFO and the time-out counter equal to TOIC (UART_TOUT[7:0]). If RXTOIEN (UART_INTEN[4]) is enabled, the RX time-out interrupt will be generated. \nNote: This bit is read only and user can read UART_DAT (RX is in active) to clear it.</description>
              <bitOffset>20</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No RX time-out interrupt flag is generated when RXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[15]) or TXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[14]) is 1</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>RX time-out interrupt flag is generated when RXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[15]) or TXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[14]) is 1</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>HWBUFEIF</name>
              <description>PDMA Mode Buffer Error Interrupt Flag (Read Only)\nThis bit is set when the TX or RX FIFO overflows (TXOVIF (UART_FIFOSTS[24]) or RXOVIF (UART_FIFOSTS[0]) is set). When BUFERRIF (UART_INTSTS[5]) is set, the transfer maybe is not correct. If BUFERRIEN (UART_INTEN[5]) is enabled, the buffer error interrupt will be generated.\nNote: This bit is cleared when both TXOVIF (UART_FIFOSTS[24]]) and RXOVIF  (UART_FIFOSTS[0]) are cleared.</description>
              <bitOffset>21</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No buffer error interrupt flag is generated when RXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[15]) or TXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[14]) is 1</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Buffer error interrupt flag is generated when RXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[15]) or TXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[14]) is 1</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TXENDIF</name>
              <description>Transmitter Empty Interrupt Flag (Read Only)\nThis bit is set when TX FIFO (UART_DAT) is empty and the STOP bit of the last byte has been transmitted (TXEMPTYF (UART_FIFOSTS[28]) is set). If TXENDIEN (UART_INTEN[22]) is enabled, the Transmitter Empty interrupt will be generated.\nNote: This bit is cleared automatically when TX FIFO is not empty or the last byte transmission has not completed.</description>
              <bitOffset>22</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No transmitter empty interrupt flag is generated</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Transmitter empty interrupt flag is generated</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SWBEINT</name>
              <description>Single-wire Bit Error Detect Interrupt Indicator (Read Only)\nThis bit is set if SWBEIEN (UART_INTEN[16]) and SWBEIF (UART_INTSTS[16]) are both set to 1.</description>
              <bitOffset>24</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No Single-wire Bit Error Detection Interrupt generated</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Single-wire Bit Error Detection Interrupt generated</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>HWRLSINT</name>
              <description>PDMA Mode Receive Line Status Interrupt Indicator (Read Only)\nThis bit is set if RLSIEN (UART_INTEN[2]) and HWRLSIF(UART_INTSTS[18]) are both set to 1.</description>
              <bitOffset>26</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No RLS interrupt is generated when RXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[15]) or TXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[14]) is 1</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>RLS interrupt is generated when RXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[15]) or TXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[14]) is 1</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>HWMODINT</name>
              <description>PDMA Mode MODEM Status Interrupt Indicator (Read Only)\nThis bit is set if MODEMIEN (UART_INTEN[3]) and HWMODIF(UART_INTSTS[19]) are both set to 1.</description>
              <bitOffset>27</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No Modem interrupt is generated when RXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[15]) or TXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[14]) is 1</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Modem interrupt is generated when RXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[15]) or TXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[14]) is 1</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>HWTOINT</name>
              <description>PDMA Mode RX Time-out Interrupt Indicator (Read Only)\nThis bit is set if RXTOIEN (UART_INTEN[4]) and HWTOIF(UART_INTSTS[20]) are both set to 1.</description>
              <bitOffset>28</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No RX time-out interrupt is generated when RXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[15]) or TXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[14]) is 1</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>RX time-out interrupt is generated when RXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[15]) or TXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[14]) is 1</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>HWBUFEINT</name>
              <description>PDMA Mode Buffer Error Interrupt Indicator (Read Only)\nThis bit is set if BUFERRIEN (UART_INTEN[5]) and HWBUFEIF (UART_INTSTS[21]) are both set to 1.</description>
              <bitOffset>29</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No buffer error interrupt is generated when RXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[15]) or TXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[14]) is 1</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Buffer error interrupt is generated when RXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[15]) or TXPDMAEN (UART_INTEN[14]) is 1</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TXENDINT</name>
              <description>Transmitter Empty Interrupt Indicator (Read Only) \nThis bit is set if TXENDIEN (UART_INTEN[22]) and TXENDIF(UART_INTSTS[22]) are both set to 1.</description>
              <bitOffset>30</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No Transmitter Empty interrupt is generated</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Transmitter Empty interrupt is generated</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ABRINT</name>
              <description>Auto-baud Rate Interrupt Indicator (Read Only)\nThis bit is set if ABRIEN (UART_INTEN[18]) and ABRIF (UART_ALTCTL[17]) are both set to 1.</description>
              <bitOffset>31</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No Auto-baud Rate interrupt is generated</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>The Auto-baud Rate interrupt is generated</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>UARTx_TOUT</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>UARTx_TOUT</displayName>
          <description>UART Time-out Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x20</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>TOIC</name>
              <description>Time-out Interrupt Comparator</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>8</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>DLY</name>
              <description>TX Delay Time Value \nThis field is used to program the transfer delay time between the last STOP bit and next START bit. The unit is bit time.</description>
              <bitOffset>8</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>8</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BITOMEN</name>
              <description>Bus Idle Time-out Mode Enable Bit\nIf BITOMEN (UART_TOUT[31]) is enabled, the reset conditions of the time-out counter and RXTOIF (UART_INTSTS[4]) will be changed to detect the bus idle.\nWhen BITOMEN (UART_TOUT[31]) is disabled, the time-out counter and RXTOIF (UART_INTSTS[4]) maintain reset value whenever the RX FIFO is empty. In addition, Any RX FIFO activities, such as a new incoming data or user reading data, will clear RXTOIF (UART_INTSTS[4]) and reset RX time-out counter.. \nOn the other hand, when BITOMEN (UART_TOUT[31]) is enabled, the RX FIFO empty state will not reset the time-out counter and RXTOIF (UART_INTSTS[4]), and RX FIFO activities, such as a new incoming data or user reading data, will not clear RXTOIF (UART_INTSTS[4]). However, the time-out counter needs to receive a first incoming data before it can start counting. After receiving the first incoming data, the time-out counter will count even if the RX FIFO is empty.</description>
              <bitOffset>31</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Bus idle time-out mode Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Bus idle time-out mode Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>UARTx_BAUD</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>UARTx_BAUD</displayName>
          <description>UART Baud Rate Divider Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x24</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x0F0000FF</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>BRD</name>
              <description>Baud Rate Divider\nThe field indicates the baud rate divider. This filed is used in baud rate calculation. The detail description is shown in Table 6.214.</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>16</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BRFD</name>
              <description>Baud Rate Fractional Divider\nThis field is the fractional part of the baud rate divisor.  \nWhen BRFDEN (UART_BAUD[30]) is set, the Baud Rate Equation goes to UART_CLK / ((BRD+2) + (BRFD/256)). </description>
              <bitOffset>16</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>8</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>EDIVM1</name>
              <description>Extra Divider for BAUD Rate Mode 1\nThis field is used for baud rate calculation in mode 1 and has no effect for baud rate calculation in mode 0 and mode 2. The detail description is shown in Table 6.214.</description>
              <bitOffset>24</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>4</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BAUDM0</name>
              <description>BAUD Rate Mode Selection Bit 0\nThis bit is baud rate mode selection bit 0. UART provides three baud rate calculation modes. This bit combines with BAUDM1 (UART_BAUD[29]) to select baud rate calculation mode. The detail description is shown in Table 6.214.</description>
              <bitOffset>28</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BAUDM1</name>
              <description>BAUD Rate Mode Selection Bit 1\nThis bit is baud rate mode selection bit 1. UART provides three baud rate calculation modes. This bit combines with BAUDM0 (UART_BAUD[28]) to select baud rate calculation mode. The detail description is shown in Table 6.214.\nNote: In IrDA mode must be operated in mode 0.</description>
              <bitOffset>29</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BRFDEN</name>
              <description>Baud Rate Fractional Divider Enable Bit\nNote: This bit has effect only at baud rate mode 2 (BAUDM1 (UART_BAUD[29]) and BAUDM0 (UART_BAUD[28]) must be 1).</description>
              <bitOffset>30</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Baud Rate Fractional Divider Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Baud Rate Fractional Divider Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>UARTx_IRDA</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>UARTx_IRDA</displayName>
          <description>UART IrDA Control Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x28</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000040</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>TXEN</name>
              <description>IrDA Receiver/Transmitter Selection Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>1</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>IrDA Transmitter Disabled and Receiver Enabled. (Default)</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>IrDA Transmitter Enabled and Receiver Disabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TXINV</name>
              <description>IrDA Inverse Transmitting Output Signal \nNote 1: Before setting this bit, TXRXDIS (UART_FUNCSEL[3]) should be set then waited for TXRXACT (UART_FIFOSTS[31]) is cleared. When the configuration is done, cleared TXRXDIS (UART_FUNCSEL[3]) to activate UART controller.\nNote 2: This bit is valid when FUNCSEL (UART_FUNCSEL[2:0]) is select IrDA function.</description>
              <bitOffset>5</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>None inverse transmitting signal. (Default)</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Inverse transmitting output signal</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RXINV</name>
              <description>IrDA Inverse Receive Input Signal \nNote 1: Before setting this bit, TXRXDIS (UART_FUNCSEL[3]) should be set then waited for TXRXACT (UART_FIFOSTS[31]) is cleared. When the configuration is done, cleared TXRXDIS (UART_FUNCSEL[3]) to activate UART controller.\nNote 2: This bit is valid when FUNCSEL (UART_FUNCSEL[2:0]) is select IrDA function.</description>
              <bitOffset>6</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>None inverse receiving input signal</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Inverse receiving input signal. (Default)</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>UARTx_ALTCTL</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>UARTx_ALTCTL</displayName>
          <description>UART Alternate Control/Status Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x2C</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x0000000C</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>BRKFL</name>
              <description>UART LIN Break Field Length\nThis field indicates a 4-bit LIN TX break field count.\nNote 1: This break field length is BRKFL + 1.</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>4</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>LINRXEN</name>
              <description>LIN RX Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>6</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>LIN RX mode Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>LIN RX mode Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>LINTXEN</name>
              <description>LIN TX Break Mode Enable Bit\nNote: When TX break field transfer operation finished, this bit will be cleared automatically.</description>
              <bitOffset>7</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>LIN TX Break mode Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>LIN TX Break mode Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RS485NMM</name>
              <description>RS-485 Normal Multi-drop Operation Mode\nNote: It cannot be active with RS-485_AAD operation mode.</description>
              <bitOffset>8</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>RS-485 Normal Multi-drop Operation mode (NMM) Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>RS-485 Normal Multi-drop Operation mode (NMM) Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RS485AAD</name>
              <description>RS-485 Auto Address Detection Operation Mode\nNote: It cannot be active with RS-485_NMM operation mode.</description>
              <bitOffset>9</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>RS-485 Auto Address Detection Operation mode (AAD) Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>RS-485 Auto Address Detection Operation mode (AAD) Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RS485AUD</name>
              <description>RS-485 Auto Direction Function\nNote: It can be active with RS-485_AAD or RS-485_NMM operation mode.</description>
              <bitOffset>10</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>RS-485 Auto Direction Operation function (AUD) Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>RS-485 Auto Direction Operation function (AUD) Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ADDRDEN</name>
              <description>RS-485 Address Detection Enable Bit\nThis bit is used to enable RS-485 Address Detection mode. \nNote: This bit is used for RS-485 any operation mode.</description>
              <bitOffset>15</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Address detection mode Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Address detection mode Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ABRIF</name>
              <description>Auto-baud Rate Interrupt Flag (Read Only) \nThis bit is set when auto-baud rate detection function finished or the auto-baud rate counter was overflow and if ABRIEN(UART_INTEN[18]) is set then the auto-baud rate interrupt will be generated. \nNote: This bit is read only, but it can be cleared by writing '1' to ABRDTOIF (UART_FIFOSTS[2]) and ABRDIF(UART_FIFOSTS[1]).</description>
              <bitOffset>17</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No auto-baud rate interrupt flag is generated</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Auto-baud rate interrupt flag is generated</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ABRDEN</name>
              <description>Auto-baud Rate Detect Enable Bit\nNote: This bit is cleared automatically after auto-baud detection is finished.</description>
              <bitOffset>18</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Auto-baud rate detect function Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Auto-baud rate detect function Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ABRDBITS</name>
              <description>Auto-baud Rate Detect Bit Length \nNote: The calculation of bit number includes the START bit.</description>
              <bitOffset>19</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>2</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>1-bit time from START bit to the 1st rising edge. The input pattern shall be 0x01</description>
                    <value>#00</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>2-bit time from START bit to the 1st rising edge. The input pattern shall be 0x02</description>
                    <value>#01</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>2</name>
                    <description>4-bit time from START bit to the 1st rising edge. The input pattern shall be 0x08</description>
                    <value>#10</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>3</name>
                    <description>8-bit time from START bit to the 1st rising edge. The input pattern shall be 0x80</description>
                    <value>#11</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ADDRMV</name>
              <description>Address Match Value \nThis field contains the RS-485 address match values.\nNote: This field is used for RS-485 auto address detection mode.</description>
              <bitOffset>24</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>8</bitWidth>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>UARTx_FUNCSEL</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>UARTx_FUNCSEL</displayName>
          <description>UART Function Select Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x30</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>FUNCSEL</name>
              <description>Function Select</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>3</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>UART function</description>
                    <value>#000</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>LIN function</description>
                    <value>#001</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>2</name>
                    <description>IrDA function</description>
                    <value>#010</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>3</name>
                    <description>RS-485 function</description>
                    <value>#011</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>4</name>
                    <description>UART Single-wire function</description>
                    <value>#100</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TXRXDIS</name>
              <description>TX and RX Disable Bit\nSetting this bit can disable TX and RX.\nNote: The TX and RX will not be disabled immediately when this bit is set. The TX and RX complete current task before TX and RX are disabled. When TX and RX are disabled, the TXRXACT (UART_FIFOSTS[31]) is cleared.</description>
              <bitOffset>3</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>TX and RX Enabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>TX and RX Disabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>DGE</name>
              <description>Deglitch Enable Bit\nNote 1: When this bit is set to logic 1, any pulse width less than about 150 ns will be considered a glitch and will be removed in the serial data input (RX). This bit acts only on RX line and has no effect on the transmitter logic.\nNote 2: It is recommended to set this bit only when operating at baud rate under 2.5 Mbps.</description>
              <bitOffset>6</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Deglitch Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Deglitch Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TXRXSWP</name>
              <description>TX and RX Swap Enable Bit\nSetting this bit Swaps TX pin and RX pin.</description>
              <bitOffset>7</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>TX and RX Swap Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>TX and RX Swap Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>UARTx_LINCTL</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>UARTx_LINCTL</displayName>
          <description>UART LIN Control Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x34</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x000C0000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>SLVEN</name>
              <description>LIN Slave Mode Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>LIN slave mode Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>LIN slave mode Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SLVHDEN</name>
              <description>LIN Slave Header Detection Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>1</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>LIN slave header detection Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>LIN slave header detection Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SLVAREN</name>
              <description>LIN Slave Automatic Resynchronization Mode Enable Bit\nNote 2: When operation in Automatic Resynchronization mode, the baud rate setting must be mode 2 (BAUDM1 (UART_BAUD[29]) and BAUDM0 (UART_BAUD[28]) must be 1).\nNote 3: The control and interactions of this field are explained in 6.21.5.10 (Slave mode with automatic resynchronization).\nNote 4: The minimum detection range of the target BRD (UART_BAUD[15:0]) is 0x30.</description>
              <bitOffset>2</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>LIN automatic resynchronization Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>LIN automatic resynchronization Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SLVDUEN</name>
              <description>LIN Slave Divider Update Method Enable Bit\nNote 2: This bit used for LIN Slave Automatic Resynchronization mode. (for Non-Automatic Resynchronization mode, this bit should be kept cleared) \nNote 3: The control and interactions of this field are explained in 6.21.5.10 (Slave mode with automatic resynchronization).</description>
              <bitOffset>3</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>UART_BAUD updated is written by software (if no automatic resynchronization update occurs at the same time)</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>UART_BAUD is updated at the next received character. User must set the bit before checksum reception</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>MUTE</name>
              <description>LIN Mute Mode Enable Bit\nNote: The exit from mute mode condition and each control and interactions of this field are explained in 6.21.5.10 (LIN slave mode).</description>
              <bitOffset>4</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>LIN mute mode Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>LIN mute mode Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RTOUTEN</name>
              <description>LIN Response Time-out Detection Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>5</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>LIN response time-out detection Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>LIN response time-out detection Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SENDH</name>
              <description>LIN TX Send Header Enable Bit\nThe LIN TX header can be 'break field' or 'break and sync field' or 'break, sync and frame ID field', it is depend on setting HSEL (UART_LINCTL[23:22]).\nNote 1: This bit is shadow bit of LINTXEN (UART_ALTCTL[7]); user can read/write it by setting LINTXEN (UART_ALTCTL[7]) or SENDH (UART_LINCTL[8]).\nNote 2: When transmitter header field (it may be 'break' or 'break + sync' or 'break + sync + frame ID' selected by HSEL (UART_LINCTL[23:22]) field) transfer operation finished, this bit will be cleared automatically.</description>
              <bitOffset>8</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Send LIN TX header Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Send LIN TX header Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>IDPEN</name>
              <description>LIN ID Parity Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>9</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>LIN frame ID parity Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>LIN frame ID parity Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BRKDETEN</name>
              <description>LIN Break Detection Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>10</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>LIN break detection Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>LIN break detection Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>LINRXOFF</name>
              <description>LIN Receiver Disable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>11</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>LIN receiver Enabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>LIN receiver Disabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BITERREN</name>
              <description>Bit Error Detect Enable Bit </description>
              <bitOffset>12</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Bit error detection function Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Bit error detection function Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BRKFL</name>
              <description>LIN Break Field Length \nThis field indicates a 4-bit LIN TX break field count.\nNote 1: These registers are shadow registers of BRKFL (UART_ALTCTL[3:0]). User can read/write it by setting BRKFL (UART_ALTCTL[3:0]) or BRKFL (UART_LINCTL[19:16]).\nNote 2: This break field length is BRKFL + 1.</description>
              <bitOffset>16</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>4</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BSL</name>
              <description>LIN Break/Sync Delimiter Length \nNote: This bit used for LIN master to sending header field.</description>
              <bitOffset>20</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>2</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>The LIN break/sync delimiter length is 1-bit time</description>
                    <value>#00</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>The LIN break/sync delimiter length is 2-bit time</description>
                    <value>#01</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>2</name>
                    <description>The LIN break/sync delimiter length is 3-bit time</description>
                    <value>#10</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>3</name>
                    <description>The LIN break/sync delimiter length is 4-bit time</description>
                    <value>#11</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>HSEL</name>
              <description>LIN Header Select </description>
              <bitOffset>22</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>2</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>The LIN header includes 'break field'</description>
                    <value>#00</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>The LIN header includes 'break field' and 'sync field'</description>
                    <value>#01</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>2</name>
                    <description>The LIN header includes 'break field', 'sync field' and 'frame ID field'</description>
                    <value>#10</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>3</name>
                    <description>Reserved.</description>
                    <value>#11</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>PID</name>
              <description>LIN PID Bits\nIf the parity generated by hardware, user fill ID0~ID5 (PID[29:24]), hardware will calculate P0 (PID[30]) and P1 (PID[31]), otherwise user must filled frame ID and parity in this field.\nNote 1: User can fill any 8-bit value to this field and the bit 24 indicates ID0 (LSB first).\nNote 2: This field can be used for LIN master mode or slave mode.</description>
              <bitOffset>24</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>8</bitWidth>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>UARTx_LINSTS</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>UARTx_LINSTS</displayName>
          <description>UART LIN Status Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x38</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>SLVHDETF</name>
              <description>LIN Slave Header Detection Flag\nThis bit is set by hardware when a LIN header is detected in LIN slave mode and be cleared by writing 1 to it.\nNote 3: When enable ID parity check IDPEN (UART_LINCTL[9]), if hardware detect complete header ('break + sync + frame ID'), the SLVHDETF will be set whether the frame ID correct or not.</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>LIN header not detected</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>LIN header detected (break + sync + frame ID)</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SLVHEF</name>
              <description>LIN Slave Header Error Flag\nThis bit is set by hardware when a LIN header error is detected in LIN slave mode and be cleared by writing 1 to it. The header errors include 'break delimiter is too short (less than 0.5 bit time)', 'frame error in sync field or Identifier field', 'sync field data is not 0x55 in Non-Automatic Resynchronization mode', 'sync field deviation error with Automatic Resynchronization mode', 'sync field measure time-out with Automatic Resynchronization mode' and 'LIN header reception time-out'.</description>
              <bitOffset>1</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>LIN header error not detected</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>LIN header error detected</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SLVIDPEF</name>
              <description>LIN Slave ID Parity Error Flag \nThis bit is set by hardware when receipted frame ID parity is not correct.</description>
              <bitOffset>2</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No active</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Receipted frame ID parity is not correct</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SLVSYNCF</name>
              <description>LIN Slave Sync Field\nThis bit indicates that the LIN sync field is being analyzed in Automatic Resynchronization mode. When the receiver header have some error been detect, user must reset the internal circuit to re-search new frame header by writing 1 to this bit.\nNote 2: This bit can be cleared by writing 1 to it.\nNote 3: When writing 1 to it, hardware will reload the initial baud rate and re-search a new frame header.</description>
              <bitOffset>3</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>The current character is not at LIN sync state</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>The current character is at LIN sync state</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SLVHTOF</name>
              <description>LIN Slave Header Time-out Flag\nThis bit is set by hardware when a LIN header reception time-out is detected in LIN slave mode and be cleared by writing 1 to it. When this bit is set, SLVHEF (UART_LINSTS[1]) will also be set.</description>
              <bitOffset>4</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>LIN header time-out not detected</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>LIN header time-out detected</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RTOUTF</name>
              <description>LIN Response Time-out Flag\nThis bit is set when no LIN response received and the time-out counter equal to or bigger than LINRTOIC (UART_LINRTOUT[23:0]). If LINIEN (UART_INTEN[8]) is enabled, the LIN Bus Interrupt will be generated.\nNote 1: This bit can be cleared by writing 1 to it.</description>
              <bitOffset>5</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>LIN response time-out not detected</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>LIN response time-out detected</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BRKDETF</name>
              <description>LIN Break Detection Flag\nThis bit is set by hardware when a break is detected and be cleared by writing 1 to it through software.</description>
              <bitOffset>8</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>LIN break not detected</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>LIN break detected</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BITEF</name>
              <description>Bit Error Detect Status Flag \nAt TX transfer state, hardware will monitor the bus state, if the input pin (UART_RXD) state not equals to the output pin (UART_TXD) state, BITEF (UART_LINSTS[9]) will be set.</description>
              <bitOffset>9</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Bit error not detected</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Bit error detected</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>UARTx_BRCOMP</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>UARTx_BRCOMP</displayName>
          <description>UART Baud Rate Compensation Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x3C</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>BRCOMP</name>
              <description>Baud Rate Compensation Patten\nThese 9-bits are used to define the relative bit is compensated or not. \nBRCOMP[7:0] is used to define the compensation of DAT (UART_DAT[7:0]) and BRCOM[8] is used to define PARITY (UART_DAT[8]).</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>9</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BRCOMPDEC</name>
              <description>Baud Rate Compensation Decrease</description>
              <bitOffset>31</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Positive (increase one module clock) compensation for each compensated bit</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Negative (decrease one module clock) compensation for each compensated bit</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>UARTx_WKCTL</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>UARTx_WKCTL</displayName>
          <description>UART Wake-up Control Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x40</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>WKCTSEN</name>
              <description>nCTS Wake-up Enable Bit\nNote: When the system is in Power-down mode, an external nCTS change will wake up system from Power-down mode.</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>nCTS Wake-up system function Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>nCTS Wake-up system function Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>WKDATEN</name>
              <description>Incoming Data Wake-up Enable Bit\nNote: When the system is in Power-down mode, incoming data will wake-up system from Power-down mode.</description>
              <bitOffset>1</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Incoming data wake-up system function Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Incoming data wake-up system function Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>WKRFRTEN</name>
              <description>Received Data FIFO Reached Threshold Wake-up Enable Bit\nNote: When the system is in Power-down mode, Received Data FIFO reached threshold will wake-up system from Power-down mode.</description>
              <bitOffset>2</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Received Data FIFO reached threshold wake-up system function Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Received Data FIFO reached threshold wake-up system function Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>WKRS485EN</name>
              <description>RS-485 Address Match Wake-up Enable Bit\nNote 1: When the system is in Power-down mode, RS-485 Address Match will wake-up system from Power-down mode.\nNote 2: This bit is used for RS-485 Auto Address Detection (AAD) mode in RS-485 function mode and ADDRDEN (UART_ALTCTL[15]) is set to 1.</description>
              <bitOffset>3</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>RS-485 Address Match (AAD mode) wake-up system function Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>RS-485 Address Match (AAD mode) wake-up system function Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>WKTOUTEN</name>
              <description>Received Data FIFO Reached Threshold Time-out Wake-up Enable Bit\nNote 1: When the system is in Power-down mode, Received Data FIFO reached threshold time-out will wake up system from Power-down mode.\nNote 2: It is suggested the function is enabled when the WKRFRTEN (UART_WKCTL[2]) is set to 1.</description>
              <bitOffset>4</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Received Data FIFO reached threshold time-out wake-up system function Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Received Data FIFO reached threshold time-out wake-up system function Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>UARTx_WKSTS</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>UARTx_WKSTS</displayName>
          <description>UART Wake-up Status Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x44</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>CTSWKF</name>
              <description>nCTS Wake-up Flag\nThis bit is set if chip wake-up from power-down state by nCTS wake-up.\nNote 1: If WKCTSEN (UART_WKCTL[0]) is enabled, the nCTS wake-up cause this bit is set to '1'.\nNote 2: This bit can be cleared by writing '1' to it.</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Chip stays in power-down state</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Chip wake-up from power-down state by nCTS wake-up</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>DATWKF</name>
              <description>Incoming Data Wake-up Flag\nThis bit is set if chip wake-up from power-down state by data wake-up.\nNote 1: If WKDATEN (UART_WKCTL[1]) is enabled, the Incoming Data wake-up cause this bit is set to '1'.\nNote 2: This bit can be cleared by writing '1' to it.</description>
              <bitOffset>1</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Chip stays in power-down state</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Chip wake-up from power-down state by Incoming Data wake-up</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RFRTWKF</name>
              <description>Received Data FIFO Reached Threshold Wake-up Flag\nThis bit is set if chip wake-up from power-down state by Received Data FIFO reached threshold wake-up.\nNote 1: If WKRFRTEN (UART_WKCTL[2]) is enabled, the Received Data FIFO Reached Threshold wake-up cause this bit is set to '1'.\nNote 2: This bit can be cleared by writing '1' to it.</description>
              <bitOffset>2</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Chip stays in power-down state</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Chip wake-up from power-down state by Received Data FIFO Reached Threshold wake-up</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RS485WKF</name>
              <description>RS-485 Address Match Wake-up Flag\nThis bit is set if chip wake-up from power-down state by RS-485 Address Match (AAD mode).\nNote 1: If WKRS485EN (UART_WKCTL[3]) is enabled, the RS-485 Address Match (AAD mode) wake-up cause this bit is set to '1'.\nNote 2: This bit can be cleared by writing '1' to it.</description>
              <bitOffset>3</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Chip stays in power-down state</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Chip wake-up from power-down state by RS-485 Address Match (AAD mode) wake-up</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TOUTWKF</name>
              <description>Received Data FIFO Threshold Time-out Wake-up Flag\nThis bit is set if chip wake-up from power-down state by Received Data FIFO Threshold Time-out\nwake-up.\nNote 1: If WKTOUTEN (UART_WKCTL[4]) is enabled, the Received Data FIFO reached threshold time-out wake-up cause this bit is set to '1'.\nNote 2: This bit can be cleared by writing '1' to it.</description>
              <bitOffset>4</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Chip stays in power-down state</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Chip wake-up from power-down state by Received Data FIFO reached threshold time-out wake-up</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>UARTx_DWKCOMP</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>UARTx_DWKCOMP</displayName>
          <description>UART Incoming Data Wake-up Compensation Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x48</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>STCOMP</name>
              <description>START Bit Compensation Value\nThese bits field indicate how many clock cycle selected by UART_CLK do the UART controller can get the 1st bit (START bit) when the device is wake-up from Power-down mode.\nNote: It is valid only when WKDATEN (UART_WKCTL[1]) is set.</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>16</bitWidth>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>UARTx_RS485DD</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>UARTx_RS485DD</displayName>
          <description>UART RS485 Transceiver Deactivate Delay Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x4C</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>RTSDDLY</name>
              <description>RS485 Transceiver Deactivate Delay Value\nThese bits field indicate how many clock cycles selected by UART_CLK do the UART controller delay the RS485 transceiver state trancing when the state trancing of RS485 transceiver is from TX to RX state. These bits field have no effect when the state trancing of RS485 transceiver is from RX to TX state.\nNote: It is valid only when RS485AUD (UART_ALTCTL[10]) is set.</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>16</bitWidth>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>UARTx_LINRTOUT</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>UARTx_LINRTOUT</displayName>
          <description>UART LIN Response Time-out Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x50</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00FFFFFF</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>LINRTOIC</name>
              <description>LIN Response Time-out Comparator</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>24</bitWidth>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>UARTx_LINWKCTL</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>UARTx_LINWKCTL</displayName>
          <description>UART LIN Wake-up Control Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x54</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x000000C0</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>LINWKC</name>
              <description>LIN Send Wake-up Signal Length Counter</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>24</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SENDLINW</name>
              <description>LIN Send Wake-up Enable Bit\nNote 1: When this bit is set, the UART will send LIN wake-up automatically. When LIN wake-up transfer operation finished, this bit will be cleared automatically.</description>
              <bitOffset>24</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Send LIN Wake-up Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Send LIN Wake-up Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>LINWKEN</name>
              <description>LIN Wake-up Enable Bit\nNote 1: When the system is in Power-down mode, LIN wake-up event will wake up system from Power-down mode.</description>
              <bitOffset>28</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>LIN wake-up system function Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>LIN wake-up system function Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>LINWKF</name>
              <description>LIN Wake-up Flag\nThis bit is set if chip wake-up from power-down state by LIN wake-up.\nNote 1: If LINWKEN (UART_LINWKCTL[28]) is enabled, the LIN wake-up event will cause this bit set to '1'.\nNote 2: This bit can be cleared by writing '1' to it.</description>
              <bitOffset>29</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Chip stays in power-down state</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Chip wake-up from power-down state by LIN wake-up</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
      </registers>
    </peripheral>
    <peripheral>
      <name>I2C0</name>
      <description>I2C Register Map</description>
      <groupName>I2C</groupName>
      <baseAddress>0x40080000</baseAddress>
      <!-- d -> no access permission -->
      <addressBlock>
        <offset>0x0</offset>
        <size>0x34</size>
        <usage>registers</usage>
      </addressBlock>
      <addressBlock>
        <offset>0x3C</offset>
        <size>0x30</size>
        <usage>registers</usage>
      </addressBlock>
      <registers>
       <register>
          <name>I2C_CTL0</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>I2C_CTL0</displayName>
          <description>I2C Control Register 0</description>
          <addressOffset>0x0</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>AA</name>
              <description>Assert Acknowledge Control</description>
              <bitOffset>2</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SI</name>
              <description>I2C Interrupt Flag\nWhen a new I2C state is present in the I2C_STATUS0 register, the SI flag is set by hardware. If bit INTEN (I2C_CTL0 [7]) is set, the I2C interrupt is requested. SI must be cleared by software. Clear SI by writing 1 to this bit.\nFor ACKMEN is set in slave read mode, the SI flag is set in 8th clock period for user to confirm the acknowledge bit and 9th clock period for user to read the data in the data buffer.</description>
              <bitOffset>3</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>STO</name>
              <description>I2C STOP Control\nIn Master mode, setting STO to transmit a STOP condition to bus then I2C controller will check the bus condition if a STOP condition is detected. This bit will be cleared by hardware automatically.</description>
              <bitOffset>4</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>STA</name>
              <description>I2C START Control\nSetting STA to logic 1 to enter Master mode, the I2C hardware sends a START or repeat START condition to bus when the bus is free.</description>
              <bitOffset>5</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>I2CEN</name>
              <description>I2C Controller Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>6</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>I2C controller Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>I2C controller Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>INTEN</name>
              <description>Enable Interrupt</description>
              <bitOffset>7</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>I2C interrupt Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>I2C interrupt Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>I2C_ADDR0</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>I2C_ADDR0</displayName>
          <description>I2C Slave Address Register0</description>
          <addressOffset>0x4</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>GC</name>
              <description>General Call Function</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>General Call Function Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>General Call Function Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ADDR</name>
              <description>I2C Address \nThe content of this register is irrelevant when I2C is in Master mode. In the slave mode, the seven most significant bits must be loaded with the chip's own address. The I2C hardware will react if either of the address is matched.\nNote: When software set 10'h000, the address cannot be used.</description>
              <bitOffset>1</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>10</bitWidth>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>I2C_DAT</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>I2C_DAT</displayName>
          <description>I2C Data Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x8</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>DAT</name>
              <description>I2C Data \nBit [7:0] is located with the 8-bit transferred/received data of I2C serial port.</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>8</bitWidth>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>I2C_STATUS0</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>I2C_STATUS0</displayName>
          <description>I2C Status Register 0</description>
          <addressOffset>0xC</addressOffset>
          <access>read-only</access>
          <resetValue>0x000000F8</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>STATUS</name>
              <description>I2C Status</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>8</bitWidth>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>I2C_CLKDIV</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>I2C_CLKDIV</displayName>
          <description>I2C Clock Divided Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x10</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>DIVIDER</name>
              <description>I2C Clock Divided \nNote: The minimum value of DIVIDER is 4.</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>10</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>NFCNT</name>
              <description>Noise Filter Count \nThe register bits control the input filter width.\n0 : filter width 3*PCLK \n1 : filter width 4*PCLK\nN : filter width (3+N)*PCKL\nNote: Filter width Min :3*PCLK, Max : 18*PCLK</description>
              <bitOffset>12</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>4</bitWidth>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>I2C_TOCTL</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>I2C_TOCTL</displayName>
          <description>I2C Time-out Control Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x14</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>TOIF</name>
              <description>Time-out Interrupt Flag\nThis bit is set by hardware when I2C time out happened. Software can write 1 to clear this bit.</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TOCDIV4</name>
              <description>Time-out Counter Input Clock Divided by 4\nWhen enabled, the time-out period is extended 4 times.</description>
              <bitOffset>1</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Time-out period is extend 4 times Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Time-out period is extend 4 times Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TOCEN</name>
              <description>Time-out Counter Enable Bit\nWhen enabled, the 14-bit time-out counter will start counting when SI is cleared. Setting flag SI to '1' will reset counter and re-start up counting after SI is cleared.</description>
              <bitOffset>2</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Time-out counter Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Time-out counter Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TOBAUD</name>
              <description>Time-out Counter Input Clock Using Baud Rate Frequency\nWhen enabled, the time-out counter is using baud rate.</description>
              <bitOffset>3</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Time-out counter is using PCLK or PCLK/4</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Time-out counter is using IC baud rate</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TONIDLE</name>
              <description>Time-out Counter Disable in IDLE State\nWhen set 1,Time-out counter will stop.</description>
              <bitOffset>8</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Timeout counter enable when in IDLE state</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Timeout counter disable when in IDLE state</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register derivedFrom="I2C_ADDR0">
          <name>I2C_ADDR1</name>
          <displayName>I2C_ADDR1</displayName>
          <description>I2C Slave Address Register1</description>
          <addressOffset>0x18</addressOffset>
       </register>
       <register derivedFrom="I2C_ADDR0">
          <name>I2C_ADDR2</name>
          <displayName>I2C_ADDR2</displayName>
          <description>I2C Slave Address Register2</description>
          <addressOffset>0x1C</addressOffset>
       </register>
       <register derivedFrom="I2C_ADDR0">
          <name>I2C_ADDR3</name>
          <displayName>I2C_ADDR3</displayName>
          <description>I2C Slave Address Register3</description>
          <addressOffset>0x20</addressOffset>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>I2C_ADDRMSK0</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>I2C_ADDRMSK0</displayName>
          <description>I2C Slave Address Mask Register0</description>
          <addressOffset>0x24</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>ADDRMSK</name>
              <description>I2C Address Mask\nI2C bus controllers support multiple address recognition with four address mask register. When the bit in the address mask register is set to one, it means the received corresponding address bit is don't-care. If the bit is set to zero, that means the received corresponding register bit should be exact the same as address register.\nNote: The wake-up function cannot use address mask in 10-bit mode.</description>
              <bitOffset>1</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>10</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Mask Disabled (the received corresponding register bit should be exact the same as address register.)</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Mask Enabled (the received corresponding address bit is don't care.)</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register derivedFrom="I2C_ADDRMSK0">
          <name>I2C_ADDRMSK1</name>
          <displayName>I2C_ADDRMSK1</displayName>
          <description>I2C Slave Address Mask Register1</description>
          <addressOffset>0x28</addressOffset>
       </register>
       <register derivedFrom="I2C_ADDRMSK0">
          <name>I2C_ADDRMSK2</name>
          <displayName>I2C_ADDRMSK2</displayName>
          <description>I2C Slave Address Mask Register2</description>
          <addressOffset>0x2C</addressOffset>
       </register>
       <register derivedFrom="I2C_ADDRMSK0">
          <name>I2C_ADDRMSK3</name>
          <displayName>I2C_ADDRMSK3</displayName>
          <description>I2C Slave Address Mask Register3</description>
          <addressOffset>0x30</addressOffset>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>I2C_WKCTL</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>I2C_WKCTL</displayName>
          <description>I2C Wake-up Control Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x3C</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>WKEN</name>
              <description>I2C Wake-up Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>I2C wake-up function Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>I2C wake-up function Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>NHDBUSEN</name>
              <description>I2C No Hold BUS Enable Bit\nNote: The I2C controller could respond when WKIF event is not clear, it may cause error data transmitted or received. If data transmitted or received when WKIF event is not clear, user must reset I2C controller and execute the original operation again.</description>
              <bitOffset>7</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>I2C hold bus after wake-up</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>I2C don't hold bus after wake-up</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>I2C_WKSTS</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>I2C_WKSTS</displayName>
          <description>I2C Wake-up Status Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x40</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>WKIF</name>
              <description>I2C Wake-up Flag\nWhen chip is woken up from Power-down mode by I2C, this bit is set to 1. Software can write 1 to clear this bit.</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>WKAKDONE</name>
              <description>Wakeup Address Frame Acknowledge Bit Done\nNote: This bit can't release WKIF. Software can write 1 to clear this bit.</description>
              <bitOffset>1</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>The ACK bit cycle of address match frame isn't done</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>The ACK bit cycle of address match frame is done in power-down</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>WRSTSWK</name>
              <description>Read/Write Status Bit in Address Wakeup Frame\nNote: This bit will be cleared when software can write 1 to WKAKDONE bit.</description>
              <bitOffset>2</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Write command be record on the address match wakeup frame</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Read command be record on the address match wakeup frame</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>I2C_CTL1</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>I2C_CTL1</displayName>
          <description>I2C Control Register 1</description>
          <addressOffset>0x44</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>TXPDMAEN</name>
              <description>PDMA Transmit Channel Available </description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Transmit PDMA function Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Transmit PDMA function Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RXPDMAEN</name>
              <description>PDMA Receive Channel Available </description>
              <bitOffset>1</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Receive PDMA function Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Receive PDMA function Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>PDMARST</name>
              <description>PDMA Reset </description>
              <bitOffset>2</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No effect</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Reset the I2C request to PDMA</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>OVRIEN</name>
              <description>I2C Over Run Interrupt Control Bit\nSetting OVRIEN to logic 1 will send a interrupt to system when the TWOBUFEN bit is enabled and there is over run event in received buffer.</description>
              <bitOffset>3</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>UDRIEN</name>
              <description>I2C Under Run Interrupt Control Bit\nSetting UDRIEN to logic 1 will send a interrupt to system when the TWOBUFEN bit is enabled and there is under run event happened in transmitted buffer.</description>
              <bitOffset>4</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TWOBUFEN</name>
              <description>Two-level BUFFER Enable Bit\nSet to enable the two-level buffer for I2C transmitted or received buffer. It is used to improve the performance of the I2C bus.</description>
              <bitOffset>5</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Two-level buffer Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Two-level buffer Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>PDMASTR</name>
              <description>PDMA Stretch Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>8</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>I2C send STOP automatically after PDMA transfer done. (only master TX)</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>I2C SCL bus is stretched by hardware after PDMA transfer done if the SI is not cleared. (only master TX)</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ADDR10EN</name>
              <description>Address 10-bit Function Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>9</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Address match 10-bit function Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Address match 10-bit function Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SWITCHEN</name>
              <description>SCL And SDA Pin Switch Enable Bit\nNote: Original pin configuration table is shown in Basic Configuration chapter.</description>
              <bitOffset>10</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>IC use original pin configuration</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>IC switch SCL and SDA pin configuration</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>DPBITSEL</name>
              <description>Data Phase Bit Count Select</description>
              <bitOffset>16</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>2</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>DPCIF never set by hardware</description>
                    <value>#00</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>When I2C is transfer data and bit count equal to 6, DPCIF will be set by hardware</description>
                    <value>#01</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>2</name>
                    <description>When I2C is transfer data and bit count equal to 7, DPCIF will be set by hardware</description>
                    <value>#10</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>3</name>
                    <description>When I2C is transfer data and bit count equal to 8, DPCIF will be set by hardware</description>
                    <value>#11</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>DPCINTEN</name>
              <description>Data Phase Count Interrupt Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>20</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Data Phase Count Interrupt Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Data Phase Count Interrupt Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SRCINTEN</name>
              <description>Slave Read Command Interrupt Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>21</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Slave Read Command Interrupt Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Slave Read Command Interrupt Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>I2C_STATUS1</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>I2C_STATUS1</displayName>
          <description>I2C Status Register 1</description>
          <addressOffset>0x48</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>ADMAT0</name>
              <description>I2C Address 0 Match Status\nWhen address 0 is matched, hardware will inform which address used. This bit will set to 1, and software can write 1 to clear this bit.</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ADMAT1</name>
              <description>I2C Address 1 Match Status\nWhen address 1 is matched, hardware will inform which address used. This bit will set to 1, and software can write 1 to clear this bit.</description>
              <bitOffset>1</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ADMAT2</name>
              <description>I2C Address 2 Match Status\nWhen address 2 is matched, hardware will inform which address used. This bit will set to 1, and software can write 1 to clear this bit.</description>
              <bitOffset>2</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ADMAT3</name>
              <description>I2C Address 3 Match Status\nWhen address 3 is matched, hardware will inform which address used. This bit will set to 1, and software can write 1 to clear this bit.</description>
              <bitOffset>3</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>FULL</name>
              <description>TWO-LEVEL BUFFER FULL\nThis bit is set when POINTER is equal to 2\nNote: This bit is read only.</description>
              <bitOffset>4</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>EMPTY</name>
              <description>TWO-LEVEL BUFFER EMPTY\nThis bit is set when POINTER is equal to 0.\nNote: This bit is read only.</description>
              <bitOffset>5</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>OVR</name>
              <description>I2C Over Run Status Bit\nNote: This bit is read only.</description>
              <bitOffset>6</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>UDR</name>
              <description>I2C Under Run Status Bit\nNote: This bit is read only.</description>
              <bitOffset>7</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ONBUSY</name>
              <description>On Bus Busy (Read Only)\nIndicates that a communication is in progress on the bus. It is set by hardware when a START condition is detected. It is cleared by hardware when a STOP condition is detected.</description>
              <bitOffset>8</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>The bus is IDLE (both SCL and SDA High)</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>The bus is busy</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>DPCIF</name>
              <description>Data Phase Count Interrupt Flag\nThis bit is set by hardware when I2C transfer bit count equal to DPBITSEL setting \nThis bit is cleared by write 1 to it.</description>
              <bitOffset>20</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SARCIF</name>
              <description>Slave Address Read Command Interrupt Flag\nThis bit is set by hardware when I2C receive address match read command.\nThis bit is cleared by write 1 to it.</description>
              <bitOffset>21</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>I2C_TMCTL</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>I2C_TMCTL</displayName>
          <description>I2C Timing Configure Control Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x4C</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00020000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>STCTL</name>
              <description>Setup Time Configure Control\nThis field is used to generate a delay timing between SDA falling edge and SCL rising edge in transmission mode.\nNote: Setup time setting should not make SCL output less than three PCLKs.</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>9</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>HTCTL</name>
              <description>Hold Time Configure Control \nThis field is used to generate the delay timing between SCL falling edge and SDA rising edge in transmission mode.</description>
              <bitOffset>16</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>9</bitWidth>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>I2C_BUSCTL</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>I2C_BUSCTL</displayName>
          <description>I2C Bus Management Control Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x50</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>ACKMEN</name>
              <description>Acknowledge Control by Manual\nIn order to allow ACK control in slave reception including the command and data, slave byte control mode must be enabled by setting the ACKMEN bit.</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Slave byte control Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Slave byte control Enabled. The 9th bit can response the ACK or NACK according the received data by user. When the byte is received, stretching the SCL signal low between the 8th and 9th SCL pulse</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>PECEN</name>
              <description>Packet Error Checking Calculation Enable Bit\nNote: When I2C enter power-down mode, the bit should be enabled after wake-up if needed PEC calculation.</description>
              <bitOffset>1</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Packet Error Checking Calculation Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Packet Error Checking Calculation Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BMDEN</name>
              <description>Bus Management Device Default Address Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>2</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Device default address Disable. When the address 0'b1100001x comes and either BMDEN or ACKMEN is disabled, the device responses NACK</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Device default address Enabled. When the address 0'b1100001x comes and both BMDEN and ACKMEN are enabled, the device responses ACK</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BMHEN</name>
              <description>Bus Management Host Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>3</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Host function Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Host function Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ALERTEN</name>
              <description>Bus Management Alert Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>4</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Release the BM_ALERT pin high and Alert Response Header disabled: 0001100x followed by NACK if both of BMDEN and ACKMEN are enabled.\nBM_ALERT pin not supported</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Drive BM_ALERT pin low and Alert Response Address Header enables: 0001100x followed by ACK if both of BMDEN and ACKMEN are enabled.\nBM_ALERT pin supported</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SCTLOSTS</name>
              <description>Suspend/Control Data Output Status</description>
              <bitOffset>5</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>The output of SUSCON pin is low</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>The output of SUSCON pin is high</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SCTLOEN</name>
              <description>Suspend or Control Pin Output Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>6</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>The SUSCON pin in input</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>The output enable is active on the SUSCON pin</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BUSEN</name>
              <description>BUS Enable Bit\nNote: When the bit is enabled, the internal 14-bit counter is used to calculate the time out event of clock low condition.</description>
              <bitOffset>7</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>The system management function Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>The system management function Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>PECTXEN</name>
              <description>Packet Error Checking Byte Transmission/Reception</description>
              <bitOffset>8</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No PEC transfer</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>PEC transmission is requested</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TIDLE</name>
              <description>Timer Check in Idle State\nThe BUSTOUT is used to calculate the time-out of clock low in bus active and the idle period in bus Idle. This bit is used to define which condition is enabled.\nNote: The BUSY (I2C_BUSSTS[0]) indicate the current bus state.</description>
              <bitOffset>9</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>BUSTOUT is used to calculate the clock low period in bus active</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>BUSTOUT is used to calculate the IDLE period in bus Idle</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>PECCLR</name>
              <description>PEC Clear at Repeat Start\nThe calculation of PEC starts when PECEN is set to 1 and it is cleared when the STA or STO bit is detected. This PECCLR bit is used to enable the condition of REPEAT START can clear the PEC calculation.</description>
              <bitOffset>10</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>PEC calculation is cleared by 'Repeat Start' function Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>PEC calculation is cleared by 'Repeat Start' function Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ACKM9SI</name>
              <description>Acknowledge Manual Enable Extra SI Interrupt</description>
              <bitOffset>11</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>There is no SI interrupt in the 9th clock cycle when the BUSEN =1 and ACKMEN =1</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>There is SI interrupt in the 9th clock cycle when the BUSEN =1 and ACKMEN =1</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BCDIEN</name>
              <description>Packet Error Checking Byte Count Done Interrupt Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>12</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Byte count done interrupt Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Byte count done interrupt Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>PECDIEN</name>
              <description>Packet Error Checking Byte Transfer Done Interrupt Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>13</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>PEC transfer done interrupt Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>PEC transfer done interrupt Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>I2C_BUSTCTL</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>I2C_BUSTCTL</displayName>
          <description>I2C Bus Management Timer Control Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x54</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>BUSTOEN</name>
              <description>Bus Time Out Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Bus clock low time-out detection Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Bus clock low time-out detection Enabled (bus clock is low for more than BUSTO (I2C_BUSTOUT[7:0]) (in TIDLE=0) or high more than BUSTO (in TIDLE =1)</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>CLKTOEN</name>
              <description>Cumulative Clock Low Time Out Enable Bit\nFor Master, it calculates the period from START to ACK\nFor Slave, it calculates the period from START to STOP</description>
              <bitOffset>1</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Cumulative clock low time-out detection Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Cumulative clock low time-out detection Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BUSTOIEN</name>
              <description>Time-out Interrupt Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>2</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>SCL low time-out interrupt Disabled.\nBus IDLE time-out interrupt Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>SCL low time-out interrupt Enabled.\nBus IDLE time-out interrupt Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>CLKTOIEN</name>
              <description>Extended Clock Time Out Interrupt Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>3</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Clock time out interrupt Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Clock time out interrupt Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TORSTEN</name>
              <description>Time Out Reset Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>4</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>I2C state machine reset Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>I2C state machine reset Enabled. (The clock and data bus will be released to high)</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>I2C_BUSSTS</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>I2C_BUSSTS</displayName>
          <description>I2C Bus Management Status Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x58</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>BUSY</name>
              <description>Bus Busy\nIndicates that a communication is in progress on the bus. It is set by hardware when a START condition is detected. It is cleared by hardware when a STOP condition is detected</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Bus is IDLE (both SCL and SDA High)</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Bus is busy</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BCDONE</name>
              <description>Byte Count Transmission/Receive Done \nNote: Software can write 1 to clear this bit.</description>
              <bitOffset>1</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Byte count transmission/ receive is not finished when the PECEN is set</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Byte count transmission/ receive is finished when the PECEN is set</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>PECERR</name>
              <description>PEC Error in Reception \nNote: Software can write 1 to clear this bit.</description>
              <bitOffset>2</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>PEC value equal the received PEC data packet</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>PEC value doesn't match the receive PEC data packet</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ALERT</name>
              <description>SMBus Alert Status \nNote: 1. The SMBALERT pin is an open-drain pin, the pull-high resistor is must in the system. 2. Software can write 1 to clear this bit.</description>
              <bitOffset>3</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>SMBALERT pin state is low.\nNo SMBALERT event</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>SMBALERT pin state is high.\nThere is SMBALERT event (falling edge) is detected in SMALERT pin when the BMHEN = 1 (SMBus host configuration) and the ALERTEN = 1</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SCTLDIN</name>
              <description>Bus Suspend or Control Signal Input Status </description>
              <bitOffset>4</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>The input status of SUSCON pin is 0</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>The input status of SUSCON pin is 1</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BUSTO</name>
              <description>Bus Time-out Status \nIn bus busy, the bit indicates the total clock low time-out event occurred; otherwise, it indicates the bus idle time-out event occurred.\nNote: Software can write 1 to clear this bit.</description>
              <bitOffset>5</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>There is no any time-out or external clock time-out</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>A time-out or external clock time-out occurred</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>CLKTO</name>
              <description>Clock Low Cumulate Time-out Status \nNote: Software can write 1 to clear this bit.</description>
              <bitOffset>6</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Cumulative clock low is no any time-out</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Cumulative clock low time-out occurred</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>PECDONE</name>
              <description>PEC Byte Transmission/Receive Done \nNote: Software can write 1 to clear this bit.</description>
              <bitOffset>7</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>PEC transmission/ receive is not finished when the PECEN is set</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>PEC transmission/ receive is finished when the PECEN is set</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>I2C_PKTSIZE</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>I2C_PKTSIZE</displayName>
          <description>I2C Packet Error Checking Byte Number Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x5C</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>PLDSIZE</name>
              <description>Transfer Byte Number\nThe transmission or receive byte number in one transaction when the PECEN is set. The maximum transaction or receive byte is 256 Bytes.\nNote: The byte number counting includes address, command code, and data frame.</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>9</bitWidth>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>I2C_PKTCRC</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>I2C_PKTCRC</displayName>
          <description>I2C Packet Error Checking Byte Value Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x60</addressOffset>
          <access>read-only</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>PECCRC</name>
              <description>Packet Error Checking Byte Value</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>8</bitWidth>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>I2C_BUSTOUT</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>I2C_BUSTOUT</displayName>
          <description>I2C Bus Management Timer Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x64</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000005</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>BUSTO</name>
              <description>Bus Management Time-out Value\nIndicates the bus time-out value in bus is IDLE or SCL low.\nNote: If the user wants to revise the value of BUSTOUT, the TORSTEN (I2C_BUSTCTL[4]) bit shall be set to 1 and cleared to 0 first when the BUSEN(I2C_BUSCTL[7]) is set.</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>8</bitWidth>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>I2C_CLKTOUT</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>I2C_CLKTOUT</displayName>
          <description>I2C Bus Management Clock Low Timer Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x68</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000005</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>CLKTO</name>
              <description>Bus Clock Low Timer\nThe field is used to configure the cumulative clock extension time-out.\nNote: If the user wants to revise the value of CLKLTOUT, the TORSTEN bit shall be set to 1 and cleared to 0 first when the BUSEN is set.</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>8</bitWidth>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
      </registers>
    </peripheral>
    <peripheral>
      <name>I2C1</name>
      <description>I2C Register Map</description>
      <groupName>I2C</groupName>
      <baseAddress>0x40081000</baseAddress>
      <!-- d -> no access permission -->
      <addressBlock>
        <offset>0x0</offset>
        <size>0x34</size>
        <usage>registers</usage>
      </addressBlock>
      <addressBlock>
        <offset>0x3C</offset>
        <size>0x30</size>
        <usage>registers</usage>
      </addressBlock>
      <registers>
       <register>
          <name>I2C_CTL0</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>I2C_CTL0</displayName>
          <description>I2C Control Register 0</description>
          <addressOffset>0x0</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>AA</name>
              <description>Assert Acknowledge Control</description>
              <bitOffset>2</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SI</name>
              <description>I2C Interrupt Flag\nWhen a new I2C state is present in the I2C_STATUS0 register, the SI flag is set by hardware. If bit INTEN (I2C_CTL0 [7]) is set, the I2C interrupt is requested. SI must be cleared by software. Clear SI by writing 1 to this bit.\nFor ACKMEN is set in slave read mode, the SI flag is set in 8th clock period for user to confirm the acknowledge bit and 9th clock period for user to read the data in the data buffer.</description>
              <bitOffset>3</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>STO</name>
              <description>I2C STOP Control\nIn Master mode, setting STO to transmit a STOP condition to bus then I2C controller will check the bus condition if a STOP condition is detected. This bit will be cleared by hardware automatically.</description>
              <bitOffset>4</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>STA</name>
              <description>I2C START Control\nSetting STA to logic 1 to enter Master mode, the I2C hardware sends a START or repeat START condition to bus when the bus is free.</description>
              <bitOffset>5</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>I2CEN</name>
              <description>I2C Controller Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>6</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>I2C controller Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>I2C controller Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>INTEN</name>
              <description>Enable Interrupt</description>
              <bitOffset>7</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>I2C interrupt Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>I2C interrupt Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>I2C_ADDR0</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>I2C_ADDR0</displayName>
          <description>I2C Slave Address Register0</description>
          <addressOffset>0x4</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>GC</name>
              <description>General Call Function</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>General Call Function Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>General Call Function Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ADDR</name>
              <description>I2C Address \nThe content of this register is irrelevant when I2C is in Master mode. In the slave mode, the seven most significant bits must be loaded with the chip's own address. The I2C hardware will react if either of the address is matched.\nNote: When software set 10'h000, the address cannot be used.</description>
              <bitOffset>1</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>10</bitWidth>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>I2C_DAT</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>I2C_DAT</displayName>
          <description>I2C Data Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x8</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>DAT</name>
              <description>I2C Data \nBit [7:0] is located with the 8-bit transferred/received data of I2C serial port.</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>8</bitWidth>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>I2C_STATUS0</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>I2C_STATUS0</displayName>
          <description>I2C Status Register 0</description>
          <addressOffset>0xC</addressOffset>
          <access>read-only</access>
          <resetValue>0x000000F8</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>STATUS</name>
              <description>I2C Status</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>8</bitWidth>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>I2C_CLKDIV</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>I2C_CLKDIV</displayName>
          <description>I2C Clock Divided Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x10</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>DIVIDER</name>
              <description>I2C Clock Divided \nNote: The minimum value of DIVIDER is 4.</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>10</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>NFCNT</name>
              <description>Noise Filter Count \nThe register bits control the input filter width.\n0 : filter width 3*PCLK \n1 : filter width 4*PCLK\nN : filter width (3+N)*PCKL\nNote: Filter width Min :3*PCLK, Max : 18*PCLK</description>
              <bitOffset>12</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>4</bitWidth>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>I2C_TOCTL</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>I2C_TOCTL</displayName>
          <description>I2C Time-out Control Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x14</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>TOIF</name>
              <description>Time-out Interrupt Flag\nThis bit is set by hardware when I2C time out happened. Software can write 1 to clear this bit.</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TOCDIV4</name>
              <description>Time-out Counter Input Clock Divided by 4\nWhen enabled, the time-out period is extended 4 times.</description>
              <bitOffset>1</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Time-out period is extend 4 times Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Time-out period is extend 4 times Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TOCEN</name>
              <description>Time-out Counter Enable Bit\nWhen enabled, the 14-bit time-out counter will start counting when SI is cleared. Setting flag SI to '1' will reset counter and re-start up counting after SI is cleared.</description>
              <bitOffset>2</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Time-out counter Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Time-out counter Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TOBAUD</name>
              <description>Time-out Counter Input Clock Using Baud Rate Frequency\nWhen enabled, the time-out counter is using baud rate.</description>
              <bitOffset>3</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Time-out counter is using PCLK or PCLK/4</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Time-out counter is using IC baud rate</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TONIDLE</name>
              <description>Time-out Counter Disable in IDLE State\nWhen set 1,Time-out counter will stop.</description>
              <bitOffset>8</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Timeout counter enable when in IDLE state</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Timeout counter disable when in IDLE state</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register derivedFrom="I2C_ADDR0">
          <name>I2C_ADDR1</name>
          <displayName>I2C_ADDR1</displayName>
          <description>I2C Slave Address Register1</description>
          <addressOffset>0x18</addressOffset>
       </register>
       <register derivedFrom="I2C_ADDR0">
          <name>I2C_ADDR2</name>
          <displayName>I2C_ADDR2</displayName>
          <description>I2C Slave Address Register2</description>
          <addressOffset>0x1C</addressOffset>
       </register>
       <register derivedFrom="I2C_ADDR0">
          <name>I2C_ADDR3</name>
          <displayName>I2C_ADDR3</displayName>
          <description>I2C Slave Address Register3</description>
          <addressOffset>0x20</addressOffset>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>I2C_ADDRMSK0</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>I2C_ADDRMSK0</displayName>
          <description>I2C Slave Address Mask Register0</description>
          <addressOffset>0x24</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>ADDRMSK</name>
              <description>I2C Address Mask\nI2C bus controllers support multiple address recognition with four address mask register. When the bit in the address mask register is set to one, it means the received corresponding address bit is don't-care. If the bit is set to zero, that means the received corresponding register bit should be exact the same as address register.\nNote: The wake-up function cannot use address mask in 10-bit mode.</description>
              <bitOffset>1</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>10</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Mask Disabled (the received corresponding register bit should be exact the same as address register.)</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Mask Enabled (the received corresponding address bit is don't care.)</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register derivedFrom="I2C_ADDRMSK0">
          <name>I2C_ADDRMSK1</name>
          <displayName>I2C_ADDRMSK1</displayName>
          <description>I2C Slave Address Mask Register1</description>
          <addressOffset>0x28</addressOffset>
       </register>
       <register derivedFrom="I2C_ADDRMSK0">
          <name>I2C_ADDRMSK2</name>
          <displayName>I2C_ADDRMSK2</displayName>
          <description>I2C Slave Address Mask Register2</description>
          <addressOffset>0x2C</addressOffset>
       </register>
       <register derivedFrom="I2C_ADDRMSK0">
          <name>I2C_ADDRMSK3</name>
          <displayName>I2C_ADDRMSK3</displayName>
          <description>I2C Slave Address Mask Register3</description>
          <addressOffset>0x30</addressOffset>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>I2C_WKCTL</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>I2C_WKCTL</displayName>
          <description>I2C Wake-up Control Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x3C</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>WKEN</name>
              <description>I2C Wake-up Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>I2C wake-up function Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>I2C wake-up function Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>NHDBUSEN</name>
              <description>I2C No Hold BUS Enable Bit\nNote: The I2C controller could respond when WKIF event is not clear, it may cause error data transmitted or received. If data transmitted or received when WKIF event is not clear, user must reset I2C controller and execute the original operation again.</description>
              <bitOffset>7</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>I2C hold bus after wake-up</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>I2C don't hold bus after wake-up</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>I2C_WKSTS</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>I2C_WKSTS</displayName>
          <description>I2C Wake-up Status Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x40</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>WKIF</name>
              <description>I2C Wake-up Flag\nWhen chip is woken up from Power-down mode by I2C, this bit is set to 1. Software can write 1 to clear this bit.</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>WKAKDONE</name>
              <description>Wakeup Address Frame Acknowledge Bit Done\nNote: This bit can't release WKIF. Software can write 1 to clear this bit.</description>
              <bitOffset>1</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>The ACK bit cycle of address match frame isn't done</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>The ACK bit cycle of address match frame is done in power-down</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>WRSTSWK</name>
              <description>Read/Write Status Bit in Address Wakeup Frame\nNote: This bit will be cleared when software can write 1 to WKAKDONE bit.</description>
              <bitOffset>2</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Write command be record on the address match wakeup frame</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Read command be record on the address match wakeup frame</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>I2C_CTL1</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>I2C_CTL1</displayName>
          <description>I2C Control Register 1</description>
          <addressOffset>0x44</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>TXPDMAEN</name>
              <description>PDMA Transmit Channel Available </description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Transmit PDMA function Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Transmit PDMA function Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RXPDMAEN</name>
              <description>PDMA Receive Channel Available </description>
              <bitOffset>1</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Receive PDMA function Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Receive PDMA function Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>PDMARST</name>
              <description>PDMA Reset </description>
              <bitOffset>2</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No effect</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Reset the I2C request to PDMA</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>OVRIEN</name>
              <description>I2C Over Run Interrupt Control Bit\nSetting OVRIEN to logic 1 will send a interrupt to system when the TWOBUFEN bit is enabled and there is over run event in received buffer.</description>
              <bitOffset>3</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>UDRIEN</name>
              <description>I2C Under Run Interrupt Control Bit\nSetting UDRIEN to logic 1 will send a interrupt to system when the TWOBUFEN bit is enabled and there is under run event happened in transmitted buffer.</description>
              <bitOffset>4</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TWOBUFEN</name>
              <description>Two-level BUFFER Enable Bit\nSet to enable the two-level buffer for I2C transmitted or received buffer. It is used to improve the performance of the I2C bus.</description>
              <bitOffset>5</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Two-level buffer Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Two-level buffer Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>PDMASTR</name>
              <description>PDMA Stretch Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>8</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>I2C send STOP automatically after PDMA transfer done. (only master TX)</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>I2C SCL bus is stretched by hardware after PDMA transfer done if the SI is not cleared. (only master TX)</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ADDR10EN</name>
              <description>Address 10-bit Function Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>9</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Address match 10-bit function Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Address match 10-bit function Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SWITCHEN</name>
              <description>SCL And SDA Pin Switch Enable Bit\nNote: Original pin configuration table is shown in Basic Configuration chapter.</description>
              <bitOffset>10</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>IC use original pin configuration</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>IC switch SCL and SDA pin configuration</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>DPBITSEL</name>
              <description>Data Phase Bit Count Select</description>
              <bitOffset>16</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>2</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>DPCIF never set by hardware</description>
                    <value>#00</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>When I2C is transfer data and bit count equal to 6, DPCIF will be set by hardware</description>
                    <value>#01</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>2</name>
                    <description>When I2C is transfer data and bit count equal to 7, DPCIF will be set by hardware</description>
                    <value>#10</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>3</name>
                    <description>When I2C is transfer data and bit count equal to 8, DPCIF will be set by hardware</description>
                    <value>#11</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>DPCINTEN</name>
              <description>Data Phase Count Interrupt Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>20</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Data Phase Count Interrupt Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Data Phase Count Interrupt Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SRCINTEN</name>
              <description>Slave Read Command Interrupt Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>21</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Slave Read Command Interrupt Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Slave Read Command Interrupt Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>I2C_STATUS1</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>I2C_STATUS1</displayName>
          <description>I2C Status Register 1</description>
          <addressOffset>0x48</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>ADMAT0</name>
              <description>I2C Address 0 Match Status\nWhen address 0 is matched, hardware will inform which address used. This bit will set to 1, and software can write 1 to clear this bit.</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ADMAT1</name>
              <description>I2C Address 1 Match Status\nWhen address 1 is matched, hardware will inform which address used. This bit will set to 1, and software can write 1 to clear this bit.</description>
              <bitOffset>1</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ADMAT2</name>
              <description>I2C Address 2 Match Status\nWhen address 2 is matched, hardware will inform which address used. This bit will set to 1, and software can write 1 to clear this bit.</description>
              <bitOffset>2</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ADMAT3</name>
              <description>I2C Address 3 Match Status\nWhen address 3 is matched, hardware will inform which address used. This bit will set to 1, and software can write 1 to clear this bit.</description>
              <bitOffset>3</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>FULL</name>
              <description>TWO-LEVEL BUFFER FULL\nThis bit is set when POINTER is equal to 2\nNote: This bit is read only.</description>
              <bitOffset>4</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>EMPTY</name>
              <description>TWO-LEVEL BUFFER EMPTY\nThis bit is set when POINTER is equal to 0.\nNote: This bit is read only.</description>
              <bitOffset>5</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>OVR</name>
              <description>I2C Over Run Status Bit\nNote: This bit is read only.</description>
              <bitOffset>6</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>UDR</name>
              <description>I2C Under Run Status Bit\nNote: This bit is read only.</description>
              <bitOffset>7</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ONBUSY</name>
              <description>On Bus Busy (Read Only)\nIndicates that a communication is in progress on the bus. It is set by hardware when a START condition is detected. It is cleared by hardware when a STOP condition is detected.</description>
              <bitOffset>8</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>The bus is IDLE (both SCL and SDA High)</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>The bus is busy</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>DPCIF</name>
              <description>Data Phase Count Interrupt Flag\nThis bit is set by hardware when I2C transfer bit count equal to DPBITSEL setting \nThis bit is cleared by write 1 to it.</description>
              <bitOffset>20</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SARCIF</name>
              <description>Slave Address Read Command Interrupt Flag\nThis bit is set by hardware when I2C receive address match read command.\nThis bit is cleared by write 1 to it.</description>
              <bitOffset>21</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>I2C_TMCTL</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>I2C_TMCTL</displayName>
          <description>I2C Timing Configure Control Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x4C</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00020000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>STCTL</name>
              <description>Setup Time Configure Control\nThis field is used to generate a delay timing between SDA falling edge and SCL rising edge in transmission mode.\nNote: Setup time setting should not make SCL output less than three PCLKs.</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>9</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>HTCTL</name>
              <description>Hold Time Configure Control \nThis field is used to generate the delay timing between SCL falling edge and SDA rising edge in transmission mode.</description>
              <bitOffset>16</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>9</bitWidth>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>I2C_BUSCTL</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>I2C_BUSCTL</displayName>
          <description>I2C Bus Management Control Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x50</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>ACKMEN</name>
              <description>Acknowledge Control by Manual\nIn order to allow ACK control in slave reception including the command and data, slave byte control mode must be enabled by setting the ACKMEN bit.</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Slave byte control Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Slave byte control Enabled. The 9th bit can response the ACK or NACK according the received data by user. When the byte is received, stretching the SCL signal low between the 8th and 9th SCL pulse</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>PECEN</name>
              <description>Packet Error Checking Calculation Enable Bit\nNote: When I2C enter power-down mode, the bit should be enabled after wake-up if needed PEC calculation.</description>
              <bitOffset>1</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Packet Error Checking Calculation Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Packet Error Checking Calculation Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BMDEN</name>
              <description>Bus Management Device Default Address Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>2</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Device default address Disable. When the address 0'b1100001x comes and either BMDEN or ACKMEN is disabled, the device responses NACK</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Device default address Enabled. When the address 0'b1100001x comes and both BMDEN and ACKMEN are enabled, the device responses ACK</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BMHEN</name>
              <description>Bus Management Host Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>3</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Host function Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Host function Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ALERTEN</name>
              <description>Bus Management Alert Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>4</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Release the BM_ALERT pin high and Alert Response Header disabled: 0001100x followed by NACK if both of BMDEN and ACKMEN are enabled.\nBM_ALERT pin not supported</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Drive BM_ALERT pin low and Alert Response Address Header enables: 0001100x followed by ACK if both of BMDEN and ACKMEN are enabled.\nBM_ALERT pin supported</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SCTLOSTS</name>
              <description>Suspend/Control Data Output Status</description>
              <bitOffset>5</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>The output of SUSCON pin is low</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>The output of SUSCON pin is high</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SCTLOEN</name>
              <description>Suspend or Control Pin Output Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>6</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>The SUSCON pin in input</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>The output enable is active on the SUSCON pin</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BUSEN</name>
              <description>BUS Enable Bit\nNote: When the bit is enabled, the internal 14-bit counter is used to calculate the time out event of clock low condition.</description>
              <bitOffset>7</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>The system management function Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>The system management function Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>PECTXEN</name>
              <description>Packet Error Checking Byte Transmission/Reception</description>
              <bitOffset>8</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No PEC transfer</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>PEC transmission is requested</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TIDLE</name>
              <description>Timer Check in Idle State\nThe BUSTOUT is used to calculate the time-out of clock low in bus active and the idle period in bus Idle. This bit is used to define which condition is enabled.\nNote: The BUSY (I2C_BUSSTS[0]) indicate the current bus state.</description>
              <bitOffset>9</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>BUSTOUT is used to calculate the clock low period in bus active</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>BUSTOUT is used to calculate the IDLE period in bus Idle</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>PECCLR</name>
              <description>PEC Clear at Repeat Start\nThe calculation of PEC starts when PECEN is set to 1 and it is cleared when the STA or STO bit is detected. This PECCLR bit is used to enable the condition of REPEAT START can clear the PEC calculation.</description>
              <bitOffset>10</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>PEC calculation is cleared by 'Repeat Start' function Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>PEC calculation is cleared by 'Repeat Start' function Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ACKM9SI</name>
              <description>Acknowledge Manual Enable Extra SI Interrupt</description>
              <bitOffset>11</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>There is no SI interrupt in the 9th clock cycle when the BUSEN =1 and ACKMEN =1</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>There is SI interrupt in the 9th clock cycle when the BUSEN =1 and ACKMEN =1</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BCDIEN</name>
              <description>Packet Error Checking Byte Count Done Interrupt Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>12</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Byte count done interrupt Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Byte count done interrupt Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>PECDIEN</name>
              <description>Packet Error Checking Byte Transfer Done Interrupt Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>13</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>PEC transfer done interrupt Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>PEC transfer done interrupt Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>I2C_BUSTCTL</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>I2C_BUSTCTL</displayName>
          <description>I2C Bus Management Timer Control Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x54</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>BUSTOEN</name>
              <description>Bus Time Out Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Bus clock low time-out detection Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Bus clock low time-out detection Enabled (bus clock is low for more than BUSTO (I2C_BUSTOUT[7:0]) (in TIDLE=0) or high more than BUSTO (in TIDLE =1)</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>CLKTOEN</name>
              <description>Cumulative Clock Low Time Out Enable Bit\nFor Master, it calculates the period from START to ACK\nFor Slave, it calculates the period from START to STOP</description>
              <bitOffset>1</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Cumulative clock low time-out detection Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Cumulative clock low time-out detection Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BUSTOIEN</name>
              <description>Time-out Interrupt Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>2</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>SCL low time-out interrupt Disabled.\nBus IDLE time-out interrupt Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>SCL low time-out interrupt Enabled.\nBus IDLE time-out interrupt Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>CLKTOIEN</name>
              <description>Extended Clock Time Out Interrupt Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>3</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Clock time out interrupt Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Clock time out interrupt Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TORSTEN</name>
              <description>Time Out Reset Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>4</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>I2C state machine reset Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>I2C state machine reset Enabled. (The clock and data bus will be released to high)</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>I2C_BUSSTS</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>I2C_BUSSTS</displayName>
          <description>I2C Bus Management Status Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x58</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>BUSY</name>
              <description>Bus Busy\nIndicates that a communication is in progress on the bus. It is set by hardware when a START condition is detected. It is cleared by hardware when a STOP condition is detected</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Bus is IDLE (both SCL and SDA High)</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Bus is busy</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BCDONE</name>
              <description>Byte Count Transmission/Receive Done \nNote: Software can write 1 to clear this bit.</description>
              <bitOffset>1</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Byte count transmission/ receive is not finished when the PECEN is set</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Byte count transmission/ receive is finished when the PECEN is set</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>PECERR</name>
              <description>PEC Error in Reception \nNote: Software can write 1 to clear this bit.</description>
              <bitOffset>2</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>PEC value equal the received PEC data packet</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>PEC value doesn't match the receive PEC data packet</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ALERT</name>
              <description>SMBus Alert Status \nNote: 1. The SMBALERT pin is an open-drain pin, the pull-high resistor is must in the system. 2. Software can write 1 to clear this bit.</description>
              <bitOffset>3</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>SMBALERT pin state is low.\nNo SMBALERT event</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>SMBALERT pin state is high.\nThere is SMBALERT event (falling edge) is detected in SMALERT pin when the BMHEN = 1 (SMBus host configuration) and the ALERTEN = 1</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SCTLDIN</name>
              <description>Bus Suspend or Control Signal Input Status </description>
              <bitOffset>4</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>The input status of SUSCON pin is 0</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>The input status of SUSCON pin is 1</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BUSTO</name>
              <description>Bus Time-out Status \nIn bus busy, the bit indicates the total clock low time-out event occurred; otherwise, it indicates the bus idle time-out event occurred.\nNote: Software can write 1 to clear this bit.</description>
              <bitOffset>5</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>There is no any time-out or external clock time-out</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>A time-out or external clock time-out occurred</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>CLKTO</name>
              <description>Clock Low Cumulate Time-out Status \nNote: Software can write 1 to clear this bit.</description>
              <bitOffset>6</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Cumulative clock low is no any time-out</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Cumulative clock low time-out occurred</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>PECDONE</name>
              <description>PEC Byte Transmission/Receive Done \nNote: Software can write 1 to clear this bit.</description>
              <bitOffset>7</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>PEC transmission/ receive is not finished when the PECEN is set</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>PEC transmission/ receive is finished when the PECEN is set</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>I2C_PKTSIZE</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>I2C_PKTSIZE</displayName>
          <description>I2C Packet Error Checking Byte Number Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x5C</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>PLDSIZE</name>
              <description>Transfer Byte Number\nThe transmission or receive byte number in one transaction when the PECEN is set. The maximum transaction or receive byte is 256 Bytes.\nNote: The byte number counting includes address, command code, and data frame.</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>9</bitWidth>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>I2C_PKTCRC</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>I2C_PKTCRC</displayName>
          <description>I2C Packet Error Checking Byte Value Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x60</addressOffset>
          <access>read-only</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>PECCRC</name>
              <description>Packet Error Checking Byte Value</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>8</bitWidth>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>I2C_BUSTOUT</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>I2C_BUSTOUT</displayName>
          <description>I2C Bus Management Timer Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x64</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000005</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>BUSTO</name>
              <description>Bus Management Time-out Value\nIndicates the bus time-out value in bus is IDLE or SCL low.\nNote: If the user wants to revise the value of BUSTOUT, the TORSTEN (I2C_BUSTCTL[4]) bit shall be set to 1 and cleared to 0 first when the BUSEN(I2C_BUSCTL[7]) is set.</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>8</bitWidth>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>I2C_CLKTOUT</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>I2C_CLKTOUT</displayName>
          <description>I2C Bus Management Clock Low Timer Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x68</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000005</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>CLKTO</name>
              <description>Bus Clock Low Timer\nThe field is used to configure the cumulative clock extension time-out.\nNote: If the user wants to revise the value of CLKLTOUT, the TORSTEN bit shall be set to 1 and cleared to 0 first when the BUSEN is set.</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>8</bitWidth>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
      </registers>
    </peripheral>
    <peripheral>
      <name>I2C2</name>
      <description>I2C Register Map</description>
      <groupName>I2C</groupName>
      <baseAddress>0x40082000</baseAddress>
      <!-- d -> no access permission -->
      <addressBlock>
        <offset>0x0</offset>
        <size>0x34</size>
        <usage>registers</usage>
      </addressBlock>
      <addressBlock>
        <offset>0x3C</offset>
        <size>0x30</size>
        <usage>registers</usage>
      </addressBlock>
      <registers>
       <register>
          <name>I2C_CTL0</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>I2C_CTL0</displayName>
          <description>I2C Control Register 0</description>
          <addressOffset>0x0</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>AA</name>
              <description>Assert Acknowledge Control</description>
              <bitOffset>2</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SI</name>
              <description>I2C Interrupt Flag\nWhen a new I2C state is present in the I2C_STATUS0 register, the SI flag is set by hardware. If bit INTEN (I2C_CTL0 [7]) is set, the I2C interrupt is requested. SI must be cleared by software. Clear SI by writing 1 to this bit.\nFor ACKMEN is set in slave read mode, the SI flag is set in 8th clock period for user to confirm the acknowledge bit and 9th clock period for user to read the data in the data buffer.</description>
              <bitOffset>3</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>STO</name>
              <description>I2C STOP Control\nIn Master mode, setting STO to transmit a STOP condition to bus then I2C controller will check the bus condition if a STOP condition is detected. This bit will be cleared by hardware automatically.</description>
              <bitOffset>4</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>STA</name>
              <description>I2C START Control\nSetting STA to logic 1 to enter Master mode, the I2C hardware sends a START or repeat START condition to bus when the bus is free.</description>
              <bitOffset>5</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>I2CEN</name>
              <description>I2C Controller Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>6</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>I2C controller Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>I2C controller Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>INTEN</name>
              <description>Enable Interrupt</description>
              <bitOffset>7</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>I2C interrupt Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>I2C interrupt Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>I2C_ADDR0</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>I2C_ADDR0</displayName>
          <description>I2C Slave Address Register0</description>
          <addressOffset>0x4</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>GC</name>
              <description>General Call Function</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>General Call Function Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>General Call Function Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ADDR</name>
              <description>I2C Address \nThe content of this register is irrelevant when I2C is in Master mode. In the slave mode, the seven most significant bits must be loaded with the chip's own address. The I2C hardware will react if either of the address is matched.\nNote: When software set 10'h000, the address cannot be used.</description>
              <bitOffset>1</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>10</bitWidth>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>I2C_DAT</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>I2C_DAT</displayName>
          <description>I2C Data Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x8</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>DAT</name>
              <description>I2C Data \nBit [7:0] is located with the 8-bit transferred/received data of I2C serial port.</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>8</bitWidth>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>I2C_STATUS0</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>I2C_STATUS0</displayName>
          <description>I2C Status Register 0</description>
          <addressOffset>0xC</addressOffset>
          <access>read-only</access>
          <resetValue>0x000000F8</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>STATUS</name>
              <description>I2C Status</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>8</bitWidth>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>I2C_CLKDIV</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>I2C_CLKDIV</displayName>
          <description>I2C Clock Divided Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x10</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>DIVIDER</name>
              <description>I2C Clock Divided \nNote: The minimum value of DIVIDER is 4.</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>10</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>NFCNT</name>
              <description>Noise Filter Count \nThe register bits control the input filter width.\n0 : filter width 3*PCLK \n1 : filter width 4*PCLK\nN : filter width (3+N)*PCKL\nNote: Filter width Min :3*PCLK, Max : 18*PCLK</description>
              <bitOffset>12</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>4</bitWidth>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>I2C_TOCTL</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>I2C_TOCTL</displayName>
          <description>I2C Time-out Control Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x14</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>TOIF</name>
              <description>Time-out Interrupt Flag\nThis bit is set by hardware when I2C time out happened. Software can write 1 to clear this bit.</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TOCDIV4</name>
              <description>Time-out Counter Input Clock Divided by 4\nWhen enabled, the time-out period is extended 4 times.</description>
              <bitOffset>1</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Time-out period is extend 4 times Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Time-out period is extend 4 times Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TOCEN</name>
              <description>Time-out Counter Enable Bit\nWhen enabled, the 14-bit time-out counter will start counting when SI is cleared. Setting flag SI to '1' will reset counter and re-start up counting after SI is cleared.</description>
              <bitOffset>2</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Time-out counter Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Time-out counter Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TOBAUD</name>
              <description>Time-out Counter Input Clock Using Baud Rate Frequency\nWhen enabled, the time-out counter is using baud rate.</description>
              <bitOffset>3</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Time-out counter is using PCLK or PCLK/4</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Time-out counter is using IC baud rate</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TONIDLE</name>
              <description>Time-out Counter Disable in IDLE State\nWhen set 1,Time-out counter will stop.</description>
              <bitOffset>8</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Timeout counter enable when in IDLE state</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Timeout counter disable when in IDLE state</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register derivedFrom="I2C_ADDR0">
          <name>I2C_ADDR1</name>
          <displayName>I2C_ADDR1</displayName>
          <description>I2C Slave Address Register1</description>
          <addressOffset>0x18</addressOffset>
       </register>
       <register derivedFrom="I2C_ADDR0">
          <name>I2C_ADDR2</name>
          <displayName>I2C_ADDR2</displayName>
          <description>I2C Slave Address Register2</description>
          <addressOffset>0x1C</addressOffset>
       </register>
       <register derivedFrom="I2C_ADDR0">
          <name>I2C_ADDR3</name>
          <displayName>I2C_ADDR3</displayName>
          <description>I2C Slave Address Register3</description>
          <addressOffset>0x20</addressOffset>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>I2C_ADDRMSK0</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>I2C_ADDRMSK0</displayName>
          <description>I2C Slave Address Mask Register0</description>
          <addressOffset>0x24</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>ADDRMSK</name>
              <description>I2C Address Mask\nI2C bus controllers support multiple address recognition with four address mask register. When the bit in the address mask register is set to one, it means the received corresponding address bit is don't-care. If the bit is set to zero, that means the received corresponding register bit should be exact the same as address register.\nNote: The wake-up function cannot use address mask in 10-bit mode.</description>
              <bitOffset>1</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>10</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Mask Disabled (the received corresponding register bit should be exact the same as address register.)</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Mask Enabled (the received corresponding address bit is don't care.)</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register derivedFrom="I2C_ADDRMSK0">
          <name>I2C_ADDRMSK1</name>
          <displayName>I2C_ADDRMSK1</displayName>
          <description>I2C Slave Address Mask Register1</description>
          <addressOffset>0x28</addressOffset>
       </register>
       <register derivedFrom="I2C_ADDRMSK0">
          <name>I2C_ADDRMSK2</name>
          <displayName>I2C_ADDRMSK2</displayName>
          <description>I2C Slave Address Mask Register2</description>
          <addressOffset>0x2C</addressOffset>
       </register>
       <register derivedFrom="I2C_ADDRMSK0">
          <name>I2C_ADDRMSK3</name>
          <displayName>I2C_ADDRMSK3</displayName>
          <description>I2C Slave Address Mask Register3</description>
          <addressOffset>0x30</addressOffset>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>I2C_WKCTL</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>I2C_WKCTL</displayName>
          <description>I2C Wake-up Control Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x3C</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>WKEN</name>
              <description>I2C Wake-up Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>I2C wake-up function Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>I2C wake-up function Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>NHDBUSEN</name>
              <description>I2C No Hold BUS Enable Bit\nNote: The I2C controller could respond when WKIF event is not clear, it may cause error data transmitted or received. If data transmitted or received when WKIF event is not clear, user must reset I2C controller and execute the original operation again.</description>
              <bitOffset>7</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>I2C hold bus after wake-up</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>I2C don't hold bus after wake-up</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>I2C_WKSTS</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>I2C_WKSTS</displayName>
          <description>I2C Wake-up Status Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x40</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>WKIF</name>
              <description>I2C Wake-up Flag\nWhen chip is woken up from Power-down mode by I2C, this bit is set to 1. Software can write 1 to clear this bit.</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>WKAKDONE</name>
              <description>Wakeup Address Frame Acknowledge Bit Done\nNote: This bit can't release WKIF. Software can write 1 to clear this bit.</description>
              <bitOffset>1</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>The ACK bit cycle of address match frame isn't done</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>The ACK bit cycle of address match frame is done in power-down</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>WRSTSWK</name>
              <description>Read/Write Status Bit in Address Wakeup Frame\nNote: This bit will be cleared when software can write 1 to WKAKDONE bit.</description>
              <bitOffset>2</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Write command be record on the address match wakeup frame</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Read command be record on the address match wakeup frame</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>I2C_CTL1</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>I2C_CTL1</displayName>
          <description>I2C Control Register 1</description>
          <addressOffset>0x44</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>TXPDMAEN</name>
              <description>PDMA Transmit Channel Available </description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Transmit PDMA function Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Transmit PDMA function Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RXPDMAEN</name>
              <description>PDMA Receive Channel Available </description>
              <bitOffset>1</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Receive PDMA function Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Receive PDMA function Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>PDMARST</name>
              <description>PDMA Reset </description>
              <bitOffset>2</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No effect</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Reset the I2C request to PDMA</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>OVRIEN</name>
              <description>I2C Over Run Interrupt Control Bit\nSetting OVRIEN to logic 1 will send a interrupt to system when the TWOBUFEN bit is enabled and there is over run event in received buffer.</description>
              <bitOffset>3</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>UDRIEN</name>
              <description>I2C Under Run Interrupt Control Bit\nSetting UDRIEN to logic 1 will send a interrupt to system when the TWOBUFEN bit is enabled and there is under run event happened in transmitted buffer.</description>
              <bitOffset>4</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TWOBUFEN</name>
              <description>Two-level BUFFER Enable Bit\nSet to enable the two-level buffer for I2C transmitted or received buffer. It is used to improve the performance of the I2C bus.</description>
              <bitOffset>5</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Two-level buffer Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Two-level buffer Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>PDMASTR</name>
              <description>PDMA Stretch Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>8</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>I2C send STOP automatically after PDMA transfer done. (only master TX)</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>I2C SCL bus is stretched by hardware after PDMA transfer done if the SI is not cleared. (only master TX)</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ADDR10EN</name>
              <description>Address 10-bit Function Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>9</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Address match 10-bit function Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Address match 10-bit function Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SWITCHEN</name>
              <description>SCL And SDA Pin Switch Enable Bit\nNote: Original pin configuration table is shown in Basic Configuration chapter.</description>
              <bitOffset>10</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>IC use original pin configuration</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>IC switch SCL and SDA pin configuration</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>DPBITSEL</name>
              <description>Data Phase Bit Count Select</description>
              <bitOffset>16</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>2</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>DPCIF never set by hardware</description>
                    <value>#00</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>When I2C is transfer data and bit count equal to 6, DPCIF will be set by hardware</description>
                    <value>#01</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>2</name>
                    <description>When I2C is transfer data and bit count equal to 7, DPCIF will be set by hardware</description>
                    <value>#10</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>3</name>
                    <description>When I2C is transfer data and bit count equal to 8, DPCIF will be set by hardware</description>
                    <value>#11</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>DPCINTEN</name>
              <description>Data Phase Count Interrupt Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>20</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Data Phase Count Interrupt Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Data Phase Count Interrupt Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SRCINTEN</name>
              <description>Slave Read Command Interrupt Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>21</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Slave Read Command Interrupt Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Slave Read Command Interrupt Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>I2C_STATUS1</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>I2C_STATUS1</displayName>
          <description>I2C Status Register 1</description>
          <addressOffset>0x48</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>ADMAT0</name>
              <description>I2C Address 0 Match Status\nWhen address 0 is matched, hardware will inform which address used. This bit will set to 1, and software can write 1 to clear this bit.</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ADMAT1</name>
              <description>I2C Address 1 Match Status\nWhen address 1 is matched, hardware will inform which address used. This bit will set to 1, and software can write 1 to clear this bit.</description>
              <bitOffset>1</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ADMAT2</name>
              <description>I2C Address 2 Match Status\nWhen address 2 is matched, hardware will inform which address used. This bit will set to 1, and software can write 1 to clear this bit.</description>
              <bitOffset>2</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ADMAT3</name>
              <description>I2C Address 3 Match Status\nWhen address 3 is matched, hardware will inform which address used. This bit will set to 1, and software can write 1 to clear this bit.</description>
              <bitOffset>3</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>FULL</name>
              <description>TWO-LEVEL BUFFER FULL\nThis bit is set when POINTER is equal to 2\nNote: This bit is read only.</description>
              <bitOffset>4</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>EMPTY</name>
              <description>TWO-LEVEL BUFFER EMPTY\nThis bit is set when POINTER is equal to 0.\nNote: This bit is read only.</description>
              <bitOffset>5</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>OVR</name>
              <description>I2C Over Run Status Bit\nNote: This bit is read only.</description>
              <bitOffset>6</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>UDR</name>
              <description>I2C Under Run Status Bit\nNote: This bit is read only.</description>
              <bitOffset>7</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ONBUSY</name>
              <description>On Bus Busy (Read Only)\nIndicates that a communication is in progress on the bus. It is set by hardware when a START condition is detected. It is cleared by hardware when a STOP condition is detected.</description>
              <bitOffset>8</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>The bus is IDLE (both SCL and SDA High)</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>The bus is busy</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>DPCIF</name>
              <description>Data Phase Count Interrupt Flag\nThis bit is set by hardware when I2C transfer bit count equal to DPBITSEL setting \nThis bit is cleared by write 1 to it.</description>
              <bitOffset>20</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SARCIF</name>
              <description>Slave Address Read Command Interrupt Flag\nThis bit is set by hardware when I2C receive address match read command.\nThis bit is cleared by write 1 to it.</description>
              <bitOffset>21</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>I2C_TMCTL</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>I2C_TMCTL</displayName>
          <description>I2C Timing Configure Control Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x4C</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00020000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>STCTL</name>
              <description>Setup Time Configure Control\nThis field is used to generate a delay timing between SDA falling edge and SCL rising edge in transmission mode.\nNote: Setup time setting should not make SCL output less than three PCLKs.</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>9</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>HTCTL</name>
              <description>Hold Time Configure Control \nThis field is used to generate the delay timing between SCL falling edge and SDA rising edge in transmission mode.</description>
              <bitOffset>16</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>9</bitWidth>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>I2C_BUSCTL</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>I2C_BUSCTL</displayName>
          <description>I2C Bus Management Control Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x50</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>ACKMEN</name>
              <description>Acknowledge Control by Manual\nIn order to allow ACK control in slave reception including the command and data, slave byte control mode must be enabled by setting the ACKMEN bit.</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Slave byte control Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Slave byte control Enabled. The 9th bit can response the ACK or NACK according the received data by user. When the byte is received, stretching the SCL signal low between the 8th and 9th SCL pulse</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>PECEN</name>
              <description>Packet Error Checking Calculation Enable Bit\nNote: When I2C enter power-down mode, the bit should be enabled after wake-up if needed PEC calculation.</description>
              <bitOffset>1</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Packet Error Checking Calculation Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Packet Error Checking Calculation Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BMDEN</name>
              <description>Bus Management Device Default Address Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>2</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Device default address Disable. When the address 0'b1100001x comes and either BMDEN or ACKMEN is disabled, the device responses NACK</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Device default address Enabled. When the address 0'b1100001x comes and both BMDEN and ACKMEN are enabled, the device responses ACK</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BMHEN</name>
              <description>Bus Management Host Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>3</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Host function Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Host function Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ALERTEN</name>
              <description>Bus Management Alert Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>4</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Release the BM_ALERT pin high and Alert Response Header disabled: 0001100x followed by NACK if both of BMDEN and ACKMEN are enabled.\nBM_ALERT pin not supported</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Drive BM_ALERT pin low and Alert Response Address Header enables: 0001100x followed by ACK if both of BMDEN and ACKMEN are enabled.\nBM_ALERT pin supported</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SCTLOSTS</name>
              <description>Suspend/Control Data Output Status</description>
              <bitOffset>5</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>The output of SUSCON pin is low</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>The output of SUSCON pin is high</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SCTLOEN</name>
              <description>Suspend or Control Pin Output Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>6</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>The SUSCON pin in input</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>The output enable is active on the SUSCON pin</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BUSEN</name>
              <description>BUS Enable Bit\nNote: When the bit is enabled, the internal 14-bit counter is used to calculate the time out event of clock low condition.</description>
              <bitOffset>7</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>The system management function Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>The system management function Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>PECTXEN</name>
              <description>Packet Error Checking Byte Transmission/Reception</description>
              <bitOffset>8</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No PEC transfer</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>PEC transmission is requested</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TIDLE</name>
              <description>Timer Check in Idle State\nThe BUSTOUT is used to calculate the time-out of clock low in bus active and the idle period in bus Idle. This bit is used to define which condition is enabled.\nNote: The BUSY (I2C_BUSSTS[0]) indicate the current bus state.</description>
              <bitOffset>9</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>BUSTOUT is used to calculate the clock low period in bus active</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>BUSTOUT is used to calculate the IDLE period in bus Idle</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>PECCLR</name>
              <description>PEC Clear at Repeat Start\nThe calculation of PEC starts when PECEN is set to 1 and it is cleared when the STA or STO bit is detected. This PECCLR bit is used to enable the condition of REPEAT START can clear the PEC calculation.</description>
              <bitOffset>10</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>PEC calculation is cleared by 'Repeat Start' function Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>PEC calculation is cleared by 'Repeat Start' function Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ACKM9SI</name>
              <description>Acknowledge Manual Enable Extra SI Interrupt</description>
              <bitOffset>11</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>There is no SI interrupt in the 9th clock cycle when the BUSEN =1 and ACKMEN =1</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>There is SI interrupt in the 9th clock cycle when the BUSEN =1 and ACKMEN =1</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BCDIEN</name>
              <description>Packet Error Checking Byte Count Done Interrupt Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>12</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Byte count done interrupt Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Byte count done interrupt Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>PECDIEN</name>
              <description>Packet Error Checking Byte Transfer Done Interrupt Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>13</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>PEC transfer done interrupt Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>PEC transfer done interrupt Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>I2C_BUSTCTL</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>I2C_BUSTCTL</displayName>
          <description>I2C Bus Management Timer Control Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x54</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>BUSTOEN</name>
              <description>Bus Time Out Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Bus clock low time-out detection Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Bus clock low time-out detection Enabled (bus clock is low for more than BUSTO (I2C_BUSTOUT[7:0]) (in TIDLE=0) or high more than BUSTO (in TIDLE =1)</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>CLKTOEN</name>
              <description>Cumulative Clock Low Time Out Enable Bit\nFor Master, it calculates the period from START to ACK\nFor Slave, it calculates the period from START to STOP</description>
              <bitOffset>1</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Cumulative clock low time-out detection Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Cumulative clock low time-out detection Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BUSTOIEN</name>
              <description>Time-out Interrupt Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>2</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>SCL low time-out interrupt Disabled.\nBus IDLE time-out interrupt Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>SCL low time-out interrupt Enabled.\nBus IDLE time-out interrupt Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>CLKTOIEN</name>
              <description>Extended Clock Time Out Interrupt Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>3</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Clock time out interrupt Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Clock time out interrupt Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TORSTEN</name>
              <description>Time Out Reset Enable Bit</description>
              <bitOffset>4</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>I2C state machine reset Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>I2C state machine reset Enabled. (The clock and data bus will be released to high)</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>I2C_BUSSTS</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>I2C_BUSSTS</displayName>
          <description>I2C Bus Management Status Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x58</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>BUSY</name>
              <description>Bus Busy\nIndicates that a communication is in progress on the bus. It is set by hardware when a START condition is detected. It is cleared by hardware when a STOP condition is detected</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Bus is IDLE (both SCL and SDA High)</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Bus is busy</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BCDONE</name>
              <description>Byte Count Transmission/Receive Done \nNote: Software can write 1 to clear this bit.</description>
              <bitOffset>1</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Byte count transmission/ receive is not finished when the PECEN is set</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Byte count transmission/ receive is finished when the PECEN is set</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>PECERR</name>
              <description>PEC Error in Reception \nNote: Software can write 1 to clear this bit.</description>
              <bitOffset>2</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>PEC value equal the received PEC data packet</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>PEC value doesn't match the receive PEC data packet</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ALERT</name>
              <description>SMBus Alert Status \nNote: 1. The SMBALERT pin is an open-drain pin, the pull-high resistor is must in the system. 2. Software can write 1 to clear this bit.</description>
              <bitOffset>3</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>SMBALERT pin state is low.\nNo SMBALERT event</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>SMBALERT pin state is high.\nThere is SMBALERT event (falling edge) is detected in SMALERT pin when the BMHEN = 1 (SMBus host configuration) and the ALERTEN = 1</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SCTLDIN</name>
              <description>Bus Suspend or Control Signal Input Status </description>
              <bitOffset>4</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>The input status of SUSCON pin is 0</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>The input status of SUSCON pin is 1</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BUSTO</name>
              <description>Bus Time-out Status \nIn bus busy, the bit indicates the total clock low time-out event occurred; otherwise, it indicates the bus idle time-out event occurred.\nNote: Software can write 1 to clear this bit.</description>
              <bitOffset>5</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>There is no any time-out or external clock time-out</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>A time-out or external clock time-out occurred</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>CLKTO</name>
              <description>Clock Low Cumulate Time-out Status \nNote: Software can write 1 to clear this bit.</description>
              <bitOffset>6</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Cumulative clock low is no any time-out</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Cumulative clock low time-out occurred</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>PECDONE</name>
              <description>PEC Byte Transmission/Receive Done \nNote: Software can write 1 to clear this bit.</description>
              <bitOffset>7</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>PEC transmission/ receive is not finished when the PECEN is set</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>PEC transmission/ receive is finished when the PECEN is set</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>I2C_PKTSIZE</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>I2C_PKTSIZE</displayName>
          <description>I2C Packet Error Checking Byte Number Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x5C</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>PLDSIZE</name>
              <description>Transfer Byte Number\nThe transmission or receive byte number in one transaction when the PECEN is set. The maximum transaction or receive byte is 256 Bytes.\nNote: The byte number counting includes address, command code, and data frame.</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>9</bitWidth>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>I2C_PKTCRC</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>I2C_PKTCRC</displayName>
          <description>I2C Packet Error Checking Byte Value Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x60</addressOffset>
          <access>read-only</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>PECCRC</name>
              <description>Packet Error Checking Byte Value</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>8</bitWidth>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>I2C_BUSTOUT</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>I2C_BUSTOUT</displayName>
          <description>I2C Bus Management Timer Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x64</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000005</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>BUSTO</name>
              <description>Bus Management Time-out Value\nIndicates the bus time-out value in bus is IDLE or SCL low.\nNote: If the user wants to revise the value of BUSTOUT, the TORSTEN (I2C_BUSTCTL[4]) bit shall be set to 1 and cleared to 0 first when the BUSEN(I2C_BUSCTL[7]) is set.</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>8</bitWidth>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>I2C_CLKTOUT</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>I2C_CLKTOUT</displayName>
          <description>I2C Bus Management Clock Low Timer Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x68</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000005</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>CLKTO</name>
              <description>Bus Clock Low Timer\nThe field is used to configure the cumulative clock extension time-out.\nNote: If the user wants to revise the value of CLKLTOUT, the TORSTEN bit shall be set to 1 and cleared to 0 first when the BUSEN is set.</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>8</bitWidth>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
      </registers>
    </peripheral>
    <peripheral>
      <name>I3C0</name>
      <description>I3C Register Map</description>
      <groupName>I3C</groupName>
      <baseAddress>0x40086000</baseAddress>
      <!-- d -> no access permission -->
      <addressBlock>
        <offset>0x0</offset>
        <size>0x8</size>
        <usage>registers</usage>
      </addressBlock>
      <addressBlock>
        <offset>0xC</offset>
        <size>0x1C</size>
        <usage>registers</usage>
      </addressBlock>
      <addressBlock>
        <offset>0x34</offset>
        <size>0x14</size>
        <usage>registers</usage>
      </addressBlock>
      <addressBlock>
        <offset>0x4C</offset>
        <size>0x10</size>
        <usage>registers</usage>
      </addressBlock>
      <addressBlock>
        <offset>0x70</offset>
        <size>0x10</size>
        <usage>registers</usage>
      </addressBlock>
      <addressBlock>
        <offset>0x84</offset>
        <size>0x4</size>
        <usage>registers</usage>
      </addressBlock>
      <addressBlock>
        <offset>0x8C</offset>
        <size>0x4</size>
        <usage>registers</usage>
      </addressBlock>
      <addressBlock>
        <offset>0x94</offset>
        <size>0x8</size>
        <usage>registers</usage>
      </addressBlock>
      <addressBlock>
        <offset>0xA4</offset>
        <size>0x4</size>
        <usage>registers</usage>
      </addressBlock>
      <addressBlock>
        <offset>0xB0</offset>
        <size>0x14</size>
        <usage>registers</usage>
      </addressBlock>
      <addressBlock>
        <offset>0xC8</offset>
        <size>0x18</size>
        <usage>registers</usage>
      </addressBlock>
      <addressBlock>
        <offset>0xEC</offset>
        <size>0x4</size>
        <usage>registers</usage>
      </addressBlock>
      <addressBlock>
        <offset>0xF4</offset>
        <size>0x4</size>
        <usage>registers</usage>
      </addressBlock>
      <addressBlock>
        <offset>0x130</offset>
        <size>0xA0</size>
        <usage>registers</usage>
      </addressBlock>
      <addressBlock>
        <offset>0x1E0</offset>
        <size>0x10</size>
        <usage>registers</usage>
      </addressBlock>
      <addressBlock>
        <offset>0x200</offset>
        <size>0x70</size>
        <usage>registers</usage>
      </addressBlock>
      <addressBlock>
        <offset>0x280</offset>
        <size>0x1C</size>
        <usage>registers</usage>
      </addressBlock>
      <addressBlock>
        <offset>0x500</offset>
        <size>0x10</size>
        <usage>registers</usage>
      </addressBlock>
      <addressBlock>
        <offset>0x520</offset>
        <size>0x10</size>
        <usage>registers</usage>
      </addressBlock>
      <addressBlock>
        <offset>0x540</offset>
        <size>0x10</size>
        <usage>registers</usage>
      </addressBlock>
      <addressBlock>
        <offset>0x560</offset>
        <size>0x10</size>
        <usage>registers</usage>
      </addressBlock>
      <registers>
       <register>
          <name>I3C_DEVCTL</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>I3C_DEVCTL</displayName>
          <description>I3C Device Control Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x0</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00010000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>IBAINCL</name>
              <description>I3C Broadcast Address Include\nThis bit is used in Controller mode of operation.\nNote: If I3C broadcast address is not included for the private transfers, In-Band Interrupts (IBI) driven from Targets might not win address arbitration. Hence, the IBIs get delayed.</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>I3C Broadcast Address (0x7E) is not included for Private Transfer</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>I3C Broadcast Address (0x7E) is included for Private Transfers</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>PENDINT</name>
              <description>Pending Interrupt Status\nThis field is used in Target mode of operation. \nThis field is used for setting the interrupt number and is also reflected in I3C_CCCDEVS[3:0] PENDINT.</description>
              <bitOffset>3</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>4</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>HJCTL</name>
              <description>Hot-Join ACK/NACK Control \nThis bit is used in Controller mode of operation.\nThis bit acts as a global control to ACK/NACK the Hot-Join request from the devices. The I3C Controller ACK/NACKs the Hot-Join request from other devices connected on the I3C bus, based on programming of this bit.</description>
              <bitOffset>8</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>ACK the Hot-join request</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>NACK and send broadcast DISEC CCC to disable Hot-Join of Target devices</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TSCEN</name>
              <description>Timestamp Counter Enable Bit\nThis bit is used in Controller mode of operation.</description>
              <bitOffset>11</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Timestamp counter Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Timestamp counter Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TSCAUTO</name>
              <description>Timestamp Counter Automatic Enable Bit\nThis bit is used in Target mode of operation. \nWhen SIR is Enabled, Timestamp Counter will be cleared and restarted. </description>
              <bitOffset>12</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Timestamp Counter automatic Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Timestamp Counter automatic Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TSCCLR</name>
              <description>Timestamp Counter Clear Bit\nThis bit is used in Controller mode of operation.\nNote: This bit is cleared by hardware automatically.</description>
              <bitOffset>15</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Clear timestamp counter Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Clear Clear timestamp counter Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>IBIPSIZE</name>
              <description>Target Maximum IBI Payload Size\nThis value reflects the I3C Target IBI Payload Size if the Controller sends a GETMRL/SETMRL CCC.\nNote: This field is read only.</description>
              <bitOffset>16</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>8</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>IDLECNT</name>
              <description>Idle Count Multiplier \nThis field is used in Target mode of operation. \nAfter power-on reset, the Target device is enabled only after it sees both SDA and SCL lines idle for a specified time. \nThis idle time is calculated by multiplying IDLECNT with AVAILTC field in BUSFAT[31:16].</description>
              <bitOffset>24</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>2</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TPECEN</name>
              <description>Target Packet Error Check Enable Bit\nTarget application to enable Packet Error Check. The user programs this bit before enabling the Target.</description>
              <bitOffset>26</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>PEC support is Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>PEC support is Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ADAPTIVE</name>
              <description>Adaptive Mode\nThis bit is used in Target mode of operation. \nSetting of this bit is NOT required to put the Target in Adaptive Mode. It is only used to enable some features of the Target device to adapt to Adaptive I2C/I3C mode  of operation. \nThis bit is cleared automatically if the Target determines the mode as I3C. \nEffect on Hot-Join operation:</description>
              <bitOffset>27</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>The Target initiates a Hot- Join without determining the bus mode assuming itself to be on i3c bus. This bit should be set only if the Target application does not know to which bus the device is connected to.</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>The Target initiates a Hot-Join request only after it has switched to I3C mode of operation</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>DMAEN</name>
              <description>DMA Handshake Interface Enable </description>
              <bitOffset>28</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>The DMA handshake control has no significance</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Enables the DMA handshake control to interact with external DMA</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ABORT</name>
              <description>Abort Controller Operation\nThis bit is used in Controller mode of operation. \nThis bit allows the Controller to relinquish the I3C bus before completing the issued transfer In response to an ABORT request, the Controller issues the STOP condition after the complete data byte is transferred or received.\nThis bit is auto-cleared once the transfer is aborted and the Controller issues a 'Transfer Abort' interrupt (I3C_INTSTS[5]).</description>
              <bitOffset>29</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No effect</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Trigger ABORT request</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RESUME</name>
              <description>I3C Resume Bit\nThis bit is used to resume the Controller/Target after it goes to the halt state.\nNote 1: In the Controller mode of operation, the Controller goes to the halt state due to any type of error in the transfer. \nAfter the Controller gones to the halt state, the application has to write 1'b1 to this bit to resume the Controller. \nThis bit is auto-cleared once the Controller resumes the transfers by initiating the next command.\nNote 2: In the Target mode of operation, the Target goes to the halt state due to following conditions:\nAny type of error in the transfer. \nMRL in I3C_SLVMXLEN[31:16] updated by the controller through SETMRL CCC.\nAfter the Target goes to the halt state, the application has to take necessary action to handle the error condition and then write 1'b1 to this bit to resume the Target. \nThis bit is auto-cleared once the Target is ready to accept new transfers (If Target has Protocol Error, RESUME bit cannot auto-clear until GETSTATS CCC is received).</description>
              <bitOffset>30</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Resume operation is completion</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Resume operation is in progress</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ENABLE</name>
              <description>Controls whether or not I3C is Enabled\nNote 1: To Enable the Target controller, the application must set the ENABLE bit to 1. \nEnabling the Target controller might not be instantaneous because it is dependent on the availability of SCL clock on the I3C line.\nNote 2: To Disable the Target controller, the application after setting the value of 0 to the ENABLE bit must keep polling the ENABLE bit to check whether the bit value is zero. \nNote 3: Once the ENABLE bit is read with a value of zero, the application can be sure that the Target controller is disabled. If the SCL clock is present, the Target controller takes two SCL clocks to come out of the Disable state.</description>
              <bitOffset>31</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>I3C controller Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>I3C controller Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>I3C_DEVADDR</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>I3C_DEVADDR</displayName>
          <description>I3C Device Address Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x4</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00008000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>SA</name>
              <description>Device Static Address\nThe Target device uses this address to respond to SETDASA or SETAASA CCC to get the Dynamic Address or performs Legacy I2C Read/Write operation.</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>7</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SAVALID</name>
              <description>Static Address Valid\nIn Target mode of operation, this bit reflects whether the value of the static address is valid or not. </description>
              <bitOffset>15</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Static Address is invalid</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Static Address is valid</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>DA</name>
              <description>Device Dynamic Address\nIn Controller mode, the user has to program the Dynamic Address through the Target interface as it self-assigns its Dynamic Address.\nIn Target mode, this field is used to indicate the device Dynamic Address after Target completes the dynamic address assignment operation by ENTDAA, SETDASA, SETAASA or SETNEDA CCC.</description>
              <bitOffset>16</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>7</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>DAVALID</name>
              <description>Dynamic Address Valid \nIn I3C Controller mode, the user sets this bit to 1 as it self-assigns its dynamic address.\nIn I3C Target mode, this bit will be set to 1 when the Main Controller assigns the dynamic address during ENTDAA, SETDASA or SETAASA mechanism.\nNote: This bit can be auto-clear to 0 if RSTDAA CCC is reveived.</description>
              <bitOffset>31</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>I3C Dynamic Address is invalid</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>I3C Dynamic Address is valid</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>I3C_CMDQUE</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>I3C_CMDQUE</displayName>
          <description>I3C Command Queue Port Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0xC</addressOffset>
          <access>write-only</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>CMDQUE</name>
              <description>Command Queue\nThis field is used in Controller mode of operation.\nPlease refer to chapter Controller Command Data Structure.</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>32</bitWidth>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>I3C_RESPQUE</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>I3C_RESPQUE</displayName>
          <description>I3C Response Queue Port Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x10</addressOffset>
          <access>read-only</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>RESPQUE</name>
              <description>Response Queue\nPlease refer to chapter Controller Response Data Structure and chapter Extended Response Data Structure.</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>32</bitWidth>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>I3C_TXRXDAT</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>I3C_TXRXDAT</displayName>
          <description>I3C Transmit and Receive Data Port Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x14</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>DAT</name>
              <description>Transmit and Receive Data Port\nTransmit (Write) Operation:\nThe transmit operation is used in Controller mode of operation.\nThe Transmit Data port is mapped to the Tx-Data Buffer. The transmit data should always be packed as 4-byte aligned data words and written to the Transmit Data Port register. If the Command length is not aligned to 4-bytes, then the additional bytes are ignored.\nReceive (Read) Operation:\nThe receive operation is used in both Controller mode and Target mode.\nThe Receive Data port is mapped to the Rx-Data Buffer. The receive data is always packed in 4-byte aligned data words and stored in the Rx-Data Buffer. If the command length is not aligned to the 4-bytes, then the additional data bytes have to be ignored.</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>32</bitWidth>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>I3C_IBIQSTS</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>I3C_IBIQSTS</displayName>
          <description>I3C Controller In-Band Interrupt Queue Status Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x18</addressOffset>
          <access>read-only</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>DATLEN</name>
              <description>In-Band Interrupt Data Length \nThis field represents the length of data received along with the IBI, in bytes.</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>8</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>IBIID</name>
              <description>IBI Identifier \nThe byte received after START which includes the address and the R/W bit. \nDevice address and R/W bit in case of Target Interrupt or Controller Request.\nHot-Join ID (0x2) and R/W bit in case of Hot-Join request.</description>
              <bitOffset>8</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>8</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>IBISTS</name>
              <description>IBI Received Status \nDefines the Controller response for IBI received. </description>
              <bitOffset>28</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>4</bitWidth>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>I3C_IBIQUE</name>
          <alternateRegister>I3C_IBIQSTS</alternateRegister>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>I3C_IBIQUE</displayName>
          <description>I3C Controller In-Band Interrupt Queue Data Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x18</addressOffset>
          <access>read-only</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>IBIDAT</name>
              <description>In-Band Interrupt Data \nThis register when read from, reads the data from the IBI Queue.\nThis register is mapped to the IBI Queue. The IBI Data is always packed in 4-byte aligned and put to the IBI Queue. If the incoming data is not aligned to the 4-bytes, then there are unused bytes in the end location of the IBI transfer.</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>32</bitWidth>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>I3C_QUETHCTL</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>I3C_QUETHCTL</displayName>
          <description>I3C Queue Threshold Control Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x1C</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x01000101</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>CMDETH</name>
              <description>Command Buffer Empty Threshold Value\nControls the number of empty locations (or greater) in the Command Queue that trigger CMDRDY (I3C_INTSTS[3]) interrupt.\nThe valid range is 0 to 7. \nThe user programs only valid values. Value of N ranging from 1 to 7 sets the threshold to N empty locations and a value of 0 sets the threshold to indicate that the queue is completely empty.</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>8</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RESPTH</name>
              <description>Response Buffer Threshold Value\nControls the number of entries (or greater) in the Response Queue that trigger the RESPRDY (I3C_INTSTS[4]) interrupt.\nThe valid range is 0 to 3.\nThe user programs only valid values. A value of 0 sets the threshold for 1 entry, and a value of N sets the threshold for N+1 entries.</description>
              <bitOffset>8</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>8</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>IBIDATTH</name>
              <description>IBI Data Threshold Value \nThis field represents the IBI data segment size in DWORDS, 4 bytes.\nThe valid range is 0 to 15.\nThe minimum supported segment size is 1 (4 bytes) and the maximum supported size is 16 (64 bytes). The IBIDATTH field enables the slicing of the incoming IBI data and generate individual status and thereby promotes the cut-through operation in reading out the IBI data.</description>
              <bitOffset>16</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>8</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>IBISTSTH</name>
              <description>In-Band Interrupt Status Threshold Value\nEvery In-Band Interrupt received by I3C Controller generates an IBI status.\nThe valid range is 0 to 3.\nThis field controls the number of IBI status entries (or greater) in the IBI queue that trigger the IBITH (I3C_INTSTS[2]) interrupt. The user programs only valid values. A value of 0 sets the threshold for 1 entry, and a value of N sets the threshold for N+1 entries. </description>
              <bitOffset>24</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>8</bitWidth>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>I3C_DBTHCTL</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>I3C_DBTHCTL</displayName>
          <description>I3C Data Buffer Threshold Control Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x20</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x01010101</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>TXTH</name>
              <description>Transmit Buffer Threshold Value\nThis field controls the number of empty locations (or above) in the Transmit FIFO that trigger the TXTH (I3C_INTSTS[0])  interrupt. \nThe supported values for TXTH are:\nNote: Unit in DWORDS, 4 bytes.</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>3</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RXTH</name>
              <description>Receive Buffer Threshold Value\nThis field controls the number of entries (or above) in the Receive FIFO that trigger the RXTH (I3C_INTSTS[1]) interrupt.\nThe supported values for RXTH are:\nNote: Unit in DWORDS, 4 bytes.</description>
              <bitOffset>8</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>3</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TXSTATH</name>
              <description>Transfer Start Threshold Value \nIn Controller mode of operation. \nWhen the Controller is set up to initiate a write transfer, it waits until either one of the following conditions are met before it initiates the write transfer on the I3C Interface.\nData length (as specified in the command) number of locations are filled in the Transmit FIFO.\nThreshold number of entries (or more) are available in the Transmit FIFO. \nIn Target mode of operation. \nThe Target ACK's a read request from Controller only if either one of the following conditions are met:\nData length (as specified in the command) number of locations are filled in the Transmit FIFO.\nThreshold number of entries (or more) are available in the Transmit FIFO. \nThe supported values for TXSTATH are:\nNote: Unit in DWORDS, 4 bytes.</description>
              <bitOffset>16</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>3</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RXSTATH</name>
              <description>Receive Start Threshold Value \nIn Controller mode of operation. \nWhen the Controller is set up to initiate a read transfer, it waits until either one of the conditions are met before it initiates the read transfer on the I3C Interface. \nData length (as specified in the command) number of locations are empty in the Receive FIFO.\nThreshold number of locations (or more) are empty in the Receive FIFO. \nIn Target mode of operation.\nThe Target ACK's a write request from Controller only if threshold number of empty locations (or more) are available in its receive buffer.\nThe supported values for RXSTATH are:\nNote: Unit in DWORDS, 4 bytes.</description>
              <bitOffset>24</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>3</bitWidth>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>I3C_IBIQCTL</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>I3C_IBIQCTL</displayName>
          <description>I3C Controller In-Band Interrupt Queue Control Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x24</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>HJREJ</name>
              <description>Notify Rejected Hot-Join Request Control \nThis bit is used to suppress reporting to the application about Hot-Join request rejected. </description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Notify Hot-Join Request Rejected Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Notify Hot-Join Request Rejected Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>MRREJ</name>
              <description>Notify Rejected Controller Request Control\nThis bit is used to suppress reporting to the application about Controller request rejected.</description>
              <bitOffset>1</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Notify Controller Request Rejected Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Notify Controller Request Rejected Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SIRREJ</name>
              <description>Notify Rejected Target Interrupt Request Control \nThis bit is used to suppress reporting to the application about SIR request rejected. </description>
              <bitOffset>3</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Notify SIR Rejected Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Notify SIR Rejected Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>I3C_RSTCTL</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>I3C_RSTCTL</displayName>
          <description>I3C Reset Control Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x34</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>SWRST</name>
              <description>Core Software Reset\nThis reset:\nThe Queues and Buffers.\nUser programmable registers to reflect the Reset values. \nPipelining data (if any).\nThe state machine to Idle state. \nNote: This bit is cleared automatically once the core reset is completed.</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No effect</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Perform Software reset</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>CMDRST</name>
              <description>Command Queue Software Reset\nNote: This bit is cleared automatically once the Command Queue reset is completed.</description>
              <bitOffset>1</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No effect</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Perform Command Queue reset</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RESPRST</name>
              <description>Response Queue Software Reset\nNote: This bit is cleared automatically once the Response Queue reset is completed.</description>
              <bitOffset>2</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No effect</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Perform Response Queue reset</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TXRST</name>
              <description>Transmit Buffer Software Reset\nNote: This bit is cleared automatically once the Transmit Buffer reset is completed.</description>
              <bitOffset>3</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No effect</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Perform Transmit Buffer reset</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RXRST</name>
              <description>Receive Buffer Software Reset\nNote: This bit is cleared automatically once the Receive buffer reset is completed.</description>
              <bitOffset>4</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No effect</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Perform Receive Buffer reset</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>IBIQRST</name>
              <description>IBI Queue Software Reset \nThis bit is used in Controller mode of operation. \nNote: This bit is cleared automatically once the IBI Queue reset is completed.</description>
              <bitOffset>5</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No effect</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Perform IBI Queue reset</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BUSRSTPT</name>
              <description>Bus Reset Pattern Type \nThis field is used in Controller mode of operation. \nType of bus reset pattern triggered by BUSRST. </description>
              <bitOffset>28</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>3</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BUSRST</name>
              <description>Bus Reset \nThis bit is used in Controller mode of operation. \nNote: This bit is cleared automatically once the Bus Reset Pattern Generation is completed.</description>
              <bitOffset>31</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No effect</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Perform Bus Reset Pattern Generation based on Bus Reset Type (BUSRSTY) selection</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>I3C_SLVEVNTS</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>I3C_SLVEVNTS</displayName>
          <description>I3C Target Event Status Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x38</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x0000000B</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>SIREN</name>
              <description>Target Interrupt Request Enable Bit\nThis bit reflects whether the Target can initiate the SIR on the I3C bus or not. \nUsually, this bit is set or cleared by the I3C Controller through ENEC or DISEC CCC.\nNote: This bit is read only.</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Target Interrupt Request function Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Target Interrupt Request function Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>MREN</name>
              <description>Controller Request Enable Bit\nThis bit reflects whether the Target can initiate the Controller Request on the I3C bus or not.\nUsually, this bit is set or cleared by the I3C Controller through ENEC or DISEC CCC.\nNote: This bit is read only.</description>
              <bitOffset>1</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Controller Request function Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Controller Request function Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>HJEN</name>
              <description>Hot-Join Request Interrupt Enable Bit\nThis bit reflects whether the Hot-Join Request Interrupts are allowed on the I3C bus or not. \nThe Target application can choose to Disable HJ Capability of the Target (if selected) by setting this bit to 0 before 'Enabling' the Target device. When done so, the Target does not initiate Hot-Join request and takes part in Address Assignment without initiating Hot-Join.\nIf this bit is NOT set to 0 by Target application, it can be set or cleared by the I3C Controller through ENEC or DISEC CCC. Once Disabled by software, CCCs do not have any effect on this field.</description>
              <bitOffset>3</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Hot-Join Request Interrupt function Disabled</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Hot-Join Request Interrupt function Enabled</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ACTSTSTS</name>
              <description>Activity State Status\nThis field reflects the Activity State of the Target set by the Controller.\nNote: This field is read only.</description>
              <bitOffset>4</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>2</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>MRLUPD</name>
              <description>MRL Updated Status\nThis bit indicates a SETMRL CCC is received by the Target. \nThe updated MRL value can be read from MRL (I3C_SLVMXLEN[31:16]).\nNote: This bit is cleared by writing 1 to it.</description>
              <bitOffset>6</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>SETMRL CCC not Received</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>SETMRL CCC Received</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>MWLUPD</name>
              <description>MWL Updated Status\nThis bit indicates a SETMWL CCC is received by the Target. \nThe updated MWL value can be read from MWL (I3C_SLVMXLEN[15:0]).\nNote: This bit is cleared by writing 1 to it.</description>
              <bitOffset>7</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>SETMWL CCC not Received</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>SETMWL CCC Received</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
          <name>I3C_INTSTS</name>
          <!-- the display name is an unrestricted string. -->
          <displayName>I3C_INTSTS</displayName>
          <description>I3C Interrupt Status Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x3C</addressOffset>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>TXTH</name>
              <description>Transmit Buffer Threshold Status\nThis bit is generated when the number of empty locations in transmit buffer is greater than or equal to threshold value specified by TXTH (I3C_DBTHCTL[2:0]).\nNote 1: This bit is read only.\nNote 2: This bit is cleared automatically when number of empty locations in transmit buffer is less than threshold value specified.</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>The number of receive buffer is less than to threshold value specified by TXTH</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>The number of receive buffer is greater than or equal to threshold value specified by TXTH</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RXTH</name>
              <description>Receive Buffer Threshold Status\nThis bit is generated when number of entries in receive buffer is greater than or equal to threshold value specified by RXTH (I3C_DBTHCTL[10:8]).\nNote 1: This bit is read only.\nNote 2: This bit is cleared automatically when number of entries in receive buffer is less than threshold value specified.</description>
              <bitOffset>1</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>The number of receive buffer is less than to threshold value specified by RXTH</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>The number of receive buffer is greater than or equal to threshold value specified by RXTH</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>IBITH</name>
              <description>IBI Buffer Threshold Status \nThis bit is used in Controller mode of operation.\nThis bit is generated when number of entries in IBI buffer is greater than or equal to threshold value specified by IBISTSTH (I3C_QUETHCTL[31:24]).\nNote 1: This bit is read only.\nNote 2: This bit is cleared automatically when number of entries in IBI buffer is less than threshold value specified.</description>
              <bitOffset>2</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>IBI buffer is not ready</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>IBI buffer is ready</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>CMDRDY</name>
              <description>Command Queue Ready Status\nThis bit is used in Controller mode of operation.\nThis bit is generated when number of empty locations in command queue is greater than or equal to threshold value specified by CMDETH (I3C_QUETHCTL[7:0]).\nNote 1: This bit is read only.\nNote 2: This bit is cleared automatically when number of empty locations in command buffer is less than threshold value specified.</description>
              <bitOffset>3</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Command Queue is not ready</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Command Queue is ready</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RESPRDY</name>
              <description>Response Queue Ready Status\nThis bit is generated when number of entries in response queue is greater than or equal to threshold value specified by RESPTH (I3C_QUETHCTL[15:8]).\nNote 1: This bit is read only.\nNote 2: This bit is cleared automatically when number of entries in response buffer is less than threshold value specified.</description>
              <bitOffset>4</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Response Queue is not ready</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Response Queue is ready</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TFRABORT</name>
              <description>Transfer Abort Status \nThis bit is used in Controller mode of operation.\nThis bit is generated if transfer is aborted.\nNote: This bit is cleared by writing 1 to it.</description>
              <bitOffset>5</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Transfer is not aborted</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Transfer is aborted</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>CCCUPD</name>
              <description>CCC Table Updated Status\nThis bit is used in Target mode of operation.\nThis bit is generated if any of the CCC registers are updated by I3C Controller through CCC commands.\nNote: This bit is cleared by writing 1 to it.</description>
              <bitOffset>6</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Related CCC registers are not updated</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Related CCC registers are updated</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>DAA</name>
              <description>Dynamic Address Assigned Status\nThis bit is used in Target mode of operation.\nThis bit is generated if the Target Dynamic Address is assigned through ENTDAA, SETDASA, SETNEWDA or SETAASA CCC.\nNote: This bit is cleared by writing 1 to it.</description>
              <bitOffset>8</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Dynamic Address is not assigned</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Dynamic Address is assigned</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TFRERR</name>
              <description>Transfer Error Status\nThis bit is generated if any error occurs during transfer. \nThe error type is specified in the response packet associated with the command, ERRSTS field in Response Quene Port register.\nIn Controller mode of operation, ERRSTS in I3C_RESPQUE[31:28]. \nIn Target mode of operation, ERRSTS in I3C_RESPQUE[11:8].\nNote: This bit is cleared by writing 1 to it.</description>
              <bitOffset>9</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Transfer error is not occurred</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Transfer error occurred</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>DEFTGTS</name>
              <description>DEFTGTS CCC Received Status\nThis bit is used in Secondary Controller mode of operation.\nNote: This bit is cleared by writing 1 to it.</description>
              <bitOffset>10</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>DEFTGTS (DEFSLVS) CCC is not received</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>DEFTGTS (DEFSLVS) CCC is received</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>READREQ</name>
              <description>Read Request Received Status\nThis bit is used in Target mode of operation.\nRead Request received from the current Controller when EXTCMD is empty. \nNote: This bit is cleared by writing 1 to it.</description>
              <bitOffset>11</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>IBIUPD</name>
              <description>IBI Status Updated Status\nThis bit is used in Target mode of operation\nIt indicates that the IBI request initiated through SIR register is addressed and status is updated.\nNote: This bit is cleared by writing 1 to it.</description>
              <bitOffset>12</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>IBI status is not updated</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>IBI status is updated</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BUSOWNER</name>
              <description>Bus Owner Updated Status\nThis bit is set when the role of the device changes from being a Controller to Target or vice versa.\nNote 1: This bit is cleared by writing 1 to it.\nNote 2: The new Bus Owner value is updated only if the device's RESUME (I3C_DEVCTL[30]) bit is set.</description>
              <bitOffset>13</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Bus Owner status is not changed</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Bus Owner status is changed</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BUSRSTDN</name>
              <description>Bus Reset Pattern Generation Done Status\nThis bit is used in Controller mode of operation.\nThis bit is generated when the Bus Reset Pattern Generation is completed.\nNote: This bit is cleared by writing 1 to it.</description>
              <bitOffset>15</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Bus Reset Pattern Generation is not complete</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Bus Reset Pattern Generation is complete</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>STADET</name>
              <description>START Detection Status\nThis bit is used in Target mode of operation\nThis bit is provided for the Target application to determine whether a START signal is detected on the bus.\nNote: This bit is cleared by writing 1 to it.</description>
              <bitOffset>16</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>START signal no Detected</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>START signal Detected</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RSTPTDET</name>
              <description>Target Reset Pattern Detection Status\nThis bit is used in Target mode of operation. \nThis bit is generated when the Target Reset Pattern is detected.\nNote: This bit is cleared by writing 1 to it.</description>
              <bitOffset>17</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Target Reset Pattern no Detected</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Target Reset Pattern Detected</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>GRPADDRA</name>
              <description>Group Address Assigned Status\nThis bit is used in Target mode of operation. \nThis bit is generated if one of the Target's Group Address is assigned through SETGRPA CCC or reset through RSTGRPA CCC.\nNote: This bit is cleared by writing 1 to it.</description>
              <bitOffset>18</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Group Address Assigned status no Changed</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>Group Address Assigned status Changed</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SDARES</name>
              <description>SDA Released From Stuck State Status\nThis bit is used in Target mode of operation.\nThis bit is generated if the SCL was not changed for the prescribed amount of time defined by RELSDATC (I3C_RELSDA[19:0]).\nNote: This bit is cleared by writing 1 to it.</description>
              <bitOffset>19</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>Bus in Idle state</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>SDA status is released from stuck state</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>EXTFINS</name>
              <description>EXTCMD Has Finished Status\nThis bit is used in Target mode of operation. \nThis bit is generated if Read Transfer has finished (I3C_EXTCMDFS[7:0] not equal to 0) by the EXTCMD.\nNote 1: This bit is read only.\nNote 2: This bit is cleared automatically when I3C_EXTCMDFS register is read.</description>
              <bitOffset>20</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>No EXTCMD finished the transfer. (I3C_EXTCMDFS[7:0] is equal to 0)</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>EXTCMD Read Transfer has finished. (I3C_EXTCMDFS[7:0] is not equal to 0)</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>EXTTXTH</name>
              <description>EXTCMD Transmit Buffer Threshold Status\nThis bit is used in Target mode of operation. \nNote 1: This bit is read only.\nNote 2: This bit is cleared automatically when number of empty locations in all the EXTCMD transmit buffers is less than threshold value specified.</description>
              <bitOffset>21</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <enumeratedValues>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>0</name>
                    <description>The number of EXTCMD transmit buffer empty locations is less than threshold value TXEBTH (I3C_EXTTHLD[18:16])</description>
                    <value>#0</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
                 <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>1</name>
                    <description>The number of EXTCMD transmit buffer empty locations is greater than or equal to threshold value TXEBTH (I3C_EXTTHLD[18:16])</description>
                    <value>#1</value>
                 </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
       </register>
       <register>
